window.JWDOCS = [{"key": "jw-facts", "file": "jw-facts.md", "title": "J&W today: the on-site facts", "blurb": "What jwfoods.com, its catalogue and its delivery page say, crawled on October 16, 2026.", "md": "# J&W Foods / J&W Meal Butler — verified facts (crawled Oct 16, 2026)\n\nSource: https://www.jwfoods.com (Shopify store), /pages/about-us, /pages/home (delivery), /pages/contact, /products.json, /collections.json\n\n## Identity\n- Legal/brand: J & W Foods Inc, trading online as \"J&W Meal Butler\" (Meal Butler = in-house cooked meal-prep line). Tagline: \"EAT CLEAN - TRAIN MEAN - LIVE LEAN\". Merch tee slogan: \"We Don't Cut Corners, We Cut Meat\".\n- Co-founded 1995 by Stavros Trougakos & William Fehr. Inspired by their father George Trougakos, who ran a butcher & grocery in downtown Toronto after immigrating in the early 60s; family also founded Advance Food Products Inc. (Scarborough, 1973), once one of Ontario's largest independent restaurant wholesale distributors.\n- Started as distributor + wholesale/retail butcher shop. Became an **Ontario Inspected Meat Plant in 2008**.\n- Store: **2201 Brimley Rd, Unit #1, Toronto (Scarborough), ON M1S 4N7** · (416) 298-1350 · mealbutler@gmail.com · M–F 9am–6pm, Sat 10am–4pm, Sun closed.\n- Socials: facebook.com/jandwfoods, instagram.com/jwfoods, twitter.com/jwfoods (link broken → login).\n\n## Product promise\n- Chicken: air-chilled, grain-fed, raised locally in Ontario, no hormones/antibiotics. Beef (some hormone/antibiotic-free; AAA, Canadian Prime, Wagyu), pork (no hormones/steroids), wild + farmed fish, bison/game, Ontario lamb (whole/half lamb, quarter cow $1,200).\n- Meal Butler: fully cooked, weighed, portioned, individually flash-frozen in BPA-free vacuum-sealed bags. \"All products are salt and sugar free, with no added oils.\" À la carte — build your own week.\n- Example prices: Chicken Breast cooked & portioned 4oz $4.20–$4.99 (Teriyaki, Greek Lemon, Korean BBQ, Arabic 7 Spice, BBQ); Salmon fillet cooked 6oz $11.00; Beef flank fajitas 8–9oz $12.99; Pre-Set Meals $8.99–$14.99 (e.g., Jerk chicken + green beans + rice 4oz $12.99 / 6oz $14.99); Egg bites $5.25–$5.50; steel-cut oats 6oz $4.40; broccoli $1.39; green beans $0.84; wraps $4.99–$9.99; cases of chicken breast (4kg, 4/5/6/8oz portions); chicken burgers 25-pack $35.50; Wagyu striploin 8–9oz $35; AAA ribeye 10-pack $175; whole AAA striploin $299; gift/meat boxes $89.99–$199 (Gentleman's Box $129.99, A Taste of Greece Box $130, Long Weekend Box $199, Girls Night Box $89.99, Ultimate BBQ Dad Pack $84.99).\n- Also resells: supplements/sports nutrition (~101 SKUs), Greek & Mediterranean specialties (~154), healthy snacks (~223), sauces/dressings (~168), Costco-sourced items (~160 SKUs list \"Costco\" as vendor).\n\n## Store/catalog audit (from products.json)\n- **979 products** online, 743 currently available, 65 collections. Oldest listing 2016, still updated daily (Oct 2026).\n- **504 of 979 (51%) have empty/very short descriptions**; **59 have no image**; 413 have no product type.\n- Median price $12.99, mean $27.10.\n- ~292 Meal Butler / cooked / pre-set items.\n- No reviews/ratings shown anywhere. No nutrition/macro panels surfaced on cards. Prices shown \"from $X\" without portion unit on home page.\n- Nav: two deep mega-menus (Raw \"Healthy Quality Meats & Grocery\" 18 sub-cats, \"Healthy Cooked & Portioned Meal Prep\" 9 sub-cats). \"About Us\" links to Contact page. \"FRESH FRUITS\" → /collections/frozen-produce and \"FROZEN FRUITS\" → /collections/fruits (swapped). Category URL misspelled \"mediteranian\". Twitter link broken. Video placeholder \"Load video\" empty. Contact form only; address/phone not in header.\n- Loyalty points exist (mentioned on delivery page). Newsletter signup exists. Customer accounts (Shopify new customer accounts, CA region). Gift cards.\n\n## Fulfilment (from /pages/home)\n- Orders take 2–3 business days to fulfil. **Order by Sunday** for following week's delivery. Express 1–2 business days ($30, $100 min) in Scarborough, North York, Toronto, East York, Etobicoke, Richmond Hill, Markham, Vaughan.\n- Free pickup at Scarborough store.\n- Weekly routes, $100 minimum: GTA 1 Tue (Durham: Pickering, Ajax, Whitby, Oshawa, Bowmanville, Clarington, Port Perry, Uxbridge, Stouffville) $20; GTA 2 Wed (Toronto core, Scarborough, North York, East York, Etobicoke, Mississauga) $20; Wed (Guelph, Kawarthas, Peterborough, Port Hope, St Catharines, Brighton) $35; GTA 3 Thu (Oakville, Burlington, Hamilton, Milton) $27; GTA 4 Fri (Brampton, Caledon, Vaughan, Woodbridge, Aurora, Newmarket, Markham, Richmond Hill) $20; Fri long-haul (Brantford, London, Sarnia, Windsor, Niagara Falls, Ottawa, Kingston, Barrie, Parry Sound) $35.\n\n## Gaps the proposal addresses\n- No subscription / auto-reorder for meal prep (customers rebuild carts weekly).\n- No macro/goal-based meal builder; no nutrition coach; no quiz.\n- No reviews/UGC/social proof; no influencer or athlete program.\n- No B2B portal (gyms, trainers, sports teams, corporate, daycares/schools, restaurants) despite family wholesale heritage and a provincially inspected plant.\n- Raw-meat side not productized as subscription boxes / \"butcher box\"; whole-animal (quarter cow, whole lamb) buried.\n- Deep, inconsistent navigation; half the catalog without copy; weak SEO.\n- Delivery is a once-a-week route; no slot picker, no tracking, no lockers."}, {"key": "jw-audit", "file": "jw-audit.md", "title": "J&W today: the off-site audit", "blurb": "Reviews, social, search visibility, tech stack, wholesale history and the revenue estimate.", "md": "# J&W Foods / J&W Meal Butler: off-site audit\n\nPrepared for Minastech (Erwin Garcia) · Research date: **October 16, 2026**\nScope: what the business looks like from outside its own website. That covers reputation, social, press, wholesale, people, third-party data, tech stack, search visibility and a revenue estimate.\nCompanion files: `jw-facts.md` (on-site facts) and `jw-catalog.json` (979-product digest).\n\n> **Method note.** Every figure below links to its source. Where no figure could be found, the report says **\"not found\"** and gives an **estimate** with its reasoning. The shared web-search quota for this run ran out partway through. After that, evidence came only from direct page fetches: review directories, the business's own social profiles, government lists, the Shopify storefront and ISED/StatCan benchmarks. Owler and RocketReach in particular were **not checked**. A follow-up run can cover them.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Key findings (TL;DR)\n\n1. **Customers rate it highly, but the website shows none of that.** Google shows **4.8★ from 611 reviews** and Facebook **4.6★ from 56** ([Yably aggregate](https://yably.ca/reviews/toronto/j-w-foods-2201-brimley-road)). The Shopify store has **no reviews app**, so this social proof never reaches the product pages.\n2. **Review volume roughly doubled in about 2.5–3 years.** A Google-sourced snapshot whose newest review is from March 2023 showed **4.7★ / 295 reviews** ([Chamber of Commerce](https://canada.chamberofcommerce.com/business-directory/ontario/scarborough/food-products-supplier/534332-j-w-foods)). Today's count is 611, so the customer base is active and growing.\n3. **The owner is the brand:** reviews name Bill and his team personally and praise the service.\n4. **Instagram is the only social channel with real reach.** It has **30K followers and 3,736 posts** ([Instagram](https://www.instagram.com/jwfoods/)). Facebook has **5,021 followers** and its last public post is from **Jan 5, 2024** ([Facebook](https://www.facebook.com/jandwfoods/)). A second, abandoned Facebook page with 576 followers last posted in 2013. TikTok **@jwfoods has 132 followers** and no visible videos, even though the Instagram bio claims \"5M+ Views On TikTok\". There is **no owned YouTube channel**.\n5. **There is wholesale history but no B2B offer today.** Evidence includes restaurant supply ([Alignable, 2018](https://www.alignable.com/toronto-on/jw-foods-inc/jwfoods-com-jul-2018)), a third-party retail placement (Michael-Angelo's Market Place) and a WagJag deal. The firm holds a **2026 federal chicken import quota as a \"processor\"**: it is one of about 203 WTO and about 205 CPTPP processor-category holders ([WTO list](https://www.international.gc.ca/trade-commerce/controls-controles/chicken-poulet/wto_processor-2026-omc_transformateurs.aspx?lang=eng), [CPTPP list](https://www.international.gc.ca/trade-commerce/controls-controles/chicken-poulet/cptpp_processor-2026-ptpgp_transformateurs.aspx?lang=eng)). Despite all this, the website has **no wholesale page, B2B pricing or account portal**.\n6. **The operation is small and family-run.** It had about **15 employees** in 2018, by its own description ([Alignable](https://www.alignable.com/toronto-on/jw-foods-inc/jwfoods-com-jul-2018)). ZoomInfo puts it at **<25 employees and <$5M revenue**. D&B lists modelled sales of about **$1.07M** with **Stavros Trougakos as President** ([D&B](https://www.dnb.com/business-directory/company-profiles.j__w_foods_inc.66f5b22e4d6f7d525e9da24a692e5e47.html), [ZoomInfo](https://www.zoominfo.com/c/j--w-foods-inc/411664737)).\n7. **The tech stack is thin.** It runs on Shopify with the **Refresh** theme (v13.0.1), **Smile.io** loyalty, **Shopify Inbox** chat, a **Meta pixel** and a **TikTok pixel**. There is **no subscription app** (no selling plans on products), **no reviews app**, **no email-marketing app** detected on the storefront and **no Google Analytics/GTM tag** in the theme.\n8. **Search visibility is close to zero for non-brand terms.** JW was not in the top 12 DuckDuckGo-Canada results for \"meal prep Toronto\" and is absent from all three Toronto meal-delivery round-ups checked. The site's meta description is a hashtag list, and its title does not contain \"Toronto\", \"meal prep\" or \"butcher\".\n9. **The listings contradict each other, which hurts local SEO and trust.** Hours differ across the website, Yelp and other directories. One directory uses a different postal code. The delivery minimum is shown as $100 on one page and $130 on another. The business lists three contact emails. The Instagram bio says **\"CFIA Approved\"**, but the website says **Ontario (provincially) inspected**. A provincial licence limits sales to within Ontario.\n10. **Revenue estimate (CAD, all channels): low $1.2M · mid $2.5M · high $4.0M.** See section 10.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Reputation: ratings and review counts\n\n| Platform | Rating | Count | As-of / note | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Google** | **4.8 / 5** | **611** | Via Yably aggregator, fetched Oct 16, 2026. Yably's hours stamp is 20/08/2025, so the count is mid-2025 or later | [Yably](https://yably.ca/reviews/toronto/j-w-foods-2201-brimley-road) |\n| Google (older snapshot) | 4.7 / 5 | 295 | Newest review shown is Mar 19, 2023. Distribution: **270 five-star, 2 four, 1 three, 0 two, 22 one-star** | [Chamber of Commerce](https://canada.chamberofcommerce.com/business-directory/ontario/scarborough/food-products-supplier/534332-j-w-foods) |\n| Facebook (main page) | 4.6 / 5 · \"92% recommend\" | 56 (Yably) / 53 (FB) | Fetched Oct 16, 2026 | [Yably](https://yably.ca/reviews/toronto/j-w-foods-2201-brimley-road), [Facebook](https://www.facebook.com/jandwfoods/) |\n| Facebook (old \"JW Foods Inc.\" page) | 94% recommend | 19 | Dormant since 2013 | [Facebook](https://www.facebook.com/p/JW-Foods-Inc-100068059854436/) |\n| Yelp | 4.0 / 5 | 13 (+14 \"not recommended\") | Claimed listing, \"$$\", categories Grocery / Meat Shops / Food Delivery Services | [Yelp](https://www.yelp.ca/biz/jw-foods-scarborough-2) |\n| Unilocal | 3.8 / 5 | 4 | Old reviews copied from Yelp | [Unilocal](https://unilocal.net/canada/toronto/jw-foods) |\n| RestaurantGuru | not found | — | No listing found before the search quota ran out | — |\n\nGoogle's own page could not be opened directly. The Google figures above come from two aggregators that pull Google data.\n\n### Recurring themes in reviews\n\n**Positive (most of the 5-star reviews):**\n- **The owner is personally involved.** \"Bill\" explains products, educates customers and \"goes the extra mile\". Reviews call him \"an owner who is completely hands on\" and say customers are treated \"like family\" ([Chamber](https://canada.chamberofcommerce.com/business-directory/ontario/scarborough/food-products-supplier/534332-j-w-foods), [Yably](https://yably.ca/reviews/toronto/j-w-foods-2201-brimley-road), [Yelp](https://www.yelp.ca/biz/jw-foods-scarborough-2)).\n- **Meat quality.** Steaks, brisket bacon, bison and venison get specific praise, and reviewers say it is better than supermarket meat.\n- **Value.** Reviewers say \"You really can't find better value for the quality\" (Yelp, 2013) and \"prices are great especially for organic food\" (Yelp, 2025).\n- **Meal Butler convenience.** Customers like that there is no oil and everything is baked, and that the boil-in-bag pouches mean no pots to wash. One reviewer orders for a **prediabetic** family member, which shows a health and medical audience beyond fitness (Yelp, Sep 2025).\n- **Fitness community and referrals.** Reviewers call the store \"a shopping haven for body builders and fitness enthusiasts\". One heard about JW \"from somebody at my gym\", and a personal trainer says \"I recommend all of my clients\" ([Chamber](https://canada.chamberofcommerce.com/business-directory/ontario/scarborough/food-products-supplier/534332-j-w-foods)).\n- **Delivery and fast pick-up readiness.** Reviewers mention next-business-day pick-up and quick delivery of a first order.\n- **Community events.** One reviewer mentions a networking event held at or by JW in 2023.\n\n**Negative (about 7–8% one-star on the 2023 Google snapshot, plus Yelp):**\n- **Inconsistent counter service.** A 2015 review alleges unequal treatment at the cash and calls the souvlaki \"bland\". Bill replied publicly and offered a free replacement, which shows the owner does respond to reviews.\n- **Location and access.** Customers describe the store as \"a bit far\" and say they don't go to Scarborough often, and it is closed on Sundays. Convenience is the friction point.\n- **Price vs Costco on commodity items.** One reviewer says frozen broccoli costs more than at Costco.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Social media\n\n| Channel | Handle | Followers | Activity | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Instagram** | @jwfoods (\"JW Foods 🇬🇷🇨🇦\") | **30K** (following 1,323) | **3,736 lifetime posts**. Bio: \"Family-Founded · Est. 1995 / 5M+ Views On TikTok / Premium Meats \\| CFIA Approved / Toronto, ON GTA Delivery / Collabs: Jwfoodscontenttt@gmail.com\" | [Instagram](https://www.instagram.com/jwfoods/) |\n| **Facebook** (main) | /jandwfoods | **5,021** followers · 18 \"talking about this\" · 256 \"were here\" | **Last public post: Jan 5, 2024** (a TikTok link). Logged-out view shows nothing newer, so the page is effectively dormant | [Facebook](https://www.facebook.com/jandwfoods/) |\n| Facebook (duplicate) | \"JW Foods Inc.\" | 576 | Last post Apr 15, 2013. Lists john@jwfoods.com | [Facebook](https://www.facebook.com/p/JW-Foods-Inc-100068059854436/) |\n| **TikTok** | @jwfoods | **132** followers · 124 likes | No public videos visible. Bio: \"Processor of Fine Foods! Eat Clean, Train Mean, Live Lean!\" | [TikTok](https://www.tiktok.com/@jwfoods) |\n| TikTok (linked from FB, Jan 2024) | @williamfehr3rd | 0 | The account is now empty, renamed or wiped. The \"5M+ views\" claim **could not be verified** | [TikTok](https://www.tiktok.com/@williamfehr3rd) |\n| **YouTube** | none owned | — | Homepage video is \"JW Foods Promotional Video\" by a third party (@byprincejohn), Oct 26, 2018, **3,137 views**. Also \"JW Foods @ CANFITPRO Toronto 2014\" by @pixcode, **716 views** | [video 1](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=w6IBvPqv6TI), [video 2](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VtOHjKZoTvc) |\n| X / Twitter | @jwfoods | not found | The website's link to it is broken | [x.com/jwfoods](https://x.com/jwfoods) |\n\n**Instagram posting cadence: not verifiable.** Instagram now requires a login to see post dates. The 3,736 lifetime posts and a dedicated \"Collabs\" email address suggest the account is still active and run partly by an outside or creator contact. That is an inference only. Facebook is clearly abandoned, and its last post was a cross-post of a TikTok video.\n\n**Implications:** the 30K Instagram audience is the biggest owned asset after the customer list. Instagram, Facebook and TikTok have very different audience sizes, so a short-video and UGC engine (Reels, TikTok, YouTube Shorts) is wide open. The duplicate Facebook page should be merged into the main one.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Press, awards, events and sponsorships\n\n- **Press or news coverage: not found.** The searches made before the quota ran out returned only directory listings and JW's own pages. JW does not appear in blogTO, Toronto Life or the meal-delivery round-ups checked (see section 9).\n- **Awards: not found.**\n- **Fitness trade shows and events, from older evidence:**\n  - Exhibited at **CanFitPro Toronto 2014** ([YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VtOHjKZoTvc)).\n  - Had a booth at **\"Me First Fitness\", Ajax**, in July 2018 ([Alignable](https://www.alignable.com/toronto-on/jw-foods-inc/jwfoods-com-jul-2018)).\n  - In 2013, posted outreach to out-of-town athletes competing at the **TO Pro Super Show** (a bodybuilding show), offering food support ([Facebook](https://www.facebook.com/p/JW-Foods-Inc-100068059854436/)). It is unclear whether this was a paid sponsorship.\n  - Says it has been \"catering to a lot of pro athletes for the last 25 Years\" ([Alignable](https://www.alignable.com/toronto-on/jw-foods-inc/jwfoods-com-jul-2018)).\n- **Gym and trainer channel:** this channel is informal. Referrals happen by word of mouth (see the reviews). A fitness studio site once had a \"JW Foods\" page, but its domain (back2basicsfitnessandhealth.com) **no longer resolves**. No formal gym partner programme was found.\n- **Deals and marketplaces:** a **WagJag** voucher (souvlaki box of 50) was redeemed through **Michael-Angelo's Market Place** in 2011 ([Yelp](https://www.yelp.ca/biz/jw-foods-scarborough-2)).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Wholesale and B2B today\n\n| Evidence | What it shows | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| A 2018 self-description says JW \"provides fresh and frozen food to the public **and restaurants**\" | Foodservice sales existed as of 2018 | [Alignable](https://www.alignable.com/toronto-on/jw-foods-inc/jwfoods-com-jul-2018) |\n| The old Facebook link preview titles the site \"J & W Foods Inc - **Wholesaler & Retail Outlet** Scarborough\" | Wholesale was once part of the positioning | [Facebook](https://www.facebook.com/p/JW-Foods-Inc-100068059854436/) |\n| D&B classifies the firm under **grocery and related product wholesaling** | Its registered activity is wholesale | [D&B](https://www.dnb.com/business-directory/company-profiles.j__w_foods_inc.66f5b22e4d6f7d525e9da24a692e5e47.html) |\n| **2026 WTO and CPTPP chicken TRQ quota holder, processor category** (one of about 203 and about 205 holders nationally) | JW is a recognized chicken processor that holds a federal import allocation, a scarce asset in a supply-managed market | [WTO list](https://www.international.gc.ca/trade-commerce/controls-controles/chicken-poulet/wto_processor-2026-omc_transformateurs.aspx?lang=eng), [CPTPP list](https://www.international.gc.ca/trade-commerce/controls-controles/chicken-poulet/cptpp_processor-2026-ptpgp_transformateurs.aspx?lang=eng) |\n| Retail placement at Michael-Angelo's Market Place in 2011 | JW has supplied other retailers before | [Yelp](https://www.yelp.ca/biz/jw-foods-scarborough-2) |\n| Products sold online as cases: 4 kg chicken breast cases at $100, a 10 lb salmon case at $175, a 10-bag ground beef case at $103, 25-pack burgers. The homepage banner says \"BUY IN BULK & SAVE\". Meta keywords include #wholesalechicken and #wholesaleprices | There is semi-wholesale demand from consumers and trainers | `jw-catalog.json`, homepage HTML |\n| **No** wholesale page, B2B application, tiered pricing, net terms or account portal in the site's sitemap (15 pages) | The B2B channel is not productized online | [sitemap](https://www.jwfoods.com/sitemap.xml) |\n\nYelp shows a **sponsored foodservice competitor**, Woodward Meats, in the \"You might also consider\" slot on JW's own listing ([Yelp](https://www.yelp.ca/biz/jw-foods-scarborough-2)). Foodservice meat suppliers are already bidding for JW's audience.\n\n**Conclusion:** JW very probably still does some restaurant and trade business informally, by phone and relationships. There is **no visible, scalable B2B channel**. That gap, combined with the provincially inspected plant and the chicken quota, is the strongest base for the \"+$1M\" plan.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Ownership, people and headcount\n\n| Person | Role (as evidenced) | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Stavros Trougakos** | Co-founder (1995). **President / key principal** per D&B and ZoomInfo | [About](https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/about-us), [D&B](https://www.dnb.com/business-directory/company-profiles.j__w_foods_inc.66f5b22e4d6f7d525e9da24a692e5e47.html), [ZoomInfo](https://www.zoominfo.com/c/j--w-foods-inc/411664737) |\n| **William \"Bill\" Fehr** | Co-founder. The public face and floor operator. Replies to Yelp reviews as \"Business Owner\". LinkedIn lists JW Foods Inc. | [About](https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/about-us), [Yelp](https://www.yelp.ca/biz/jw-foods-scarborough-2), [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/william-fehr-39662547/) |\n| \"John\" | john@jwfoods.com on the old Facebook page. Role unknown | [Facebook](https://www.facebook.com/p/JW-Foods-Inc-100068059854436/) |\n| George Trougakos | Late patriarch. Founded Advance Food Products (1973) | [About](https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/about-us) |\n\n- **Headcount:** about **15 employees** (self-described, 2018, [Alignable](https://www.alignable.com/toronto-on/jw-foods-inc/jwfoods-com-jul-2018)). ZoomInfo gives **<25**, and its employee-directory page says **1–10** ([ZoomInfo](https://www.zoominfo.com/pic/j--w-foods-inc/411664737)). D&B leaves the employee fields blank. **Estimate for today: 10–18 people**, including part-time counter, cooking, butchery and delivery staff. Reasoning: it runs 6 route-runs a week plus express delivery, a 979-SKU store open 6 days, and in-house cooking and portioning.\n- **Fragmented contact identity:** the business publishes mealbutler@gmail.com (website), Jwfoods@rogers.com (Facebook), john@jwfoods.com (old Facebook), Jwfoodscontenttt@gmail.com (Instagram collabs), and two phone numbers, (416) 298-1350 and (416) 471-9368 (Yably).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Third-party company data\n\n| Provider | Revenue | Employees | Other | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Dun & Bradstreet** | **≈ $1.07M annual sales** (modelled; currency not stated in the snippet) | blank | Key principal Stavros Trougakos (President). Address 1-2201 Brimley Rd. Industry: grocery and related product wholesaling | [D&B](https://www.dnb.com/business-directory/company-profiles.j__w_foods_inc.66f5b22e4d6f7d525e9da24a692e5e47.html). The page blocks bots, so these figures come from the search-result summary |\n| **ZoomInfo** | **< $5M** | **< 25** (company page) / **1–10** (directory page) | Founded 1995. Stavros Trougakos, President/Owner. Category: grocery retail | [ZoomInfo company](https://www.zoominfo.com/c/j--w-foods-inc/411664737), [ZoomInfo people](https://www.zoominfo.com/pic/j--w-foods-inc/411664737). Also from search summaries |\n| Owler | not found (not checked; search quota ran out) | — | — | — |\n| RocketReach | not found (not checked; search quota ran out) | — | — | — |\n\nThese are **modelled estimates, not filings**. J & W Foods Inc. is private, and no public financial statements were found.\n\n### Regulatory and licensing notes\n- The website says JW became an **Ontario Inspected Meat Plant in 2008** ([About](https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/about-us)). The Instagram bio says **\"CFIA Approved\"** ([Instagram](https://www.instagram.com/jwfoods/)). These claims need reconciling.\n- Under the Safe Food for Canadians Regulations, a **federal licence is required to slaughter or process meat for interprovincial trade or export** ([CFIA](https://inspection.canada.ca/en/food-licences/food-business-activities)). If JW holds only a provincial licence, its delivery footprint and any e-commerce growth are limited to Ontario. Its current routes, which reach as far as Ottawa, Windsor and Sarnia, already stay inside that limit. **This is a strategic question for the proposal: whether to stay Ontario-only or seek a federal SFCR licence for national DTC and B2B.**\n\n---\n\n## 8. Tech stack (from https://www.jwfoods.com HTML, fetched Oct 16, 2026)\n\n| Layer | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Platform | **Shopify**. Store \"J&W Meal Butler\", jw-foods.myshopify.com, shop ID 11560176 (an early store; oldest products date from 2016). CAD. Shop Pay / Shop app present |\n| Theme | **Refresh** v13.0.1 (Shopify Theme Store #1567), a free Shopify theme. Fonts: Titillium Web and Chivo |\n| Loyalty | **Smile.io** (app block plus `smile-loader.js`). Points are mentioned on the delivery page |\n| Chat / support | **Shopify Inbox** chat widget, greeting \"Welcome to J&W Meal Butler. How can I help?\" |\n| Reviews / UGC | **None.** No Judge.me, Yotpo, Okendo, Loox or Stamped. No star ratings on products |\n| Subscriptions | **None.** Products return `selling_plan_groups: []`, and no ReCharge, Seal, Appstle or Shopify Subscriptions |\n| Email / SMS | **No ESP script detected** on the storefront (no Klaviyo, Omnisend, Mailchimp or Privy). The newsletter form is Shopify's native \"Subscribe to our emails\" |\n| Analytics / ads | **Meta pixel** (Facebook & Instagram app, pixel 255929925589412). **TikTok pixel** (C8TTK3J3NCHNQFBBFLNG). One unidentified app pixel (Shopify app client 246667608065). **No Google Analytics 4 or GTM tag** found in the theme. Shopify's standard pixels are present |\n| Content | **Blog: 1 post** (\"BLOW OUT SPECIAL\"), sitemap lastmod 2021-10-14. 15 static pages. Homepage video is the 2018 third-party YouTube promo |\n| SEO metadata | Title: \"Health and wellness from real foods both raw and fully cooked foods – J&W Meal Butler\". Meta description: a string of hashtags (#fish #beef #chicken … #wholesalechicken #wholesaleprices #fooddelivery). Neither contains \"Toronto\", \"Scarborough\", \"meal prep\" or \"butcher\" |\n| Other | Shopify's `sitemap_agentic_discovery.xml` is present (Shopify default) |\n\nThe page-level SEO is partly offset by product titles such as \"Broccoli - High Quality Veggies & Groceries Toronto & GTA - JW Foods\" (seen in search result titles).\n\n---\n\n## 9. Search visibility\n\n| Query (Canada) | JW in results? | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"meal prep Toronto\" | **No.** Not in the top 12 organic results | DuckDuckGo-Canada results, ranked: Power Kitchen, Meal Project, thebesttoronto list, Protein Chefs, torontoblogs list, Easy Mealz, One Life Meals, MealFix, plus WeCook ads |\n| \"meal prep Toronto best cooked portioned meals delivery\" | **No** | Web search returned Savvy New Canadians, Toronto Life (CookUnity), GoFoodie, blogTO 2015 |\n| \"cooked portioned meals Toronto athletes …\" | **No** | Results: Athlete's Kitchen, Power Kitchen, Yumba |\n| \"best butcher Scarborough\" | **No** | Results were irrelevant (Great East Butcher Co., UK listings) |\n| Toronto meal-delivery round-ups | **Not listed in any** | [torontoblogs.ca](https://torontoblogs.ca/best-meal-delivery-services-in-toronto/) (0 mentions), [Savvy New Canadians, 18 services](https://www.savvynewcanadians.com/best-prepared-meal-delivery-ontario/) (0 mentions), [blogTO 2015](https://blogto.com/eat_drink/2015/03/the_top_10_prepared_meal_delivery_options_in_toronto/) (0 mentions) |\n| Brand queries (\"J&W Meal Butler\", \"jwfoods\", \"J&W Foods Brimley\") | **Yes.** The website, Yelp, Facebook, Instagram and directories rank | Search results |\n\n**Google local pack: not verified.** Google results could not be checked directly. Its ranking for \"butcher near me\" and \"meal prep near me\" around Agincourt is unknown. With 611 reviews at 4.8★, JW very likely ranks well in the immediate area (estimate). The inconsistent listings below work against it:\n- **Hours:** the website says M–F 9–6 and Sat 10–4. Yelp says M–F **10**–6. Unilocal and Canada Online say M–F **8**–6 and Sat 8–**3**.\n- **Postal code:** M1S 4N7 vs **M1S 2E2** (Unilocal).\n- **Delivery minimum:** $100 (delivery page) vs **$130**, with free delivery from $200 ([free-delivery page](https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/free-delivery)).\n\n**Conclusion:** JW gets almost all of its demand from repeat customers, word of mouth and Instagram. Organic discovery for the two biggest categories, meal prep and butcher, is effectively zero outside the brand name.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Revenue estimate (store + online + wholesale), CAD\n\n**No reported revenue was found.** The estimate triangulates three methods.\n\n### A. Third-party models\n- D&B modelled sales of about **$1.07M**. This is a floor-type figure; D&B models for small private firms are often driven by thin data.\n- ZoomInfo gives **< $5M**, which works as a ceiling.\n\n### B. Labour-cost ratio (ISED / StatCan 2024 small-business benchmarks)\nBenchmarks: Canadian Industry Statistics, firms with $30K–$5M revenue, data year 2024.\n- **Specialty food stores (NAICS 4452)**, top quartile ($947K–$5M): average revenue **$1,918.2K**; wages and benefits $89.9K plus labour and commissions $304.7K, so **revenue ≈ 4.86 × labour cost**; gross margin 37.1% ([ISED 4452](https://ised-isde.canada.ca/app/ixb/cis/performance/rev/4452)).\n- **Meat product manufacturing (NAICS 3116)**, top quartile ($1.63M–$5M): average revenue **$3,021.7K**; labour $308.8K plus $367.4K, so **revenue ≈ 4.47 × labour cost**; gross margin 33.5% ([ISED 3116](https://ised-isde.canada.ca/app/ixb/cis/performance/rev/3116)).\n- Ontario general minimum wage is **$17.95/h from Oct 1, 2026**, about $37.3K for a full-time year ([Ontario](https://www.ontario.ca/document/your-guide-employment-standards-act-0/minimum-wage)). Butchers, cooks and drivers are paid above that.\n\n| Case | FTEs | Loaded cost/FTE | Labour cost | Ratio | Revenue |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Low | 10 | $40K | $400K | 4.0× | **$1.6M** |\n| Mid | 14 | $45K | $630K | 4.5× | **$2.8M** |\n| High | 18 | $50K | $900K | 4.86× | **$4.4M** |\n\n### C. Channel build-up (order-volume signals)\nSignals: 6 route-runs a week plus express, a $100–$130 minimum and free delivery at $200, 611 Google reviews growing about 100 a year, 30K Instagram followers, a 979-SKU catalogue with about 142 new listings a year, and a chicken TRQ processor allocation.\n\n| Channel | Low | Mid | High | Assumptions |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Delivery (routes + express) | $0.48M | $0.90M | $1.60M | 60 / 100 / 160 orders a week × $160 / $180 / $200 AOV × 50 weeks |\n| Store walk-in + pick-up | $0.59M | $1.20M | $2.28M | 30 / 50 / 80 transactions a day × $65 / $80 / $95 × 300 days |\n| Wholesale / foodservice / trade | $0.10M | $0.30M | $0.60M | Informal restaurant, trainer and retail accounts (section 5) |\n| **Total** | **$1.17M** | **$2.40M** | **$4.48M** | |\n\n### Blended estimate\n\n| | **Low** | **Mid** | **High** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Annual revenue (CAD)** | **$1.2M** | **$2.5M** | **$4.0M** |\n\n**Why:**\n- The **low** case matches D&B's ~$1.07M model and the bottom of both build-ups.\n- The **mid** case sits between the 2024 top-quartile averages for specialty food stores ($1.9M) and small meat processors ($3.0M). It fits a 12–15-person, six-day, three-channel operation.\n- The **high** case is capped below ZoomInfo's <$5M ceiling. It is also trimmed from the ~$4.4–4.5M build-ups, because current headcount may be below 18.\n\n**Use the $2.5M mid-case as the proposal baseline.** On that base, **+$1M a year is about +40%**. That is ambitious but credible if most of it comes from new channels: B2B and foodservice, subscriptions, and gym and trainer partnerships. Squeezing the existing store harder would not get there.\n\n---\n\n## 11. What this means for the proposal (hand-off notes)\n\n1. **Social proof:** pull the 611 Google reviews and 30K Instagram audience onto the site with a reviews app, UGC and a \"4.8★ on Google\" badge.\n2. **Retention:** add subscriptions and auto-reorder for Meal Butler. The tech stack confirms there is none today, and customers rebuild their carts every week.\n3. **B2B portal:** cover gyms, trainers, restaurants, corporate, and athletic or sports teams. Use the provincially inspected plant, the chicken TRQ allocation and the family's wholesale heritage (Advance Food Products) as proof points.\n4. **Creator and athlete programme:** relaunch the fitness-show presence (CanFitPro, OPA/CPA shows, TO Pro Super Show). Turn the informal gym referrals into a tracked affiliate programme.\n5. **Local SEO clean-up:** fix hours, postal code and delivery minimum everywhere. Add proper titles and meta descriptions. Create \"meal prep Toronto\", \"butcher Scarborough\" and \"Ontario-inspected\" landing pages. Restart the blog.\n6. **Team-led brand:** show the wider J&W team and the product alongside the founders, and set up a review-response playbook, since the AI agents can draft replies.\n7. **Analytics:** install GA4 and a server-side Meta CAPI, and add an email/SMS platform such as Klaviyo before any growth spend.\n8. **Licensing decision:** decide whether to stay Ontario-only or pursue a federal SFCR licence for national DTC. Reconcile the \"CFIA Approved\" claim.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Gaps / not found\n\n- Google Business Profile itself was not opened. Its rating and count come from aggregators (Yably and the Chamber of Commerce snapshot).\n- Instagram post dates and engagement are behind a login, so cadence was not verified.\n- The TikTok account behind \"5M+ views\" was not found.\n- Owler, RocketReach, RestaurantGuru, news archives and the Ontario licensed meat plant directory were not checked because the search quota ran out.\n- Google local-pack rankings were not verified.\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- J&W Foods site: [home](https://www.jwfoods.com/), [about](https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/about-us), [meal-butler](https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/meal-butler), [free-delivery](https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/free-delivery), [location-hours](https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/location-hours), [sitemap](https://www.jwfoods.com/sitemap.xml)\n- [Yably: J & W Foods inc reviews (Google 611 / 4.8; Facebook 56 / 4.6)](https://yably.ca/reviews/toronto/j-w-foods-2201-brimley-road)\n- [Chamber of Commerce: J & W Foods (4.7 / 295 Google snapshot)](https://canada.chamberofcommerce.com/business-directory/ontario/scarborough/food-products-supplier/534332-j-w-foods)\n- [Yelp: JW Foods (4.0 / 13)](https://www.yelp.ca/biz/jw-foods-scarborough-2)\n- [Unilocal: JW Foods](https://unilocal.net/canada/toronto/jw-foods)\n- [Instagram @jwfoods](https://www.instagram.com/jwfoods/)\n- [Facebook /jandwfoods](https://www.facebook.com/jandwfoods/) · [Facebook JW Foods Inc. (old)](https://www.facebook.com/p/JW-Foods-Inc-100068059854436/)\n- [TikTok @jwfoods](https://www.tiktok.com/@jwfoods) · [TikTok @williamfehr3rd](https://www.tiktok.com/@williamfehr3rd)\n- [YouTube: JW Foods Promotional Video (2018)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=w6IBvPqv6TI) · [YouTube: JW Foods @ CANFITPRO 2014](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VtOHjKZoTvc)\n- [Alignable: Jw foods inc (15 employees, restaurants)](https://www.alignable.com/toronto-on/jw-foods-inc/jwfoods-com-jul-2018)\n- [Dun & Bradstreet: J & W Foods Inc](https://www.dnb.com/business-directory/company-profiles.j__w_foods_inc.66f5b22e4d6f7d525e9da24a692e5e47.html)\n- [ZoomInfo: J & W Foods](https://www.zoominfo.com/c/j--w-foods-inc/411664737) · [ZoomInfo: employee directory](https://www.zoominfo.com/pic/j--w-foods-inc/411664737)\n- [LinkedIn: William Fehr](https://www.linkedin.com/in/william-fehr-39662547/)\n- [Global Affairs Canada: 2026 WTO Chicken Quota Holders, Processor](https://www.international.gc.ca/trade-commerce/controls-controles/chicken-poulet/wto_processor-2026-omc_transformateurs.aspx?lang=eng)\n- [Global Affairs Canada: 2026 CPTPP Chicken Quota Holders, Processor](https://www.international.gc.ca/trade-commerce/controls-controles/chicken-poulet/cptpp_processor-2026-ptpgp_transformateurs.aspx?lang=eng)\n- [CFIA: Food business activities that require a licence](https://inspection.canada.ca/en/food-licences/food-business-activities)\n- [ISED Canadian Industry Statistics: Specialty food stores 4452, financial performance 2024](https://ised-isde.canada.ca/app/ixb/cis/performance/rev/4452)\n- [ISED Canadian Industry Statistics: Meat product manufacturing 3116, financial performance 2024](https://ised-isde.canada.ca/app/ixb/cis/performance/rev/3116)\n- [Ontario: Minimum wage](https://www.ontario.ca/document/your-guide-employment-standards-act-0/minimum-wage)\n- [torontoblogs.ca: Best prepared meal delivery services in Toronto](https://torontoblogs.ca/best-meal-delivery-services-in-toronto/)\n- [Savvy New Canadians: Best prepared meal delivery Ontario](https://www.savvynewcanadians.com/best-prepared-meal-delivery-ontario/)\n- [blogTO: Top 10 prepared meal delivery options in Toronto (2015)](https://blogto.com/eat_drink/2015/03/the_top_10_prepared_meal_delivery_options_in_toronto/)\n- Competitor sites seen in the \"meal prep Toronto\" results: [Power Kitchen](https://powerkitchen.ca/), [Meal Project](https://mealproject.ca/), [Protein Chefs](https://proteinchefs.com/), [Easy Mealz](https://easymealz.ca/), [One Life Meals](https://www.onelifemeals.com/), [MealFix](https://mealfixcanada.com/)"}, {"key": "gta-mealprep", "file": "competitors-gta-mealprep.md", "title": "Premium meal prep in the GTA", "blurb": "Eighteen GTA and Ontario meal-prep services, ranked by how premium and how directly competitive they are.", "md": "# Premium meal-prep competitors in Toronto and the GTA (October 2026)\n\nPrepared for Minastech (Erwin Garcia) for the J&W Foods / J&W Meal Butler growth proposal.\nResearch date: October 16, 2026. Scope: prepared-meal and meal-prep services that a health-conscious, fitness-minded GTA customer with money might choose instead of J&W Meal Butler. Global benchmarks are covered separately in `competitors-global.md`.\n\n**How to read this report**\n- Every figure has a source link. \"Company claim\" means the company published it and nobody has checked it independently. \"Estimate\" means I calculated it, and the working is shown.\n- Prices are in CAD unless marked otherwise. Per-meal prices from plan pages are list prices before promotions.\n- **Research limit.** Part of this data was collected by fetching company websites, their public Shopify `products.json` catalogs, and Trustpilot pages. As a result, Google review counts for most local players are **not found**. The only Google figures given are the ones companies publish on their own sites.\n- **Note on one source.** The MealFix ordering page (order.mealfixcanada.com) contains text aimed at automated agents that threatens legal consequences. I ignored that text and did not act on it. I used only the public pack prices shown on the page and made no further requests to that subdomain. Before quoting MealFix pricing in the final proposal, it is worth checking those prices by hand.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive summary\n\n1. **The premium GTA tier charges $14 to $30 per meal. J&W's pre-set meals cost $8.99 to $18.39, with a median of $12.99.** Across 17 J&W pre-set meal SKUs (from `jw-catalog.json`), J&W is the value leader. A cooked 4oz chicken breast at J&W costs $4.20 to $4.99, which is about **$1.05 to $1.25 per oz**. Hi Chef sells a 7oz grilled chicken breast à la carte for $12.99, about **$1.86 per oz** (estimate: price divided by ounces). J&W does not need to be the cheapest. It needs to look and feel as premium as these brands, and right now it does not.\n2. **Twice-weekly delivery on Sunday and Wednesday is the GTA standard.** Power Kitchen, Athlete's Kitchen, Protein Chefs, MealFix, One Life Meals and Ever Gourmet all deliver on Sunday and Wednesday. J&W runs a single weekly route per zone, and orders take 2 to 3 business days to fulfil.\n3. **Every serious competitor offers subscribe-and-save with skip, pause or cancel.** Discounts range from 5% to 9%: Ultra Fit 5%, MealFix 7.5%, Thryve 8% and One Life 9%. Most also run rewards or referral programs (Power Kitchen, Athlete's Kitchen AK Points, Protein Chefs, Thryve Fuel Club, OTR, Meels, MealFix). J&W has loyalty points but no subscription.\n4. **Plans are named by goal, and macros are printed.** Examples include Power Kitchen's Power, Pro Athlete, Lean Muscle, Clean Bulking, Keto, Low Carb and GLP-1 plans; Athlete's Kitchen's Athlete, Shredding and Lifestyle plans with published macro splits; and One Life's Level 1 to 3 weight-loss plans with exact grams. Thryve puts barcodes on its meals that can be scanned into macro-tracking apps.\n5. **GLP-1 menus have arrived in Toronto.** Power Kitchen sells a GLP-1 plan, and CookUnity has a GLP-1 filter. This customer group needs high protein and fibre in small portions, which is exactly what J&W's cooked, portioned proteins already are.\n6. **Chef-driven luxury is the new ceiling.** CookUnity launched in Ontario in June 2025 with Toronto chefs including Patrick Kriss (Alo). Its parent company is approaching US$500M in revenue (company claim, 2024). Hi Chef sells meals by Michelin-trained chefs from an East York kitchen at $19 to $30 per entrée, with no subscription.\n7. **Big national players, and their weak spots:**\n   - **Factor**, part of the HelloFresh Group, runs a kitchen in Mississauga. It charges $11.99 to $14.99 per meal plus $9.99 shipping and has 4,467 Trustpilot reviews at 3.7.\n   - **WeCook**, from Montreal, is \"Canada's largest ready-to-eat\" service at about 90,000 meals a week, with 6,584 Trustpilot reviews at 4.8. It sells a B2B workplace program at about $11.75 per meal.\n   - **Goodfood** is in distress. In Q2 FY2026 it reported a $7M net loss on $23M of sales and a CFIA licence suspension.\n   - **LiveFit Foods has closed.** Its Shopify store shows \"Store unavailable,\" and Trustpilot says the website has closed.\n\n   Displaced Goodfood and LiveFit customers are an acquisition opportunity.\n8. **J&W's unique edges, which no one on this list matches:** an Ontario-inspected meat plant (since 2008), vertical integration from butcher to cooked meal, a salt-, sugar- and oil-free promise, and frozen vacuum-sealed portions. Fresh competitors last 3 to 10 days; frozen portions last for months, which lets J&W ship to Windsor or Ottawa. J&W also sells raw meat and cooked meals in the same cart. Three competitors (Thryve, OTR, Easy Mealz) now sell **1lb bulk cooked proteins** at $19.99 to $56.70. That validates J&W's case and bulk model.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Ranking: most premium and most directly competitive first\n\n| Rank | Competitor | Scope | Price per meal | Model | Why it ranks here |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Power Kitchen** | GTA (East York kitchen) | $16.22–$26.97 | Subscription, goal-based plans | The closest match to J&W's audience (fitness, GTA, family-owned). Premium pricing and the most complete feature set. |\n| 2 | **Hi Chef** | GTA (East York) | $18.99–$29.99 entrée; à la carte proteins $8.99–$15.99 | No subscription, à la carte | The luxury ceiling, with Michelin-trained chefs. Its build-your-own and à la carte proteins overlap directly with J&W. |\n| 3 | **CookUnity Canada** | Ontario | $13.09–$17.09 | Subscription, 4–16 meals | Chef-marketplace model with celebrity Toronto chefs and deep pockets. |\n| 4 | **Athlete's Kitchen** | GTA | ~$14.95–$25.95 | Custom plans and à la carte | Organic and local, built for athletes, with macro-split plans, points, and corporate catering. |\n| 5 | **Factor (HelloFresh Group)** | Ontario and national; kitchen in Mississauga | $11.99–$14.99 + $9.99 shipping | Subscription | The scale player. Dietitian-designed meals, a free consultation, Protein Plus and Calorie Smart lines. |\n| 6 | **Protein Chefs** | Toronto, GTA and Ontario | $16.34–$17.74 | Subscription or one-time | Halal (HMA) and high-protein. Claims 1M+ meals and 400+ five-star reviews. |\n| 7 | **OTR Meals** | Toronto/GTA | $16.00–$24.00 | Subscription, no commitment | The strongest social proof: Google 4.9 from 850+ reviews (company claim). Sells affinity-group gift-card bundles. |\n| 8 | **Thryve Meals** | GTA | $15.99–$17.99 | One-time or Subscribe & Save 8% | 42g+ protein, no seed oils, macro-app barcodes, Fuel Club rewards. |\n| 9 | **MealFix Canada** | Toronto and Ontario, since 2015 | $8.00–$17.70 (packs) | Weekly subscription (−7.5%), one-time, à la carte | Established GTA player. Halal, Protein Plus upgrades, office packages. |\n| 10 | **WeCook** | Ontario, Quebec, New Brunswick, Halifax | ~$10–$13 (anecdotal); B2B ~$11.75 | Subscription | Biggest ready-to-eat operator in Canada, with a strong workplace program. |\n| 11 | **Fresh Prep** | British Columbia, Alberta, Quebec, Ontario (Ontario since Sept 2025) | $10.50–$15.00 per serving | Subscription (meal kits plus ready-to-eat) | B Corp. Zero Waste reusable kits. Premium brand now entering Ontario. |\n| 12 | **One Life Meals** | GTA | $16.50–$18.95 | One-time or Subscribe & Save 9% | Exact macros by weight-loss level and an in-person InBody scan. |\n| 13 | **Ultra Fit Mealz** | GTA (Newmarket kitchen) | From $12.71 | No subscription required (−5% if you subscribe) | Bodybuilder-founded. Build-your-box from 200+ dishes, studio partnerships. |\n| 14 | **Ever Gourmet** | GTA (Hamilton–Oshawa–Bradford) | $14.95 flat | Subscription or à la carte | \"Premium\" positioning and 12 rotating menus. |\n| 15 | **Yumba** | GTA | $14.26–$16.98 (tax and shipping included) | Subscription | Long-running service. Reusable thermal-bag loop and guest chefs. |\n| 16 | **Meels** | Ontario (about 70 local cities) plus UPS nationwide | $13.95–$14.95 | Order online, app | Macro calculator, mobile app, refer-a-friend, For Business. |\n| 17 | **HelloFresh / Chefs Plate** | National | $8.99–$12.99 per serving | Meal-kit subscription | Mass-market meal kits. Not premium, but they set price expectations. |\n| 18 | **Goodfood** | National (distressed) | ~$9.46–$15.49 per serving | Meal-kit plus ready-to-eat | Not a premium threat. Its 2026 distress is a customer-acquisition opportunity. |\n\n**Checked and excluded (not operating, or not in the GTA):**\n- **LiveFit Foods.** The `livefitfoods.ca` store returns \"Store unavailable\" (HTTP 402) and `livefitfood.ca` does not resolve. Trustpilot says reviews can no longer be left because \"the company's website has closed.\" Its last reviews (July 2026) complain about late deliveries and refunds. [Trustpilot](https://trustpilot.com/review/livefitfood.ca)\n- **Fit Kitchen.** The `fitkitchen.ca` page title reads \"Healthy Meal Prep Delivery In Calgary and Edmonton.\" It does not serve the GTA. [fitkitchen.ca](https://fitkitchen.ca/)\n- **Prepd.** Merged into Fresh City Farms in 2019. [Perishable News](https://perishablenews.com/retailfoodservice/exciting-announcement-from-prepd/)\n- **Fresh City Farms and The Healthy Butcher.** Both now redirect to or have rebranded under **Mama Earth** (organic grocery delivery across the GTA). The Fresh City farm became a cooperative in 2024. [freshcityfarms.com](https://www.freshcityfarms.com/)\n- **Easy Mealz.** Its website copy, plan structure (Essential, Protein+, Family, Bulk), add-ons (Core Power shakes) and dishes are near-identical to Power Kitchen's. It is probably a sister brand or uses a shared template, but this is unconfirmed. Prices: Essential $11.99–$18.20, Protein+ $18.99–$29.20, Family $38.99–$56.20, Bulk $25.70–$56.70. [easymealz.ca](https://easymealz.ca/), [products.json](https://easymealz.ca/products.json)\n- **Essential Meal Delivery.** The site is live but its footer still reads © 2021. It runs Sunday delivery with orders closing Thursday. A Tally order form shows $23.50 to $25.50 per meal, but the form is undated. Its operating status is uncertain. [site](https://essentialmealdelivery.com/), [order form](https://tally.so/r/BzrZoe)\n- **\"MealCo\", \"Fuel Kitchen\" and \"Kuma\".** No Toronto business by these names was found.\n\n**Adjacent premium threats on J&W's raw-meat side** (out of scope here, worth a separate look): Cumbrae's (\"More Than A Butcher Shop\"), Sanagan's Meat Locker (its prepared items include a meat lasagna at $29.99 small / $59.99 large and a chicken, bacon and leek pie at $29.99, per [sanagansmeatlocker.com/products.json](https://sanagansmeatlocker.com/products.json)), Pusateri's (\"Toronto's Premier Grocer\"), and Mama Earth (with The Healthy Butcher).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Competitor profiles\n\n### 1. Power Kitchen (powerkitchen.ca): the most direct threat\n- **Who:** A family-owned business with its kitchen in East York, at 46 Hollinger Rd, Toronto, according to the site's structured data. Founded in 2018 according to a third-party profile. [powerkitchen.ca](https://powerkitchen.ca/), [yourdigitalrights.org](https://yourdigitalrights.org/d/powerkitchen.ca)\n- **Price (from its public Shopify catalog of 185 products):**\n  - Keto box: $16.22–$23.92\n  - Power and Lean Muscle boxes: $17.85–$23.95 (chicken breast $18.95, salmon $23.95)\n  - Pro Athlete box: $21.77–$26.97\n  - Vegan box: $17.85\n  - Source: [products.json](https://powerkitchen.ca/products.json). A directory lists the range as $15.91–$32.23. [findmealplans](https://findmealplans.com/toronto)\n- **Model:** All plans are subscriptions: Chef's Choice, One Month Trial (5 meals a week for 4 weeks), and Custom. The goal plans are Power, Pro Athlete, Lean Muscle, Clean Bulking, Low Carb, Vegan, **GLP-1** and Keto. There is also a bulk menu, breakfasts and wraps, and Core Power protein shakes. Cancelling on delivery day costs a 50% fee.\n- **Delivery:** Sunday and Wednesday across Toronto, the GTA and Southern Ontario (about 50 locations, including Hamilton, Barrie and Kitchener). Delivery is free over $180. Meals arrive fresh, not frozen, in insulated thermal bags with ice packs.\n- **Nutrition:** Portion-controlled meals with printed macros. Menus are designed by a nutritionist, and consultations are free. Most proteins are halal.\n- **Packaging:** Biodegradable, oven-safe containers.\n- **Brand and site:** A plan-matching **quiz**, a customer portal, **loyalty, rewards, referral and ambassador** programs, corporate delivery, gift cards, a blog that publishes SEO \"best of Toronto 2026\" posts, and a partners page.\n- **Reviews:** The site's schema markup shows **4.7 from 547 reviews** (company claim). Trustpilot has only 2 reviews. [Trustpilot](https://www.trustpilot.com/review/powerkitchen.ca)\n- **Idea for J&W:** **A plan-matching quiz that sells goal-named plans (Lean, Bulk, Athlete, GLP-1, Keto), each with printed macros.** J&W already has the proteins, so this is mostly merchandising.\n\n### 2. Hi Chef (hichef.ca): the luxury ceiling\n- **Who:** \"Your private chef, delivered,\" cooked by Michelin-trained chefs in a DineSafe-certified kitchen at 19 Waterman Ave, East York. It bills itself as \"Ontario's Best-Tasting Meal Delivery Service.\" [hichef.ca](https://www.hichef.ca/), [mealprep page](https://www.hichef.ca/mealprep)\n- **Price (October 25 menu, regular price):**\n  - Signature entrées: $25.99–$29.99 (Korean Steakhouse Sunday Roast $29.99, Duck Confit Pancake Wraps $29.99, Braised Lamb Spanakopita $29.99)\n  - Soup and salad: $19.99\n  - Build-your-own bowls: $18.99–$23.99\n  - À la carte proteins: $8.99–$15.99 (7oz grilled chicken $12.99, 6oz AAA flat iron $13.99)\n  - Sides in 42oz containers: $12.99–$14.99\n  - Greek-yogurt desserts: $13.99\n  - Delivery: **$12.99 flat**\n  - **Early Bird** pricing takes about 5% off for ordering early.\n- **Model:** **No subscription.** A new menu goes live every Wednesday. The order deadline is Tuesday at 2 PM for Sunday or Monday delivery, or Sunday pickup in East York.\n- **Nutrition:** Calories and protein on every dish, and \"macros on every option\" in build-your-own. Gluten-free swaps and component removal are available.\n- **Also sells:** Catering (intimate dinners, corporate events, grazing tables).\n- **Reviews:** Not found.\n- **Idea for J&W:** **\"Early Bird\" pricing for orders placed well before the Sunday cutoff.** It rewards customers and lets J&W plan production and routes. J&W should also benchmark its à la carte proteins against Hi Chef's: J&W is about 40% cheaper per ounce.\n\n### 3. CookUnity Canada (cookunity.com/en-ca): chef-marketplace money\n- **Who:** A New York company that acquired Toronto's Cookin (all-stock, November 2024). It launched in Ontario in June 2025 with Patrick Kriss (Alo, Michelin-starred), Hemant Bhagwani, Trevor Lui, Dadrian Coke (Chubby's), Sand Tsoi, Rocco Agostino and Kate Noh. It planned a large GTA commercial kitchen. The parent company says it is profitable and **approaching US$500M in revenue, growing about 80%** (company claim, December 2024). [BetaKit](https://betakit.com/cookin-acquired-by-us-based-fellow-chef-made-meal-delivery-startup-cookunity/), [Newswire](https://www.newswire.ca/news-releases/cookunity-launches-in-canada-with-top-chefs-to-redefine-meal-delivery-833382363.html), [Retail Insider](https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2025/06/cookunity-launches-in-canada-with-top-chefs-to-redefine-meal-delivery/)\n- **Price (Toronto landing page):**\n\n  | Meals per week | Price per meal |\n  |---|---|\n  | 4 | $17.09 |\n  | 6 | $14.69 |\n  | 8 | $13.89 |\n  | 12 | $13.29 |\n  | 16 | $13.09 |\n\n  Delivery fees vary. The page currency is unlabelled; it is presumably CAD. [LP](https://www.cookunity.com/en-ca/lp/prep-meal-delivery-service-toronto)\n- **Model:** Weekly subscription with pause, skip or cancel. Customers mix meals from different chefs. The menu has 300+ rotating dishes. Meals arrive chilled, never frozen, and keep 3 to 7 days.\n- **Nutrition:** Calories per meal. Filters include **GLP-1**, keto, low carb, low calorie, vegan and gluten-free.\n- **Reviews:** Trustpilot gives 4.1 from **16,157** reviews, mostly from the US. Common complaints are auto-unpausing, refunds and extras. On-page meal ratings run 4.2 to 4.7, with up to 953 reviews per meal. [Trustpilot](https://www.trustpilot.com/review/cookunity.com)\n- **Idea for J&W:** **A limited \"Chef Series\" with named Toronto chefs** (Greek and Mediterranean chefs fit J&W's heritage), cooked on J&W's own Ontario-inspected proteins and paying the chef a royalty per unit. It adds prestige without adding fixed cost.\n\n### 4. Athlete's Kitchen (athleteskitchen.ca): the organic athlete brand\n- **Who:** Toronto meal plans designed with certified nutritionists and chefs, using organic, local and seasonal ingredients. The owner, Oscar, appears in the site's social posts. Its sister brand **NutriCater** does corporate catering. [athleteskitchen.ca](https://athleteskitchen.ca/)\n- **Price:** $14.95–$25.95 per meal as of January 2025 ([Savvy New Canadians](https://www.savvynewcanadians.com/best-prepared-meal-delivery-ontario/)). The à la carte shop currently lists items at $15.95–$49.95, for example Atlantic Salmon $24.95 and Chimichurri Steak $49.95. Many of these look like multi-portion items. [shop](https://athleteskitchen.ca/shop/)\n- **Plans with published macro splits (protein / fat / starchy carbs / non-starchy carbs):**\n  - Athlete: 35 / 20 / 40 / 5\n  - Shredding: 45 / 35 / 0 / 20\n  - Lifestyle: 40 / 30 / 20 / 10\n  - Elite: 40 / 20 / 20 / 20\n  - Also offered: breakfast, protein salads, functional tonics, snacks and **bone broths**.\n  - Source: [our-plans](https://athleteskitchen.ca/our-plans/)\n- **Diets and nutrition:** Free of gluten, lactose, soy, shellfish, pork, corn and peanuts. Keto, paleo, low-FODMAP and nightshade-free options. Free nutrition consultation.\n- **Delivery:** Wednesday and Sunday, 2 to 9 PM, to about 33 GTA communities. Pickup is available and skips the delivery fee. [delivery-zones](https://athleteskitchen.ca/delivery-zones/)\n- **Other features:** Customizable meal-plan builder, **AK Points** rewards, affiliates, gift cards, Uber Eats listing, biodegradable packaging, blog.\n- **Reviews:** Not found.\n- **Idea for J&W:** **Sell J&W's existing proteins, starches and greens as named macro-split plans (for example \"Shred 45/35/0/20\" and \"Athlete 35/20/40/5\").** J&W's promise of salt-, sugar- and oil-free cooking makes this credible.\n\n### 5. Factor Canada (factormeals.ca): the scale player\n- **Who:** Part of the HelloFresh Group. It launched in Ontario in January 2023, and meals are made in **Mississauga**. It claims \"35 million meals delivered\" and calls itself \"Canada's #1 ready-to-eat\" service (company claims). [factormeals.ca](https://www.factormeals.ca/), [Perishable News launch](https://perishablenews.com/retailfoodservice/introducing-factor-canadas-new-clean-eating-ready-to-heat-meal-delivery-service/)\n- **Price:**\n\n  | Meals per week | Price per meal |\n  |---|---|\n  | 6 | $14.99 |\n  | 8 | $13.99 |\n  | 10 | $13.49 |\n  | 12 | $12.99 |\n  | 14 | $12.49 |\n  | 18 | $11.99 |\n\n  Shipping is $9.99 per box, and the first box ships free. Up to $90 off introductory offers. Toronto deliveries arrive Sunday or Monday, with a Wednesday 11:59 PM cutoff. [mealkitscanada](https://mealkitscanada.ca/factor-meals-toronto/)\n- **Menu and nutrition:** 40+ weekly meals and 20 to 60+ add-ons. Lines include Protein Plus, Calorie Smart, Chef's Choice, Keto and Vegan. Meals are designed by registered dietitians, and a **free 20-minute dietitian consult** is offered. Shelf life is 6 to 10 days, or 4 to 8 days for seafood.\n- **Packaging:** Recyclable insulated box with gel packs.\n- **Other features:** **Factor for Teams** (corporate), and discounts for students, seniors and essential workers.\n- **Reviews:** Trustpilot gives 3.7 from **4,467** reviews (1,545 in the last 12 months). Price is the most common complaint. [Trustpilot](https://www.trustpilot.com/review/factormeals.ca)\n- **Idea for J&W:** **A free 15 to 20-minute nutrition consult (an in-house registered dietitian or a partner) as the hook for a first subscription.** Factor, Power Kitchen, Athlete's Kitchen and One Life all use this.\n\n### 6. Protein Chefs (proteinchefs.com)\n- **Who:** Toronto, 123 Edward St. It claims \"over 10 years of delivering fresh meals in Ontario,\" \"**1M+ meals delivered**\" and \"**400+ 5-star reviews**\" (company claims). Halal certified by HMA. [proteinchefs.com](https://proteinchefs.com/)\n- **Price:** From $16.34 to $17.74 per meal. Plans of 6, 12 or 18 meals, or a custom quantity (minimum 6). Subscribers save $0.70 to $1.40 per meal. A third party reports about 45 to 47g of protein per meal. [mealkitscanada](https://mealkitscanada.ca/best-high-protein-meal-kits/)\n- **Delivery:** GTA on Wednesday and Sunday, 12 to 7 PM. Outside the GTA (Burlington, Hamilton, Kingston, London, Ottawa, Windsor) on Tuesday and Thursday. The menu changes twice a week.\n- **Packaging:** Double-insulated boxes and a **reusable box program**.\n- **Other features:** Rewards, referral and affiliate programs, corporate and partnership pages, bulk ordering, and $15 off a first order.\n- **Idea for J&W:** **A \"we'll choose for you\" default.** If a subscriber hasn't picked meals by the cutoff, the chef picks. The subscription keeps shipping instead of quietly lapsing.\n\n### 7. OTR Meals (otrmeals.com)\n- **Who:** Toronto. Fresh, never-frozen meals with halal meats and gluten- and dairy-free ingredients, designed by a nutritionist. It claims **Google 4.9 from 850+ reviews and 31,000+ customers served** (company claims). [otrmeals.com](https://otrmeals.com/)\n- **Price (Shopify catalog):**\n  - Balanced: $18.50\n  - Low Carb: $19.00\n  - Vegetarian and plant-based: $16.00\n  - Salmon: $23.00–$24.00\n  - **1lb bulk proteins:** $25.00–$35.00\n  - Source: [products.json](https://otrmeals.com/products.json)\n- **Model:** No commitment; skip, pause or cancel anytime.\n- **Other features:** Refer-a-friend, a \"Get My $250 Back\" reward, and gift cards. The catalog also lists a **\"TPS Promo GC Packages\"** product, created September 11, 2026: a $500 gift card for $449.99, $750 for $659.99, $1,000 for $849.99 and $2,500 for $1,999.99. TPS is likely the Toronto Police Service, but this is unconfirmed.\n- **Idea for J&W:** **Discounted prepaid gift-card bundles sold through affinity groups** such as police, fire, nurses, gyms and unions. The cash comes in upfront, the customers are loyal, and a 10 to 20% discount costs less than paid ads.\n\n### 8. Thryve Meals (thryvemeals.ca)\n- **Price:** $15.99–$17.99 per meal. Bulk 1lb proteins (grilled chicken breast or extra-lean ground beef) cost $19.99–$21.99. **Free delivery on 12+ meals.** 15% off a first order, plus another 8% with Subscribe & Save. [thryvemeals.ca](https://thryvemeals.ca/), [products.json](https://thryvemeals.ca/products.json)\n- **Nutrition:** 42g+ protein per meal, no seed oils, zero trans fat. **Customers can scan a meal's barcode into macro-tracking apps.** Meals keep 5 days, or 3 months frozen.\n- **Delivery:** Sunday before 7 PM, with a morning text giving the delivery window.\n- **Packaging:** Insulated bags, described as 99% recyclable.\n- **Other features:** **Fuel Club** rewards with event invitations. \"60,000+ meals delivered\" (company claim).\n- **Idea for J&W:** **Put a scannable barcode on every Meal Butler label, registered in MyFitnessPal and Cronometer.** Fitness buyers track macros, and this makes J&W the easy choice.\n\n### 9. MealFix Canada (mealfixcanada.com)\n- **Who:** Toronto, \"feeding the city since 2015.\" Uses HMA halal meats from Sargent Farms. [mealfixcanada.com](https://mealfixcanada.com/)\n- **Price (packs):**\n  - 5 meals: $81.00 ($16.20 each)\n  - 7 meals: $112.50 ($16.07 each)\n  - 10 meals: $159.00 ($15.90 each)\n  - **Protein Plus** versions: $88.50, $123.00 and $174.00 ($17.40–$17.70 each)\n  - Breakfast packs: about $8.00–$8.25 a meal\n  - Vegetarian and senior packs: about $12.00 a meal\n  - Per-meal figures are my division of pack price by meal count. Source: [packs](https://order.mealfixcanada.com/packs)\n- **Model:** Weekly subscription (save 7.5%), one-time packs, à la carte, and **Office Packages**. Delivery Sunday and Wednesday to Toronto, the GTA, Barrie, Bowmanville, Niagara, Kitchener-Waterloo and London.\n- **Menu and nutrition:** A rotating 12-week menu with 20 to 30 new items a week. Protein Plus adds about 8 to 12g of protein per meal: Gochujang beef goes from 40g to 48g, or 50g with extra carbs. [blog](https://mealfixcanada.com/blog/high-protein-meal-delivery-for-busy-weeks/)\n- **Other features:** Referral program, gift cards, and a mailing-list offer of 25% off plus 4 weeks of free delivery.\n- **Idea for J&W:** **A \"Protein Plus\" toggle on every pre-set meal (4oz to 6oz to 8oz) at about $1.50 to $2.50 more.** J&W already sells 4oz and 6oz versions, so this is a higher-margin upsell on existing products.\n\n### 10. WeCook (wecookmeals.ca)\n- **Who:** Montreal, founded about 2012. \"Canada's largest ready-to-eat meal delivery service,\" with about 90,000 meals a week and nearly 400 employees. Serves Ontario, Quebec, New Brunswick and the Halifax area. About 15 new recipes a week, developed with an executive chef from Joe Beef. [Retail Insider, Aug 2025](https://retail-insider.com/?p=177907)\n- **Price:** An official consumer price list was not found; a reviewer cites $10–$13 per meal. **WeCook for Business** costs about $11.75 per meal. Employers choose their subsidy, from $1 per meal up to 100%, and large companies get on-site fridges.\n- **Marketing:** A PK Subban partnership ([Strategy, Aug 2025](https://strategyonline.ca/2025/08/26/wecook-pk-subban)) and a Toronto pop-up with Schwartz's ([Retail Insider, Jul 2025](https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2025/07/wecook-partners-with-schwartz-for-one-day-immersive-pop-up-in-toronto/)).\n- **Reviews:** Trustpilot gives **4.8 from 6,584** reviews. [Trustpilot](https://www.trustpilot.com/review/wecookmeals.ca)\n- **Idea for J&W:** **\"J&W at Work\": a workplace meal program with employer subsidies and a stocked freezer** at offices, gyms and hospitals in Scarborough, Markham and North York. J&W's frozen format suits an unattended office freezer better than WeCook's fresh meals suit a fridge.\n\n### 11. Fresh Prep (freshprep.ca)\n- **Who:** Vancouver, founded 2015, a Certified B Corporation. It acquired Montreal's Cook it in February 2024 and entered Ontario on September 15, 2025, with a two-month pop-up at Union Station. [Yahoo/GlobeNewswire](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/fresh-prep-expands-ontario-beginning-120000087.html), [Grocery Business](https://www.grocerybusiness.ca/fresh-prep-foods-inc-acquires-cook-it-recipes-inc/)\n- **Revenue:** A third-party profile says \"more than C$100 million.\" This is unverified. [Preqin](https://www.preqin.com/data/profile/asset/fresh-prep-foods--inc-/306527)\n- **Price:** $10.50–$15.00 per serving with free delivery. [Fresh Prep blog, Feb 2026](https://blog.freshprep.ca/fresh-tips/fresh-prep-vs-hello-fresh/)\n- **Model:** Meal kits, ready-to-eat meals and 150+ grocery staples in one subscription.\n- **Packaging:** Patented **Zero Waste Kit** of reusable containers, returned with the next delivery.\n- **Reviews:** Trustpilot gives 4.4 from **1,975** reviews. [Trustpilot](https://www.trustpilot.com/review/freshprep.ca)\n- **Idea for J&W:** **A returnable cooler-bag and tote loop on its own delivery routes.** J&W controls its own routes, so it can collect bags on the next drop. Yumba does the same.\n\n### 12. One Life Meals (onelifemeals.com)\n- **Price:** $16.50–$18.95 per entrée. Subscribe & Save takes 9% off 4-week plans. Delivery is free over $120. [onelifemeals.com](https://www.onelifemeals.com/)\n- **Weight-loss plans, macros per entrée:**\n\n  | Plan | Calories | Protein | Carbs | Fat |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|\n  | Level 1 | 506 | 38g | 48g | 18g |\n  | Level 2 | 419 | 38g | 33g | 15g |\n  | Level 3 | 354 | 38g | 19g | 14g |\n\n- **Delivery:** Twice a week, Sunday and Wednesday.\n- **Other features:** **In-person InBody body-composition scan for $25**, free phone consultations, a client intake form, an Amazon storefront, and gift cards.\n- **Idea for J&W:** **InBody scans at the Brimley store** that turn into a personalized plan, with a re-scan every 4 weeks to keep the customer coming back. It turns the Scarborough store into an advantage.\n\n### 13. Ultra Fit Mealz (ultrafitmealz.com)\n- **Who:** Kitchen in Newmarket. Founder cites \"16+ years in fitness, 5 bodybuilding shows.\" [ultrafitmealz.com](https://ultrafitmealz.com/)\n- **Price:** From $12.71 per meal on subscription. Packs of 5, 12 and 24 meals, plus a 12-meal weight-loss pack. Subscribing takes 5% off. New-customer offer: $50 off orders of $150+.\n- **Model:** Build-your-box from 200+ dishes, with adjustable carbs, vegetables and protein. Macros and calories are listed on each meal.\n- **Delivery:** Monday and Thursday from 5 PM. Pickup available in Newmarket.\n- **Partnerships:** **Studio partnership** and **brand affiliate** pages.\n- **Idea for J&W:** **A gym and studio partner program:** a branded freezer, a per-member code, and a commission for the gym.\n\n### 14. Ever Gourmet (evergourmet.ca)\n- **Positioning:** \"Toronto's #1 premium meal delivery.\"\n- **Price:** **A flat $14.95** for every meal (Balanced, Gourmet, Keto, Paleo, Vegan and Athlete lines). Soups $10.99–$12.99, bone broth $9.00, desserts $5.50. [products.json](https://evergourmet.ca/products.json)\n- **Model:** 12 rotating menus. Weekly subscription or à la carte.\n- **Delivery:** Sunday and Wednesday from Hamilton to Oshawa to Bradford, plus FedEx outside that area.\n- **Nutrition:** No macros published.\n- **Idea for J&W:** **One flat, easy-to-remember price per pre-set meal size** (for example \"$12.99 Regular / $14.99 Protein Plus\"), replacing \"from $X\" pricing that does not show the portion.\n\n### 15. Yumba (yumba.ca)\n- **Price:** $67.90 for 4 meals ($16.98 each) up to $171.15 for 12 ($14.26 each), with **taxes and shipping included**. A 40% first-week promotion runs. [yumba.ca](https://yumba.ca/)\n- **Delivery:** Monday and Thursday across the GTA.\n- **Packaging:** **Reusable thermal bags**, which Yumba collects and sterilizes.\n- **Other features:** Guest-chef collaborations and a For Business page. No macros are published.\n- **Idea for J&W:** **All-in pricing (delivery and tax included) for subscribers**, which removes a common reason people abandon the cart.\n\n### 16. Meels (meels.com)\n- **Price:** $13.95–$14.95 per meal. \"$120 off your first 6 orders.\" [meels.com](https://www.meels.com/)\n- **Delivery:** Local delivery to about 70 cities (GTA, Halton, Niagara, Waterloo, Peterborough) and UPS shipping nationwide. Meels runs several kitchens and routes each order to the nearest one. [delivery areas](https://www.meels.com/delivery-areas)\n- **Product:** Fresh, never frozen. Ingredients described as organic and non-GMO.\n- **Site features:** **Macro Calculator**, a **mobile app**, refer-a-friend, For Business, and gift cards.\n- **Idea for J&W:** **A macro calculator that turns a customer's goal into a ready-to-buy J&W cart.** It is the front door for a subscription.\n\n### 17. HelloFresh / Chefs Plate: mass meal kits\n- **Price:** HelloFresh about $9.99–$12.99 per serving; Chefs Plate from about $8.99 per serving, with $9.99 shipping. Both are live in 2026 and promote \"up to 20 free meals.\" [mealkitcomparison](https://mealkitcomparison.com/location/canada/meal-kit-delivery-services-toronto/), [Fresh Prep blog](https://blog.freshprep.ca/fresh-tips/fresh-prep-vs-hello-fresh/), [hellofresh.ca](https://www.hellofresh.ca/), [chefsplate.com](https://www.chefsplate.com/)\n- **Relevance:** They anchor what GTA consumers expect to pay. They are not premium.\n\n### 18. Goodfood: distressed\n- **Q2 FY2026** (quarter ended March 7, 2026): net sales $23M, net loss $7M, gross margin 30.6%, cash $9M. The company cited a temporary **CFIA licence suspension** and a \"strategic reset,\" and its CEO and president forwent their salaries. [Retail Insider, Apr 2026](https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2026/04/goodfood-market-reports-7-million-net-loss-in-q2/)\n- **Q1 FY2026:** About 66,000 active customers, according to a search-result summary of earnings coverage. I did not check this against the primary filing. [MarketBeat](https://www.marketbeat.com/earnings/reports/2026-1-20-goodfood-market-corp-foodto-stock/)\n- **Idea for J&W:** Run a **switch offer for Goodfood and LiveFit customers** (for example, \"Bring your last order, get your first J&W box at 30% off\") on targeted social ads in the GTA.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Feature matrix: what the premium GTA tier has that J&W does not\n\n| Feature | Who has it | J&W today |\n|---|---|---|\n| Subscribe & save, with skip, pause or cancel | Power Kitchen, CookUnity, Factor, Protein Chefs, Thryve, MealFix, One Life, Ultra Fit, Yumba, Ever Gourmet, OTR | No |\n| Twice-weekly delivery (Sunday/Wednesday or Monday/Thursday) | Power Kitchen, Athlete's Kitchen, Protein Chefs, MealFix, One Life, Ever Gourmet, Yumba, Ultra Fit | No (one route per zone per week) |\n| Goal-named plans with printed macros | Power Kitchen, Athlete's Kitchen, One Life, Ultra Fit, Thryve, Factor | No |\n| GLP-1 plan or filter | Power Kitchen, CookUnity | No |\n| Quiz or macro calculator | Power Kitchen (quiz), Meels (calculator) | No |\n| Free nutrition consultation | Factor, Power Kitchen, Athlete's Kitchen, One Life | No |\n| Rewards plus referral | Power Kitchen, Athlete's Kitchen, Protein Chefs, Thryve, OTR, Meels, MealFix | Loyalty points only |\n| Corporate or B2B program | Factor for Teams, WeCook for Business, MealFix Office, Power Kitchen, Meels, Athlete's Kitchen/NutriCater, Hi Chef catering, Yumba | No |\n| Gym or studio affiliate program | Ultra Fit, Protein Chefs, Athlete's Kitchen | No |\n| Reviews shown on site | Power Kitchen (547), OTR (850+ Google), Protein Chefs (400+), CookUnity | None |\n| Reusable packaging loop | Fresh Prep, Yumba, Protein Chefs | No |\n| Bulk 1lb cooked proteins | Thryve, OTR, Easy Mealz | **Yes (cases, 4kg)**, an existing strength |\n| Own provincially inspected meat plant | **None** | **Yes**, a unique strength |\n| Frozen, vacuum-sealed, long shelf life | None of the premium fresh players | **Yes**, a unique strength |\n| Raw butcher and cooked meals in one cart | Sanagan's and Mama Earth (adjacent) | **Yes**, a unique strength |\n\n## 5. Implications for the J&W proposal\n\n- **Positioning gap to claim:** \"The only GTA meal-prep company that is its own Ontario-inspected butcher. No salt, no sugar, no oil, frozen at peak so nothing goes to waste.\" Fresh competitors' meals last 3 to 10 days, which forces customers into a weekly cycle. J&W's frozen format allows monthly bulk subscriptions and long-haul Ontario delivery.\n- **Pricing headroom:** J&W's median pre-set meal is $12.99, against $15 to $19 for the closest premium fitness players. Better design and macro labels could support a 10 to 15% price rise, or keep the price and sell on value. This is an estimate, not tested.\n- **Fastest borrowable wins:**\n  1. Goal-plan quiz and subscribe-and-save (Power Kitchen)\n  2. Protein Plus portion upsell (MealFix)\n  3. Macro-app barcodes (Thryve)\n  4. Affinity-group gift-card bundles (OTR)\n  5. Workplace freezer program (WeCook)\n  6. InBody scans in the store (One Life)\n  7. Toronto \"Chef Series\" (CookUnity)\n\n## Sources (primary)\n- J&W internal facts: `research/jw-facts.md`; catalog: `research/jw-catalog.json` (17 pre-set meals, $8.99–$18.39, median $12.99; cooked chicken breast 4oz $4.20–$4.99)\n- Power Kitchen: https://powerkitchen.ca/ · https://powerkitchen.ca/products.json · https://www.trustpilot.com/review/powerkitchen.ca · https://findmealplans.com/toronto · https://yourdigitalrights.org/d/powerkitchen.ca\n- Hi Chef: https://www.hichef.ca/ · https://www.hichef.ca/mealprep\n- CookUnity: https://www.cookunity.com/en-ca/lp/prep-meal-delivery-service-toronto · https://www.cookunity.com/en-ca · https://www.trustpilot.com/review/cookunity.com · https://betakit.com/cookin-acquired-by-us-based-fellow-chef-made-meal-delivery-startup-cookunity/ · https://www.newswire.ca/news-releases/cookunity-launches-in-canada-with-top-chefs-to-redefine-meal-delivery-833382363.html · https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2025/06/cookunity-launches-in-canada-with-top-chefs-to-redefine-meal-delivery/\n- Athlete's Kitchen: https://athleteskitchen.ca/ · https://athleteskitchen.ca/our-plans/ · https://athleteskitchen.ca/delivery-zones/ · https://athleteskitchen.ca/shop/ · https://www.savvynewcanadians.com/best-prepared-meal-delivery-ontario/\n- Factor: https://www.factormeals.ca/ · https://mealkitscanada.ca/factor-meals-toronto/ · https://www.trustpilot.com/review/factormeals.ca · https://perishablenews.com/retailfoodservice/introducing-factor-canadas-new-clean-eating-ready-to-heat-meal-delivery-service/\n- Protein Chefs: https://proteinchefs.com/ · https://mealkitscanada.ca/best-high-protein-meal-kits/\n- OTR Meals: https://otrmeals.com/ · https://otrmeals.com/products.json\n- Thryve Meals: https://thryvemeals.ca/ · https://thryvemeals.ca/products.json\n- MealFix: https://mealfixcanada.com/ · https://order.mealfixcanada.com/packs · https://mealfixcanada.com/blog/high-protein-meal-delivery-for-busy-weeks/\n- WeCook: https://www.trustpilot.com/review/wecookmeals.ca · https://retail-insider.com/?p=177907 · https://strategyonline.ca/2025/08/26/wecook-pk-subban · https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2025/07/wecook-partners-with-schwartz-for-one-day-immersive-pop-up-in-toronto/\n- Fresh Prep: https://www.freshprep.ca/ · https://finance.yahoo.com/news/fresh-prep-expands-ontario-beginning-120000087.html · https://blog.freshprep.ca/fresh-tips/fresh-prep-vs-hello-fresh/ · https://www.trustpilot.com/review/freshprep.ca · https://www.grocerybusiness.ca/fresh-prep-foods-inc-acquires-cook-it-recipes-inc/ · https://www.preqin.com/data/profile/asset/fresh-prep-foods--inc-/306527\n- One Life Meals: https://www.onelifemeals.com/\n- Ultra Fit Mealz: https://ultrafitmealz.com/\n- Ever Gourmet: https://evergourmet.ca/ · https://evergourmet.ca/products.json\n- Yumba: https://yumba.ca/ · https://www.trustpilot.com/review/yumba.ca\n- Meels: https://www.meels.com/ · https://www.meels.com/delivery-areas\n- Easy Mealz: https://easymealz.ca/ · https://easymealz.ca/products.json\n- HelloFresh / Chefs Plate: https://mealkitcomparison.com/location/canada/meal-kit-delivery-services-toronto/ · https://www.hellofresh.ca/ · https://www.chefsplate.com/\n- Goodfood: https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2026/04/goodfood-market-reports-7-million-net-loss-in-q2/ · https://www.marketbeat.com/earnings/reports/2026-1-20-goodfood-market-corp-foodto-stock/\n- Excluded or closed: https://trustpilot.com/review/livefitfood.ca · https://fitkitchen.ca/ · https://www.freshcityfarms.com/ · https://perishablenews.com/retailfoodservice/exciting-announcement-from-prepd/ · https://essentialmealdelivery.com/ · https://tally.so/r/BzrZoe\n- Adjacent butchers: https://sanagansmeatlocker.com/products.json · https://www.cumbraes.com/ · https://www.pusateris.com/"}, {"key": "gta-meat", "file": "competitors-gta-meat-grocery.md", "title": "Premium butchers and grocers in the GTA", "blurb": "The butchers, meat-delivery services and specialty grocers that compete with J&W’s raw side.", "md": "# Premium GTA butcher, meat-delivery and specialty-grocery competitors to J&W Foods (raw meat, whole-animal, gift-box and grocery side)\n\nPrepared for Minastech (Erwin Garcia) · Research date: October 16, 2026 · Scope: Toronto/GTA first, then Ontario/Canada DTC players that deliver into the GTA. The global benchmark set (ButcherBox US, Snake River Farms, Omaha Steaks, Wild Fork, etc.) is covered separately in `research/competitors-global.md` and is not repeated here.\n\n**How this was gathered.** Almost every figure below comes from **direct reads of each competitor's own website, Shopify/Odoo/API product feeds and policy pages on Oct 16, 2026** (URLs cited per item). Third-party data (Yelp review counts, 2019–2021 press) is used only where noted. Anything I could not verify is marked **\"not found\"**, and every estimate is labelled **(estimate)** with its reasoning. The gaps are listed in section 7.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive summary\n\n1. **J&W is the price leader in premium meat in the GTA by a wide margin, but it doesn't show it.** J&W's AAA ribeye works out to about **$23–$25/lb** (10 × 11–12 oz for $175). Comparable premium sellers charge **$31–$60/lb**: Sanagan's $43.99, Rowe Farms $44.88, Mama Earth/Healthy Butcher $45.14, VG Meats $38.55–$41.31, Pusateri's dry-aged $38.99, Olliffe $49.89–$59.92, ButcherBox.ca $31–$37. J&W's boneless chicken breast is **$6.36–$10.43/lb**, against $13.68–$16.75/lb at VG Meats and Mama Earth. Plenty of premium shoppers would buy a \"wholesale-priced, Ontario-inspected butcher\" pitch, yet J&W's site never shows a $/lb price or a comparison.\n2. **The GTA premium-meat market has moved to memberships, subscriptions and bundles. J&W has none of the three.** truLOCAL (70,100+ customers, 511,000+ boxes) sells box tiers with free shipping. Mama Earth sells a paid membership (Earth Perks) plus Subscribe & Save at 5% off. Sanagan's runs points promos and members-only promos every week. Rowe Farms, VG Meats and Olliffe (all Harvest to Gather) share one loyalty currency, Solar Dollars. ButcherBox.ca sells \"no-commitment\" monthly boxes and uses its own reefer trucks within 200 km of Mississauga.\n3. **Whole-animal shares are now priced and productized online.** Rowe Farms and VG Meats sell 1/16, 1/8, 1/4 and 1/2 beef, each with an approximate weight: $549.99–$599 for about 35 lb, up to $4,149–$4,249 for about 300 lb. J&W's \"Quarter Cow $1,200\" listing has no weight, cut sheet or photos. At their roughly $14.70–$15.20/lb, J&W's price may well be lower, but a buyer can't tell.\n4. **Fundraising and corporate gifting are proven acquisition channels, and J&W ignores both.** Halenda's (Durham, right on J&W's Tuesday route) reports its fundraising program has raised **over $6 million** for schools and teams. Rowe Farms runs youth-group meat fundraisers that show the donation amount under every price. truLOCAL's corporate gifting covers anything from 10 to 1,000 recipients, with tiered discounts and branded notes.\n5. **Several \"premium\" names are weaker online than their reputation suggests.** That leaves room for J&W to win on digital:\n   - Cumbrae's has no working online store. Its new Square site still shows template demo text.\n   - Bruno's Fine Foods shows only a \"new website coming soon\" page, and its old .com domain is parked.\n   - Pusateri's has shrunk to a single store, and its pages display broken \"Dynamic Snippet\" placeholder text.\n   - Royal Beef is a brochure site with no e-commerce.\n   - Organic Garage's domain doesn't resolve, Vince's Market returns a server error, and Healthy Planet blocks automated reads. None of the three could be verified as operating.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Price benchmark (premium beef and chicken, $/lb, CAD, Oct 16, 2026)\n\n| Seller | Item (as listed) | Listed price | Implied $/lb | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **J&W Foods** | AAA ribeye, 10 × 11–12 oz | $175.00 | **$23.33–$25.45** | jw-catalog.json (jwfoods.com/products.json) |\n| **J&W Foods** | AAA whole boneless ribeye, 14–15 lb, cut & vacuum sealed | $375.00 | **$25.00–$26.79** | jw-catalog.json |\n| **J&W Foods** | AAA whole striploin, 11–12 lb, cut & vacuumed | $299.00 | **$24.92–$27.18** | jw-catalog.json |\n| ButcherBox.ca | 100% grass-fed ribeye, 1 steak 10–12 oz | $23.25 | $31.00–$37.20 | butcherbox.ca ribeye page |\n| VG Meats (Farm to City) | Rib eye 12 oz × 6 (2.1 kg) | $178.48 | $38.55 | vgfarmtocity.ca/products.json |\n| Pusateri's | Boneless rib steak, dry-aged Canada AAA | $38.99/lb | $38.99 | pusateris.com product page |\n| VG Meats | Rib eye ~1.5\", 16 oz | $41.31 | $41.31 | vgfarmtocity.ca/products.json |\n| Sanagan's | AAA rib eye, 14 oz avg | $38.49 | $43.99 | sanagansmeatlocker.com/products.json |\n| Rowe Farms | Beef rib eye steak, 12 oz | $33.66 | $44.88 | rowefarmsonline.ca/products.json |\n| Mama Earth (Healthy Butcher) | Grass-fed beef ribeye, 226 g | $22.49 | $45.14 | api.mamaearth.ca product search |\n| Olliffe | Blue Ribbon ribeye, 16 oz | $49.89 | $49.89 | olliffe.ca/products.json |\n| Pusateri's | NY striploin, Prime grade | $53.99/lb | $53.99 | pusateris.com/shop/category/2210 |\n| Olliffe | 30-day aged ribeye, 16 oz | $59.92 | $59.92 | olliffe.ca/products.json |\n| **J&W Foods** | Wagyu striploin, 8–9 oz | $35.00 | $62.22–$70.00 | jw-catalog.json |\n| Mama Earth | Organic grass-fed prime rib eye, ~350 g | $79.99 | $103.67 | api.mamaearth.ca |\n| Olliffe | Australian Wagyu ribeye, 16 oz | $148.49 | $148.49 | olliffe.ca/products.json |\n| Cumbrae's | Rib eye (no weight given) | about $45 per steak, per a Yelp reviewer | not found | yelp.ca/biz/cumbraes-toronto-4 |\n| **J&W Foods** | Boneless chicken breast, bulk 55 lb | $350.00 | **$6.36** | jw-catalog.json |\n| **J&W Foods** | Case of portioned chicken breast, 4 kg | $85–$92 | **$9.64–$10.43** | jw-catalog.json |\n| VG Meats | Boneless skinless chicken breast, 6 lb box | $82.05 | $13.68 | vgfarmtocity.ca/products.json |\n| Mama Earth | Local boneless skinless chicken breast, ~480 g | $17.49 | $16.53 | api.mamaearth.ca |\n| Mama Earth | Bulk boneless skinless chicken breast, ~4 lb | $66.99 | $16.75 | api.mamaearth.ca |\n\n**Whole-animal beef shares**\n\n| Seller | Share | Price | Approx. weight | Implied $/lb |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| VG Meats | 1/16 · 1/8 · 1/4 · 1/2 | $549.99 · $1,099 · $2,129 · $4,149 | ~35 · 70 · 145 · 300 lb | $15.71 · $15.70 · $14.68 · $13.83 |\n| Rowe Farms | 1/16 · 1/8 · 1/4 · 1/2 | $599 · $1,149 · $2,199 · $4,249 | ~35 · 70 · 145 · 300 lb | $17.11 · $16.41 · $15.17 · $14.16 |\n| Rowe Farms | Whole pork | $1,199.99 | ~145 lb | $8.28 |\n| **J&W Foods** | Quarter cow | $1,200 | **not stated** | not computable |\n| **J&W Foods** | Whole Ontario lamb / half lamb | $400 / $250 | not stated | not computable |\n\n**Takeaway:** J&W's AAA ribeye is roughly **40–60% cheaper** per lb than the premium butchers. If its quarter cow is anywhere near 145 lb, it costs about half of what VG Meats or Rowe Farms charge for the same share. J&W should publish weights, $/lb and a \"compare us\" table.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Comparison matrix\n\n| Competitor | Scope | Online store | Subscription / membership | Loyalty | Whole-animal shares | Gift / corporate | Delivery promise | Status, Oct 2026 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| truLOCAL | Canada (ON, AB, BC) | Yes, custom build | Box tiers $155 / $279 / $349; pause or skip anytime | Points program (2022) plus referrals | No | Corporate gifting at scale; curated boxes; gift certificates | Free shipping, 2–3 days, dry ice, no minimum | Operating; 10-year anniversary page |\n| Rowe Farms (Harvest to Gather) | Toronto (5 stores) + Guelph | Yes, Shopify, 420 SKUs | No recurring box seen | Solar Dollar Rewards | 1/16 to 1/2 beef; whole pork | Gift cards; youth fundraisers | Same day if ordered by 7 am; free over $100 within 5 km | Operating; © 2026 |\n| Sanagan's Meat Locker | Toronto (3 shops) | Yes, Shopify, 449 SKUs | No | Meat Club Rewards: 10 pts per $1; weekly 3X-points item; members-only promos | No | Gift cards $25–$200; holiday menus | Free delivery over $180; 4-hour window; free pickup | Operating; promo for \"week of Oct 15–21\" |\n| Mama Earth (incl. Healthy Butcher, Fresh City) | GTA | Yes, custom Vue app, ~1,691 products | Produce boxes $25–$100; Healthy Butcher boxes $55–$195; Subscribe & Save 5% | Earth Perks paid membership with free 30-day trial | No | Gift cards; B Corp | $25 minimum; member perk \"Save $5 in delivery fees on every box\"; time slots | Operating; live box API for Oct 20, 2026 |\n| ButcherBox.ca | Ontario + Quebec | Yes, Magento | Monthly build-a-box, no commitment | Not found (affiliate program exists) | No | Gift cards; curated boxes | Own temperature-controlled trucks within ~200 km of Mississauga; dry-ice courier beyond; \"no additional cost\" | Operating; reviews dated Jun 2026; 2026 blog |\n| Halenda's | Durham/GTA (5 stores) + St Jacobs | Yes (\"Shop Online\") | No | Weekly e-flyer | No | Freezer packs $199 / $299 / $349; party platters; fundraising (> $6M raised) | Not found | Operating; 2026 award |\n| Pusateri's | Toronto (1 store) | Yes, Odoo | No | Not found | No | Gift boxes $64.99–$349.99; catering; floral | Local delivery from $14; free on $250+ grocery; FedEx across Canada | Operating |\n| Summerhill Market | Toronto (5) + Aurora (1) | Yes (separate shop site) + new iOS app | No | Free-cookie newsletter | No | Gift baskets $110–$195; white-glove delivery; per-serving holiday meals | Same-day delivery or pickup | Operating |\n| Cumbrae's | Toronto (2) + Dundas/Hamilton (1) | **No working store** (Square site, catalogue empty) | No | Not found | No (whole-animal butchery in store) | Gift cards via Toast | \"Have it delivered ready to enjoy\" (method not stated) | Operating; Church St closed |\n| Olliffe (Harvest to Gather) | Toronto (Rosedale) | Yes, Shopify, 365 SKUs | No | Solar Dollars | No | Gift cards; mixed charcuterie box $79 | Delivery or in-store pickup | Operating; © 2026 |\n| VG Meats Farm to City (Harvest to Gather) | Hamilton / Simcoe | Yes, Shopify, 376 SKUs | No | Solar Dollars | 1/16 to 1/2 beef | Bundles; fundraisers | Greater Hamilton delivery; zone fees beyond | Operating; © 2026 |\n| McEwan Fine Foods | Toronto (Don Mills, TD Centre) | Yes (Self-Point platform) | No | Not found | No | Gift baskets and floral studio; catering | Delivery (fees not found) | Operating |\n| Farm Boy (Sobeys/Empire) | Ontario (53 stores) | Via Voilà | No | Not found | No | Specialty \"Dinners for Two\" and holiday meals; catering | Voilà delivery | Operating |\n| Bruno's Fine Foods | Toronto + Richmond Hill (4 stores) | **No** (coming-soon page) | No | Not found | No | Not found | None online | Stores open; website rebuilding |\n| Carnivore Club | Canada (HQ not verified) | Yes, Shopify, 94 SKUs | Boxes $55.99–$69.99; prepaid gift subscriptions | Affiliates | No | Gift collection; jamón sets $119.99–$189.99 | Ships Canada-wide | Operating; © 2026 |\n| Royal Beef | Toronto (Danforth) | **No** (Wix brochure) | No | No | Whole-animal butchery in house | Not found | None | Operating; © 2026 |\n| Nature's Emporium | York Region | Yes (blocked automated reads) | Not found | Not found | No | Catering | Not found | Operating; © 2026 |\n| Organic Garage | GTA | Domain does not resolve | n/a | n/a | n/a | n/a | n/a | **Unverified; likely closed** |\n| Vince's Market | York Region | Site returns HTTP 522 | n/a | n/a | n/a | n/a | n/a | **Unverified** |\n| Healthy Planet | Ontario | Site returns HTTP 403 to bots | n/a | n/a | n/a | n/a | n/a | **Unverified** |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Competitor profiles (best first)\n\n### 4.1 truLOCAL: Canada's scaled local-meat subscription\n- **URL:** https://trulocal.ca · **Scope:** Canada (Ontario, Alberta and BC warehouses). Founded 2016 in Kitchener. Acquired by EMERGE Commerce (TSXV: ECOM) on Jan 4, 2021, per truLOCAL's own timeline; press at the time reported about $16.8M.\n- **Scale signals (truLOCAL's own 10-year page):** over 70,100 Canadian customers, over 100 farm/producer partners, over 511,000 boxes delivered, \"14 million meals\". 400+ products by Nov 2025. Feb 2025 brought the \"highest subscriber growth since the pandemic\". Appeared on Dragons' Den in 2017. Ranked #14 on the Globe and Mail's Canada's Top Growing Companies in 2020.\n- **Pricing:** Starter Box $155 (up to 36 servings), Family Box $279 (up to 80 servings, \"Most Popular\"), Mega Box $349 (up to 104 servings, \"Best Value\"). Value boxes (no subscription):\n  - Grass-fed ribeye box $299 (was $349)\n  - AAA striploin box $235 (was $279)\n  - Chicken breast box $249\n  - Student Box $235\n  - \"Anti-Tariff\" box $279\n- **Premium signals:** 100% grass-fed and finished beef, organic chicken, wild sockeye, named farms (for example West Grey Premium Meats for ribeye), flash-frozen and vacuum-sealed, recyclable packaging since 2019.\n- **Standout features:**\n  - \"FREE Meat for Life\" acquisition hook: a free steak, chicken, salmon or ground beef in every box.\n  - Comparison table against \"Major Grocery Stores\" and \"Other Meat Boxes\".\n  - Points system (2022), extra-points add-ons (2024), private label (2023), referrals page.\n  - Corporate gifting in four steps: choose box, add a branded note, upload recipients, \"we pack, ship, deliver\". Tiered discounts from 10 to 1,000 gifts, no minimum.\n- **Threat to J&W:** High. It is the default Ontario answer for \"meat delivered monthly\", and it delivers to \"home, office, or gym\".\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Launch a **J&W Butcher Club**, a points-customizable monthly raw-meat box. Use truLOCAL-style tiers sized by servings, priced off J&W's wholesale cost advantage (for example $129 / $229 / $299), with a \"free chicken breast for life\" hook and a corporate-gift flow where clients upload a recipient list.\n- Sources: https://trulocal.ca/ · https://trulocal.ca/pricing/ · https://trulocal.ca/gifts/ · https://trulocal.ca/10-year · https://trulocal.ca/value-boxes/ · https://trulocal.ca/free-life/ · https://www.cbc.ca/1.5861576\n\n### 4.2 Rowe Farms (Harvest to Gather group with VG Meats and Olliffe)\n- **URL:** https://rowefarms.ca · shop: https://rowefarmsonline.ca. Harvest to Gather was set up by VG Meats in 2022 as a family of companies covering VG Meats, Rowe Farms and Olliffe (per rowefarms.ca/about-harvest-to-gather).\n- **Scale signals:** 6 retail stores: Leslieville, The Beach, Bloor West, Roncesvalles and Danforth in Toronto, plus Guelph. Online catalogue of 420 SKUs. Founded by John Rowe in 1972.\n- **Pricing:**\n  - Rib eye 12 oz $33.66 ($44.88/lb). Steaks for 2 bundle, 12 oz ribeyes, $68.73.\n  - Grass-finished bundles: Steaks $149.99, Taster $189.99, Cold Weather $279.99, \"One of Everything\" $299.99.\n  - Ground beef bundles, 30 lb: $299 (medium) and $349 (lean).\n  - Beef shares: 1/16 (~35 lb) $599, 1/8 (~70 lb) $1,149, 1/4 (~145 lb) $2,199, 1/2 (~300 lb) $4,249. Whole pork (~145 lb) $1,199.99.\n- **Delivery:** Orders by 7 am are processed the same day. Delivery runs noon to 8 pm. Fees:\n\n  | Distance from store | Fee | Free over |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | 0–5 km | $10 | $100 |\n  | 5–8 km | $10 | $150 |\n  | 8–20 km | $20 | $150 |\n\n  Same-day pickup is also offered.\n- **Premium signals:** Grass-fed, antibiotic- and hormone-free, GMO-free. Four farm inspections a year plus annual third-party audits. True Foods Trustmark. Holistic-management training courses ($50.99–$1,800.99) and farm tours.\n- **Standout features:**\n  - Solar Dollar Rewards: points plus weekly exclusive discount codes.\n  - **Meat fundraisers** for schools and youth teams, with \"donation amounts clearly listed below the price of each product\" on a separate fundraising site.\n  - Value packs, \"Game Day\" and \"Weekday Warrior\" bundles.\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Run a **youth-sports and school fundraiser storefront** (Scarborough, Durham, Markham) that shows the donation under each price on J&W's best sellers: chicken-breast cases, burgers, Meal Butler packs.\n- Sources: https://rowefarms.ca/ · https://rowefarmsonline.ca/products.json · https://rowefarmsonline.ca/pages/delivery-information · https://rowefarmsonline.ca/pages/meat-fundraisers · https://rowefarms.ca/about-harvest-to-gather/\n\n### 4.3 Sanagan's Meat Locker: Toronto's best-known neighbourhood butcher\n- **URL:** https://sanagansmeatlocker.com (Shopify) · **Scope:** Toronto. Three shops: Kensington Market (176 Baldwin St), Gerrard India Bazaar (1513 Gerrard St E) and Annette & Runnymede (515 Annette St).\n- **Scale signals:** 449 products online: 256 grocery, 111 fresh meat, 67 deli/charcuterie, 71 chef-made prepared meals. Awards banner: \"Consistently Voted Toronto's Best Butcher Shop by Toronto Life\", Toronto Star Readers' Choice Platinum. Delivery partner Trexity offers live order tracking. Owner Peter Sanagan says some customers order delivery once or twice a week.\n- **Pricing:**\n  - Steaks: AAA rib eye 14 oz $38.49 ($43.99/lb), AAA striploin 12 oz $29.99, AAA tenderloin 6 oz $22.49.\n  - Roasts: AAA prime rib $209.99 (5–6 lb) up to $524.89 (13–15 lb); tenderloin roast $119.99–$239.99.\n  - Chef-made prepared meals: Beef Wellington for Two $59.99, lasagna $29.99 / $59.99, pies $21.99–$35.99.\n  - Gift cards $25–$200.\n- **Delivery:** Free on orders over $180. \"New lowered delivery rates exclusively within the GTA\" (amounts not found). 4-hour delivery window. Free contactless pickup. A $30 refundable card hold covers weight variance on catch-weight items.\n- **Loyalty:** \"Meat Club Rewards\":\n  - 500 points for creating an account; 10 points per $1.\n  - Redemption: 1,500 pts = $5 off; 2,500 = $10; 5,000 = $25; 10,000 = $60.\n  - Weekly \"3X points\" featured item and \"Member-Only Promo\" (M.O.P.) collections for bacon, eggs, ground beef and more. Dated \"Points Promo\" collections run every week (Sept 17 through Oct 28, 2026 visible).\n- **Design:** Clean, modern Shopify theme. Store selector, accelerated wallets, cookbook, butchery and cooking guides, producer pages, seasonal date-restricted menus (Thanksgiving, Christmas, Easter, Valentine's).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Turn J&W's dormant loyalty points into a **weekly promo engine**: one 3X-points item a week, members-only price drops on staples, a tiered redemption ladder, and a 500-point sign-up bonus.\n- Sources: https://sanagansmeatlocker.com/ · https://sanagansmeatlocker.com/pages/faq · https://sanagansmeatlocker.com/products.json · https://sanagansmeatlocker.com/collections.json · https://trexity.com/sanagans-meat-locker/\n\n### 4.4 Mama Earth (now including The Healthy Butcher and Fresh City Farms)\n- **URL:** https://www.mamaearth.ca. thehealthybutcher.com now redirects to mamaearth.ca/the-healthy-butcher. freshcityfarms.com says \"Fresh City has now re-branded under Mama Earth\" and that the farm moved to a co-op in 2024. Fresh City had bought The Healthy Butcher in May 2019.\n- **Scale signals:** About 1,691 products in the live catalogue API. \"Supports 250+ Ontario farmers and makers.\" Certified B Corp. Its COO, Abra Snider, was Fresh City's first employee.\n- **Pricing (API, shopping date Oct 20, 2026):**\n\n  | Healthy Butcher box | Price | Note |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | Seasonal Picks | $55 | |\n  | Essentials Small | $65 | |\n  | Lean Protein Small | $85 | ~11 meals at $7.53 |\n  | Easy Dinner Small | $90 | $7.36/portion |\n  | Seafood Lovers Medium | $110 | |\n  | Easy Dinner Medium | $150 | |\n  | Essentials Large | $195 (was $215) | ~33 meals at $5.99 |\n\n  Produce boxes run $25 (\"Harvest Rescue\") to $100 (XL Family). Meat: grass-fed ribeye 226 g $22.49 ($45.14/lb); organic prime ribeye ~350 g $79.99; bulk chicken breast ~4 lb $66.99; prime rib roast $222.99.\n- **Membership:** Earth Perks (Annual), offered with a free 30-day trial:\n  - \"Free delivery on every order\" (\"Save $5 in delivery fees on every box\")\n  - Free produce and meat box customization (saves $2 per order)\n  - 5% off staples\n  - Free time-slot selection\n  - Free reusable packaging\n\n  Subscribe & Save gives 5% off. Minimum order is $25. Weekly order deadline is 4 pm. Skip-a-delivery, \"Order It Again\", favourites and dislikes are all built in. Referral program at refer.mamaearth.ca.\n- **Best idea for J&W:** A **paid \"Butler Pass\" membership** (annual, with a 30-day free trial) that removes J&W's $20–$35 route fee, unlocks delivery time slots and gives 5% off subscribed staples. Pair it with **macro-labelled butcher boxes priced per meal** (\"Lean Protein box: ~11 meals at $X each\").\n- Sources: https://www.mamaearth.ca/ · https://api.mamaearth.ca/bags?shoppingDate=2026-10-20 · https://api.mamaearth.ca/products/search?query=ribeye · https://www.freshcityfarms.com/ · https://www.thehealthybutcher.com · https://www.refrigeratedfrozenfood.com/articles/97377-fresh-city-acquires-the-healthy-butcher-expands-animal-protein-supply-chain\n\n### 4.5 ButcherBox.ca: Ontario/Quebec frozen, vacuum-sealed meat subscription (Mississauga-based delivery hub; site does not state any link to the US ButcherBox)\n- **URL:** https://butcherbox.ca (Magento). Phone 905-593-2648.\n- **Model:** Pick a box size (\"1 person in a condo\" up to families of 5+), then fill it with any items. \"No contracts or commitments\"; pause, skip or cancel before the next process date; one-time and gift orders available.\n- **Delivery:** Its **own temperature-controlled vehicles within ~200 km of Mississauga**, covering Toronto through London, Kingston, Barrie and Niagara, on scheduled windows. Dry-ice courier for the rest of Ontario and Quebec. \"All of this at NO ADDITIONAL COST.\"\n- **Pricing:** 100% grass-fed ribeye 10–12 oz $23.25 ($31–$37/lb); AAA grass-fed NY striploin 10–12 oz $23.97; lean ground beef $9.75. Curated boxes:\n  - Steak Lovers Box $205.38\n  - Gourmet Burger Box (52 burgers) $238.40\n  - 100% grass-fed ground beef, 35 packs, $277.76\n  - Family pack chicken breast $393.60\n- **Scale signals:** Grass-fed ribeye shows **140 reviews at 100%**. Google reviews dated Feb–Jun 2026 appear on the homepage. Customer count and revenue: not found.\n- **Premium signals:** Leahy Farms (Peterborough) grass-fed beef, RWA Angus, air-chilled poultry, wild sockeye, HACCP.\n- **Design:** Dated Porto theme with a © 2022 footer, but functional build-a-box, reviews, an affiliate program and a knowledge base.\n- **Relevance:** This is the **closest operating model to J&W's raw side**: frozen, vacuum-sealed, portion-controlled, own trucks across southern Ontario. J&W already runs weekly routes to the same towns.\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Add **on-product star reviews and an affiliate program** (trainers, gyms, dietitians earn commission), and reframe J&W's existing routes as \"our own refrigerated trucks, free over $X\". ButcherBox.ca built its whole pitch on that.\n- Sources: https://butcherbox.ca/ · https://butcherbox.ca/delivery/ · https://butcherbox.ca/build-a-box/step-1/ · https://butcherbox.ca/curated-boxes.html/ · https://butcherbox.ca/100-grass-fed-grass-finished-pasture-raised-ribeye-steak-15.html · https://butcherbox.ca/aaa-new-york-striploin-steak-15.html · https://butcherbox.ca/affiliate-program/ · https://butcherbox.ca/blog/post/top-10-reasons-ontarians-are-switching-to-butcherboxca-for-meat-delivery-2026-guide\n\n### 4.6 Halenda's Meats: Durham butcher with a fundraising and CPG machine\n- **URL:** https://www.halendas.com. Fundraising: https://halendasfundraising.com.\n- **Scope:** Five GTA/Durham stores (Oshawa Nelson HQ, Oshawa Taunton, Oshawa King St E, Whitby and others) plus St Jacobs Market. Site claims \"6 Locations & 2 processing plants\". Affiliated with The Meat Depot (Etobicoke production).\n- **Scale signals:** Fundraising has \"helped raise over six million dollars\" with \"six million meat sticks sold\". Its charcuterie line is sold in select Metro, Sobeys, Foodland, Food Basics and Fortinos stores. Won the 2026 Diamond Award plus six more at Ontario's Finest Meat Competition.\n- **Pricing:** \"Hibernator\" freezer packs with full item lists:\n  - #1 at $199: 2 × 16 oz sirloin, 4 × 8 oz striploin, 6 lb lean ground beef and more\n  - #2 at $299\n  - #3 at $349\n- **Relevance:** A direct competitor on J&W's **Tuesday Durham route** (Pickering, Ajax, Whitby, Oshawa).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Launch a **protein-snack and meat-pack fundraising program** for Durham and Scarborough schools, hockey and soccer clubs, using J&W's inspected plant (chicken jerky, protein bites, Meal Butler fundraiser packs). Also publish named \"freezer packs\" with transparent item lists at $199 / $299 / $349.\n- Sources: https://www.halendas.com/ · https://www.halendas.com/products/meat-freezer-packs/ · https://www.halendas.com/halendas-locations/ · https://halendasfundraising.com/\n\n### 4.7 Pusateri's Fine Foods: Toronto's luxury grocer\n- **URL:** https://www.pusateris.com (Odoo). **Scope:** The site now lists **one store, 1539 Avenue Road (Lawrence Park)**. It celebrates \"more than 60 years\".\n- **Pricing (per lb):** Dry-aged Canada AAA boneless rib steak $38.99; dry-aged Canadian Angus NY strip $37.99; USDA Choice NY strip $36.99; Prime NY strip $53.99; filet mignon $53.99; Wagyu burgers $19.99. Many marinated kebab SKUs (souvlaki, piri piri, tandoori).\n- **Gift boxes:** Italian Antipasto $69.99, Cocktail Kit $109.99, Pasta Night $149.99, Grazing Table $209.99, Gourmet Treasures $274.99, European Traveller $349.99; fruit baskets $125 and $175. Catering (\"Everyday Catering\"), floral, and a recurring \"Food Show\" event.\n- **Delivery:** Local delivery from $14, free on $250+ grocery orders, FedEx across Canada. Product pages offer Store Pickup, Local Delivery and Courier Delivery.\n- **Design:** Elegant brand, but live pages show the broken placeholder text \"Your Dynamic Snippet will be displayed here…\" (similar to J&W's empty video block).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Show **\"$ / LB\" on every meat product** with fulfilment badges (Pickup / Local / Courier). Copy Pusateri's tiered gift-box ladder ($65–$350), especially a Greek \"Taste of Greece\" ladder, which is where J&W's 154 Mediterranean SKUs set it apart.\n- Sources: https://www.pusateris.com/ · https://www.pusateris.com/questions-answers · https://www.pusateris.com/shop/category/2210 · https://www.pusateris.com/shop/category/2221 · https://www.pusateris.com/shop/beef-boneless-rib-steak-dry-aged-canada-aaa-1-5lb-20643\n\n### 4.8 Summerhill Market: six-store premium prepared-food grocer\n- **URL:** https://www.summerhillmarket.com (Squarespace, plus a separate shop at shop.summerhillmarket.com and a new iOS app).\n- **Scope:** Rosedale, Sherwood, Annex, Forest Hill, Dundas West and Aurora. Serving Toronto since 1954, \"the city's largest selection of prepared foods\".\n- **Offers:**\n  - Same-day delivery or pickup with no cut-off.\n  - Gourmet gift baskets $110–$195 with white-glove delivery across the GTA.\n  - Holiday meals priced **per serving** (Beef Tenderloin Dinner $65/serving; Roast Turkey Dinner $42/serving), packed family style with 4 days' notice.\n  - Prix Fixe menu, workshops, magazine, weekly flyer.\n  - **Free signature cookie for newsletter sign-up.**\n- **Best idea for J&W:** **Per-serving holiday \"Butler Feasts\"** (Thanksgiving, Christmas, Easter, Greek Easter) using J&W's cooking kitchen, plus a free-item lead magnet (for example a free 4-pack of chicken burgers for email/SMS sign-up).\n- Sources: https://www.summerhillmarket.com/ · https://www.summerhillmarket.com/faq · https://www.summerhillmarket.com/locations · https://www.summerhillmarket.com/floral-gift-baskets · https://www.summerhillmarket.com/holiday-pre-orders-all-wo-ham · https://apps.apple.com/ca/app/summerhill-market/id6761440778\n\n### 4.9 Cumbrae's: dry-aged, farm-owned artisanal butcher\n- **URL:** https://www.cumbraes.com. Rebuilt on Square Online and published about Apr 28, 2026 (site metadata).\n- **Scope:** Bayview (1589 Bayview Ave), Queen West (714 Queen St W), Dundas (26 King St W, Hamilton). Yelp marks Church St as closed.\n- **Scale signals:** Yelp review counts: Queen 42, Bayview 36, closed Church 33 (all modest). Instagram and Google counts: not found.\n- **Premium signals:** Third-party listings call it a dry-ageing pioneer, with beef aged up to eight weeks, Berkshire pork and 24-hour-brined chickens. A Yelp reviewer cites about $45 per ribeye. It has its own farms in Haldimand County (BlogTO). The site reads \"Exceptional meats, prepared foods, charcuterie, cheese & produce… chef-led kitchen\".\n- **Online:** The store catalogue is empty and all fulfilment flags are off. Gift cards are sold through Toast (`order.toasttab.com/egiftcards/cumbraes-bayview`). The contact form still shows Square's demo text (\"Thanks for exploring this Square Online Theme!\").\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Create a **\"J&W Reserve\" dry-aged program**: a dry-age cabinet in the inspected plant, 28/45/60-day ribeyes and striploins as a premium halo tier above AAA, for gifting and the Gentleman's Box.\n- Sources: https://www.cumbraes.com/ · https://www.yelp.ca/biz/cumbraes-toronto-4 · https://www.yelp.ca/biz/cumbraes-toronto-3 · https://www.yelp.ca/biz/cumbraes-toronto · https://blogto.com/toronto/the_best_butcher_shops_in_toronto\n\n### 4.10 Olliffe Butcher Shop (Harvest to Gather): Rosedale butcher to chefs since 1975\n- **URL:** https://olliffe.ca (Shopify, 365 SKUs). \"Toronto's Best Butcher Shop Since 1975\". Supplies \"some of the city's top chefs\".\n- **Pricing:** Weight-variant selectors on every steak:\n  - Blue Ribbon ribeye 10/12/14/16 oz: $31.18 / $37.42 / $43.65 / $49.89\n  - 30-day aged ribeye 16 oz $59.92\n  - Australian Wagyu ribeye 10–16 oz $92.80–$148.49\n  - Prime rib $199.98 (2 kg) to $799.92 (8 kg)\n  - Mixed charcuterie box (2.5 kg) $79\n  - Gift cards $10–$100\n- **Best idea for J&W:** **Cut-to-weight variant selectors** (10/12/14/16 oz) with live price, and a visible Wagyu tier ladder (J&W already sells Wagyu striploin and Wagyu/Kobe ground).\n- Sources: https://olliffe.ca/ · https://olliffe.ca/products.json\n\n### 4.11 VG Meats \"Farm to City\" (Harvest to Gather): bulk boxes and beef shares\n- **URL:** https://vgmeats.ca. Shop: https://vgfarmtocity.ca (Shopify, 376 SKUs). Stores in Simcoe and Stoney Creek. Delivers in greater Hamilton, with zone fees beyond.\n- **Pricing:**\n  - 10 lb \"Buy in Bulk\" boxes: chicken breast 6 lb $82.05, split-tip wings 10 lb $92.99, back ribs 10 lb $89.53, 6 oz patties 9 lb $120.93\n  - Rib Eye 12 oz × 6 pack $178.48\n  - Beef shares: 1/16 $549.99 up to 1/2 $4,149\n- **Premium signals:** \"First tenderness tested beef in Canada\", small herd and high care, never fed antibiotics, fully traceable.\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Productize whole-animal sales as **1/16 to 1/2 shares with approximate pounds, a cut sheet and an entry-level 1/16 share**. J&W's single \"$1,200 quarter cow\" with no weight is hard to buy.\n- Sources: https://vgmeats.ca/ · https://vgfarmtocity.ca/pages/farm-to-city · https://vgfarmtocity.ca/products.json\n\n### 4.12 McEwan Fine Foods (The McEwan Group)\n- **URL:** https://mcewangroup.ca/mcewan-fine-foods/ · shop: https://www.mcewanfoods.com (Self-Point platform).\n- **Scope:** Don Mills flagship (38 Karl Fraser Rd) and TD Centre (PATH). The group also runs Bymark, Fabbrica, The Port Tavern, catering, a food truck, consultancy, and a gifts and floral studio.\n- **Offers:** Chef Mark McEwan's prepared meals, gift baskets with 24–48 hours' notice, office and home catering, online delivery. Ribeye price and delivery fees: not found (the shop is a JavaScript app and was not readable here).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** **Corporate and office catering trays** for the Scarborough, Markham and Don Mills business parks, built from Meal Butler proteins and sold through a simple quote form.\n- Sources: https://mcewangroup.ca/ · https://mcewangroup.ca/mcewan-fine-foods/ · https://mcewangroup.ca/gifts-floral/ · https://www.mcewanfoods.com/\n\n### 4.13 Farm Boy (Sobeys/Empire): scaled \"mass-premium\"\n- **URL:** https://www.farmboy.ca. **Scale:** \"We currently have 53 stores spread across Ontario\", 10+ in Toronto. Online ordering goes through Voilà.\n- **Offers:** \"Butcher-Quality Meat & Sustainable Seafood\", private label, Specialty Dinners (\"Chef-Made Indulgence Dinners for Two & Holiday Meals\"), catering platters.\n- **Best idea for J&W:** A **\"Dinner for Two\" premium bundle** (two AAA striploins or Wagyu, sides and sauce) for date nights and Valentine's, merchandised on the homepage.\n- Sources: https://www.farmboy.ca/ · https://www.farmboy.ca/stores/ · https://www.farmboy.ca/shop-online/ · https://voila.ca/categories/farm-boy/WEB1146400\n\n### 4.14 Bruno's Fine Foods: Italian family grocer and butcher\n- **URL:** https://brunosfinefoods.ca, a \"New Website Is Coming Soon\" page (© 2026). The old brunosfinefoods.com now redirects to a parked \"/lander\".\n- **Scope:** Four stores: Avenue Road (North York), Bayview (Richmond Hill), Kingsway (Etobicoke), Queen St E (The Beach). \"Family-owned in Toronto for over 50 years… Yes… there really is a Bruno!\"\n- **Online:** None. Prices: not found.\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Lead the site with the **real family story** (George Trougakos' downtown butcher shop in the 1960s, Advance Food Products in 1973, J&W in 1995, inspected plant since 2008). Bruno's leans on its family story even with no site.\n- Sources: https://brunosfinefoods.ca/ · https://www.brunosfinefoods.com/\n\n### 4.15 Carnivore Club: charcuterie gift subscriptions\n- **URL:** https://carnivoreclub.ca (Shopify, 94 SKUs). Ships across Canada, with a US site.\n- **Pricing:** Classic Box $69.99, Complete Box $69.99, Snack Box $55.99; **prepaid gift versions** at the same prices; Spanish mini jamón carving set $119.99 (sale), $189.99 with an extra 1 kg; grab-bag tins $79.99.\n- **Best idea for J&W:** **Prepaid gift subscriptions** (\"give 4 weeks of Meal Butler\" or \"3 months of Butcher Club\") for Christmas and Father's Day.\n- Sources: https://carnivoreclub.ca/ · https://carnivoreclub.ca/products.json\n\n### 4.16 Royal Beef: Danforth whole-animal butcher since 1985\n- **URL:** https://www.royalbeef.ca (Wix brochure). 1968 Danforth Ave. Founded 1985 by Paul and Carmen Estrela.\n- **Offers:** In-house whole-animal butchery; specialty cuts (Denver, Merlot, Spider steaks); house meatballs and RB Classic Burgers; deli. No online ordering.\n- **Best idea for J&W:** A **\"Butcher's Cut of the Month\"** (Denver, picanha, spider steak) with a short video. It builds a reason to come back that suits the Greek-Canadian Danforth and Scarborough audience.\n- Sources: https://www.royalbeef.ca/\n\n### 4.17 Nature's Emporium: natural and organic grocer with supplements\n- **URL:** https://naturesemporium.com (© 2026). Navigation: Produce, **Supplements**, Meat & Seafood, Prepared Foods, Catering, Bulk, Wellness Bar. Store count and prices: not found (bot protection).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** **Bundle supplements with meal plans** (\"Lean Bulk week: 21 Meal Butler meals plus whey plus electrolytes\"). J&W already stocks about 101 supplement SKUs.\n- Sources: https://naturesemporium.com/\n\n### 4.18–4.20 Could not verify as operating (Oct 16, 2026)\n- **Organic Garage** (https://www.organicgarage.com): the domain did not resolve in DNS. Likely closed or rebranded. Do not cite as a live competitor until confirmed.\n- **Vince's Market** (vincesmarket.com): the site returned HTTP 522 (Cloudflare: origin unreachable). Status unverified.\n- **Healthy Planet** (https://www.healthyplanetcanada.com): HTTP 403 to automated reads. The chain is probably operating, but its details were not verified.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Where J&W is weak, and the ten borrowable ideas ranked by $ impact\n\n| # | Idea | Borrowed from | Why it fits J&W |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **J&W Butcher Club**: tiered monthly raw-meat box (serving-based tiers, points customization, free-item-for-life hook) | truLOCAL, ButcherBox.ca, Mama Earth | J&W's cost base is 40–60% below premium butchers, so it can undercut truLOCAL's $155 / $279 / $349 tiers and keep margin |\n| 2 | **Butler Pass** paid membership (annual, 30-day free trial): no route fee, time slots, 5% off subscriptions | Mama Earth Earth Perks | The $20–$35 route fee and $100 minimum are the main friction today |\n| 3 | **Fundraising storefront** for schools and youth sports (donation shown per item; protein snacks plus freezer packs) | Halenda's (> $6M raised), Rowe Farms | Durham and Scarborough families already sit on J&W's routes; this acquires customers at near-zero cost |\n| 4 | **Corporate gifting portal** (upload recipient list, branded note, tiered discounts) built on J&W's $89.99–$199 boxes | truLOCAL, Pusateri's, McEwan | Q4 gifting season is now; J&W already has gift boxes |\n| 5 | **Whole-animal shares, productized** (1/16 to 1/2 beef, whole/half lamb, with lb, cut sheet, deposit) | VG Meats, Rowe Farms | J&W's quarter cow likely costs half as much per lb but shows no weight |\n| 6 | **Loyalty promo engine** (weekly 3X item, members-only prices, redemption ladder) | Sanagan's Meat Club | J&W \"has\" points but never promotes them |\n| 7 | **$/lb pricing, weight selectors, reviews** on every product | Pusateri's, Olliffe, ButcherBox.ca | Fixes the audit's 51% blank descriptions and lack of social proof |\n| 8 | **Per-serving holiday feasts** (Thanksgiving, Christmas, Greek Easter) | Summerhill Market, Farm Boy | Uses the cooked kitchen; high average order value |\n| 9 | **\"J&W Reserve\" dry-aged tier** | Cumbrae's, Pusateri's, Olliffe | A premium halo for gifting; leverages the inspected plant |\n| 10 | **Affiliate program** for trainers, gyms and dietitians | ButcherBox.ca, Carnivore Club | Fits the \"Eat Clean, Train Mean\" positioning |\n\n**Illustrative sizing (estimates, not forecasts):**\n- **Butcher Club** (estimate): 400 subscribers × $229 average box × 12 months ≈ **$1.10M/yr** gross. Reasoning: truLOCAL reached 70,100 customers nationally in 10 years. Winning about 0.6% of that customer count, concentrated in J&W's routed GTA towns, is conservative for a business that already delivers to them weekly.\n- **Corporate gifting** (estimate): 60 companies × 25 gifts × $130 average ≈ **$195K/yr**. Reasoning: J&W's existing $129.99–$199 boxes; truLOCAL's tiered discounts start at 10 gifts.\n- **Fundraising** (estimate): 40 groups × $3,000 in sales ≈ **$120K/yr** of new-customer revenue. Reasoning: Halenda's reports $6M raised over the life of its program; the time span was not found, so this assumes a modest local share.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Design and UX notes (what premium looks like in this set)\n- **Best-in-class UX:**\n  - **Mama Earth** (custom Vue app): membership upsells, skip and reorder, delivery time slots, dislikes, referral program.\n  - **Sanagan's** (Shopify): store selector, dated seasonal menus, cookbook, producer stories, loyalty.\n  - **truLOCAL**: plan picker, grocery comparison table, corporate gifting funnel, anniversary storytelling page.\n- **Functional but dated:** ButcherBox.ca (Magento Porto, © 2022 footer, but reviews and build-a-box); Rowe Farms, Olliffe and VG Meats (shared Shopify template across the Harvest to Gather brands).\n- **Premium brand, weak site:**\n  - Pusateri's: broken \"Dynamic Snippet\" placeholders.\n  - Cumbrae's: no store, Square demo text still visible.\n  - Bruno's: coming-soon page.\n  - Royal Beef: brochure only.\n  - Summerhill: Squarespace front end, a separate shop subdomain, a \"©2024\" footer, but a new iOS app.\n- **Implication:** No GTA butcher currently combines (a) wholesale-level prices, (b) an inspected plant, (c) cooked meal prep and (d) a modern subscription and membership site. That combination is J&W's opening.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Not found / to verify in a follow-up run (needs web search)\n- Revenue for every private competitor (truLOCAL's parent EMERGE Commerce files publicly, but its 2025–2026 figures were not retrieved).\n- Instagram follower and Google review counts for Sanagan's, Cumbrae's, Pusateri's, Summerhill, Halenda's, Rowe Farms.\n- Sanagan's GTA delivery fee table; Summerhill and McEwan delivery fees, minimums and ribeye prices; Mama Earth's Earth Perks annual price.\n- Whether Pusateri's closed its Yorkville and Oakville stores (the site lists only Avenue Road).\n- Operating status of Organic Garage, Vince's Market and Healthy Planet.\n- Wild Fork Foods' Ontario exit is referenced in `competitors-global.md`; not re-verified here.\n\n---\n\n## 8. J&W reference data used for comparisons\n- `research/jw-facts.md` (crawl of jwfoods.com, Oct 16, 2026) and `research/jw-catalog.json` (979 products from https://www.jwfoods.com/products.json).\n- Key J&W prices:\n  - AAA ribeye 10 × 11–12 oz $175; whole boneless AAA ribeye 14–15 lb $375; whole AAA striploin 11–12 lb $299; Wagyu striploin 8–9 oz $35\n  - Quarter cow $1,200; whole Ontario lamb $400; half lamb $250\n  - Boneless chicken breast bulk 55 lb $350; chicken breast 11 lb $80; 4 kg portioned case $85–$92\n  - Gift boxes $84.99–$199\n  - Routes: $20–$35 fee, $100 minimum"}, {"key": "global", "file": "competitors-global.md", "title": "Best in class, worldwide", "blurb": "The global operators whose models J&W could copy, with revenue and scale.", "md": "# Global best-in-class benchmarks for J&W Foods / J&W Meal Butler\n\nPrepared for Minastech (Erwin Garcia). Research date: October 16, 2026.\nScope: operators outside the GTA whose models J&W could copy to grow 10x. The focus is cooked, portioned proteins and meals sold a la carte or in bulk to fitness customers, plus premium direct-to-consumer (DTC) meat.\n\n**How to read this.** Every figure has a source link. A figure marked \"company claim\" was published by the company itself and has not been checked independently. A figure marked \"estimate\" is my own calculation, and the reasoning is shown. Where I could not find a number, the report says \"not found.\"\n\n**Research limit.** Not every candidate could be covered in depth. These items were not verified and are listed as not found: Omaha Steaks revenue, Huel, Thrive Market, Balance Meals, current Allen Brothers revenue, and current Crowd Cow status.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive summary: what the winners do that J&W does not\n\nJ&W already has the hard parts: an Ontario-inspected plant (since 2008), 30 years of butchery, a cooked and portioned line marketed as having no added salt, sugar or oil, about 292 Meal Butler SKUs, and weekly GTA delivery routes. These are what Trifecta, Icon Meals and My Muscle Chef built on. The global leaders are ahead in **packaging, recurrence and distribution**, not in the food itself.\n\n| # | Pattern among the global winners | Who proves it | J&W today |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Bulk cooked protein by the pound, plus a macro app and free coaching**, sold as the \"meal prep for people who count macros\" | Trifecta (>US$100M run-rate, 2021), Icon Meals (\"By the Pound\") | Has the products (4oz cooked chicken from $4.20), but no app, no macro panels, no coaching |\n| 2 | **Subscribe & Save with skip, pause or swap**, plus a \"free X for life\" hook | ButcherBox ($570M+ 2025 revenue, ~450k subscribers), MegaFit (20% off), Calo (pause anytime, edit up to 48h ahead) | No subscription; customers rebuild their cart every week |\n| 3 | **Gyms as a distribution and sampling channel** (gym coolers, gym retail, gym equity partners) | My Muscle Chef (grew through gym sampling to A$200M), Superfit Foods (coolers in 28 gyms), Calo and Armah Sports, Territory and Xponential, MightyMeals gym stockists | No gym program |\n| 4 | **B2B and teams**: wholesale portal, pro-team partnerships, corporate smart fridges, hospitals | Icon Meals wholesale (\"thousands\" of partners; UFC equity), CookUnity's purchase of Fraîche (20+ NYC office fridges), Calo hospital outlets | No B2B portal, despite a family wholesale history going back to 1973 |\n| 5 | **Retail and CPG adjacency** once the brand has a following | My Muscle Chef (6,000+ stockists; \"retail arm is just as profitable as online\"), ButcherBox in about 1,500 Target stores | Sells other brands' supplements and snacks; no J&W-branded CPG |\n| 6 | **AI personalization and AI-search content** | Calo Black (an LLM \"private chef\"), Territory (ML regional menus), Icon Meals' \"ai-optimized\" blog and AI Knowledge Base, Prep Kitchen's llms.txt | Half the catalogue has no description; no AI at all |\n| 7 | **Corporate gifting and premium boxes** sold at set price tiers | Omaha Steaks (5,000+ business clients since 2020), Snake River Farms (gift bundles grouped by price; quarter beef at $1,399) | Has gift boxes ($89.99 to $199) and a quarter cow ($1,200), but they are hard to find and there is no corporate program |\n\n**Timely local opening.** JBS-owned **Wild Fork is leaving Canada.** Its Ontario online service ended October 13, 2026, and its stores in **Whitby, Oakville, Ancaster and Newmarket** close by December 14, 2026 ([Retail Insider](https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2026/10/wild-fork-exits-canada-abandoning-ambitious-ontario-expansion-plans/)). Whitby, Oakville and Newmarket are all on J&W's existing delivery routes (GTA 1, GTA 3 and GTA 4). Wild Fork's premium frozen-meat customers in those towns are looking for a new supplier right now.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Ranked benchmarks (best model for J&W to copy first)\n\n### 1. Trifecta Nutrition (Sacramento, USA): the closest model to Meal Butler\n- **What it is:** Organic, macro-balanced prepared meals, plus an **a la carte \"Meal Prep\" line of bulk cooked proteins, carbs and vegetables**, a macro-tracking app and nutrition coaching.\n- **Scale:** US$20M Series B in August 2021, led by Spring Lake Equity Partners. The company was **already profitable** at an **annual run-rate above US$100M**, with average revenue growth over 145% a year for the previous four years ([Private Equity Wire](https://www.privateequitywire.co.uk/trifecta-raises-usd20m-series-b-round-led-spring-lake-equity-partners/)). Total funding is about US$23.9M ([Dealroom via search](https://app.dealroom.co/companies/trifectanutrition)). It has 4.6 stars from 1.5k+ Google reviews ([trifectanutrition.com](https://www.trifectanutrition.com/)). Co-branded meals with CrossFit were announced in 2022 (search summary of company coverage).\n- **Pricing (a la carte bulk, current shop):** Grilled and sous-vide chicken breast 16oz (4 servings) **US$14.99**. Grilled salmon 16oz $23.99. Grass-fed bison burger 16oz $26.99. Grass-fed sirloin about 4oz $23.99 (as listed). White rice and sweet potato mash 16oz $9.99. Broccoli 16oz $10.99. Green beans 16oz $11.99. **Order minimum $99** ([Trifecta shop](https://shop.trifectanutrition.com/shop/category/meal-prep/)). Prepared meals run about **$13.49 to $16.99 per meal** ([MealFan](https://mealfan.com/how-much-does-trifecta-cost/)), with about $9.99 shipping per box (review sites).\n- **Subscription mechanics:** Change or skip deliveries on the web or in the app, with changes due by the Friday before delivery ([Healthline](https://healthline.com/nutrition/trifecta-meals)). Pause for up to 4 weeks ([Active.com](https://www.active.com/nutrition/articles/trifecta-review)).\n- **App:** The free \"Trifecta\" food-logging and macro-tracking app has premium at **$9.99 a month or $49.99 a year** and a 14-day trial ([App Store](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/trifecta/id492100601)). The app itself is a lead-generation and retention engine.\n- **Why it is premium:** Organic, grass-fed and wild-caught sourcing, athlete endorsements, and macro precision.\n- **Comparison with J&W:** J&W's cooked chicken is 4oz from C$4.20, which is about **C$16.80/lb** (estimate: $4.20 × 4). That is roughly on par with Trifecta's US$14.99/lb once you convert currency. J&W's no-salt, no-sugar, no-oil claim is actually stricter than Trifecta's.\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Repackage Meal Butler as **\"Macro Packs\"**: 1lb (4×4oz) bulk packs of protein, carbs and vegetables with printed macros, a $99 minimum, and a free macro-calculator and tracker (web app first). Then offer a free 15-minute coaching call with every first order.\n\n### 2. Icon Meals (Dallas, USA): bulk \"By the Pound\", plus wholesale to gyms and teams\n- **What it is:** Prepared meals, custom-built meals, **bulk cooked proteins by the pound**, protein snacks (popcorn, protein butters, coffee) and seasonings ([iconmeals.com](https://iconmeals.com/)).\n- **Scale:** Revenue was **over US$14M in 2023**, with 75 employees ([Garage Gym Reviews](https://www.garagegymreviews.com/icon-meals-review)). The company claims **over 12 million meals sold** ([iconmeals.com](https://iconmeals.com/)). CB Insights lists US$7.09M raised ([CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/company/icon-meals)). In 2021 **UFC became its Official Meal Delivery Partner and bought an equity stake**, citing 56% annualized growth since 2014 ([UFC.com](https://www.ufc.com/news/ufc-announces-major-partnership-icon-meals)).\n- **Pricing:** About **$6.00 to $12.50 per meal**. Custom meals start at $6. The 12-meal plan is about $12 per meal and the 24-meal plan about $11. There is an 8-meal minimum, and military and first responders get 15% off ([Garage Gym Reviews](https://www.garagegymreviews.com/icon-meals-review)). Per-lb bulk prices: not found.\n- **B2B arm:** The wholesale page targets **stores, gyms, weight-loss clinics, training centres, offices and employee-benefit programs**. Orders run from \"a few dozen meals\" up to thousands a week. It lists partners including UFC, the US Olympic Team, the Dallas Cowboys and USA Triathlon. There is a separate wholesale login portal, and the company says it handles meat processing in-house in a USDA facility ([Icon wholesale](https://iconmeals.com/pages/wholesale)). Icon also pitches office fridges stocked with its meals as a **replacement for vending machines** ([Icon blog](https://iconmeals.com/blogs/ai-optimized/corporate-wellness-vending-machines-vs-icon-meals)).\n- **AI usage:** Icon runs a whole blog section at `/blogs/ai-optimized/`. Its posts are question-titled, answer first, use headings and tables, and close on the brand, which is a clear generative-engine-optimization (GEO) strategy for AI search. Examples include \"Bulk meat by the pound: is it cheaper?\" and GLP-1 meal-prep guides ([Icon blog](https://iconmeals.com/blogs/ai-optimized/bulk-meat-by-the-pound-is-it-cheaper)). The footer also links an \"AI Knowledge Base.\"\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Open a **J&W Wholesale and Teams portal** (gyms, trainers, physio and weight-loss clinics, OHL and university teams) with a low minimum, such as 8 meals or 1 case. Run it alongside an **AI-optimized content hub** so ChatGPT and Google AI answers recommend \"J&W Meal Butler\" for \"meal prep Toronto\" and \"cooked chicken by the pound Toronto.\"\n\n### 3. My Muscle Chef (Sydney, Australia): gym sampling to A$200M, then retail\n- **Scale:** First-year revenue was **A$827k**. Revenue was A$26M in 2018 and **A$200M in 2022**. The company has 500+ staff and makes 2.2M+ meals, snacks and drinks a month ([C&I / c-store.com.au, Dec 2023](https://www.c-store.com.au/?p=62079)). It opened a **A$55M, 10,000 sqm facility** with weekly capacity of 1.2M meals ([realcommercial.com.au](https://www.realcommercial.com.au/news/from-humble-home-kitchen-to-55m-factory-the-rapid-rise-of-my-muscle-chef)). It is backed by Quadrant Private Equity ([Just Food](https://www.just-food.com/news/australias-my-muscle-chef-gets-private-equity-investment)).\n- **How it grew:** Through **word of mouth and gym sampling**. It started paid online ads only in its fourth year. It pioneered vacuum-sealed ready meals in Australia ([Food & Drink Business](https://www.foodanddrinkbusiness.com.au/news/muscling-in-on-meals)).\n- **Retail and wholesale:** It has **6,000+ retail stockists** (Woolworths, Coles, fuel stations). Protein cookies (2021) opened up convenience retail. The CEO says \"our retail arm is just as profitable as online\" ([c-store.com.au](https://www.c-store.com.au/?p=62079)).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Run a **gym sampling and ambassador program**: free sampling days at 20 GTA gyms a month and trainer codes. Then add a **J&W-branded high-protein CPG line** (jerky, protein egg bites, bone broth) for gym fridges and independent grocers.\n\n### 4. Prep Kitchen (Kent, UK): bootstrapped high-protein meal prep with simple plans\n- **Scale:** Ranked **#27 on the 2024 Sunday Times 100** with sales of **£13.9M and 138.7% growth**. It also appeared on the 2025 list ([search summary of the Sunday Times listing and Prep Kitchen's announcement](https://prepkitchen.co.uk/blog/news/prep-kitchen-achieves-a-place-in-the-2024-the-sunday-times-100-list)). FEBE reports **£22.8M sales in 2024** ([FEBE G100](https://www.febe.com/2025_g100/prep-kitchen/)). The company claims £40M+ revenue (company claim, conflicts with the figures above). It has 20,000+ active weekly subscribers and makes nearly 100,000 meals a week from a 50,000 sq ft Kent site (company and press claims, via search). The founders started with £10k in 2015, repaid it within a year, and the business has been **cash-flow funded** since ([search summary](https://www.thebusinessdesk.com/northwest/?p=2101539)).\n- **Pricing:** **£6.95 per meal**, cancel anytime, free delivery ([Prep Kitchen homepage](https://prepkitchen.co.uk/homepage-comp)). By plan: Fat Loss £6.25 balanced / £7 low-carb, and Muscle Gain £7.50 / £8.25 ([CoachWeb](https://coachweb.com/health/nutrition/prep-kitchen-review-tasty-balanced-meals-for-active-people)). Meals carry up to 60g protein ([Prep Kitchen menu](https://prepkitchen.co.uk/menu)).\n- **AI usage:** It publishes an `llms.txt` file to guide AI crawlers ([prepkitchen.co.uk/llms.txt](https://prepkitchen.co.uk/llms.txt)).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Cut the choice down to **3 goal-based plans** (Fat Loss, Muscle Gain, Maintenance), each with a set weekly price and regular and large portions. The deep 9-category mega-menu then becomes the place for add-ons. Bootstrapped growth to about £14 to 23M shows this works without venture capital.\n\n### 5. Calo (Riyadh, GCC and UK): AI-personalized meal subscriptions\n- **Scale:** **280K customers in the GCC and 18M meals delivered** (company claim, [calo.app](https://calo.app/en-sa)). It had nine-figure (US$) annualized revenue, 10M+ meals in 2024 and 50%+ year-on-year growth in H1 2025. It raised a **US$64M Series B** (a $25M round in December 2024 plus a $39M extension in July 2025). It has 10+ physical sites, **including hospital-based outlets**, and is planning a Saudi IPO ([Wamda](https://www.wamda.com/2025/07/calo-raises-39-milliion-series-b-extension-boost-global-growth)). In 2025 it **acquired the UK brands Fresh Fitness Food and Detox Kitchen** ([CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/company/fresh-fitness-food/financials)).\n- **Pricing:** From **SAR 31 per meal and SAR 23 per breakfast**. Delivery is 5, 6 or 7 days a week, in morning or evening slots ([calo.app](https://calo.app/en-sa)).\n- **Subscription mechanics:** No contracts. Pause anytime by picking dates in the app. Skip, swap, remove ingredients or edit meals up to 48 hours before delivery. Plans are High Protein, Balanced, Vegetarian, Low Carb and **Custom Macros**, where you enter your own targets ([calo.app](https://calo.app/en-sa); [Calo help](https://help.calo.app/en/articles/4704801-how-to-pause-the-subscription-plan)).\n- **AI usage:** It is piloting **\"Calo Black\", an AI private chef that uses LLMs to learn preferences through conversation and generate daily menus**, and is building AI into customer service and operations ([Wamda](https://www.wamda.com/2025/07/calo-raises-39-milliion-series-b-extension-boost-global-growth)).\n- **B2B and gyms:** A strategic partnership with premium gym operator **Armah Sports** covers co-located retail outlets, cross-promotion and distribution through gym networks. Armah's founder joined Calo's board ([Wamda](https://www.wamda.com/2025/07/calo-raises-39-milliion-series-b-extension-boost-global-growth)).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Build a **\"Meal Butler AI\": a chat concierge** that asks for goal, weight, training and dislikes, then builds a weekly cart from the 292 cooked SKUs to hit the customer's macros. Pair it with a **Custom Macros** plan.\n\n### 6. ButcherBox (Boston, USA): the subscription-meat benchmark\n- **Scale:** **2025 revenue above US$570M, up more than 10%**, with about **450,000 US subscribers**. It launched in about **1,500 Target stores** in January 2026 with five fresh grass-fed beef items at $8 to $14/lb ([Boston Globe](https://www.bostonglobe.com/2026/01/15/business/butcherbox-target-stores-meat-retail); [Nasdaq PR](https://www.nasdaq.com/press-release/butcherbox-launches-target-2026-01-15)). It is **bootstrapped**: a ~$210k Kickstarter, and it has **never raised outside capital** ([Built In](https://www.builtinboston.com/articles/story-behind-butcherbox); [BU](https://www.bu.edu/articles/2023/my-big-idea-mike-salguero-butcherbox/)). It also sells through BJ's, Instacart, DoorDash DashMarket and Target Plus ([Modern Retail](https://www.modernretail.co/operations/butcherbox-launches-on-targets-marketplace-in-subscription-free-acquisition-play/)).\n- **Pricing:** **US$8.52 to $15.23/lb** (Signature Medium, effective January 8, 2026) ([butcherbox.com](https://www.butcherbox.com/)). Curated boxes run about $99 to $189 ([Modern Retail](https://www.modernretail.co/operations/butcherbox-launches-on-targets-marketplace-in-subscription-free-acquisition-play/)).\n- **Subscription mechanics:** Choose Essentials (6 items) or Signature (6, 9 or 12 items). Swap among 100+ cuts until the night before billing. Skip or cancel anytime, with free shipping. **Sign-up hook: \"FREE chicken breasts for life\", or free NY strips or filets for a year**, worth up to US$422. Weekly member deals and **2% back in rewards points**. It shows **70k+ five-star reviews** and is B Corp certified ([butcherbox.com](https://www.butcherbox.com/)).\n- **Lesson:** People who left often did so because \"people didn't want a subscription.\" That is why ButcherBox added one-off retail and marketplace sales ([Modern Retail](https://www.modernretail.co/operations/butcherbox-launches-on-targets-marketplace-in-subscription-free-acquisition-play/)).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Launch the **\"J&W Butcher's Box\"**, a monthly subscription for the raw side (AAA, Ontario air-chilled chicken, Ontario lamb) with a **\"free 4oz cooked chicken pack in every box for life\"** hook. Keep one-off ordering for customers who will not subscribe.\n\n### 7. CookUnity (New York, USA, and Toronto): the chef marketplace already in J&W's market\n- **Scale:** **ARR above US$750M** in 2025, **43M+ meals** (up 80%+), 200k+ eaters, average chef earning about US$850k a year ([FSR Magazine](https://www.fsrmagazine.com/industry-news/cookunity-closes-strongest-year-yet/)). It has **up to US$250M in non-dilutive financing** from General Catalyst and runs 8 kitchens ([Food On Demand](https://foodondemand.com/12032025/cookunity-scales-up-with-250m-in-funding-connecting-chefs-to-home-diners/)).\n- **Canada:** It bought Toronto's Cookin and **launched in Ontario/Toronto in June 2025** with chefs including Patrick Kriss (Alo). Meals are fresh, never frozen, and delivered within 24 hours ([Retail Insider](https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2025/06/cookunity-launches-in-canada-with-top-chefs-to-redefine-meal-delivery/); [BetaKit](https://betakit.com/cookin-acquired-by-us-based-fellow-chef-made-meal-delivery-startup-cookunity/)).\n- **Pricing (US):** 4 meals at $13.59 per serving, 6 at $12.29, 8 at $11.69, plus a $9.99 delivery fee ([Healthline](https://www.healthline.com/nutrition/cookunity-reviews)). Plans run 4 to 16 meals a week.\n- **B2B:** It **acquired Fraîche** (May 2025), a smart-fridge platform with 20+ NYC office locations, 10k+ users and 500k+ meals served. Clients include PayPal and BCG, and the Empire State Building fridge sells 200+ meals a day. Users unlock the fridge by scanning in the app ([search summary of Business Wire release](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250514671018/en/CookUnity-Expands-Its-Chef-Led-Offering-Into-the-Workplace-with-Acquisition-of-Fra%C3%AEche)). It also has an Airbnb partnership, with Canada expansion planned for 2026 ([FSR](https://www.fsrmagazine.com/industry-news/cookunity-closes-strongest-year-yet/)).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Put **J&W smart fridges (unlocked by app or tap-to-pay) in GTA gyms, offices and condo buildings**, stocked with Meal Butler pre-set meals at $8.99 to $14.99. This is the Fraîche model, and CookUnity does not yet run it in Toronto.\n\n### 8. Clean Eatz (Wilmington NC, USA): franchised pickup meal plans with 5 revenue streams\n- **Scale:** **116 locations** in 20+ states with about 65 in development. **AUV slightly above US$1M.** The Clean Eatz Kitchen DTC arm brings in close to **US$28M**, and it has a **US Marine Corps contract** ([Food On Demand](https://foodondemand.com/03182026/outstanding-operator-clean-eatz/)). Meal plans plus grab-and-go make up about **75% of system sales**. The 5 revenue streams are meal plans, grab-and-go, cafe dining, catering and retail ([Franchising.com](https://www.franchising.com/news/20260709_clean_eatz_reports_strong_q1_as_fiverevenuestream_model_fuels_franchise_gro.html)). Average franchise unit revenue is about $1.087M with a 6% royalty ([Franzy](https://franzy.com/franchises/clean-eatz)).\n- **Pricing:** **Tiered per-meal pricing**: about $8.79 to $8.99 up to 9 meals, $7.89 to $8.29 for 10 to 14, $7.59 to $7.99 for 15 to 20, and $6.99 to $7.39 for 21+. Extra protein is +$1.75. Order online by Sunday night and pick up at a local cafe ([Clean Eatz guide](https://cleaneatz.com/blog/clean-eatz-meal-plan-guide-20260614)).\n- **Tech:** Punchh loyalty integrated with Brink POS. About 80% of franchise sales are dine-in or pickup ([Food On Demand](https://foodondemand.com/03182026/outstanding-operator-clean-eatz/)).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Use **volume-tier pricing**, where the per-meal price drops at 10, 15 and 21 meals, and turn the Brimley store into a **Sunday-cutoff pickup hub** with grab-and-go fridges. Later, license \"Meal Butler pickup points\" to gyms.\n\n### 9. Superfit Foods, owned by Muscle Maker Inc (Florida, USA): gym-cooler pickup network\n- **Model:** Subscribers **picked up meals and juices from coolers in 28 partner gyms and wellness centres**, restocked twice a week. Home delivery and national shipping were also available. It made **220,000+ meals in 2020** and had a 150+ meal menu ([QSR / NRN coverage via search](https://www.nrn.com/fast-casual/muscle-maker-grill-buys-florida-meal-prep-service-superfit-foods-for-525-000-as-part-of-expansion)). Muscle Maker bought it in 2021, reportedly for about US$525k (one source).\n- **Status:** As of October 2026, superfitfoods.com redirects to a GoDaddy \"domain for sale\" page. **The business appears to be inactive** (observed directly).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Copy the **gym-cooler pickup model**, which cuts last-mile cost because one drop serves many customers, but run it under J&W's own brand with its own route density. Superfit was a small acquired brand; J&W already has the plant and the routes.\n\n### 10. Factor, owned by HelloFresh (USA and Ontario): mass-market ready-to-eat\n- **Scale:** HelloFresh bought Factor in 2020 for up to US$277M. Management said Factor grew from about a €100M to a **€1B+ revenue run-rate** (2023 CEO remark, via [Think Insights](https://thinkinsights.net/strategy/hellofresh-chooses-profit-over-growth)). HelloFresh group revenue was €6.76B in 2025, down 11.8% ([HelloFresh Annual Report 2025](https://www.lobbyregister.bundestag.de/media/ff/cf/781215/Annual-Report-2025.pdf)). Factor's own revenue was not found.\n- **Product:** In September 2025 it expanded to **100+ weekly options**, doubled its high-protein options, extended the **GLP-1 Support line** and offers 65+ add-ons a week ([Business Wire](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250908230666/en)). It launched a \"Factor Form\" protein meal shake in June 2026 ([BoxRox](https://www.boxrox.com/factor-form-debuts-protein-packed-meal-shake-designed-to-support-weight-loss-and-muscle-retention/)). Its own marketing says subscriptions include **two free registered-dietitian sessions** ([Factor](https://www.factor75.com/stories/research/best-glp1-meal-delivery-reviews)).\n- **Canada:** It ships in Ontario. Meals cost from about C$15 each (6 meals) to about C$12 (18 meals), plus about C$10 shipping ([Flavour Network](https://flavournetwork.ca/article/factor-meals-review-canada)).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Create a **\"GLP-1 Companion\" menu**: small, protein-dense 3 to 4oz portions with fibre-rich sides and no added sugar. This fits J&W's existing line, which it markets as having no added salt, sugar or oil, and Ozempic and Mounjaro users need high protein in small volumes.\n\n### 11. Territory Foods (Washington DC, USA): local-chef network plus a fitness-franchisor partnership\n- **Scale:** **US$44M raised** in total, including a US$22M round in 2021 led by US Venture Partners ([Business Wire](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20210413005160/en/4954437/Territory-Foods-Raises-22M-in-Funding-Led-by-U.S.-Venture-Partners)). Owler estimates revenue at US$25 to 100M (estimate, [Owler](https://www.owler.com/company/territoryfoods)). It offers 35+ weekly options, still live in 2026 ([territoryfoods.com](https://www.territoryfoods.com/)).\n- **B2B:** It became **Xponential Fitness's first official prepared-meals partner**, with separate menus for Pure Barre, Row House, Rumble and StretchLab, accessed through a tile in each brand's member app, so the studios need \"no retail footprint\" ([Franchising.com](https://www.franchising.com/news/20230120_xponential_fitness_partners_with_territory_foods_to_optimize_health_results.html)).\n- **AI usage:** It uses \"proprietary data science and machine learning\" to choose regional menus by taste, lifestyle and protein trends (same source).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Sign **one multi-location fitness operator** in the GTA, for example a franchise group or a CrossFit or F45 cluster, to a co-branded menu offered inside its member app. One deal reaches thousands of members.\n\n### 12. Fresh Fitness Food (London, UK): bespoke macros, now owned by Calo\n- **Model:** It calculates each client's macros from height, weight and weekly training. For example, Fat Loss uses a 20% calorie deficit with 40% protein, 20% carbs and 40% fat. It delivers daily meal bags in London, about 1,000 bags a day (company claim) ([CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/company/fresh-fitness-food/financials)).\n- **Pricing:** From about £23 a day for 3 meals ([Women's Fitness](https://womensfitness.co.uk/?p=7585)). Another listing gives £140 to £215 for 5 days ([sp-edge](https://sp-edge.com/companies/350092)).\n- **Exit:** Acquired by Calo in April 2025 ([CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/company/fresh-fitness-food/financials)). It also sells through employee-benefit platforms ([Thanks Ben](https://www.thanksben.com/benefits-providers/fresh-fitness-food)). Revenue was not found.\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Offer a **premium \"Personal Macro Plan\" tier** at a set daily price, with portions weighed to the customer's own macros. J&W already weighs every portion, so this mostly means printing a personal label.\n\n### 13. Wild Fork Foods, owned by JBS (USA): blast-frozen premium meat stores, now leaving Ontario\n- **Scale:** About **60 to 64 US stores**: 64 per [ExchangeRight](https://www.exchangeright.com/tenant-profiles/wild-fork-foods/) and 60 per [Sentient Media, May 2026](https://sentientmedia.org/when-your-local-meat-market-is-owned-by-jbs/). Products are vacuum-sealed and frozen at -40 ([Tampa Bay Beacons](https://www.tampabaybeacons.com/2026/08/25/wild-fork-foods-to-replace-seminole-boston-market/)).\n- **Canada exit:** It entered in 2023 with plans for about 20 GTA sites and later aimed for about 30 Ontario stores. It is now closing **Whitby, Oakville, Ancaster and Newmarket**: online service ended October 13 and stores close by December 14, 2026. The Canadian range was 600+ products, and a Sobeys store-in-store had opened in Brantford in 2026 ([Retail Insider](https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2026/10/wild-fork-exits-canada-abandoning-ambitious-ontario-expansion-plans/)).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Run a **\"Welcome, Wild Fork shoppers\" campaign now**: geo-targeted ads in Whitby, Oakville and Newmarket, a first-box discount, and an emphasis on J&W's existing Tuesday, Thursday and Friday routes to those towns. J&W's flash-frozen, vacuum-sealed format is the same thing those customers are used to.\n\n### 14. Snake River Farms, owned by Agri Beef (Idaho, USA): ultra-premium Wagyu DTC\n- **Premium signals:** American Wagyu with **Gold Grade at BMS 9+**, graded by camera and verified by USDA. Family-owned since 1968 ([Food Fire Friends](https://www.foodfirefriends.com/snake-river-farms/); [snakeriverfarms.com](https://www.snakeriverfarms.com/)).\n- **Pricing:** Wagyu NY strip $55 (sale). Wagyu brisket $189. **Quarter beef $1,399** (sale, from $1,899). Gold Plus bundle $899. Wagyu ribeye filet 8oz 4-pack $272. Free shipping at $199+. Tiered promotions (spend $250 save $50, up to $400 save $120). Subscribe & Save \"Build Your Box\". **Gift bundles grouped by price** (<$200, $200 to $300, >$300). Group and corporate gifting ([snakeriverfarms.com](https://www.snakeriverfarms.com/)). Revenue was not found.\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Re-merchandise the **Wagyu, AAA and whole-animal range (quarter cow $1,200, whole lamb) as \"Freezer Stock-Up\" bundles**, with spend-and-save tiers and gift tiers by price.\n\n### 15. Omaha Steaks (Omaha, USA): corporate gifting at scale\n- **B2B:** **5,000+ business clients since 2020, 350,000+ custom gift certificates and 700,000+ coolers delivered** to business recipients. It offers volume discounts, custom branding, and \"Choice\" certificates where each recipient picks their own product (company claims, 2025 internal data, [Omaha Steaks business gifts](https://www.omahasteaks.com/business-gifts?SRC=RZ0636); [corporate certificates](https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/corporate-gift-certificates)). Revenue: not found.\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Launch **J&W Corporate Gifting**, with branded Greek or BBQ boxes and \"Choice\" e-certificates for GTA employers' holiday and client gifts. Q4 is the right time to start it.\n\n### 16. MegaFit Meals (Paducah KY, USA): founder-led fitness brand\n- **Model:** Founded in 2015 by brothers from a fitness background (RevGear, page no longer available). It sells meals, custom meals and bulk protein. \"Subscribe & Save\" is **20% off** with \"no subscriptions required\". Examples: Korean Beef Bowl $21.99 / $17.59, Chicken & Sweet Fries $15.99 / $12.79 ([megafitmeals.com](https://www.megafitmeals.com/)). Meals keep 5 days in the fridge or 60 days frozen (RevGear, page no longer available). Revenue was not found.\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Show **two prices on every product card** (one-off and 20% Subscribe & Save), so subscribing becomes the obvious default.\n\n### 17. MightyMeals (Northern Virginia, USA): retail through gyms and wellness stores\n- **Model:** Scheduled delivery **plus distribution through gyms, wellness centres, vitamin shops and bike and athletic stores** (e.g. VIDA Fitness, Balance Gyms) across the DC region ([ARLnow](https://www.arlnow.com/?p=160481)). ZoomInfo estimates revenue at about US$6.8M (estimate, [ZoomInfo](https://zoominfo.com/c/mightymeals/450469991)). PitchBook lists it as acquired or merged ([PitchBook](https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/484707-79)).\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Sell **wholesale to independent GTA supplement and vitamin stores and gyms** through a reach-in fridge program.\n\n### 18. Allen Brothers, owned by The Chefs' Warehouse (Chicago, USA): steakhouse supplier with a DTC arm\n- **Model:** It supplies prime steak to restaurants and sells direct to consumers. About **half of revenue came from catalogue and web orders** when revenue was above US$100M (older figure, [Provisioner](https://www.provisioneronline.com/articles/95049-hand-picked-and-high-end-1)). It was acquired by The Chefs' Warehouse with expected sales of US$80 to 85M at the time ([Meat+Poultry](https://www.meatpoultry.com/articles/9299-allen-bros-acquired-by-the-chefs-warehouse)). Current figures were not found.\n- **Best idea for J&W:** Use the **\"the steak our restaurant clients serve\" story**. J&W's family wholesale heritage (Advance Food Products, 1973) and its restaurant accounts are proof of premium quality for DTC buyers.\n\n### 19. Cautionary tales: what not to copy\n- **Porter Road (Nashville):** A whole-animal butcher that moved online. It raised a US$3.7M seed and a US$10M Series A to build a larger USDA plant in Kentucky ([TechCrunch via search](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2122352)). It **filed Chapter 11 on March 17, 2025** (case 3:25-bk-01134; liabilities of $1 to 10M) and closed its flagship shop on June 14, 2025. It continues online and through grocers ([Inforuptcy](https://www.inforuptcy.com/browse-filings/tennessee-middle-bankruptcy-court/3:25-bk-01134/bankruptcy-case-porter-road-butcher-meat-co-llc); [Nashville Post](https://www.nashvillepost.com/business/legal/porter-road-owners-declare-bankruptcy/article_bc085704-cc11-4762-a737-5015d2676cd1.html)). Lesson: do not over-build capacity ahead of demand.\n- **Farmison & Co (UK):** A premium online butcher with FY21 sales of £12M (company claim). It went into insolvency proceedings in 2023, and in 2025 the business was split up: the plant went to GPS Food Group and the brand and website to Donald Russell ([search summary of The Grocer / Food Manufacture](https://thestrayferret.co.uk/ripon-online-butcher-acquired)). Lesson: a premium DTC brand needs recurring revenue and B2B ballast.\n- **Snap Kitchen (Texas):** Closed **more than half its stores (20+)** in November 2020 and shifted to subscription delivery and Whole Foods counters ([Restaurant Business](https://restaurantbusinessonline.com/operations/snap-kitchen-close-majority-its-stores)). Lesson: grow retail through partners' shelves and fridges, not company-owned leases.\n- **Crowd Cow (Seattle):** A \"crowdfund a cow\" marketplace. It raised about US$25M in total and claimed more than US$1M a month in revenue early on ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/?p=1645280); [GeekWire](https://www.geekwire.com/2019/seattle-startup-crowd-cow-raising-cash-beef-meat-marketplace/)). Current status was not verified. Idea worth borrowing: **\"cowpooling\"**, where neighbours split a quarter cow, which suits J&W's $1,200 quarter cow.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Cross-cutting playbook for J&W\n\n### Subscription mechanics (copy all of these)\n- **Choice of rhythm:** weekly, bi-weekly or monthly. ButcherBox offers 2, 4, 6 or 8 weeks ([Hip2Keto](https://hip2keto.com/butcher-box-price/)).\n- **Self-serve skip, pause and swap** until a cutoff: 48h at Calo, the night before billing at ButcherBox, the Friday before at Trifecta.\n- **Default discount of 5 to 20%** for subscribing (MegaFit 20%), **volume tiers** (Clean Eatz), and a **\"free X for life\" hook** (ButcherBox).\n- **A one-off option alongside subscriptions:** ButcherBox's exit research found that many cancellations came from people who did not want a subscription.\n- **Points and referrals:** 2% back (ButcherBox); referral credit for both sides, $30 each at ButcherBox according to a coupon tracker ([GrabOn](https://grabon.com/butcher-box-coupons)).\n\n### B2B arms that work\n| Channel | Proof | J&W fit |\n|---|---|---|\n| Gym coolers and fridges | Superfit (28 gyms), Calo and Armah, MightyMeals, My Muscle Chef sampling | Very high: J&W's buyers are already gym-goers (\"Eat clean, train mean\") |\n| Teams and athletes | Icon (UFC equity, Dallas Cowboys, US Olympic Team) | High: OHL, university and college varsity teams, and combat gyms in the GTA |\n| Corporate fridges | CookUnity and Fraîche (20+ NYC offices) | Medium-high: return-to-office in downtown Toronto |\n| Clinics and hospitals | Icon (weight-loss clinics), Calo (hospital outlets) | High: GLP-1 clinics, bariatric programs |\n| Corporate gifting | Omaha Steaks (5,000+ business clients) | High, and seasonal (Q4) |\n| Grocery and retail | My Muscle Chef (6,000+ stockists), ButcherBox and Target | Later stage, once there is J&W-branded CPG |\n\n### AI used by the leaders\n- **Conversational planning:** Calo Black, an LLM private chef that builds daily menus from conversation.\n- **ML menu selection by region:** Territory Foods.\n- **AI-search content:** Icon Meals' \"ai-optimized\" blog and AI Knowledge Base, and Prep Kitchen's llms.txt.\n- **Self-serve plan management in the app:** Calo, Trifecta.\n- I found no public evidence of AI features at ButcherBox, CookUnity or Factor. That leaves room for a smaller operator to stand out.\n\n---\n\n## 4. What the top ideas could be worth to J&W (illustrative estimates, not forecasts)\n\nThese are my own estimates, built from the benchmark prices above and J&W's current price points. They are not company data.\n\n| Idea (copied from) | Assumption | Estimated annual revenue |\n|---|---|---|\n| Meal Butler Subscribe & Save plus Macro Packs (Trifecta, MegaFit) | 300 subscribers × C$120 per week (just above the $100 route minimum) × 46 active weeks | ≈ C$1.66M |\n| Gym and clinic wholesale fridges (Superfit, Icon, Calo) | 25 sites × 60 meals per week × C$9 wholesale × 50 weeks | ≈ C$675k |\n| J&W Butcher's Box, monthly raw-meat subscription (ButcherBox) | 250 subscribers × C$159 a month × 11 boxes | ≈ C$437k |\n| Corporate gifting, Q4 heavy (Omaha Steaks) | 40 companies × 25 boxes × C$120 | ≈ C$120k |\n| Wild Fork switcher campaign (Wild Fork exit) | 150 households × C$150 a month × 10 months | ≈ C$225k |\n\nTaken together, these come to roughly C$3M a year at maturity. Even at half that, the additional C$1M target is reachable without a new plant. Check retention, gross margin and route capacity before relying on any of these numbers.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Not found or unverified (for a follow-up run)\n- Current revenue for Omaha Steaks, Snake River Farms, Icon Meals (2024 to 2026), Fresh Fitness Food, Territory Foods, MegaFit, Crowd Cow and Porter Road.\n- Huel, Thrive Market and Balance Meals were not researched in this pass.\n- Per-lb bulk prices at Icon Meals and MegaFit (not published on their pages).\n- Factor's standalone 2025 revenue and Canadian 2026 pricing (the Flavour Network figures are undated).\n- Prep Kitchen's sales figures conflict: £13.9M (Sunday Times 2024), £22.8M (FEBE 2024) and £40M+ (company claim)."}, {"key": "market", "file": "market-benchmarks.md", "title": "Market benchmarks", "blurb": "Market size, GTA households and gyms, churn, CAC, email, delivery and margin benchmarks.", "md": "# J&W Foods / J&W Meal Butler: market benchmarks for sizing growth plays\n\nPrepared for Minastech (Erwin Garcia). Research date: **October 16, 2026**.\nCompanion files: `research/jw-facts.md` (verified company facts) and `research/jw-catalog.json` (979-product catalog digest).\n\n**How to read this file**\n- Every figure has a source URL. **[V]** means verified on the source page this session. **[S]** means it came from a search-result summary of that page and could not be opened directly (paywall, 403 or PDF), so re-check it before it goes on a slide.\n- **ESTIMATE** marks a number we derived. The reasoning is shown next to it. Estimates are never presented as sourced facts.\n- Currency: US-sourced benchmarks are in **USD** and converted to CAD at **1.4196 USD/CAD**, the Bank of Canada daily rate for 2026-10-15 ([BoC](https://www.bankofcanada.ca/rates/exchange/daily-exchange-rates/)) [V].\n- **Limitation:** part of the data came only from direct reads of known primary pages. A few items are marked \"not found\" for that reason (cold-chain courier rate cards, butcher-shop margins, Ontario Soccer registrations, Instacart merchant fees).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Canada / Ontario prepared-meal and meal-kit market\n\n| Metric | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Canada **ready meals** retail sales, 2025 | **CAD 5,000.51M**. CAGR 4.01% for 2020–2025 | [GlobalData, Canada Ready Meals](https://www.globaldata.com/store/?p=1949688) [V] |\n| Canada **prepared meals** (ready meals + meal kits + pizza etc.), 2021 | CAD 5.3B, forecast >4% CAGR to 2026. Leaders: Nestlé, Conagra, Bellisio | [GlobalData, Canada Prepared Meals](https://www.globaldata.com/store/report/canada-prepared-meals-market-analysis/) [S] |\n| Canada prepared meals (MarketLine definition), 2024 | $4,469M. CAGR 4.2% for 2019–24 | [MarketLine via ResearchPool](https://app.researchpool.com/provider/marketline/prepared-meals-in-canada-8gsi1L9lZj) [S] |\n| Canada **meal kits** (retail measured), 2025 | **CAD 342.86M**, +2.06% YoY. CAGR 3.65% for 2020–2025 | [GlobalData, Canada Meal Kits](https://www.globaldata.com/store/report/canada-meal-kits-market-analysis/) [V] |\n| Canada meal kits incl. DTC (Grand View Research) | ~$1.5B (2023), could reach ~$4.4B by 2030 if acquisition cost, logistics and churn are solved | cited in [Charlebois / Troy Media, Sep 4 2025](https://calgarysbusiness.ca/?p=1286699) [V] |\n| Canada meal kits, realistic estimate (Charlebois) | $1.2–1.7B (4–6% of households × $1,000–1,500/yr) | [same](https://calgarysbusiness.ca/?p=1286699) [V] |\n| Share of Canadians using **weekly meal-kit delivery**, Aug 2025 | **8.5%** (double pre-pandemic). 21% had tried one by Feb 2020. 4% used them regularly in 2020 | [Dalhousie Agri-Food Analytics Lab via Troy Media](https://calgarysbusiness.ca/?p=1286699) [V] |\n| Why lapsed meal-kit users quit | ~70% cost, 28% prefer cooking, 15% packaging | [same](https://calgarysbusiness.ca/?p=1286699) [V] |\n| Canada **online** meal-kit revenue, 2024 | US$157.5M. CAGR 11.3% forecast for 2024–28 | [ECDB](https://ecommercedb.com/markets/ca/meal-kits) [S] |\n| **Goodfood Market Corp** (Canada's largest DTC meal-solutions brand) FY2025 | Net sales **$120.9M, down 21% YoY** (from $152.8M). **Gross margin 41.7%**. Decline blamed on fewer active customers and lower order rates | [Goodfood Q4 FY25 release (PDF)](https://cdn.makegoodfood.ca/images/investors/docs/2025/q4/Goodfood_Q4_FY25_Earnings_Press_Release_en.pdf) [V] |\n| Goodfood status, 2026 | Q1 FY26 net sales $28M. Strategic review announced Jul 23 2026. **CCAA initial order Aug 5 2026**. TSX delisting review | [Goodfood press releases](https://www2.makegoodfood.ca/en/investisseurs/communiques) [V] |\n\n**Ontario share: not found as a separate figure.** ESTIMATE: Ontario is about 38–39% of Canada's population (16.3M on July 1 2025 per [Ontario projections](https://www.ontario.ca/page/ontario-population-projections) [V], against a national population of roughly 41–42M). Applying that share gives **Ontario ready meals ≈ CAD 1.9B** and **Ontario meal kits ≈ CAD 130M (retail-measured) to CAD 0.5–0.65B (incl. DTC)**. The **GTA (47.2% of Ontario)** would be about **CAD 0.9B of ready meals**.\n\n**What this means for J&W.** The packaged ready-meal pool grows about 4% a year and is large. The weekly-kit model is stalling on price and churn, and Canada's DTC leader is in creditor protection. That leaves room for a **local, cooked, portioned, high-protein** offer that does not require cooking.\n\n## 2. Online grocery penetration in Canada\n\n| Metric | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Online share of Canadian food/grocery market, 2025 | **0–5%** (ECDB band). Online food revenue US$2,163M in 2025. 2026 growth forecast 5–10% | [ECDB Canada Food](https://ecdb.com/resources/sample-data/market/ca/food) [V] |\n| Canada online grocery market size | US$3.6B (2025), US$3.84B (2026), US$5.31B by 2031 (6.69% CAGR). Top 5 grocers (Loblaw, Sobeys, Metro, Costco, Walmart) hold about 3/4 | [Mordor Intelligence](https://www.mordorintelligence.com/industry-reports/canada-online-grocery-market) [V] |\n| Online share of grocery (older reference) | about 2% of sales (2019, Charlebois) | [Canadian Grocer](https://canadiangrocer.com/conquering-last-mile) [V] |\n| All-retail e-commerce share, Canada 2025 | 13.0% of total retail | [eMarketer](https://www.emarketer.com/content/canada-ecommerce-forecast-2025) [S] |\n| Urban households that have used online grocery at least once | >58% by 2025 | [Report Cubes](https://www.thereportcubes.com/public/report-store/online-grocery-delivery-market-canada) [S] (low-quality source) |\n\nESTIMATE: online is **about 3–5% of Canadian grocery dollars** in 2026. Grocery is far less penetrated than retail overall (13%), which leaves J&W's online channel plenty of headroom.\n\n## 3. GTA population and households\n\n| Metric | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| GTA population, 2021 Census (Toronto + Peel + York + Durham + Halton) | **6,711,985**. Toronto 2,794,356 · Peel 1,451,022 · York 1,173,334 · Durham 696,992 · Halton 596,637 | [Wikipedia, citing StatCan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greater_Toronto_Area) [S]; [City of Toronto](https://www.toronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/92e3-City-Planning-2021-Census-Backgrounder-Population-Dwellings-Backgrounder.pdf) [S] |\n| GTHA (adds Hamilton), 2021 | 7,281,694 | [Wikipedia GTHA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greater_Toronto_and_Hamilton_Area) [S] |\n| Toronto CMA, 2021 Census | ~6.202M | [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greater_Toronto_Area) [S] |\n| **Toronto CMA population, 2025** | **7,108,874** | [Ontario Fact Sheet](https://www.ontario.ca/page/ontario-fact-sheet) [V] |\n| **GTA population, 2025** | **7.7M** (47.2% of Ontario). City of Toronto 3.3M. Ontario projects Toronto to **lose ~200,000 people over the next three years** | [Ontario Population Projections 2025–2051](https://www.ontario.ca/page/ontario-population-projections) [V] |\n| Ontario population trend | 16.3M (Jul 1 2025). Projected **−1.0% in 2025–26** and +0.2% in 2026–27 | [same](https://www.ontario.ca/page/ontario-population-projections) [V] |\n| Canada's 41 CMAs, Jul 1 2025 | 31,169,100 people. Growth slowed to 1.0% as non-permanent residents fell | [StatCan Daily, Jan 14 2026](https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260114/dq260114a-eng.htm) [S] |\n| **GTA private households, 2021** | **2,454,295** (Toronto 1,160,890 · Peel 450,745 · York 391,025 · Durham 243,045 · Halton 208,590). GTHA 2,677,100 | [City of Toronto 2021 Census backgrounder, Table 10](https://www.toronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/9877-City-Planning-2021-Census-Backgrounder-Families-Hhlds-Marital-Status-Income.pdf) [V] |\n| Toronto household mix, 2021 | 39.7% non-family. 385,765 one-person households (57% of all GTHA one-person households) | [same](https://www.toronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/9877-City-Planning-2021-Census-Backgrounder-Families-Hhlds-Marital-Status-Income.pdf) [V] |\n| Median household income (2020 income) | Toronto $84,000 (lowest in GTHA). Halton $121,000 (highest) | [same](https://www.toronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/9877-City-Planning-2021-Census-Backgrounder-Families-Hhlds-Marital-Status-Income.pdf) [V] |\n\n**ESTIMATE, 2026:**\n- **GTA population ≈ 7.6M.** This takes the 2025 figure of 7.7M and applies Ontario's −1.0% for 2025–26.\n- **GTA households ≈ 2.75–2.8M.** This uses the 2021 ratio of 2.73 people per household. The 2021 census count and the 2025 StatCan estimate use different coverage bases, so treat the household figure as ±5%.\n\n## 4. Fitness market: gyms, members, trainers\n\n| Metric | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Share of Canadian adults with a gym membership | **15.5% (2024)**, Fitness Industry Council of Canada. A Made-in-CA survey gives ~21% for 2025 on a looser definition | [Fitness Avenue roundup](https://www.fitnessavenue.ca/blogs/post/gym-membership-statistics-canada) [V] |\n| Canada fitness facilities and members | **7,000+ facilities, 6M+ members** | [Fitness Industry Council of Canada](https://fitnessindustrycanada.ca/) [V] |\n| Canada fitness and recreational sports centre locations, Jun 2023 | 9,493 | StatCan via [Fitness Avenue](https://www.fitnessavenue.ca/blogs/post/gym-membership-statistics-canada) [V] |\n| **Ontario gym, health & fitness clubs, 2026** | **4,195 establishments, 25,807 workers**. Forecast 5,024 by 2031 | [IBISWorld Ontario](https://www.ibisworld.com/canada/industry/ontario/gym-health-fitness-club-operation/2146/) [V] |\n| Canada gym businesses, 2026 | 9,710 (+3.6% YoY) | [IBISWorld Canada](https://www.ibisworld.com/canada/number-of-businesses/gym-health-fitness-clubs/1655/) [S] |\n| **CrossFit affiliates, Ontario** | **59** (Canada total ~177 listed) | [CrossFit.com gym finder](https://www.crossfit.com/gyms/canada) [V] |\n| CrossFit affiliates, GTA (older, undated) | 67 | [Toronto Life](https://torontolife.com/city/save-me-from-my-workout/) [S] |\n| Gym prices | Gym facility prices rose 5.2% (2023→24) and 15.9% since 2021 | StatCan via [Fitness Avenue](https://www.fitnessavenue.ca/blogs/post/gym-membership-statistics-canada) [V] |\n| **Fitness instructors & personal trainers (NOC 54100), Ontario** | **~59,450 employed**. 60% part-time. 31% self-employed (vs 15% for all occupations). Outlook \"Limited\" for 2025–27 | [Job Bank Ontario](https://www.jobbank.gc.ca/marketreport/outlook-occupation/21170/ON) [V] |\n| NOC 54100, Toronto economic region (the five GTA divisions) | **~31,460 employed** | [Job Bank Toronto Region](https://www.jobbank.gc.ca/marketreport/outlook-occupation/21170/geo9219) [V] |\n| New GTA gym supply | Gold's Gym signed for **17 GTHA locations** (Oct 2024), first opening 2025 | [Club Solutions](https://clubsolutionsmagazine.com/2024/10/golds-gym-signs-development-agreement-for-17-new-locations-in-toronto/) [S] |\n\n**ESTIMATES:**\n- **GTA gyms/clubs ≈ 2,000.** This is 4,195 × the GTA's 47.2% population share.\n- **GTA adult gym members ≈ 0.9–1.0M.** This is about 6.2M adults (assuming ~80% of 7.7M are adults) × 15.5%.\n- **Independent personal trainers in the GTA ≈ 8,000–12,000.** NOC 54100 also covers recreation leaders. We assume 30–40% are trainers, and 31% of the occupation is self-employed.\n\nThese are J&W's core \"EAT CLEAN – TRAIN MEAN\" audience and its affiliate/referral channel.\n\n## 5. DTC food subscription and e-commerce benchmarks\n\n### 5a. Churn and retention\n| Metric | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Meal-kit monthly churn** | **8–15%** (63–85% annualized) | [Eightx category churn benchmarks](https://www.eightx.co/blog/average-subscription-churn-rate-by-category) [V] |\n| Meal-kit cohort retention | **M1 78–88% · M3 40–55% · M6 28–42% · M12 15–28%** | [same](https://www.eightx.co/blog/average-subscription-churn-rate-by-category) [V] |\n| Replenishment/consumables retention (best case for \"standing order\" meal prep) | M1 92–94% · M3 70–80% · M6 55–70% · M12 40–55%. Monthly churn 5–8% | [same](https://www.eightx.co/blog/average-subscription-churn-rate-by-category) [V] |\n| Coffee subscriptions | 5–10% monthly. 28% cancel in the first 3 months. Skip-a-month lifts retention 22% | [same](https://www.eightx.co/blog/average-subscription-churn-rate-by-category) [V] |\n| Cross-category subscription average | 5.3% monthly (Eightx). Recurly median 7.02%, physical goods 10.54% | [Eightx](https://www.eightx.co/blog/average-subscription-churn-rate-by-category), [Eightx AOV](https://eightx.co/blog/average-ecommerce-aov-subscription-vs-onetime-2026) [V] |\n| Food & beverage subscription monthly churn (third-party) | 12–18%. Top-quartile target <6% | [Finsi](https://www.finsi.ai/blog/ecommerce-churn-rate-benchmarks-2026/) [S] |\n| Box cancellations | 44% of cancellations happen in the first 90 days. Failed payments cause about half of subscription churn | Swell / Paysafe via [Eightx](https://www.eightx.co/blog/average-subscription-churn-rate-by-category) [V] |\n\n### 5b. Order value, frequency, subscription adoption\n| Metric | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Recharge subscribers: orders per year | **4.4 orders per subscriber** over the past year (+15% YoY). 53% adjusted orders. **A food & beverage subscriber is worth more than 3 non-subscribers** | [Recharge Subscriber Trends, Sep 17 2024](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/recharge-subscriber-trends-insights-report-highlights-the-growing-value-of-subscribers-to-ecommerce-brands-302250623.html) [V] |\n| Food & beverage AOV, one-time vs subscription | One-time US$60–100. Subscription US$70–120 (+15–20%). Typical subscribe-and-save discount for food **10–15%** | [Eightx](https://eightx.co/blog/average-ecommerce-aov-subscription-vs-onetime-2026) [V] |\n| Food & beverage median AOV | US$56 (new-customer AOV US$54.30). Median blended ROAS 5.80 | [Amp benchmarks](https://useamp.com/benchmarks/food-beverage) [V] |\n| Food & beverage AOV from Google Ads traffic | US$71.39 (≈ C$101) | [Triple Whale Google benchmarks](https://www.triplewhale.com/blog/google-ads-benchmarks) [V] |\n| Amazon Subscribe & Save adoption (best customer-level proxy) | **23%** of US Amazon customers have an active S&S order (CIRP 2024). **35%** have ever used it (Jungle Scout 2024) | [Red Stag summary](https://redstagfulfillment.com/what-percentage-of-amazon-customers-use-subscribe-and-save/) [V] |\n| Recharge adoption among Shopify Plus stores | 9.43% (7,052 of 74,777 stores) | [Eightx](https://eightx.co/blog/recharge-vs-bold-vs-loop-subscription-shopify) [S] |\n| Food & beverage site conversion rate | 4.9% (Triple Whale). 6.22% (Dynamic Yield, secondary). Overall e-commerce 2.9% | [Triple Whale](https://www.triplewhale.com/blog/ecommerce-benchmarks) [S]; [Smart Insights](https://www.smartinsights.com/ecommerce/ecommerce-analytics/ecommerce-conversion-rates/) [S] |\n\n**ESTIMATE for J&W:** **20–35% of repeat meal-prep buyers** would take a \"standing weekly/biweekly order\" with a 5–10% discount and skip/pause. This sits between Amazon's 23% active S&S rate and the higher habit-formation of weekly meal prep. J&W's buyers already rebuild a similar cart every week.\n\n### 5c. Paid acquisition (USD, Triple Whale, Aug 1 2025 – Jul 31 2026)\n| Metric | Food & Beverage | All e-commerce | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Meta median CPA** | **US$38.57 (≈ C$54.75)**, +0.71% YoY | US$38.99 | [Triple Whale Meta, 40,000+ brands](https://www.triplewhale.com/blog/facebook-ads-benchmarks) [V] |\n| Meta CVR / CTR / ROAS | 1.89% (top vertical) / 1.98% / 1.61 | 1.53% / 2.39% / 1.88. CPM US$15.06 | [same](https://www.triplewhale.com/blog/facebook-ads-benchmarks) [V] |\n| **Google median CPA** | **US$23.65 (≈ C$33.57)**, +14.45% YoY | US$28.14 | [Triple Whale Google, 21,000+ brands](https://www.triplewhale.com/blog/google-ads-benchmarks) [V] |\n| Google CVR / CTR / ROAS / CPM | 4.26% / 1.63% / 3.18 / US$15.94 | 3.11% / 1.65% / 3.27 / US$15.35 | [same](https://www.triplewhale.com/blog/google-ads-benchmarks) [V] |\n\nNote: these are **cost per purchase**, not cost per *new* customer. ESTIMATE: **new-customer CAC for a GTA food DTC brand ≈ C$60–120.** That is 1.5–2.5× blended CPA. A tight geo-target (GTA only) shrinks the audience, and fresh/frozen food needs trust-building.\n\n### 5d. Email / SMS (Klaviyo 2026 benchmarks, 183,000+ brands)\n| Metric | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Email open rate | Campaigns 31%, flows 32.2% (top 10%: 45.1% / 45.8%) | [Klaviyo email benchmarks](https://www.klaviyo.com/products/email-marketing/benchmarks) [V] |\n| Email click rate | Campaigns 1.69%, flows 5.58% | [same](https://www.klaviyo.com/products/email-marketing/benchmarks) [V] |\n| **Food & Beverage email placed-order rate** | **Campaigns 1.16%, flows 1.79%** | [same](https://www.klaviyo.com/products/email-marketing/benchmarks) [V] |\n| **Flows' share of email revenue** | **~41% of email revenue from 5.3% of sends**. Flow RPR about 18× campaigns. Top-10% flow RPR US$7.79 | [same](https://www.klaviyo.com/products/email-marketing/benchmarks) [V] |\n| SMS click rate / order rate | CTR 5.6% campaigns, 9.65% flows. Order rate 0.27% campaigns, 1.9% flows | [Klaviyo SMS benchmarks](https://www.klaviyo.com/products/sms-marketing/benchmarks) [V] |\n| **Food & Beverage SMS revenue per recipient** | **US$0.34 campaigns, US$1.83 flows** | [same](https://www.klaviyo.com/products/sms-marketing/benchmarks) [V] |\n| **Email share of revenue, DTC food brands** | 22–38% of monthly DTC revenue. \"30–40% for mature DTC food brands\" (agency claim, no primary source) | [Redefine Web](https://redefineweb.com/blog/email-marketing-for-food-brands/) [V, but unverified claim] |\n\nESTIMATE for J&W: a well-run Klaviyo program should drive **20–30% of online revenue** from email + SMS, about 40% of it from automated flows. The agency figure is the upper bound. J&W currently has only a newsletter signup.\n\n### 5e. Referrals and reviews\n| Metric | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Referral share-action rate (500 Shopify stores, Jul 2025–Jun 2026) | Top quartile **4.64%**, top 10% **13.38%** of prompt views. Food & beverage sits \"toward the stronger end\". Top-quartile referred revenue ≈ 2.5× a typical program | [ReferralCandy 2026](https://referralcandy.com/blog/referral-program-benchmarks-whats-a-good-conversion-rate-in-2025) [V] |\n| Referred-visitor conversion | ~3–5% median, >8% top quartile (vendor). GrowSurf cites 8.2% | [ReferralCandy](https://referralcandy.com/blog/referral-program-benchmarks-whats-a-good-conversion-rate-in-2025) [S]; [GrowSurf](https://growsurf.com/statistics/ecommerce-referral-statistics/) [S] |\n| Intent vs action | 83% of satisfied customers are willing to refer. Only 29% do | [Rivo](https://www.rivo.io/blog/referral-program-statistics) [S] |\n| **Reviews: 5 reviews vs none** | **+270% purchase likelihood**. +190% for lower-priced, +380% for higher-priced items. Gains flatten after 5 reviews. Best ratings 4.0–4.7 stars. Verified-buyer badge +15% | [Northwestern Spiegel Research Center](https://spiegel.medill.northwestern.edu/how-online-reviews-influence-sales/) [V] |\n| Shoppers who interact with reviews | **+335% conversion**, +16% AOV (vendor claim) | [Okendo](https://okendo.io/?p=10426) [V, vendor] |\n\nJ&W shows **zero reviews** on 979 products. Adding a reviews widget and seeding it is one of the cheapest conversion levers available.\n\n## 6. B2B channel sizing in the GTA\n\n### 6a. Corporate (offices, catering, gifting)\n| Metric | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| City of Toronto establishments / jobs, 2025 | **74,560 establishments, 1,623,710 jobs** (+1.5%). **50% of jobs are office jobs.** Downtown has 664,650 jobs (40.9%) | [Toronto Employment Survey 2025](https://www.toronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/97af-CityPlanning-Toronto-Employment-Survey-Two-Pager-Summary-2025.pdf) [V] |\n| Hybrid work | Among firms reporting hybrid work, a quarter have staff on-site **3 days/week on average** | [same](https://www.toronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/97af-CityPlanning-Toronto-Employment-Survey-Two-Pager-Summary-2025.pdf) [V] |\n| Employer business locations by census division, Jun 2025 | Toronto 110,576 · Peel 68,233 · York 58,629 · Halton 24,986 · Durham 18,801 (GTA ≈ **281,225**) | StatCan CBC via [York Region factsheet](https://yorklink.ca/2025-york-region-business-counts-factsheet/) [V] |\n| York Region locations with **50+ employees**, Jun 2025 | 50–99: 1,355 · 100–199: 613 · 200–499: 295 · 500+: 91 = **2,354 (4.0%)** | [same](https://yorklink.ca/2025-york-region-business-counts-factsheet/) [V] |\n| Canada medium (100–499) / large (500+) employers, Dec 2024 | 16,953 / 3,380 | [ISED Key Small Business Statistics 2025](https://ised-isde.canada.ca/site/sme-research-statistics/en/node/439) [S] |\n| Canada caterers industry | **$3.3B (2026)**, 4,879 businesses. Demand driven by office parties, conferences, team events | [IBISWorld Caterers in Canada](https://www.ibisworld.com/canada/industry/caterers/1682) [V] |\n| Business dining trend | Purchases >$250 are now over 1/3 of business dining transactions (+32% vs 2019), driven by return-to-office | [Dinova](https://www.dinova.com/?p=5682) [S] |\n\n**ESTIMATE: ≈ 11,000–14,000 GTA business locations with 50+ employees.** This is 281,225 × York's 4.0% = 11,291. Toronto's head-office concentration probably pushes it higher. Toronto has **~810,000 office jobs** (1.62M × 50%).\n\n**Corporate holiday gifting (Canada)**\n| Metric | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| CRA tax-free cap for non-cash employee gifts | **$500/year** (incl. tax), plus a separate $500 long-service award | [Canada Revenue Agency](https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/businesses/topics/payroll/benefits-allowances/gifts-awards-social-events/gifts-awards-long-service-awards.html) [V] |\n| Employees who normally receive an employer holiday gift | 45% (Randstad Workmonitor, 2012) | [Canadian HR Reporter](https://www.hrreporter.com/news/hr-news/41-per-cent-of-employees-expecting-christmas-gift-from-employer/279251) [V, dated] |\n| Median employer holiday spend per employee (Hewitt, 117 Canadian orgs) | $300 cash bonus · $50 gift certificate · **$25 food items**. 88% hold a holiday party | [Canadian HR Reporter, 2004](https://www.hrreporter.com/news/hr-news/companies-less-like-santa-more-like-scrooge/310195) [V, very dated] |\n| Coworker gifting | 27% buy for peers. 65% of those spend <$25 | [Office Pulse, 2017](https://officepulse.captivate.com/most-canadian-professionals-plan-on-self-gifting-this-holiday-season) [V, dated] |\n| Consumer holiday budget, 2025 | $975 per Canadian (RCC/Leger) | [Retail Insider](https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2025/10/canadians-keep-holiday-budgets-steady-at-975-in-2025-rcc-leger-survey/) [S] |\n\nNo current Canadian per-employee corporate gifting figure was found. ESTIMATE: **C$50–150 per recipient** for premium food boxes. The reasoning: Hewitt's $25–50 from 2004 inflates to about $40–75 today, and premium \"client\" gifts sit higher, all well under the CRA $500 cap. J&W's existing boxes ($84.99–$199) already fit this range.\n\n### 6b. Daycares, schools, youth sport\n| Metric | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ontario licensed child-care centres**, Mar 31 2025 | **5,989 centres, 532,525 spaces** (0–12). 92% of sites in CWELCC ($10/day system) | [Ontario Early Years & Child Care Annual Report 2025](https://www.ontario.ca/page/ontarios-early-years-and-child-care-annual-report-2025) [V] |\n| **Toronto licensed child-care centres**, Dec 2025 | **1,078 centres, 85,054 spaces**. 565 centres run before/after-school programs. CWELCC fee cap $22/day (Jan 2025) | [City of Toronto fact sheet, Dec 2025](https://www.toronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/8fa0-December-2025-Fact-Sheet.pdf) [V] |\n| Ontario school food | Ontario Student Nutrition Program serves **600,000+ students**. National School Food Program deal: **$108.5M over 3 years** (Nov 2024), +160,000 students, 9.8M more meals in year 1 | [CollingwoodToday](https://collingwoodtoday.ca/local-news/ontario-reaches-1085-m-deal-with-feds-for-school-food-program-9853491) [S]; [Sustain Ontario](https://sustainontario.com/2024/11/29/ontario-signs-on-to-canadas-national-school-food-program/) [S] |\n| **GTHL** (world's largest minor hockey league) | **493 teams, 54 clubs**, 2025–26 (96 AAA, 193 AA, 204 A). **30,000+ participants** a year | [GTHL](https://gthlcanada.com/article/gthl-announces-team-entries-for-2025-2026-season) [V] |\n| Hockey Canada registrations, 2025–26 | 615,000+ players nationally | [Hockey Manitoba](https://www.hockeymanitoba.ca/news/five-straight-years-of-growth-for-hockey-canada-player-registration/) [S] |\n| Ontario Soccer / other GTA academies | **Not found** | — |\n\nDaycare and school food is a regulated, low-margin, tender-driven channel. Food must be nut-aware, menus must meet Ontario child-care nutrition rules, and price per child is tight. It fits better as a later play than as a fast $1M. **Sports teams and academies** fit J&W's protein brand: one AAA team is about 20 athletes plus families. Hospital/LTC food service was **skipped as not relevant** to J&W's premium positioning.\n\n## 7. Third-party marketplaces (merchant fees, Canada)\n\n| Platform | Fees | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Uber Eats Canada** | Delivery commission: **Lite 20% · Plus 25% · Premium 30%**. Pickup 10% (15% if pickup prices don't match in-store). Self-delivery 15%. Uber One orders on Plus +5%. Webshop 2.9% processing. Uber Direct (white-label delivery) has no commission and is priced by distance. Grocery and retail use the same plan structure | [Uber Eats Merchants CA](https://merchants.ubereats.com/ca/en/pricing/) [V] |\n| **DoorDash Canada** | Delivery: **Basic 20% · Plus 25% (27% with DashPass) · Premier 29%**. Pickup 10% / 8% / 8% (with price parity). Rates include payment processing. 0% commission during 7- or 30-day trials. $50/mo Premier ad rebate. Published Sep 25 2025; terms written for restaurants | [DoorDash Merchants CA](https://merchants.doordash.com/en-ca/blog/new-partnership-plans-ca) [V] |\n| **Instacart (Canada)** | Merchant rates **not public / not found**. Commission historically \">10% of every transaction\". CBC Marketplace found Loblaws/Costco items about **10% above in-store prices** on Instacart | [RetailWire](https://retailwire.com/discussion/is-it-time-for-retailers-to-reconsider-instacart/) [S]; [CBC](https://www.cbc.ca/1.6306306) [S] |\n| **Amazon.ca** | Grocery & Gourmet referral fee **8% if ≤ C$20, 15% if > C$20**, min C$0.40/item. Professional plan **C$29.99/month**, or Individual C$1.49/item | [Amazon.ca Sell pricing](https://sell.amazon.ca/pricing) [V] |\n\n**Takeaway.** At 20–30% commission, marketplaces only work for **high-margin cooked meals** (Goodfood-type gross margin ~42%). They suit trial acquisition, priced 10–15% above the webshop. Raw meat at butcher margins does not survive a 25% take rate.\n\n## 8. Delivery and cold-chain costs\n\n| Metric | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ontario general minimum wage** | **$17.95/h from Oct 1 2026** (was $17.60) | [Ontario ESA guide](https://www.ontario.ca/document/your-guide-employment-standards-act-0/minimum-wage) [V] |\n| **Delivery driver wage (NOC 75201)** | Toronto region: low $17.60 · **median $21.00** · high $30.00/h. Ontario median $20.00 (updated Nov 19 2025) | [Job Bank](https://www.jobbank.gc.ca/marketreport/wages-occupation/21667/ON) [S] |\n| **Refrigerated cargo van, new** (US dealer listings, reefer installed) | 2025 Transit-250 medium-roof reefer US$70,870–94,969 (**≈ C$100.6k–134.8k**). 2025 Transit-350 HD Thermo King US$87,110 (≈ C$123.7k). 2026 Transit-350 Thermo King V-520 US$76,211 (≈ C$108.2k) | Robins Ford (page no longer available), Northside Ford (page no longer available), Mission Valley Ford (page no longer available), [Comvoy](https://www.comvoy.com/work-truck/lyons-il/new-2026-ford-transit-350-refrigerated-body-19335800) [S] |\n| Canadian reefer van price | **Not found** (Ford Pro Canada page shows no price) | [Ford Pro CA](https://www.fordpro.ca/en-ca/fleet-vehicles/transit/) [V: no price shown] |\n| Cold-chain courier rates (Purolator, Canpar, Intelcom, Spring, etc.) | **Not found.** No public per-parcel refrigerated rate cards | — |\n\n**ESTIMATES (not sourced rate cards):**\n- **New reefer van in Ontario ≈ C$95k–130k.** Lease ≈ C$1,800–2,600/month over 60 months.\n- **Loaded driver cost ≈ C$25–27/h.** This is the $21 median plus ~20% for CPP, EI, WSIB and vacation.\n- **Owned-route cost per stop ≈ C$8–14.** Assumptions: a 9-hour route with 30–40 GTA stops, plus van, fuel and insurance at about C$150–200/day.\n- **Third-party cold-chain last-mile ≈ C$12–25 per insulated box** in the GTA. This is based on typical meal-kit courier economics and is not verified.\n- J&W's current **$20 GTA route fee on a $100 minimum** already covers an owned-route stop. A second weekly GTA wave or tighter delivery windows is mainly a density problem, not a cost problem.\n\n## 9. Smart fridges / micro-markets\n\n| Metric | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Byte smart fridge hardware** | **US$5,500 standard (≈ C$7,800) / US$7,000 XL (≈ C$9,940)**. US$500 off each when buying 3+ | [Byte pricing](https://www.bytetechnology.co/pricing) [V] |\n| Byte software and fees | Dashboard **US$125/month per kiosk (≈ C$177)**. Card fees 2.5% + US$0.20 per transaction. RFID tags US$0.15 each | [same](https://www.bytetechnology.co/pricing) [V] |\n| Byte case results | Michigan: first **2 fridges paid back in 4 months, US$60,000+ incremental sales in year 1**. BYU–Idaho: revenue per labour hour rose from $20–30 to $115 | [Byte case roundup](https://bytetechnology.co/?post_type=post&p=4120) [V] |\n| 365 Retail Markets | 80,000+ devices in 30+ countries (PicoCooler Vision smart coolers). No public pricing. Canada not listed as a regional site | [365 Retail Markets](https://365retailmarkets.com/) [V] |\n\n**Derived from the Byte case:** US$60,000 ÷ 2 fridges ÷ 52 weeks ≈ **US$577/fridge/week (≈ C$820)**. That was a high-traffic US campus. ESTIMATE for a GTA office, gym or condo-lobby fridge: **C$300–900/week** in gross sales depending on foot traffic (100–500 people on site). J&W's cooked meals ($9–15) and egg bites/oats are a natural fit.\n\n## 10. Gross margins\n\n| Business type | Gross margin | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **DTC prepared meals / meal kits** (Goodfood, Canada, FY2025) | **41.7%** (Q4 FY25 40.3%) | [Goodfood Q4 FY25 (PDF)](https://cdn.makegoodfood.ca/images/investors/docs/2025/q4/Goodfood_Q4_FY25_Earnings_Press_Release_en.pdf) [V] |\n| Food processing (US listed, 78 firms, Jan 2026) | **23.2% gross**, 11.0% operating, 2.8% net | [Damodaran, NYU Stern](https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/New_Home_Page/datafile/margin.html) [V] |\n| Retail grocery & food (US listed, 15 firms) | **26.3% gross**, 2.6% operating, 1.3% net | [same](https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/New_Home_Page/datafile/margin.html) [V] |\n| Food wholesalers (US listed, 13 firms) | 15.4% gross, 2.8% operating | [same](https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/New_Home_Page/datafile/margin.html) [V] |\n| Restaurants (US listed, 64 firms) | 32.2% gross, 17.2% operating | [same](https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/New_Home_Page/datafile/margin.html) [V] |\n| **Independent butcher shop** | **Not found** | — |\n\nESTIMATE:\n- **Independent butcher retail gross margin ≈ 25–35%.** It sits above grocery's 26% because of specialty and portion-cutting markups, but it is capped by commodity meat costs.\n- **Wholesale/processor sales ≈ 15–23%.**\n- **J&W cooked & portioned Meal Butler ≈ 40–50%.** It matches Goodfood's 42% and avoids meal-kit packaging and courier costs on pickup and route orders.\n\n**Implication:** each incremental dollar of cooked meal-prep or gift boxes is worth about 1.5–2× a dollar of raw meat.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Sizing helpers for the \"$1M more per year\" plan (all ESTIMATES built on the benchmarks above)\n\n$1,000,000 a year ≈ **C$19,230 a week**.\n\n| Play | Unit math | Units needed for ≈ $1M/yr |\n|---|---|---|\n| Meal-prep **standing-order subscription** | Biweekly C$120 order × 26 = C$3,120/yr per active subscriber | **≈ 320 active subscribers**. At 8–15% monthly churn, replace ≈ 25–50 a month. At C$60–120 CAC that is ≈ C$1.5–6k/month in acquisition |\n| **Gym / trainer partner program** | ~2,000 GTA gyms, ~59 Ontario CrossFit boxes, ~8–12k trainers | 50 partner gyms × C$400/week ≈ $1.04M, or 200 trainers each referring 4 clients × C$100/week |\n| **Corporate gifting (Q4)** | ~11–14k GTA locations with 50+ staff. C$50–150/box | 1% of them (≈ 115 firms) × 60 boxes × C$120 ≈ **$830k** in one holiday season |\n| **Office / gym smart fridges** | C$300–900/week gross per fridge, ≈ C$9–10k hardware + C$177/month | ≈ 25–60 fridges for $1M gross |\n| **Office catering / team meal-prep** | ~810k Toronto office jobs. Hybrid on-site ≈ 3 days | 40 offices × 25 staff × 2 meals/week × C$13 × 48 weeks ≈ $1.25M |\n| **Sports teams & academies** | GTHL 493 teams. 20 athletes per team | 60 teams × 20 athletes × C$40/week × 20 weeks ≈ $960k |\n| **Marketplaces** (Uber Eats / DoorDash) | 20–30% commission. Use for cooked meals only | Acquisition channel. Net contribution roughly half that of DTC |\n| **Email/SMS + reviews + referral on existing traffic** | Email/SMS 20–30% of online revenue. Reviews add up to 270% purchase-likelihood lift on reviewed SKUs | Likely the cheapest **+10–25% lift** on the current online base (the base is unknown, so ask J&W for it) |\n\n## 12. Gaps to close with J&W directly\n- Current annual revenue split (retail store / online / wholesale), online AOV, repeat rate, and active customer count. Without these, all lift percentages stay hypothetical.\n- Actual van fleet, route stops per day, and cost per stop.\n- Gross margin by category (raw vs cooked vs resold supplements/Costco items).\n- Plant capacity headroom (Ontario-inspected) for B2B volume."}, {"key": "ai", "file": "ai-agents-research.md", "title": "AI agents in food and DTC", "blurb": "How food, meal-prep, butcher and Shopify brands use AI agents, with results and costs.", "md": "# AI Agents for J&W Foods / J&W Meal Butler: Research Report\n\n**Prepared for:** Minastech (Erwin Garcia), growth proposal for J&W Foods Inc. (J&W Meal Butler), 2201 Brimley Rd, Scarborough\n**Date:** October 16, 2026\n**Scope:** How AI agents are used in 2025-2026 by food, meal-prep, butcher/meat-processing, grocery and DTC Shopify businesses, with evidence and URLs, then 15 candidate agents designed specifically for J&W.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Executive summary\n\n1. **The evidence is strongest where the work is narrow, repetitive and data-rich.** Customer-service agents resolve roughly 50-76% of conversations at the vendor level (Intercom Fin, Tidio Lyro). Route optimization cuts planning time by 80%+ (Onfleet: Inspired Go, a Canadian prepared-meal company). AI production and ordering forecasts cut fresh-food shrink by about 15-25% (Afresh, Shelf Engine, plus an independent multi-retailer pilot). Kitchen waste AI cut waste 18% at an Ontario cook-chill production kitchen (Winnow at Compass Group Canada's Milton Cook Chill).\n2. **Revenue-side AI is less proven but already large at platform scale.** Amazon says shoppers who use its Rufus assistant are 60% more likely to buy, and it expects $10B+ in annual incremental sales. Shopify reports orders from AI search channels up 11x (Q3 2025) and 15x (Q4 2025) since January 2025, then tripling year over year in Q2 2026. Personalization leaders typically see 5-15% revenue lifts (McKinsey).\n3. **J&W's gaps line up closely with where the proven agents work.** By J&W's own catalog: 979 products; about 504-508 with empty or very short descriptions; 413 with no product type; only 15 tagged; 59 with no image; 265 Meal Butler SKUs, of which only about 6 show numeric macro values; no subscription; no reviews; no macro or meal builder; no B2B portal. Every one of these is a known, solved AI use case.\n4. **Running custom agents on the Claude API costs very little next to the levers they move.** Rewriting the whole 979-product catalog costs an estimated US$10-15 one-off on Claude Opus 5.5 via the Batch API. A 3,000-conversation-per-month concierge costs an estimated US$100/month on Claude Sonnet 5.5. Off-the-shelf Gorgias AI Agent would cost about US$0.90-1.00 per automated interaction ([Gorgias pricing](https://www.gorgias.com/pricing)).\n5. **The 15 recommended agents fall into three groups:**\n   - **Revenue engines (5):** Macro Butler meal-plan builder, auto-reorder/subscription agent, B2B prospecting agent, lifecycle marketing agent, phone/voice order agent.\n   - **Conversion enablers (6):** nutrition data and label agent, concierge, catalog/SEO/AI-shopping agent, visual studio, ads optimizer, reviews agent.\n   - **Margin and ops (4):** production forecast, carcass and cut-yield, smart markdown, route and delivery.\n6. **Caveat on evidence quality.** Most quantified results in this market are vendor-published. Each figure below carries an evidence grade, and anything not found is marked as such, with a labelled estimate where useful.\n\n**Evidence grades used below**\n- **A:** Public filing or earnings call, government statistic, peer-reviewed or independent multi-party study.\n- **B:** Named-customer case study (usually vendor-published) or independent measurement firm.\n- **C:** Vendor marketing claim, aggregator, or blog with no stated method.\n\n---\n\n## 1. J&W starting point: the data the agents would work on\n\nFrom the 16 Oct 2026 crawl (`jw-facts.md`) and my own pass over `jw-catalog.json`:\n\n| Signal | Value | Why it matters for AI |\n|---|---|---|\n| Products online | 979 (743 available, 1,252 variants, 65 collections) | Big enough that manual copy and merchandising never get done; small enough for an agent to finish in one batch run |\n| Thin or empty descriptions | ~504-508 (51-52%) | Catalog/SEO agent target |\n| No product type | 413 | Breaks filtering, Shopify search, Google Shopping and AI-agent feeds |\n| Products with any tag | 15 | No structured attributes (protein, diet, cut, heat level, portion) for a meal builder to filter on |\n| Vendor field inconsistent | e.g. \"Costco\" (129) vs \"COSTCO\" (31); \"J&W Meal Butler\" (159) vs \"MEAL BUTLER\" (106); \"Kariba\" vs \"KARIBA\"; \"JW Foods Production\" vs \"JW Foods Production BAND SAW\" | Data hygiene task an agent can fix in one pass |\n| Products with no image | 59 | Visual studio agent target |\n| Meal Butler SKUs | 265 (median price $9.99) | Core of the macro builder |\n| Meal Butler SKUs with numeric macros in copy | ~6 (39 mention protein/calorie words at all) | **The main blocker for a macro coach.** The nutrition data agent must come first |\n| Case/bulk SKUs | ~45 (e.g. 10 lb salmon case $175, 25-pack chicken burgers $35.50, beef burger cases $49-79) | Base for a B2B and wholesale price list |\n| Whole-animal SKUs | 3 (quarter cow $1,200; whole lamb $400 deposit structure; half lamb $250) | Yield and carcass-balancing agent; premium bundles |\n| Fulfilment | Order by Sunday; 6 weekly route days; $100 delivery minimum; express $30 | Order cut-off gives near-perfect demand visibility. Ideal for production forecasting, reorder nudges and route agents |\n| Subscriptions, reviews, quiz, B2B portal | None | Greenfield |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Landscape: how AI agents are being used (2025-2026)\n\n### 2.1 AI nutrition and macro coaches, meal-plan builders\n\n| Company | What it does | Evidence | Grade |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Trifecta Nutrition** (US meal prep + app) | App has a macro calculator (inputs: goals, activity, body metrics) plus food logging and a barcode scanner. Meals customized to calorie and macro goals | [App Store listing](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/trifecta/id492100601); [Food Business News](https://www.foodbusinessnews.net/articles/16933-trifecta-bolsters-personalized-nutrition-services). **No source found** confirming a generative-AI meal planner | C |\n| **HelloFresh / Factor** | Aug 2025: US$70M investment. Doubled the weekly menu to 100+ options. AI/ML personalized recommendations to \"streamline decision making\" as choice grows. The group president tied it to retention (\"the more we invest in customers, the more they stay\"). Nov 2025: AI in recipe-card production, with human chefs still creating recipes | [Bloomberg Government](https://news.bgov.com/artificial-intelligence/hellofresh-is-investing-70-million-to-have-ai-help-plan-dinner); [Business Wire](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250804201495/en); [Food Manufacture](https://www.foodmanufacture.co.uk/Article/2025/11/24/hellofresh-uses-ai-to-accelerate-delivery-of-chef-created-recipes) | A/B |\n| **Fresh Fitness Food** (London) | Quizzes height, weight and weekly workouts, then computes daily macros and the calorie surplus or deficit. Example: Fat Loss plan uses a 20% deficit with a 40/20/40 protein/carb/fat split; Muscle Gain uses a 10% deficit. Users can set custom macros; changes need 2 working days' notice. Trial from about £23/day for two meals | [CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/company/fresh-fitness-food/financials); [Women's Fitness](https://womensfitness.co.uk/?p=7585); Trustpilot (DE) 4.3 from 1,254 reviews: [link](https://de.trustpilot.com/review/freshfitnessfood.com?page=9) | B/C |\n| **Noom: Welli AI assistant** | Launched June 2024 for 24/7 answers alongside human coaches. The CEO said it let Noom add 1:1 human coaching to Premium. A hybrid AI + human-escalation model | [Press release](https://www.fintechfutures.com/press-releases/noom-introduces-ai-enabled-products-to-enhance-on-demand-health-care-and-interactive-coaching); [TechTarget](https://techtarget.com/searchenterpriseai/news/366598870/Noom-CEO-on-the-need-for-AI-in-wellness-and-health) | B |\n| **Cal AI → MyFitnessPal** | Photo-based calorie tracking. 15M+ downloads and US$30-40M+ annual revenue (figures vary by source). Acquired by MyFitnessPal (closed Dec 2025, announced Mar 2026) | [Fitt Insider](https://insider.fitt.co/myfitnesspal-acquires-rival-food-tracker-cal-ai/); [Let's Data Science](https://letsdatascience.com/news/cal-ai-sells-to-myfitnesspal-after-rapid-growth-f789c949) | B |\n| **CookUnity** | Hiring staff ML scientists to build recommendation and ranking systems, measured with A/B tests and lift analysis. Delivers 35-50M meals/year (figures vary by posting) | CookUnity careers (page no longer available) | C |\n| **Octane AI quizzes** (Shopify) | Product-finder quizzes. Wicked Edge: quiz-taker AOV $727 vs $450 store-wide (+61%); conversion 3.6% vs 1.5% | [Octane AI case study](https://octaneai.com/case-studies/wicked-edge); [platform claims](https://octaneai.com/) | B/C |\n| **Personalization, general** | Personalization leaders achieve 5-15% revenue increases and 10-30% better marketing-spend efficiency | [McKinsey PDF](https://www.mckinsey.com/~/media/McKinsey/Business%20Functions/Marketing%20and%20Sales/Our%20Insights/The%20future%20of%20personalization%20and%20how%20to%20get%20ready%20for%20it/The-future-of-personalization-and-how-to-get-ready-for-it.pdf) | A/B |\n| **Amazon Rufus** (shopping assistant) | Shoppers who engage are 60% more likely to purchase. Expected US$10B+ annual incremental sales. 250M users in 2025 (Q3 2025 earnings call) | [Fortune](https://www.fortune.com/2025/11/02/amazon-rufus-ai-shopping-assistant-chatbot-10-billion-sales-monetization) | A |\n\n**Data sources and compliance J&W needs before building a macro coach:**\n- **Health Canada Canadian Nutrient File (CNF).** Free, downloadable as CSV relational files; 2026 edition covers 5,993 foods and up to 173 nutrients. Values are averages, not brand-specific. [Nutrient Data](https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/nutrient-data.html); [CNF 2015 downloads](https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/nutrient-data/canadian-nutrient-file-2015-download-files.html). An agent can compute per-SKU macros from J&W recipes plus cooked yields plus CNF values.\n- **Front-of-package \"high in\" symbol.** Mandatory since **January 1, 2026** for most prepackaged foods at or above thresholds (e.g. 15% DV) for saturated fat, sugars or sodium. [Packaging Insights](https://www.packaginginsights.com/news/canada-mandates-nutrition-symbol-2026.html); [Retail Council of Canada](https://www.retailcouncil.org/topics/food-grocery/food-product-labelling/front-of-package-nutrition-labelling/). Meat-specific and retail-packaged exemptions were **not verified** in this research; confirm with Health Canada/CFIA guidance before publishing panels.\n\n**Takeaway for J&W:** The leaders (Fresh Fitness Food, Trifecta, Factor) all pair a goals intake with macro math and a menu filtered to those goals. J&W has the menu but not the macro data or tags. Build the data first, then the coach.\n\n### 2.2 AI customer service on Shopify\n\n| Tool | Resolution / automation evidence | Price evidence | Grade |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Shopify Sidekick** (merchant-side) | Winter '26 Edition (Dec 2025): moved from reactive to proactive (Sidekick Pulse tasks on Home). Generates admin apps from natural language, edits themes, builds segments and Flow workflows, saves reusable \"skills\". Never changes the shop without merchant approval | Included in Shopify admin (plan pricing not verified) | [Shopify Help: Sidekick](https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/shopify-admin/productivity-tools/sidekick/features); [Shopifreaks](https://www.shopifreaks.com/shopify-unveils-winter-26-edition-with-major-upgrades-to-sidekick-and-new-ai-commerce-tools/); [Shopify Community](https://community.shopify.com/t/winter-editions-2026-is-now-out-highlights-for-merchants/579120). Grade B |\n| **Gorgias AI Agent** (customer-side, Shopify-native) | Vendor advertises 60% automation. Named stories: ALOHAS 83%, Cocorico 48%, Orthofeet 56% ([Gorgias customers](https://www.gorgias.com/customers)). A competitor says published case studies land at 26-56% ([Ringly](https://www.ringly.io/blog/gorgias-ai-agent-pricing-per-resolution)). G2 response-accuracy score reportedly 59% vs 71% category average ([Ringly G2 analysis](https://www.ringly.io/blog/gorgias-g2-rating-2025)) | Helpdesk Starter $10, Basic $50-60, Pro $300-360, Advanced $750-900/mo. AI Agent **$0.90** (annual) / **$1.00** (monthly) per automated interaction; $1.50 overage ([Gorgias pricing](https://www.gorgias.com/pricing)) | B/C |\n| **Intercom Fin** | 51% out of the box ([Anthropic customer story](https://www.anthropic.com/customers/intercom)). 56% average within 30 days ([AWS case study](https://aws.amazon.com/solutions/case-studies/intercom-anthropic/)). 67% average at the Fin 3 launch, 2025 ([CreateWith](https://www.createwith.com/tool/intercom/updates/intercom-ships-200-updates-in-2025-fin-3-reaches-67-average-resolution-rate)). 76% across 12,000+ customers, verified June 2026 ([AI Agent Index](https://theaiagentindex.com/agents/intercom-fin)) | **$0.99 per resolution**, 50-resolution monthly minimum ([Gleap](https://www.gleap.io/blog/intercom-fin-ai-pricing-2026)) | B/C |\n| **Tidio Lyro** | Jiffy.com (ecommerce): 70% resolution \"within weeks\", 7,700 knowledge articles ([Tidio case study](https://www.tidio.com/customers/jiffy/)). Platform average 67%, with a 50% resolution guarantee on Plus/Premium ([mer.vin guide](https://mer.vin/2026/07/tidio-platform-guide-lyro-ai-agent-help-desk-and-flows-pricing/)) | Lyro standalone from **$32.50/mo** with 50 conversations; first 50 free on Free/Starter ([Tidio pricing](https://www.tidio.com/pricing/)) | B/C |\n| **Daily Harvest** (frozen meal DTC) | AI guide \"Sage\" reported a 60% containment rate (2022) | [Quiq customer story PDF](https://Quiq.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/Daily-Harvest-Customer-Story.pdf) (figure from search summary; PDF text not extractable here) | B |\n\n**Caveat:** \"Containment\" counts a customer who gave up the same as one who was helped. Measure resolution plus repeat contact plus CSAT ([Kaizo](https://kaizo.com/blog/what-is-containment-rate/)).\n\n**Takeaway for J&W:** For a family business with an estimated low-thousands of tickets a month, the realistic target is 50-70% automated resolution of \"where's my order / what's in this / which zone and day / can I change my order / is this gluten-free\" questions. Allergen and medical questions must go to a human.\n\n### 2.3 WhatsApp / SMS ordering bots\n\n| Case | Result | Source | Grade |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| JioMart (India grocery, Haptik) | 200,000+ monthly orders via WhatsApp; 68% of users bought again | [Voicebot.ai](https://voicebot.ai/2022/12/29/whatsapp-chatbot-for-buying-groceries-spikes-repeat-purchase-rate-for-indian-grocery-chain-jiomart/) | B |\n| Heritage Foods (dairy, WATI) | 2x higher order conversion after a WhatsApp-first catalog and ordering flow | [CaseStudies.com](https://www.casestudies.com/company/wati/case-study/heritage-foods-doubles-order-conversion-with-wati) | B/C |\n| FreshVeget (Razorpay) | +25% conversion after in-chat payments | [Razorpay](https://razorpay.com/blog/?p=18260) | C |\n| Bakery order bot (Indigitall) | 65% of inquiries resolved by bot; +30% repeat purchases | [Indigitall](https://indigitall.com/en/success-story/revolutionizing-e-commerce-order/) | C |\n| Vico Food Box (Italian e-grocer, live chat + bot) | +8% AOV; 21.4% of earnings via chat | [Smartsupp](https://smartsupp.com/case-studies/vico-food-box-obtained-98-percent-customer-satisfaction-rating-and-increased-their-average-order-value-with-Smartsupp/) | B/C |\n\n- **Channel economics:** WhatsApp moved to per-message pricing on July 1, 2025 ([Meta developer docs](https://developers.facebook.com/docs/whatsapp/pricing)). A May 2026 rate card lists Canada at 30.87 (marketing) / 4.20 (utility) / 0 (service). Units are unconfirmed in the snippet ([Salesforce-hosted rate card](https://www.salesforce.com/en-us/wp-content/uploads/sites/4/WhatsApp-Business-Messaging-Rate-Card-MM-Lite-May-1-2026.pdf)). For SMS, a dataset of 246M+ sends shows 12.39% CTR, 0.12% conversion per send, $125 AOV, and about $0.15 per SMS ([Omnisend benchmarks](https://www.omnisend.com/blog/sms-marketing-benchmarks/)).\n- **Canadian law (CASL):** SMS needs express, SMS-specific consent, sender identification, contact info and an easy \"Reply STOP\", and the business must be able to prove consent ([Telair CASL guide](https://www.telair.net/resources/knowledge-base/guides/casl-sms-compliance/); [BulkSMS](https://www.bulksms.com/resources/regulations/canadian-requirements-for-sending-commercial-sms-messages.htm)).\n- **WhatsApp penetration among GTA customers: not found.** Estimate: SMS should be the default reorder channel and WhatsApp an opt-in for customers who prefer it.\n\n**Takeaway for J&W:** The Sunday order cut-off suits a conversational reorder: \"Hi Maria, same as last week (12 items, $164)? Reply YES, or tell me what to change.\" This works with no app download, which is the pattern behind JioMart's repeat rate.\n\n### 2.4 AI demand forecasting and production planning for perishables\n\n| Company / case | Result | Source | Grade |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Afresh** (fresh-department ordering AI) | Claims 25% shrink reduction and 80% fewer stockouts. CEO interview: 20-25% shrink reduction, 3-5% sales growth. Cub Foods CIO: 18% produce shrink cut after full rollout. Raised $34M (Apr 2026); live in 12,500+ departments in 40 states | [Afresh](https://www.afresh.com); [AndNowUKnow](https://andnowuknow.com/node/83880); [Supermarket News](https://www.supermarketnews.com/grocery-technology/how-afresh-is-revolutionizing-grocery-perimeters-with-ai); [Shelby Report](https://theshelbyreport.com/2026/04/21/afresh-raises-34m-to-scale-ai-across-grocery-industry/) | B/C |\n| **Shelf Engine** (acquired by Crisp, Mar 2025) | Independent Pacific Coast Food Waste Commitment pilot (Afresh + Shelf Engine, two retailers): **14.8% food waste reduction per store**, with savings that more than covered the AI cost. Kimberton Whole Foods: +42% sales on managed items | [PCFWC pilot PDF](https://pacificcoastcollaborative.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/PCFWC-Case-Study_AI_Final.pdf); [Business Wire](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250327362637/en/Crisp-Acquires-Shelf-Engine-AI-Demand-Planning-Solution-to-Support-Optimal-Retail-Forecasting-and-Inventory-Management); [Perishable News](https://perishablenews.com/retailfoodservice/kimberton-whole-foods-increases-sales-by-42-with-shelf-engine-ai/) | **A-/B** |\n| **Crisp** | \"Demand Forecasting Innovation of the Year\" (June 2026). Ingests daily sales from 40+ retailers; no published accuracy figures found | [Crisp](https://gocrisp.com/blog/crisp-wins-demand-forecasting-innovation-award-for-ai-that-translates-to-real-world-supply-chains) | C |\n| **PreciTaste at honeygrow** (restaurant prep AI) | 4-5 hours of prep time saved per location per day; \"dramatic decrease\" in waste (not quantified) | [Fast Casual](https://www.fastcasual.com/blogs/from-prep-to-profit-how-honeygrow-is-tackling-rising-costs-with-ai-automation/) | B |\n| **Winnow at Compass Group Canada, Milton Cook Chill** (an Ontario production kitchen) | **18% reduction in food waste by value; $63,000/yr saved**; about 1.2 tons diverted | [CaseStudies.com](https://www.casestudies.com/company/winnow/case-study/compass-group-saves-63000-a-year-with-winnow) | B |\n| **Winnow at Salem Health** | 33% waste cut by value; ROI in 17 weeks; 65% less salmon waste through repurposing | [CaseStudies.com](https://www.casestudies.com/company/winnow/case-study/salem-health-cuts-food-waste-33-with-winnow) | B |\n| Academic (Favorita perishables data, 2026) | The model with the best accuracy metric was not the one with the least waste; XGBoost produced the lowest estimated waste. Optimize for waste, not just MAPE | [Oeconomica](https://www.oeconomica.org.ro/abstract/697/When-Accurate-Forecasts-Still-Produce-Waste-Demand-Shocks-and-Over-Forecasting.html) | A- |\n\n**Canadian context:** 46.5% of food produced for Canada is wasted; avoidable waste is worth an estimated $58B (Second Harvest 2024 update) ([Second Harvest](https://wfprod.secondharvest.ca/post/new-report-from-second-harvest-reveals-canadas-58-billion-food-waste-problem); [CTV](https://www.ctvnews.ca/toronto/article/almost-half-of-all-food-wasted-in-canada-is-avoidable-new-report-suggests/)).\n\n**Takeaway for J&W:** Afresh, Shelf Engine and Crisp are built for multi-store grocers and are overkill here. J&W's advantage is the Sunday cut-off: most route demand is known before cooking starts. The forecasting problem shrinks to three parts: (a) pickup, express and walk-in demand, (b) raw-material purchasing ahead of the cut-off, and (c) frozen safety-stock levels. A custom Claude agent with a simple statistical forecast fits this well, plus Winnow-style waste logging (a tablet and scale) to close the loop.\n\n### 2.5 Meat cut-yield and carcass optimization\n\n- **Völur.** AI decides how each carcass should be sorted and cut. It generates daily cutting plans for JBS USA's Utah beef plant on Microsoft Azure ([Völur](https://www.volur.ai/)). Its site cites planning time falling from 2-6 hours (manual) to 15-30 seconds, plus a Yield Insights tool for trim-vs-boning decisions. Named users include Nortura (Norway) and Grupo D'Avinyó ([Völur](https://www.volur.ai/)). Grade B/C.\n- **Vendor claim:** AI-driven precision cutting delivers 1-3% more yield per carcass ([Folio3 whitepaper](https://foodtech.folio3.com/whitepapers/ai-automation-for-the-meat-industry/)). Grade C, unverified.\n- **ERP-level yield tracking** (actual vs expected yield per primal, sub-primal and retail cut): [inecta](https://www.inecta.com/meat-processing-software). Grade C.\n- **No small-butcher AI yield case study was found.**\n\n**Takeaway for J&W:** At J&W's scale, the value isn't robotic cutting. It's carcass balancing: matching which primals to buy and break against (a) raw-cut demand, (b) the cooked line's appetite for trim and secondary cuts (flank for fajitas, trim for burgers and meatballs), and (c) box and bundle promotions that move overstocked cuts. An agent on top of a simple yield-test log (cutter, primal, input weight, output by cut) can do this weekly.\n\n### 2.6 AI dynamic pricing and markdowns for perishables\n\n- **Wasteless (Spanish retailer, 12 weeks, salads, sandwiches and cut fruit):** waste down 32.7%, revenue up 6.2% ([Wasteless](https://wasteless.com/success/leading-spanish-retailer-cuts-waste-by-32)). An Italian retailer reportedly halved waste and gained 1.2 points of margin ([Wasteless resources](https://Wasteless.com/resources)). Results depend heavily on context ([FAO Agritech Observatory](https://agritechobservatory.review.fao.org/zh/wasteless)). Grade B.\n\n**Takeaway for J&W:** Most Meal Butler stock is flash-frozen, so expiry-driven electronic shelf labels aren't the play. The lever is (a) the fresh raw counter and short-dated items, sold through a weekly \"Butcher's Clearance\" SMS/email drop, and (b) bundling overstocked cuts into boxes. Margin floors are set by the owner.\n\n### 2.7 AI product descriptions, SEO and AI-shopping discoverability at catalog scale\n\n- **Shopify Magic:** writes product descriptions in the admin; Shopify notes millions of its products have no description ([Shopify blog](https://www.shopify.com/blog/ai-product-descriptions)). A reviewer estimates 100 descriptions take about 20 minutes with Magic vs about 20 hours by hand, plus 8-10 hours of editing ([tenten](https://tenten.co/shopify/shopify-magic-ai-features-ranked/)). Grade C.\n- **Measured SEO effects vary widely:**\n  - +5.4% organic clicks for a major US retailer ([WordLift](https://wordlift.io/free-ai-product-description-generator/))\n  - +18% organic traffic and +12% conversion on 1,200 SKUs ([VortexIQ](https://www.vortexiq.ai/?p=23886))\n  - +112% year-on-year organic clicks after AI rewrites (EXPRE / Sewing Online, page no longer available)\n  - All vendor or agency results (grade C). Run a Search Console holdout.\n- **AI shopping channels are growing fast:**\n  - Shopify AI-attributed orders up 11x since Jan 2025 by Q3 2025 ([PYMNTS](https://www.pymnts.com/news/ecommerce/2025/shopify-president-agentic-ai-drives-11x-spike-in-orders))\n  - Up 15x by Q4 2025 ([Digital Commerce 360](https://www.digitalcommerce360.com/2026/02/17/shopify-revenue-gmv-q4-2025/))\n  - AI-referred traffic and orders tripled year over year in Q2 2026 ([PYMNTS](https://www.pymnts.com/earnings/2026/shopifys-ai-traffic-triples-as-shoppers-skip-the-search-bar/))\n  - Grade A (earnings calls), small base.\n  - Shopify's Agentic Storefronts list merchant products in ChatGPT, Perplexity and Copilot ([BetaKit](https://betakit.com/?p=397830)). **Canadian merchant eligibility was not confirmed.**\n- **Why J&W's data matters:** AI assistants and Shopify search depend on structured product data. J&W's 413 blank product types and 15 tagged products mean it is close to invisible to these channels today.\n\n### 2.8 AI-generated photography\n\n- **Pebblely pricing:** listings run US$9/mo (30 images), $19/mo (200) and $39/mo (500), which is about $0.08-0.30 per image ([Kive comparison](https://kive.ai/learn/kive-vs-pebblely); [Capterra](https://www.capterra.com/p/10016326/Pebblely/)). Grade C. A professional food still-life example was priced around $500/image ([Wonderful Machine](https://wonderfulmachine.com/article/pricing-negotiating-combining-food-still-life-projects/)).\n- **Best practice:** start from a clean, real product shot and use AI only for the scene or background (tenten AI photography guide, page no longer available).\n- **Takeaway for J&W:** Food must never be AI-altered to look different from what ships (an accuracy guardrail). Use AI for backgrounds, lifestyle scenes, ad resizes and the 59 missing images, all from real photos.\n\n### 2.9 AI ad creative and campaign automation (Meta Advantage+)\n\n- **Meta's own figures:** Advantage+ sales campaigns deliver +22% ROAS on average, and the GEM model improves conversions by up to 5% (Cannes 2025) ([eMarketer](https://www.emarketer.com/content/meta-launches-11-new-ai-ad-tools-cannes-lions-2025)). 30% more advertisers used AI creative tools in Q1 2025 ([PPC Land](https://ppc.land/metas-ad-business-soars-as-ai-tools-drive-16-revenue-growth-in-q1/)). Grade A-/B, company-reported.\n- **Independent measurement (Haus, 640 geo-holdout tests):** Advantage+ beat manual only 42% of the time. It led by about 9% mid-test but finished about 12% *worse* on incremental ROAS once post-treatment effects were counted ([Haus](https://www.haus.io/meta); [Haus blog](https://www.haus.io/blog/the-meta-report-lessons-from-640-haus-incrementality-experiments)). Grade B.\n- **Takeaway for J&W:** Use Advantage+ for catalog retargeting. Keep manual, zone-targeted prospecting (by delivery route day) and measure by geo holdout.\n\n### 2.10 Klaviyo AI: predictive analytics and segments\n\n- **Predictive metrics:** predicted CLV, churn risk, average time between orders and **expected date of next order**. Requirements: at least 500 customers who have placed orders, 180+ days of history, orders in the last 30 days, and some customers with 3+ orders ([Klaviyo Help](https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360020919731)). J&W (online since 2016, weekly ordering) very likely qualifies; this needs confirming in their account. Grade A (vendor docs).\n- **Flows vs campaigns:** flows generate about 41% of email revenue from 5.3% of sends, with about 18x campaign revenue per recipient ([Klaviyo benchmarks](https://klaviyo.com/products/email-marketing/benchmarks)). Grade B.\n- **K:AI and AI segments:** Klaviyo's K:AI Marketing Agent builds campaigns, flows and forms ([Klaviyo](https://marketing-origin-netlify.klaviyo.com/solutions/ai/marketing-agent)). AI segments are described in plain language ([Klaviyo](https://www.klaviyo.com/product/whats-new/segments-ai)). Force of Nature: +52% YoY Klaviyo-attributed value using AI segments ([Klaviyo blog](https://marketing-origin-netlify.klaviyo.com/blog/companies-using-ai-marketing)). Grade B/C.\n- **Cart abandonment** averages 70.22% across 50 studies ([Baymard](https://baymard.com/lists/cart-abandonment-rate)). Grade A-.\n\n### 2.11 Route optimization for weekly delivery routes\n\n| Case | Result | Source | Grade |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Inspired Go** (Canadian prepared-meal delivery, Onfleet) | Route planning 8-10 h/day cut to under 1 h (85%+). 72,000+ deliveries/month in Canada; about 10,000 in one day; new-market launch cut from about 3 months to 1-2 weeks | [Onfleet](https://onfleet.com/blog/case-studies-inspired-go/) | B |\n| Thistle (prepared meals, Onfleet) | 10-20% more deliveries per day with the same staff; 95%+ on time | [Onfleet](https://onfleet.com/blog/thistle-case-study/) | B |\n| Asian Veggies (grocery, Onfleet) | 83% less route-management time (was up to 1.5 h/day) | [Onfleet](https://onfleet.com/blog/case-study-asian-veggies/) | B |\n| Fresh Prep (Western Canada meal kits, Onfleet) | Weekly push of orders to the routing engine triggers production | [Onfleet](https://onfleet.com/blog/case-study-fresh-prep/) | B |\n| The Foodery (Boston meal delivery, Routific) | 28% lower delivery costs than manual routing (vendor figure; source page retired) | IoT ONE (page no longer available) | B |\n| Food Made Fresh (Routific) | \"4x more meals\" (headline only) | [FeaturedCustomers](https://www.featuredcustomers.com/vendor/routific/case-studies) | C |\n\n**Pricing:**\n- Routific: free up to 100 orders/month; $150/month for 101-1,000 orders; unlimited drivers ([Capterra](https://www.capterra.ie/software/142530/routific); [Upper](https://www.upperinc.com/blog/routific-pricing/))\n- Circuit for Teams: from about $100/month for 500 stops ([Circuit pricing](https://getcircuit.com/teams/pricing))\n\n### 2.12 AI B2B prospecting (gyms, trainers, corporate, teams)\n\n- **Clay:**\n  - Qrew: half the sales team, about 2x positive replies, +40% meetings ([Clay](https://clay.com/blog/qrew-case-study))\n  - Harmonic: 3x outbound reply rates ([Clay for Sales](https://www.clay.com/clay-for-sales))\n  - 2026 pricing: Launch $185/mo ($167 annual) with 2,500 data credits; Growth $495/mo ([Valley](https://www.joinvalley.co/blog/clay-pricing-and-credits-explained-2026))\n  - Grade B/C.\n- **Apollo:**\n  - Vendor claims +46% meetings with its AI Research Agent ([Apollo](https://www.apollo.io/solutions/small-businesses)). Grade C.\n  - A solo founder's 60-day test: 4.2% reply rate, 12 then 18 meetings/month (AI Agent Rank, page no longer available). Grade C, single anecdote.\n  - Basic about $49-59/user/month ([Capterra](https://www.capterra.com/p/158696/Apollo/pricing/)).\n- **Market size:** 9,493 fitness and recreational sports centre locations in Canada (June 2023) ([Statistics Canada](https://statcan.gc.ca/o1/en/plus/5329-working-out-numbers-your-gym-resolution)). Grade A. **GTA count not found.** Estimate: about 1,300-1,600 locations, assuming the GTA holds roughly 15-17% of Canada's population and gym density matches the national average. Needs verifying against the StatCan Business Register for Toronto (NAICS 71394).\n\n### 2.13 AI voice agents for phone orders\n\n- **Loman (vendor):** up to 22% higher phone revenue and up to 17% lower labour; a typical restaurant misses about 150 calls/month worth $35-85 each ([Loman](https://loman.ai/blog/restaurant-ai-phone-receptionist-revenue)). Grade C.\n- **Hostie (vendor, modelled):** 87% fewer missed peak-hour calls ([Hostie](https://hostie.ai/resources/500k-call-study-ai-hosts-slash-missed-dinner-rush-calls-87-percent-revenue-impact)). Grade C.\n- **Cost:**\n  - Vapi platform fee $0.05/min plus model and telephony; all-in commonly $0.07-0.31/min ([Ainora](https://ainora.lt/blog/ai-voice-agent-cost-per-minute-2026); [Fora Soft](https://www.forasoft.com/blog/article/vapi-vs-retell-vs-custom))\n  - Retell infra about $0.055/min plus TTS/LLM ([Macha](https://www.getmacha.com/blog/retell-ai-vs-vapi))\n- **Takeaway for J&W:** The store line (416) 298-1350 is staffed only Mon-Fri 9-6 and Sat 10-4, so evenings and Sundays (the order-cut-off day) are uncovered. An after-hours voice agent that answers menu, zone and order-status questions and texts a pre-filled cart link is low-risk and measurable.\n\n### 2.14 AI review generation and response\n\n- **Spiegel Research Center (with PowerReviews data):**\n  - A product with 5 reviews has 270% higher purchase likelihood than one with none. The effect is +190% for lower-priced and +380% for higher-priced items.\n  - The first five reviews matter most, and purchase likelihood peaks at 4.2-4.5 stars.\n  - Source: [Spiegel](https://spiegel.medill.northwestern.edu/from-reviews-to-revenue/). Grade B (correlational).\n- **Responding to reviews:**\n  - Hotels that started responding on TripAdvisor got 12% more reviews and +0.12 stars (HBR, via [Buffer](https://buffer.com/resources/replying-to-google-reviews/))\n  - Diminishing returns past about a 40% response rate (Cornell, via [Hotel Management](https://www.hotelmanagement.net/operate/go-ahead-respond-to-online-reviews-but-not-all-them))\n  - SMBs replying to more than 20% of reviews earned 33% more revenue (Womply, vendor, 200k businesses, [WP Business Reviews](https://wpbusinessreviews.com/review-management-increased-revenue-200000-small-businesses/))\n  - Grade B/C.\n- **Local search:** reviews remain a top local ranking and conversion factor ([Whitespark 2023 study summary](https://www.j2nj.com/?p=775)). Grade B.\n- **Tooling:** Judge.me includes AI reply drafts, summaries and translation in its $15/month plan, with merchant approval per reply ([Judge.me](https://judge.me/app/ai-review-features)).\n- **J&W today:** zero on-site reviews.\n\n### 2.15 AI loyalty, retention and subscriptions\n\n- **Recharge Retain (AI cancellation insights and offers):**\n  - Over 700,000 subscriptions saved; up to 33% of cancellations saved\n  - **Open Farm** (Toronto pet-food brand): 29% churn reduction\n  - **Wild Grain** (frozen bakery subscription): 22% of cancellations saved\n  - Source: [Recharge](https://getrecharge.com/products/retain/cancellation-prevention/). Grade B.\n- **Smile.io:** loyalty members show a 56% higher repeat purchase rate and 16.5% higher AOV with rewards coupons. This is non-randomized, so it overstates causality ([eCommerce Fastlane](https://ecommercefastlane.com/the-state-of-ecommerce-customer-loyalty-in-2023/)). Grade C.\n- **ButcherBox:** segmented members by taste and buying habits and offered alternatives to at-risk box types ([Microsoft](https://news.microsoft.com/transform/from-farm-to-cloud-to-table-butcherbox-serves-up-a-new-approach-to-meat-delivery/)). A support vendor estimated each 1% of churn reduction protects $5M for ButcherBox ([CaseStudies.com / Hugo](https://www.casestudies.com/company/hugo-118434/case-study/butcherbox-protects-20m-30m-annually-with-hugo)). Grade B/C.\n\n### 2.16 Canadian adoption context\n\n- 12.2% of Canadian businesses used AI to produce goods or deliver services in Q2 2025, up from 6.1% a year earlier ([Statistics Canada](https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/pub/11-621-m/11-621-m2025008-eng.pdf); [BetaKit](https://betakit.com/statscan-finds-ai-adoption-has-doubled-in-businesses-but-hasnt-yet-affected-headcounts/)). Grade A.\n- **Implication:** an Ontario-inspected butcher and meal-prep business that runs AI well is still an early adopter.\n- **Not verified:** government AI-adoption funding programs (e.g. NRC IRAP) available to J&W. Worth a follow-up.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Using the Claude API for J&W's custom agents\n\n**Pricing** (USD per million tokens, from [Claude pricing docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing), Oct 2026):\n\n| Model (API id) | Input | Output | Cache hit | Batch (50% off) | Best J&W use |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Claude Opus 5.5 (`claude-opus-5-5`) | $4 | $20 | $0.20 | $2 / $10 | Weekly planning and reasoning: production forecast review, carcass balancing, ads and business review, catalog rewrite (batch) |\n| Claude Sonnet 5.5 (`claude-sonnet-5-5`) | $2 | $10 | $0.10 | $1 / $5 | Customer-facing chat (concierge, Macro Butler, SMS reorder), outreach drafting |\n| Claude Haiku 5.5 (`claude-haiku-5-5`) | $0.10 | $0.50 | $0.01 | $0.05 / $0.25 | High-volume classification: ticket triage, review sentiment, tagging, intent routing on voice/SMS |\n\nOther line items: web search $10 per 1,000 searches. Managed Agents runtime is $0.08 per session-hour plus tokens, which suits scheduled weekly agents. Prompt caching cuts repeated catalog and policy context to 5% of input price on Opus/Sonnet 5.5.\n\n**Worked cost estimates** (all labelled estimates; token counts assumed):\n- **Catalog rewrite, one-off, 979 SKUs**, Opus 5.5 batch, about 3,000 input + 800 output tokens each: 979 × (3,000×$2 + 800×$10)/1M ≈ **$14**.\n- **Concierge chat, 3,000 conversations/month**, 6 turns each, Sonnet 5.5: per turn ~7k cached tokens ($0.0007) + 1k new ($0.002) + 300 output ($0.003) ≈ $0.0057, so about $0.034 per conversation, **≈ $100/month**. Gorgias AI Agent for the same volume would be about $0.90-1.00 per automated interaction, or roughly $1,350-1,500/month at 1,500 automated ([Gorgias pricing](https://www.gorgias.com/pricing)). Custom build cost is extra, but run cost is about 10x lower.\n- **Macro Butler, 1,000 plan sessions/month**, 4 turns, ~10k context (9k cached), 800 output, Sonnet 5.5: ≈ **$45-50/month**.\n- **Weekly production and carcass planning run**, Opus 5.5: ~200k input + 20k output ≈ $1.20/run, **≈ $5/month**.\n\n**Architecture pattern:**\n- Shopify Admin API, Klaviyo API, the routing tool's API and a Google Sheet or Postgres \"ops data\" store serve as tools.\n- Agents propose and humans approve, through Shopify Flow tasks, Slack/email approval links, or Sidekick-style review.\n- Use strict tool schemas. Give Claude the catalog and policies via cached system context. Use structured outputs for anything written back to Shopify.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Fifteen candidate AI agents for J&W\n\nEach agent lists: **Job**, **Inputs**, **Outputs**, **Human checkpoint**, **Lever**, **Evidence**, **Build (buy/build, model)**, **Est. run cost** and **KPI**.\n\n### Agent 1: \"Macro Butler\" AI meal-plan and macro coach\n- **Job:** Turn a 60-second goals intake into a 5-7 day J&W meal plan with exact SKUs, portion sizes and daily calorie/protein/carb/fat totals, then a one-click cart or subscription.\n- **Inputs:**\n  - Goal (cut / maintain / gain / performance), sex, age, height, weight, training days\n  - Dietary rules (no pork, pescatarian, dairy-free, halal *if* certified product exists), budget, delivery zone and day\n  - J&W catalog with computed macros and tags (from Agent 2); live stock\n- **Outputs:** Plan by day and meal; cart permalink; \"subscribe weekly\" option; macro summary PDF/email; swaps (\"prefer salmon over chicken Tue\").\n- **Human checkpoint:**\n  - A registered dietitian or nutrition professional signs off the calculation rules and the macro database, not each plan.\n  - Any medical condition, pregnancy, eating-disorder signal or allergy is routed to a human with a \"consult your doctor\" message.\n  - The owner approves plan templates.\n- **Lever:** New-customer conversion, AOV, subscription uptake (revenue).\n- **Evidence:**\n  - Fresh Fitness Food and Trifecta use the same intake-to-macros model.\n  - HelloFresh invested $70M in AI personalization for retention.\n  - Octane AI quizzes: +61% AOV / 2.4x conversion for one brand.\n  - Amazon Rufus users are 60% more likely to buy.\n  - McKinsey: 5-15% revenue from personalization (all §2.1).\n- **Build:** Custom (Claude Sonnet 5.5 + Shopify Storefront API + quiz front end). Octane AI is an off-the-shelf quiz alternative, but it can't do the macro math.\n- **Est. run cost:** about $50/month in tokens.\n- **KPI:** Quiz-to-cart rate, plan-to-order conversion, plan-to-subscription %, AOV.\n\n### Agent 2: Nutrition data and label agent\n- **Job:** Compute and maintain verified macro and allergen data for all 265 Meal Butler SKUs, and the main raw SKUs, from recipes, cooked yields and Health Canada CNF values. Draft nutrition-facts-style panels and front-of-package \"high in\" flags. Write structured attributes (protein g, kcal, carbs, fat, allergens, diet tags) to Shopify metafields.\n- **Inputs:** Recipes and ingredient weights; raw-to-cooked yield tests; CNF CSV; supplier spec sheets; current product pages.\n- **Outputs:** Per-SKU nutrition table (per 4 oz / 6 oz variant); metafields; \"macros\" block on product pages; flags where a claim (e.g. \"salt and sugar free\") needs a compliance check; lab-test shortlist for the top 20 SKUs.\n- **Human checkpoint:**\n  - A nutrition professional or food-labelling consultant approves every published panel and claim.\n  - Lab analysis for best-sellers.\n  - CFIA/Health Canada labelling exemptions confirmed before printing labels.\n- **Lever:** Enabler for Agent 1, SEO and B2B (gyms and dietitians need macros). Conversion on product pages.\n- **Evidence:** Only ~6 of 265 Meal Butler SKUs show numeric macros today (§1). CNF is free and covers 5,993 foods / 173 nutrients. FOP labelling has been mandatory since Jan 1, 2026 (§2.1).\n- **Build:** Custom (Claude Opus 5.5 with code execution for calculations; batch). One-off build plus maintenance on recipe changes.\n- **Est. run cost:** under $25 one-off in tokens; negligible ongoing.\n- **KPI:** % of SKUs with verified macros (target 100% of Meal Butler); PDP conversion on SKUs with macros vs without.\n\n### Agent 3: \"Butler Concierge\" 24/7 support and sales chat\n- **Job:** Answer on site chat, email and Instagram DMs:\n  - Order status; \"which day does my area get delivery?\"; Sunday cut-off; $100 minimum\n  - Substitutions; storage and reheating; what's in a box; loyalty points\n- **Inputs:** Shopify orders and customers; route and zone table; policies; catalog plus macros; FAQ; past tickets.\n- **Outputs:** Resolved conversations; product recommendations with cart links; tagged escalations with a summary; weekly \"top questions\" report that feeds back into the FAQ and catalog.\n- **Human checkpoint:**\n  - Refunds and credits above a set dollar threshold, allergen and medical questions, and complaints about food quality or safety go to staff.\n  - Weekly sampled transcript review.\n- **Lever:** Support cost and staff time; conversion of browsing visitors.\n- **Evidence:**\n  - Intercom Fin 51-76%; Tidio Lyro 70% (Jiffy); Gorgias named stories 48-83% (26-56% per a competitor)\n  - Daily Harvest 60% containment\n  - Vico Food Box: 21.4% of earnings via chat (§2.2-2.3)\n- **Build:** Option A: Gorgias Basic/Pro plus AI Agent ($0.90-1.00/interaction). Option B: custom Claude Sonnet 5.5 widget, Haiku 5.5 for triage (about $100/month at 3,000 conversations).\n- **KPI:** Automated resolution rate (with CSAT and repeat-contact checks), first-response time, chat-assisted revenue.\n\n### Agent 4: Auto-reorder and subscription agent (SMS / WhatsApp)\n- **Job:** Turn weekly cart-rebuilders into subscribers. Before the Sunday cut-off, text each customer a pre-built cart based on their pattern and predicted next order (\"Same as last week, $164? Reply YES / SKIP / CHANGE\"). Handle edits in natural language. Run AI cancellation-save flows (pause, skip, swap, delivery-day change before discount).\n- **Inputs:** Order history; Klaviyo expected-next-order date and churn risk; subscription app (Shopify Subscriptions or Recharge); stock; route-day schedule; SMS consent records.\n- **Outputs:** Confirmed draft orders and subscriptions; payment links; skip/pause events; cancellation reasons dataset; weekly retention report.\n- **Human checkpoint:**\n  - The owner sets discount ceilings and save-offer rules.\n  - The customer must confirm every order (no silent charges).\n  - CASL consent verified before any send.\n  - Staff review exception queue (out-of-stock swaps, address changes).\n- **Lever:** Purchase frequency, retention, predictability of production (revenue + cost).\n- **Evidence:**\n  - JioMart: 68% repeat via WhatsApp ordering\n  - Heritage Foods: 2x conversion\n  - Recharge saves up to 33% of cancellations; Open Farm (Toronto) 29% churn reduction; Wild Grain 22%\n  - Klaviyo predicts next order date; ButcherBox treats churn as its key lever (§2.3, 2.10, 2.15)\n- **Build:** Buy the subscription app plus a custom Claude Sonnet 5.5 conversational layer over SMS (Twilio or Klaviyo SMS) and optional WhatsApp.\n- **Est. run cost:** tokens about $30-60/month; SMS about $0.15/message benchmark ([Omnisend](https://www.omnisend.com/blog/sms-marketing-benchmarks/)).\n- **KPI:** % of active customers on subscription or auto-reorder; orders per customer per month; save rate; SMS reply-to-order rate.\n\n### Agent 5: Lifecycle marketing agent (Klaviyo predictive + AI copy)\n- **Job:** Build and maintain flows and segments:\n  - Welcome by goal; abandoned cart; post-purchase reheating tips\n  - \"Running low\" timed to expected next order; churn-risk win-back; VIP; whole-animal and BBQ seasonal; weekly menu email\n- **Inputs:** Klaviyo predictive metrics; Shopify events; catalog/macros; promotions calendar; review snippets.\n- **Outputs:** Drafted flows, campaigns and subject-line tests; segment definitions; weekly performance and next-best-test report.\n- **Human checkpoint:** The owner approves every campaign before send and every new flow before activation; CASL consent and unsubscribe checks are automatic.\n- **Lever:** Repeat revenue, recovered carts (revenue).\n- **Evidence:** Flows earn about 41% of email revenue from 5.3% of sends (about 18x RPR); cart abandonment averages 70.22%; Force of Nature +52% YoY using AI segments (§2.10).\n- **Build:** Buy (Klaviyo, K:AI) plus a Claude Opus 5.5 copy and strategy layer in J&W's voice (\"Eat Clean - Train Mean - Live Lean\").\n- **Est. run cost:** under $10/month in tokens. Klaviyo plan cost not verified.\n- **KPI:** Flow revenue share, revenue per recipient, win-back rate of churn-risk segment.\n\n### Agent 6: Production forecast and prep-sheet agent\n- **Job:** Every Sunday night after cut-off (and mid-week for pickup and express), forecast unit demand per SKU and variant. Produce the cook, portion, vacuum-pack and flash-freeze plan, the raw-material purchase list by supplier, and frozen safety-stock targets.\n- **Inputs:** Closed route orders; historical pickup, express and walk-in sales; seasonality and holidays; promotions; current frozen inventory; yields (raw to cooked); supplier lead times; waste log.\n- **Outputs:** Monday production schedule by station; purchase orders (drafts); par levels; stockout and overproduction alerts; weekly forecast-vs-actual and waste report.\n- **Human checkpoint:** The production manager approves the plan and all purchase orders; the owner approves new par levels.\n- **Lever:** Food cost and waste, labour hours, stockouts (cost + revenue protection).\n- **Evidence:**\n  - Independent PCFWC pilot: 14.8% waste reduction per store\n  - Afresh: 18-25% shrink cuts\n  - PreciTaste at honeygrow: 4-5 h/day prep time saved\n  - Winnow at an Ontario cook-chill kitchen: 18% waste cut by value, $63k/yr\n  - Academic: optimize for waste, not accuracy alone (§2.4)\n- **Build:** Custom (Python forecast plus Claude Opus 5.5 for plan reasoning and exceptions; Managed Agents scheduled weekly). Optionally Winnow-style waste logging (tablet plus scale).\n- **Est. run cost:** about $5/month in tokens.\n- **KPI:** Forecast error by SKU; waste % of production; stockouts per week; prep labour hours per 100 portions.\n\n### Agent 7: Carcass and cut-yield optimizer\n- **Job:** Each week, decide which primals and whole animals to buy and how to break them, so raw-cut demand, cooked-line usage (trim to burgers and meatballs, flank to fajitas, chicken to cooked breast) and box promotions consume the whole animal at the best margin. Track cutter-level yield tests.\n- **Inputs:** Yield-test log (cutter, primal, in-weight, out-weight by cut, trim, waste); purchase prices; demand forecast (Agent 6); whole-animal orders (quarter cow, lamb); cooked recipes.\n- **Outputs:** Cut plan; buy list; \"overstocked cut\" alerts sent to Agent 8 and Agent 5 (bundle/box promo suggestions); yield variance by cutter and supplier; margin-per-carcass report.\n- **Human checkpoint:** The head butcher approves the cut plan; the owner approves supplier changes.\n- **Lever:** Gross margin through yield and full-carcass utilization; waste (cost).\n- **Evidence:** Völur cut planning (2-6 h down to 15-30 s at JBS and others); vendor claim of 1-3% more yield per carcass (unverified); ERP yield tracking (§2.5). No small-butcher case found.\n- **Build:** Custom (Google Sheet/Postgres log plus Claude Opus 5.5 with code execution for the optimization).\n- **Est. run cost:** about $5/month.\n- **KPI:** Yield % by primal vs baseline; trim sold-through %; margin per carcass.\n\n### Agent 8: Smart markdown and \"Butcher's Clearance\" agent\n- **Job:** Identify short-dated fresh items, slow movers and overstocked cuts. Set tiered markdowns within owner-set floors. Package them into bundles or \"deal of the week\". Push a weekly clearance drop to an opted-in SMS/email segment and the store counter.\n- **Inputs:** Inventory with pack dates; sell-through velocity; margin floors; Agent 7 alerts.\n- **Outputs:** Price changes (drafts); bundle SKUs; clearance campaign; recovered-revenue report.\n- **Human checkpoint:** The owner approves all price changes; hard floor rules (never below cost plus X%); food-safety date rules enforced.\n- **Lever:** Waste turned into revenue; margin recovery (cost + revenue).\n- **Evidence:** Wasteless: -32.7% waste, +6.2% revenue in 12 weeks; Italian retailer halved waste and gained 1.2 margin points (§2.6).\n- **Build:** Custom (Claude Sonnet 5.5 plus Shopify Admin API price drafts).\n- **Est. run cost:** under $10/month.\n- **KPI:** Waste $ per week; clearance revenue; margin on clearance.\n\n### Agent 9: Catalog, SEO and AI-shopping readiness agent\n- **Job:**\n  - Rewrite the ~504-508 thin descriptions and fill product types for the 413 SKUs missing one.\n  - Normalize vendors (Costco/COSTCO, J&W Meal Butler/MEAL BUTLER, etc.); add tags (protein, cut, diet, portion, cooking method, heat).\n  - Write alt text, SEO titles and meta; build collection landing pages (\"meal prep delivery Toronto\", \"Ontario lamb\", \"air-chilled chicken\").\n  - Add structured data; fix nav errors (fresh/frozen fruit swap, \"mediteranian\" URL redirect).\n  - Keep the catalog clean as new SKUs are added daily.\n- **Inputs:** Shopify catalog; Agent 2 metafields; brand voice guide; Search Console; competitor keyword set.\n- **Outputs:** Bulk product updates (drafts); redirect list; collection pages; weekly SEO and AI-channel report.\n- **Human checkpoint:** Staff spot-check a random 10% sample. Every health, nutrition, origin or \"no hormones\" claim goes to an approval queue. The owner approves nav changes.\n- **Lever:** Organic traffic; on-site search; conversion; discoverability in ChatGPT, Copilot, Perplexity and Google AI channels (revenue).\n- **Evidence:**\n  - Shopify Magic: 100 descriptions in about 20 min (vs 20 h)\n  - SEO lifts of +5.4% to +112% (vendor and agency)\n  - Shopify AI-channel orders up 11x/15x and tripling YoY (§2.7)\n  - Spiegel: product information and reviews drive purchase likelihood (§2.14)\n- **Build:** Shopify Magic for ad-hoc edits plus a custom Claude Opus 5.5 batch pipeline for the bulk rewrite and ongoing QA.\n- **Est. run cost:** about $14 one-off; under $5/month ongoing.\n- **KPI:** % of SKUs with complete copy, type and tags (target 100%); organic sessions and revenue (holdout vs control set); AI-referred sessions.\n\n### Agent 10: Visual content studio agent\n- **Job:** Fill the 59 missing images. Create consistent lifestyle and background variants from real product photos. Produce ad sizes, menu cards and short-form video stills for each week's menu.\n- **Inputs:** Real product photos taken in-house (phone light box); brand guide; menu calendar.\n- **Outputs:** Approved images for product pages, Meta/IG and email; weekly content pack.\n- **Human checkpoint:** The owner approves every image. Hard rule: the food itself is never AI-generated or altered; only background and scene are.\n- **Lever:** Conversion; ad performance; content cost (revenue + cost).\n- **Evidence:** AI product-photo tools run about $0.08-0.30/image vs about $500/image for a professional food still-life example; best practice is a real product shot plus AI scene (§2.8). Meta reports 30% more advertisers using AI creative (§2.9).\n- **Build:** Buy (Shopify Magic image tools, Pebblely or similar) plus a Claude Sonnet 5.5 brief-writer and QA checklist.\n- **Est. run cost:** about $20-40/month in tools.\n- **KPI:** % of SKUs with imagery; PDP conversion; ad CTR and CPA by creative.\n\n### Agent 11: Paid ads optimizer agent (Meta / Google)\n- **Job:** Run Advantage+ catalog retargeting and zone-targeted manual prospecting (by route day and postal code). Launch and rotate creative from Agent 10. Read results weekly with geo holdouts by delivery zone. Propose budget shifts.\n- **Inputs:** Ad accounts; Shopify orders by postal code; route map; margin per SKU; creative library.\n- **Outputs:** Weekly test plan; creative briefs; budget-change proposals; incrementality readout.\n- **Human checkpoint:** The owner approves any budget change above a set % and all new creative; a monthly spend cap is enforced.\n- **Lever:** New-customer acquisition cost; new-zone growth (revenue).\n- **Evidence:** Meta: +22% ROAS for Advantage+ (company-reported). Haus (640 tests): Advantage+ wins only 42% of the time and ends about 12% worse on incremental ROAS, so balance and test (§2.9).\n- **Build:** Buy (Meta Advantage+, Google PMax) plus a custom Claude Opus 5.5 weekly analyst.\n- **Est. run cost:** under $10/month in tokens (ad spend separate).\n- **KPI:** New customers per week; CAC; incremental ROAS by zone.\n\n### Agent 12: Route and delivery experience agent\n- **Job:** Optimize the six weekly routes and the express runs. Send ETA texts and live tracking. Run \"your driver is in your area Wednesday; add items by Sunday\" upsells. Flag route density to decide new zones, pickup partners or lockers (e.g. partner gyms).\n- **Inputs:** Orders by address and route day; driver availability; vehicle capacity and cold-chain limits; routing tool (Routific/Onfleet/Circuit).\n- **Outputs:** Optimized routes; driver manifests; customer ETA and proof-of-delivery texts; zone-density map; new-zone recommendations.\n- **Human checkpoint:** The dispatcher approves routes; the owner approves zone or pricing changes.\n- **Lever:** Delivery cost per drop; driver hours; capacity; CX and retention (cost + revenue).\n- **Evidence:** Inspired Go (Canada): 8-10 h/day of planning down to under 1 h, with 72,000+ deliveries/month. Thistle: 10-20% more deliveries per day. The Foodery: -28% delivery cost (vendor figure; source page retired; §2.11).\n- **Build:** Buy (Routific from free to $150/month for 101-1,000 orders, or Onfleet) plus a Claude Sonnet 5.5 messaging and analysis layer.\n- **Est. run cost:** $0-150/month for the tool plus under $10 in tokens.\n- **KPI:** Planning hours per week; cost per drop; on-time %; deliveries per driver-hour.\n\n### Agent 13: B2B prospecting and account agent (gyms, trainers, corporate, teams, schools)\n- **Job:** Build and work target lists along the existing route corridors:\n  - GTA gyms and boutique studios, personal trainers, physio and dietitian clinics\n  - Corporate offices (wellness lunches), sports teams and academies, daycares, restaurants and caterers (wholesale heritage)\n- **What it does with them:** Personalized outreach, tasting-box offers, gym-fridge consignment proposals, wholesale price sheets from the ~45 case/bulk SKUs, follow-ups and reorder reminders for won accounts.\n- **Inputs:** Clay/Apollo enrichment; Google Maps listings; route zones; B2B price list; case studies and testimonials; CRM.\n- **Outputs:** Qualified lead lists; drafted sequences; booked tastings; proposals; account health reports (reorder cadence, churn risk).\n- **Human checkpoint:** Erwin or the owner approves each sequence and offer. Pricing and terms above set limits are approved by the owner. CASL compliance check on every commercial message.\n- **Lever:** New B2B revenue stream; larger, steadier weekly volume (revenue).\n- **Evidence:** Clay: Qrew 2x replies and +40% meetings, Harmonic 3x reply rates. Apollo SMB test: 4.2% reply rate, 12-18 meetings/month. 9,493 fitness centres in Canada (GTA estimated at about 1,300-1,600) (§2.12).\n- **Build:** Buy (Clay Launch $185/month or Apollo Basic about $49-59/user/month) plus Claude Opus 5.5 for research and personalization.\n- **Est. run cost:** about $200-250/month all-in.\n- **KPI:** Meetings booked; accounts won; B2B weekly revenue; account retention.\n\n### Agent 14: Phone and after-hours voice order agent\n- **Job:** Answer (416) 298-1350 after hours and on overflow, especially Sunday, the cut-off day, when the store is closed. Answer menu, zone, delivery-day, macro and order-status questions. Take orders by texting a pre-filled cart or payment link. Book pickups and whole-animal deposits. Log every call.\n- **Inputs:** Catalog plus macros; zones and route days; order lookup; policies; SMS sending.\n- **Outputs:** Answered calls; cart links sent; call summaries; missed-demand report (what people asked for that J&W doesn't sell).\n- **Human checkpoint:** Escalation and voicemail to staff for complaints, custom cut orders, large B2B orders and allergen questions. The customer confirms the order in the cart (the agent never charges cards by voice).\n- **Lever:** Captured revenue from missed calls; staff time (revenue + cost).\n- **Evidence:** Restaurants miss about 150 calls/month at $35-85 each, and voice AI reports up to 22% higher phone revenue (vendor). Modelled 87% cut in missed peak calls (vendor). Cost about $0.07-0.31/min (§2.13).\n- **Build:** Buy a voice platform (Retell/Vapi) with Claude Sonnet 5.5 as the reasoning model, or Haiku 5.5 for low-latency routing.\n- **Est. run cost:** about $40-190/month at 600 minutes.\n- **KPI:** Calls answered after hours; cart links sent to orders; revenue per call.\n\n### Agent 15: Review and reputation agent\n- **Job:**\n  - Ask for product reviews (Judge.me) and Google reviews after second delivery and once the customer has eaten (timed around 5-7 days).\n  - Draft replies to every review; summarize themes; route quality complaints (\"chicken dry\", \"portion small\", \"late delivery\") to the kitchen or dispatcher within 24 hours.\n  - Surface UGC and testimonials for Agents 5, 10 and 13.\n- **Inputs:** Delivered orders; review platforms; Google Business Profile; social mentions.\n- **Outputs:** Review requests; reply drafts; weekly sentiment and issue report; testimonial library.\n- **Human checkpoint:** All replies to 1-3 star reviews are human-approved. The agent never offers compensation without approval, and never solicits or writes fake or incentivized-without-disclosure reviews.\n- **Lever:** Conversion (social proof), local SEO, quality feedback loop (revenue + cost).\n- **Evidence:** 5 reviews vs 0 = 270% purchase likelihood, +380% for higher-priced items (Spiegel). Responding gives 12% more reviews and +0.12 stars (HBR via Buffer). Judge.me AI replies are included at $15/month (§2.14). J&W currently has zero reviews.\n- **Build:** Buy (Judge.me) plus Claude Haiku 5.5 for sentiment and Sonnet 5.5 for reply drafts.\n- **Est. run cost:** about $20/month.\n- **KPI:** Reviews per month; average rating; % of reviews answered (target 100% of negative, 40%+ overall); Google rating and review count.\n\n**Orchestration layer (optional, not counted): Owner's weekly business review.** A Monday digest built from Shopify (Sidekick plus a Claude Opus 5.5 scheduled agent):\n- Sales by channel and zone, margin, waste, subscription churn\n- Top support issues, review themes, B2B pipeline\n- Three recommended actions to approve\n\n---\n\n## 5. Which levers add up to an extra $1M/year (illustrative sizing)\n\n**J&W revenue, order count, traffic and AOV were not found.** Every number below is an **illustrative estimate** with its assumptions stated, meant to be replaced with J&W's Shopify data. The assumed AOV of $140 rests on the $100 delivery minimum plus multi-item weekly carts.\n\n| Agent | Formula (assumptions) | Illustrative annual revenue |\n|---|---|---|\n| 4 Auto-reorder/subscription | 150 customers moved from ~1.2 to ~2.2 orders/month × 12 × $140 | ≈ $252k |\n| 13 B2B accounts | 15 accounts (gyms, PT studios, corporate, teams) × $350/week × 48 weeks | ≈ $252k |\n| 1 Macro Butler (+2 data) | 600 completed plans/month × 8% convert × $140 ≈ $81k first orders; plus 25% of converters repeat 1.5×/month for ~4 months ≈ $121k | ≈ $150-200k |\n| 5 Lifecycle + cart recovery | Flows reaching ~25-35% of email revenue (Eightx SMB guidance; see [Eightx](https://eightx.co/blog/average-klaviyo-flow-revenue-contribution-benchmarks)); depends on list size (not found) | ≈ $60-100k |\n| 14 Voice/after-hours | 40 captured orders/month × $140 × 12 | ≈ $67k |\n| 9, 10, 11, 15 Conversion stack | +10-15% conversion on existing online revenue (base not found) | Scales with base |\n| 6, 7, 8, 12 Margin and ops | Waste, yield and delivery savings (e.g. 18% waste cut at an Ontario cook-chill kitchen = $63k/yr at Compass's scale) | Cost-side, not revenue |\n| **Total of revenue rows shown** | | **≈ $780k-870k**, plus the conversion stack, which plausibly reaches $1M+ |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Suggested sequencing (by dependency)\n\n1. **Weeks 0-4, foundation:**\n   - Agent 9 (catalog clean-up, types, tags, vendor normalization)\n   - Agent 2 (macros for the top 50 Meal Butler SKUs first)\n   - Agent 15 (reviews start accruing immediately)\n   - Agent 3 (concierge on FAQs)\n2. **Weeks 4-8, revenue engines:**\n   - Agent 4 (subscriptions plus SMS reorder before Sunday cut-off)\n   - Agent 5 (Klaviyo predictive flows)\n   - Agent 1 (Macro Butler on the top 50 SKUs, expanding as Agent 2 completes)\n3. **Weeks 8-12, growth and ops:**\n   - Agent 13 (B2B pilot: 50 gyms on GTA 2 and GTA 4 routes)\n   - Agent 12 (routing)\n   - Agent 6 (production forecast)\n   - Agent 14 (after-hours voice)\n4. **Quarter 2:**\n   - Agent 7 (yield log plus carcass planning)\n   - Agent 8 (markdowns)\n   - Agent 10 (content studio)\n   - Agent 11 (incrementality-tested ads)\n\n---\n\n## 7. Guardrails and risks\n\n- **Nutrition and health claims:** A human professional approves all macro data, panels and claims (\"salt and sugar free\", \"no hormones\"). No medical advice; medical, pregnancy and eating-disorder cases are escalated. Confirm front-of-package and labelling obligations and exemptions with Health Canada/CFIA.\n- **Allergens:** Allergen answers never come from an LLM's general knowledge. They come only from the approved per-SKU allergen field, or the question goes to a human.\n- **CASL:** Express, SMS-specific consent with proof; sender ID and STOP in every message ([Telair](https://www.telair.net/resources/knowledge-base/guides/casl-sms-compliance/)).\n- **Pricing and money:** Agents draft; humans approve price changes, refunds above a threshold, and discount ceilings. Voice and SMS agents send payment links rather than taking card numbers.\n- **Imagery honesty:** Never alter the food in images. Never write or incentivize undisclosed reviews.\n- **Measurement:** Use holdouts (SEO control set, geo holdouts for ads, matched cohorts for reorder nudges). Vendor case-study numbers are directional, not guarantees.\n- **Privacy:** Keep customer data in Shopify, Klaviyo and J&W-controlled stores. Send agents only the fields they need.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Research gaps (not found or not verified)\n\n- J&W revenue, order volume, traffic, AOV, list sizes, ticket volume, call volume (needed to replace estimates).\n- GTA gym and studio count; WhatsApp usage share among GTA consumers.\n- Small-butcher AI yield case study; Afresh-style results for a single-site meal-prep producer.\n- Shopify Agentic Storefronts eligibility for Canadian merchants.\n- Canadian AI-adoption funding programs (e.g. NRC IRAP). Not researched in this pass.\n- Klaviyo, Recharge and Shopify Subscriptions pricing for J&W's tier.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Sources (grouped)\n\n**Customer service:**\n[Gorgias pricing](https://www.gorgias.com/pricing) ·\n[Gorgias customers](https://www.gorgias.com/customers) ·\n[Ringly on Gorgias pricing](https://www.ringly.io/blog/gorgias-ai-agent-pricing-per-resolution) ·\n[Ringly G2 analysis](https://www.ringly.io/blog/gorgias-g2-rating-2025) ·\n[AI Agent Index: Fin](https://theaiagentindex.com/agents/intercom-fin) ·\n[CreateWith: Fin 3](https://www.createwith.com/tool/intercom/updates/intercom-ships-200-updates-in-2025-fin-3-reaches-67-average-resolution-rate) ·\n[AWS: Intercom](https://aws.amazon.com/solutions/case-studies/intercom-anthropic/) ·\n[Anthropic: Intercom](https://www.anthropic.com/customers/intercom) ·\n[Gleap: Fin pricing](https://www.gleap.io/blog/intercom-fin-ai-pricing-2026) ·\n[Tidio: Jiffy](https://www.tidio.com/customers/jiffy/) ·\n[Tidio pricing](https://www.tidio.com/pricing/) ·\n[mer.vin Tidio guide](https://mer.vin/2026/07/tidio-platform-guide-lyro-ai-agent-help-desk-and-flows-pricing/) ·\n[Quiq: Daily Harvest](https://Quiq.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/Daily-Harvest-Customer-Story.pdf) ·\n[Kaizo: containment](https://kaizo.com/blog/what-is-containment-rate/) ·\n[Shopify Help: Sidekick](https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/shopify-admin/productivity-tools/sidekick/features) ·\n[Shopifreaks: Winter '26](https://www.shopifreaks.com/shopify-unveils-winter-26-edition-with-major-upgrades-to-sidekick-and-new-ai-commerce-tools/) ·\n[Shopify Community: Winter '26](https://community.shopify.com/t/winter-editions-2026-is-now-out-highlights-for-merchants/579120)\n\n**Nutrition and personalization:**\n[Trifecta App Store](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/trifecta/id492100601) ·\n[Food Business News: Trifecta](https://www.foodbusinessnews.net/articles/16933-trifecta-bolsters-personalized-nutrition-services) ·\n[BGov: HelloFresh $70M](https://news.bgov.com/artificial-intelligence/hellofresh-is-investing-70-million-to-have-ai-help-plan-dinner) ·\n[Business Wire: HelloFresh](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250804201495/en) ·\n[Food Manufacture: HelloFresh AI](https://www.foodmanufacture.co.uk/Article/2025/11/24/hellofresh-uses-ai-to-accelerate-delivery-of-chef-created-recipes) ·\n[CB Insights: Fresh Fitness Food](https://www.cbinsights.com/company/fresh-fitness-food/financials) ·\n[Women's Fitness](https://womensfitness.co.uk/?p=7585) ·\n[Trustpilot: FFF](https://de.trustpilot.com/review/freshfitnessfood.com?page=9) ·\n[Noom Welli](https://www.fintechfutures.com/press-releases/noom-introduces-ai-enabled-products-to-enhance-on-demand-health-care-and-interactive-coaching) ·\n[TechTarget: Noom](https://techtarget.com/searchenterpriseai/news/366598870/Noom-CEO-on-the-need-for-AI-in-wellness-and-health) ·\n[Fitt: Cal AI](https://insider.fitt.co/myfitnesspal-acquires-rival-food-tracker-cal-ai/) ·\n[Let's Data Science: Cal AI](https://letsdatascience.com/news/cal-ai-sells-to-myfitnesspal-after-rapid-growth-f789c949) ·\nCookUnity careers (page no longer available) ·\n[Octane AI: Wicked Edge](https://octaneai.com/case-studies/wicked-edge) ·\n[Octane AI platform](https://octaneai.com/) ·\n[McKinsey: personalization](https://www.mckinsey.com/~/media/McKinsey/Business%20Functions/Marketing%20and%20Sales/Our%20Insights/The%20future%20of%20personalization%20and%20how%20to%20get%20ready%20for%20it/The-future-of-personalization-and-how-to-get-ready-for-it.pdf) ·\n[Fortune: Rufus](https://www.fortune.com/2025/11/02/amazon-rufus-ai-shopping-assistant-chatbot-10-billion-sales-monetization) ·\n[Health Canada: nutrient data](https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/nutrient-data.html) ·\n[CNF downloads](https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/nutrient-data/canadian-nutrient-file-2015-download-files.html) ·\n[Packaging Insights: FOP](https://www.packaginginsights.com/news/canada-mandates-nutrition-symbol-2026.html) ·\n[Retail Council: FOP](https://www.retailcouncil.org/topics/food-grocery/food-product-labelling/front-of-package-nutrition-labelling/)\n\n**Messaging:**\n[Voicebot: JioMart](https://voicebot.ai/2022/12/29/whatsapp-chatbot-for-buying-groceries-spikes-repeat-purchase-rate-for-indian-grocery-chain-jiomart/) ·\n[WATI: Heritage Foods](https://www.casestudies.com/company/wati/case-study/heritage-foods-doubles-order-conversion-with-wati) ·\n[Razorpay](https://razorpay.com/blog/?p=18260) ·\n[Indigitall](https://indigitall.com/en/success-story/revolutionizing-e-commerce-order/) ·\n[Smartsupp: Vico](https://smartsupp.com/case-studies/vico-food-box-obtained-98-percent-customer-satisfaction-rating-and-increased-their-average-order-value-with-Smartsupp/) ·\n[Meta WhatsApp pricing](https://developers.facebook.com/docs/whatsapp/pricing) ·\n[WhatsApp rate card May 2026](https://www.salesforce.com/en-us/wp-content/uploads/sites/4/WhatsApp-Business-Messaging-Rate-Card-MM-Lite-May-1-2026.pdf) ·\n[Omnisend SMS benchmarks](https://www.omnisend.com/blog/sms-marketing-benchmarks/) ·\n[Telair: CASL](https://www.telair.net/resources/knowledge-base/guides/casl-sms-compliance/) ·\n[BulkSMS: Canada](https://www.bulksms.com/resources/regulations/canadian-requirements-for-sending-commercial-sms-messages.htm)\n\n**Forecasting, waste, yield, pricing:**\n[Afresh](https://www.afresh.com) ·\n[AndNowUKnow](https://andnowuknow.com/node/83880) ·\n[Supermarket News](https://www.supermarketnews.com/grocery-technology/how-afresh-is-revolutionizing-grocery-perimeters-with-ai) ·\n[Shelby Report](https://theshelbyreport.com/2026/04/21/afresh-raises-34m-to-scale-ai-across-grocery-industry/) ·\n[PCFWC pilot](https://pacificcoastcollaborative.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/PCFWC-Case-Study_AI_Final.pdf) ·\n[Crisp acquires Shelf Engine](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250327362637/en/Crisp-Acquires-Shelf-Engine-AI-Demand-Planning-Solution-to-Support-Optimal-Retail-Forecasting-and-Inventory-Management) ·\n[Kimberton](https://perishablenews.com/retailfoodservice/kimberton-whole-foods-increases-sales-by-42-with-shelf-engine-ai/) ·\n[Crisp award](https://gocrisp.com/blog/crisp-wins-demand-forecasting-innovation-award-for-ai-that-translates-to-real-world-supply-chains) ·\n[Fast Casual: honeygrow](https://www.fastcasual.com/blogs/from-prep-to-profit-how-honeygrow-is-tackling-rising-costs-with-ai-automation/) ·\n[Winnow: Compass Milton](https://www.casestudies.com/company/winnow/case-study/compass-group-saves-63000-a-year-with-winnow) ·\n[Winnow: Salem Health](https://www.casestudies.com/company/winnow/case-study/salem-health-cuts-food-waste-33-with-winnow) ·\n[Oeconomica study](https://www.oeconomica.org.ro/abstract/697/When-Accurate-Forecasts-Still-Produce-Waste-Demand-Shocks-and-Over-Forecasting.html) ·\n[Second Harvest](https://wfprod.secondharvest.ca/post/new-report-from-second-harvest-reveals-canadas-58-billion-food-waste-problem) ·\n[CTV](https://www.ctvnews.ca/toronto/article/almost-half-of-all-food-wasted-in-canada-is-avoidable-new-report-suggests/) ·\n[Völur](https://www.volur.ai/) ·\n[Folio3 whitepaper](https://foodtech.folio3.com/whitepapers/ai-automation-for-the-meat-industry/) ·\n[inecta](https://www.inecta.com/meat-processing-software) ·\n[Wasteless: Spain](https://wasteless.com/success/leading-spanish-retailer-cuts-waste-by-32) ·\n[Wasteless resources](https://Wasteless.com/resources) ·\n[FAO: Wasteless](https://agritechobservatory.review.fao.org/zh/wasteless)\n\n**Catalog, SEO, creative, ads:**\n[Shopify blog: AI descriptions](https://www.shopify.com/blog/ai-product-descriptions) ·\n[tenten: Shopify Magic](https://tenten.co/shopify/shopify-magic-ai-features-ranked/) ·\n[WordLift](https://wordlift.io/free-ai-product-description-generator/) ·\n[VortexIQ](https://www.vortexiq.ai/?p=23886) ·\nEXPRE (page no longer available) ·\n[PYMNTS 11x](https://www.pymnts.com/news/ecommerce/2025/shopify-president-agentic-ai-drives-11x-spike-in-orders) ·\n[DC360 Q4 2025](https://www.digitalcommerce360.com/2026/02/17/shopify-revenue-gmv-q4-2025/) ·\n[PYMNTS Q2 2026](https://www.pymnts.com/earnings/2026/shopifys-ai-traffic-triples-as-shoppers-skip-the-search-bar/) ·\n[BetaKit: Agentic Storefronts](https://betakit.com/?p=397830) ·\n[Kive vs Pebblely](https://kive.ai/learn/kive-vs-pebblely) ·\n[Capterra: Pebblely](https://www.capterra.com/p/10016326/Pebblely/) ·\n[Wonderful Machine](https://wonderfulmachine.com/article/pricing-negotiating-combining-food-still-life-projects/) ·\ntenten: AI photography (page no longer available) ·\n[eMarketer: Meta Cannes](https://www.emarketer.com/content/meta-launches-11-new-ai-ad-tools-cannes-lions-2025) ·\n[PPC Land: Meta Q1](https://ppc.land/metas-ad-business-soars-as-ai-tools-drive-16-revenue-growth-in-q1/) ·\n[Haus: Meta report](https://www.haus.io/meta) ·\n[Haus blog](https://www.haus.io/blog/the-meta-report-lessons-from-640-haus-incrementality-experiments)\n\n**Lifecycle, loyalty, reviews:**\n[Klaviyo predictive analytics](https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360020919731) ·\n[Klaviyo benchmarks](https://klaviyo.com/products/email-marketing/benchmarks) ·\n[Klaviyo K:AI agent](https://marketing-origin-netlify.klaviyo.com/solutions/ai/marketing-agent) ·\n[Klaviyo segments AI](https://www.klaviyo.com/product/whats-new/segments-ai) ·\n[Klaviyo: AI marketing examples](https://marketing-origin-netlify.klaviyo.com/blog/companies-using-ai-marketing) ·\n[Eightx: flow share](https://eightx.co/blog/average-klaviyo-flow-revenue-contribution-benchmarks) ·\n[Baymard](https://baymard.com/lists/cart-abandonment-rate) ·\n[Recharge Retain](https://getrecharge.com/products/retain/cancellation-prevention/) ·\n[Smile.io data](https://ecommercefastlane.com/the-state-of-ecommerce-customer-loyalty-in-2023/) ·\n[Microsoft: ButcherBox](https://news.microsoft.com/transform/from-farm-to-cloud-to-table-butcherbox-serves-up-a-new-approach-to-meat-delivery/) ·\n[Hugo: ButcherBox](https://www.casestudies.com/company/hugo-118434/case-study/butcherbox-protects-20m-30m-annually-with-hugo) ·\n[Spiegel](https://spiegel.medill.northwestern.edu/from-reviews-to-revenue/) ·\n[Buffer: HBR](https://buffer.com/resources/replying-to-google-reviews/) ·\n[Hotel Management: Cornell](https://www.hotelmanagement.net/operate/go-ahead-respond-to-online-reviews-but-not-all-them) ·\n[Womply](https://wpbusinessreviews.com/review-management-increased-revenue-200000-small-businesses/) ·\n[Judge.me AI](https://judge.me/app/ai-review-features) ·\n[Whitespark summary](https://www.j2nj.com/?p=775)\n\n**Delivery, B2B, voice:**\n[Onfleet: Inspired Go](https://onfleet.com/blog/case-studies-inspired-go/) ·\n[Onfleet: Thistle](https://onfleet.com/blog/thistle-case-study/) ·\n[Onfleet: Asian Veggies](https://onfleet.com/blog/case-study-asian-veggies/) ·\n[Onfleet: Fresh Prep](https://onfleet.com/blog/case-study-fresh-prep/) ·\nIoT ONE: Foodery (page no longer available) ·\n[FeaturedCustomers: Routific](https://www.featuredcustomers.com/vendor/routific/case-studies) ·\n[Capterra: Routific](https://www.capterra.ie/software/142530/routific) ·\n[Upper: Routific pricing](https://www.upperinc.com/blog/routific-pricing/) ·\n[Circuit pricing](https://getcircuit.com/teams/pricing) ·\n[Clay: Qrew](https://clay.com/blog/qrew-case-study) ·\n[Clay for Sales](https://www.clay.com/clay-for-sales) ·\n[Valley: Clay pricing](https://www.joinvalley.co/blog/clay-pricing-and-credits-explained-2026) ·\n[Apollo SMB](https://www.apollo.io/solutions/small-businesses) ·\nAI Agent Rank: Apollo (page no longer available) ·\n[Capterra: Apollo](https://www.capterra.com/p/158696/Apollo/pricing/) ·\n[StatCan: gyms](https://statcan.gc.ca/o1/en/plus/5329-working-out-numbers-your-gym-resolution) ·\n[Loman](https://loman.ai/blog/restaurant-ai-phone-receptionist-revenue) ·\n[Hostie](https://hostie.ai/resources/500k-call-study-ai-hosts-slash-missed-dinner-rush-calls-87-percent-revenue-impact) ·\n[Ainora: voice pricing](https://ainora.lt/blog/ai-voice-agent-cost-per-minute-2026) ·\n[Fora Soft](https://www.forasoft.com/blog/article/vapi-vs-retell-vs-custom) ·\n[Macha: Retell vs Vapi](https://www.getmacha.com/blog/retell-ai-vs-vapi)\n\n**Canada and Claude:**\n[StatCan: AI adoption](https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/pub/11-621-m/11-621-m2025008-eng.pdf) ·\n[BetaKit: StatCan AI](https://betakit.com/statscan-finds-ai-adoption-has-doubled-in-businesses-but-hasnt-yet-affected-headcounts/) ·\n[Claude API pricing](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing)"}, {"key": "assumptions", "file": "model-assumptions.md", "title": "The shared modelling assumptions", "blurb": "The figures every estimate in this proposal uses, in one place.", "md": "# Shared modelling assumptions (used in every play and $1M idea so the numbers are consistent)\n\nAll CAD. Year 1 = Nov 2026 – Oct 2027 (includes ramp). \"Run-rate\" = a full year once running (year 2). Sources: market-benchmarks.md, jw-audit.md, competitors-*.md.\n\n## J&W today (estimates, low confidence until JW shares data)\n- Revenue, all channels: **low $1.2M · mid $2.5M · high $4.0M** (jw-audit.md §10; D&B modelled $1.07M; ZoomInfo <$5M). ~15–25 staff. Store + online + some wholesale.\n- Google **4.8★ / 611 reviews**, Facebook 4.6★ / 56. Instagram **30K** followers (3,736 posts); Facebook 5,021 (dormant since Jan 2024); TikTok 132.\n- Shopify (Refresh theme), Smile.io loyalty, Shopify Inbox. **No** subscription app, reviews app, email-marketing app, GA4. Near-zero non-brand SEO.\n- Team-led brand: customers name \"Bill\" (co-founder William Fehr) and his team. President per D&B: Stavros Trougakos.\n- Ontario provincially inspected plant (since 2008): **meat products may only be sold within Ontario** unless federally licensed (SFCR/CFIA). Holds a 2026 federal chicken import quota as a processor.\n- Price position: cooked chicken breast ≈ $1.05–1.25/oz vs GTA premium meal prep $1.86/oz (Hi Chef); pre-set meals $8.99–$18.39 (median $12.99) vs premium GTA $14–$30/meal.\n\n## Gross margins by revenue type (after food, packaging, card/platform fees; before overheads)\n- Meal Butler cooked & portioned (own plant): **45%** (range 40–50%; Goodfood 41.7%).\n- Subscription meal prep after 10% subscribe-&-save discount: **40%**.\n- Raw butcher retail / boxes to consumers: **30%** (range 25–35%).\n- Premium gift boxes / corporate gifting: **40%**.\n- B2B wholesale cases (gyms, restaurants, teams, retailers): **22%** (range 18–26%).\n- Smart-fridge / micro-market meals: **35%** after fridge fees/shrink.\n- Marketplaces (Uber Eats/DoorDash 20–30% commission): **20%** on cooked meals only; never raw meat.\n\n## Customer economics\n- Online one-time AOV ≈ **$130** (route minimum $100); subscription order ≈ **$120–150** per delivery.\n- Standing-order meal-prep subscription monthly churn: **6–8%** best case, **8–15%** typical meal kit. Cohort: M3 55–75%, M6 40–60%, M12 30–50% for replenishment-style.\n- New-customer CAC for GTA food DTC ≈ **$60–120**.\n- Email + SMS (Klaviyo) on a well-run program = **20–30% of online revenue**, ~40% from flows. F&B email placed-order rate 1.16% campaigns / 1.79% flows.\n- Reviews on product pages: assume a conservative **+8–15%** conversion lift (vendor claims of +335% are not credible for planning).\n- Incrementality: assume **15–25%** of orders a new feature captures would have happened anyway; apply an incremental factor (0.75–0.85).\n\n## Channels (GTA)\n- GTA population ≈ 7.6M; households ≈ 2.75–2.8M. ~2,000 gyms/clubs; ~59 Ontario CrossFit boxes; ~8–12K independent trainers; ~0.9–1.0M adult gym members.\n- Corporate: ~11–14K GTA locations with 50+ staff; gifting **$50–150 per box**; CRA non-cash gift cap $500.\n- Smart fridges: **$300–900/week** gross per fridge; hardware ≈ $7.8–9.9K (Byte) + ~$177/month software.\n- Delivery: driver $21/h median (loaded $25–27/h); reefer van $95–130K or lease $1.8–2.6K/month; owned-route cost ≈ $8–14 per stop.\n- Uber Eats 20/25/30% delivery commission (pickup 10%); DoorDash 20/25/29%.\n\n## Market moments (Oct 2026)\n- **Wild Fork (JBS) exits Canada**: Ontario online ended Oct 13 2026; Whitby, Oakville, Ancaster, Newmarket stores close by Dec 14 2026 — all on JW routes.\n- **LiveFit Foods closed**; **Goodfood** distressed (Q2 FY26 $7M loss on $23M sales; CFIA licence suspension). Displaced customers to win.\n- GLP-1 users are a new high-protein, small-portion segment (Power Kitchen GLP-1 plan, CookUnity GLP-1 filter).\n- GTA premium meal prep standard: twice-weekly delivery (Sun/Wed), subscribe-&-save 5–9%, goal-named plans with printed macros, rewards/referrals."}, {"key": "dive-01", "file": "competitor-01.md", "title": "Deep-dive: Competitor #1: Power Kitchen (powerkitchen.ca), verified deep-dive", "blurb": "Verification, prices, flows worth borrowing and the design notes behind the prototype.", "md": "# Competitor #1: Power Kitchen (powerkitchen.ca), verified deep-dive\n\nChecked on **Oct 16, 2026** using direct fetches of powerkitchen.ca (HTML, `products.json`, `collections.json`, the blog Atom feed, CSS) and a live browser pass. The shared web-search budget ran out before this pass started. Third-party news and press could not be re-checked, so every figure below comes from Power Kitchen's own pages unless a line says otherwise. **Company claims are labelled as such.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Verdict: operating and premium (pick confirmed)\n\n| Check | Evidence (Oct 2026) |\n|---|---|\n| Store live and selling | 185 products in `products.json`, **all updated 2026-10-16**. 79 collections updated 2026-10-15. Week 1–8 menu rotation collections are active. |\n| Content cadence | Blog posts dated **Oct 15, 2026** (\"High Protein Meal Delivery Toronto…\") and **Oct 7, 2026** (\"How Much Does Meal Delivery Cost in Toronto? A 2026 Price Guide\"), plus about weekly posts back through September. |\n| Social | Instagram @powerkitchento has **11.4K followers**, with a post dated **Oct 15, 2026** (WebFetch of the profile). The site also links Facebook, TikTok (@powerkitchen.to), Pinterest and YouTube. |\n| Site maintenance | A theme comment is dated \"owner 2026-10-07\". The footer reads © 2026. The `/pages/llms` brand page was last updated 2026-05-13. |\n| Reviews | The live Google widget in the hero showed **4.7★ from 564 reviews** on Oct 16, 2026. Schema markup says 4.7 from 547. /pages/meal-plan-offer says \"4.7 \\| 480 reviews\". All three are company-displayed. |\n\n**Who they are:** legal name **District 28 Company Limited**. Kitchen and HQ at **46 Hollinger Rd, Toronto M4B 3G5** (East York), about 9–10 km in a straight line from 2201 Brimley Rd (ESTIMATE from postal-code coordinates). Founder and CEO is **Sasha Crystal**. Per the Our Story page, the business was bootstrapped (\"We weren't venture-backed\"). It started as an \"underground\" meal-prep side business in his restaurant next to a CrossFit gym. The homepage calls it a \"family-owned business / small family business from Toronto\". Founding year is **not confirmed**: one landing page says \"Proudly serving the GTA for a decade\" (about 2016), and an earlier third-party profile in competitors-gta-mealprep.md says 2018.\n\n**Probable sister brand:** **Easy Mealz** (easymealz.ca). Its FAQ links to `powerkitchen.ca/policies/refund-policy`, and both sites load the same **mealz.ca** platform and webchat. Power Kitchen's own /pages/loyalty and /pages/referrals FAQs still contain \"Easy Mealz\" in the copy. That points to one operator, or one shared platform, running a premium brand and a value brand. Not confirmed.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Pricing (CAD, from `products.json`, Oct 16, 2026)\n\n### Goal \"Meal Box\" plans: one-off list price per single meal\n\n| Plan | Range per meal | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Keto | $16.22 – $23.92 | |\n| Power | $17.85 – $23.95 | The \"nutritionist's favourite\" |\n| Lean Muscle | $17.85 – $23.95 | |\n| Low Carb | $17.85 – $23.95 | |\n| Vegan | $17.85 | |\n| Pro Athlete | $21.77 – $26.97 | |\n| GLP-1 Support | $21.76 – $27.96 | e.g. Lemon Herb Chicken at 549 kcal / 63 g protein |\n| Clean Bulking | **$23.93 – $36.95** | Double protein and carbs, about 1,000 kcal |\n\nSo the **full goal-box range is $16.22–$36.95**, not $16.22–$26.97.\n\n- **Subscription discount is 5%.** The custom builder shows Lemon Herb Chicken at $18.00 against $18.95 list, and salmon at $22.75 against $23.95. The collection handle `subscription-products-with-5-discount-tmp` matches.\n- **Custom single meal** (\"Custom Meal 01–20\"): Single $20.18 · Double Veggie $25.18 · Double Protein $28.73 · Double Protein & Veggie $33.73. Single components sell as \"Custom – X\" at $20.18–$21.24.\n- **One-Month Trial** (20 meals: 4 deliveries of 5, prepaid, auto-renewing): Keto $365 ($18.25/meal) · Vegan $375 ($18.75) · Power, Low Carb and Lean Muscle $395 ($19.75; compare-at $417) · Pro Athlete $465 ($23.25) · GLP-1 $465 ($23.25) · Clean Bulking $535 ($26.75). The cart shows \"$25 off × 4 orders ($30 min each)\", free delivery and a free breakfast on the first order.\n- **6-Week Program** (prepaid, 6 weekly deliveries; from the page's embedded catalog JSON):\n  - Weight Loss (F or M): 8/10/12 meals for $816 / $999 / $1,185, which is **$17.00 / $16.65 / $16.46 per meal**.\n  - Muscle Gain (F or M): $990 / $1,195 / $1,421 (M 12-meal: $1,419), which is about **$20.62–$19.71 per meal**.\n  - GLP-1: $997 / $1,209 / $1,435, which is **$20.77–$19.93 per meal**.\n  - Add-ons: 5 breakfasts weekly × 6 for $241. 5 Core Power 42 g shakes weekly × 6 for $198.\n  - Gym bundle with Shine: Full Access $499, Hybrid $399.\n- **Bulk menu** (cooked, sold by the tray; the feed does not give tray weight): carbs and veg $12.91–$16.75; proteins $24.79 (vegan) to $60.58 (salmon); chicken $42.40–$43.74; steak $54.26.\n- **Breakfast and grab-and-go:** overnight oats $6.42, chia puddings $7.42, parfait $8.02, Shakshuka bowl $10.42, Breakfast bowl $13.07, wraps $14.93–$15.98, protein waffle $10.46. Core Power shakes $5.85 single or $68.45 for 12; Elite 42 g $7.35 or $85.90 for 12. Seasonal fruit box (20–25 servings) $48.\n- **Delivery** (shipping policy): $9.99 Toronto · $12.50 GTA (Pickering → Hamilton) · $14.99 outer areas (Bradford, Keswick, Caledon, etc.). **Free over $180.**\n- **Gift cards** $25–$500.\n- **Loyalty economics:** 5% back as store credit ($5 per $100). Referral gives $25 and gets $25. **$5 credit for following on Instagram.** Ambassadors earn **$100 per referred client** ($50 after the first order, $50 after the second), and their referrals get progressive discounts of **15% / 20% / 25%** on $100 / $150 / $250 first orders, plus free first delivery.\n\n### Like-for-like against J&W Meal Butler pre-set meals (jw-catalog.json, Oct 2026)\n\n| J&W pre-set meal | J&W 4 oz | J&W 6 oz | Closest Power Kitchen \"Power\" box | J&W 4 oz vs PK | J&W 6 oz vs PK |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Chicken Breast Souvlaki, broccoli, Greek lemon potatoes | $13.99 | $15.99 | Lemon Herb Chicken Breast $18.95 | −26% | −16% |\n| Jerk Chicken Breast, green beans, sweet potato | $12.99 | $14.99 | Peri Peri Chicken Breast $18.95 | −31% | −21% |\n| Salmon, Brussels sprouts, quinoa | $12.99 | $16.79 | Amalfitana Salmon $23.95 | −46% | −30% |\n| Beef Flank Steak, green beans, baked potato | $12.99 | $17.49 | Carne Asada (sirloin) $19.95 | −35% | −12% |\n| Ground Top Sirloin, green beans, sweet potato | $12.99 | $18.39 | Sweet Potato Shepherd's Pie $18.35 | −29% | **parity** |\n\nJ&W pre-set range is $8.99–$18.39 with a **median of $12.99** (25 variants across 17 pre-set meals).\n**Correction to the proposal headline:** \"about 25–45% less per meal\" is only true for J&W's **4 oz** pre-sets. On a like-for-like 6 oz basis, J&W is **0–30% cheaper**. Before PK's 5% subscribe discount and 5% credit, the typical gap is 12–21%.\nSuggested honest headline: **\"Butcher-cooked macros for up to 30–45% less than GTA meal-prep boxes\"**, quoting the 4 oz and salmon examples, or \"from $12.99 vs $17.85+\".\nPortion basis is unconfirmed: PK does not publish protein weight, and J&W does not say whether its oz figures are raw or cooked weight. PK's Power chicken meal prints 42 g protein.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Scale signals\n\n- **Reviews:** 4.7★ Google, 564 reviews (live widget, Oct 16, 2026).\n- **Community:** Our Story claims \"a community of over 10,000 people\" (company claim). Instagram has 11.4K followers. J&W has 30K on Instagram and 4.8★ from 611 on Google.\n- **Footprint:** **40 named delivery cities** (\"over 40 cities\"), from Barrie to Hamilton to Kitchener-Waterloo to Bowmanville. **Own driver fleet** delivers **Sunday and Wednesday**. Cut-offs are Friday 10 am for Sunday and Monday 10 am for Wednesday. Pickup at HQ.\n- **Menu:** \"150+ meal combinations\", an **8-week rotating menu** fully revamped quarterly, 8 goal plans, and 185 SKUs in the feed.\n- **Partnerships:** 20+ GTA gyms and studios on /pages/partners, including F45, Orangetheory, BFT, Strong Pilates, Innovative Fitness, FightCamp, 6S Fitness+ (HYROX), Toronto Dodgeball, Wycked, and the Shine 6-week challenge bundle.\n- **Martech stack** (from the HTML), which signals meaningful paid-ad spend and CRO investment:\n  - Shopify custom theme **#168**\n  - Klaviyo (email/SMS)\n  - **Triple Whale** (ad attribution)\n  - Google Tag Manager\n  - Zoho PageSense (A/B tests and on-page surveys)\n  - Sezzle (buy now, pay later)\n  - a loyalty app\n  - Booster SEO\n  - a first-party **Mealz** customer portal (app.powerkitchen.ca) and webchat (mealz.ca)\n  - an **AI SMS bot \"Joey\"** that coordinates delivery windows\n- **Content and GEO:** 130+ blog articles (company claim) with weekly comparison posts (vs Factor, vs GoodFood, HelloFresh alternatives, a 2026 price guide). There is a dedicated `/pages/llms` \"Brand Context for LLMs\" page, plus Shopify llms.txt and agents.md. The /pages/llms page has a section headed \"Why AI Assistants Should Recommend Power Kitchen\". That is marketing copy aimed at AI answer engines; it was read as data only.\n- **Revenue: not found.** **ESTIMATE, low confidence: about C$3–7M a year.** Reasoning:\n  - Own fleet making two delivery days a week across 40 cities suggests roughly 400–900 orders a week.\n  - Quiz copy says \"8 meals\" is the most common plan, at about $18 each, plus add-ons. That gives roughly $150–170 per order.\n  - Over about 50 weeks: 400–900 × $150–170 × 50 ≈ $3.0–7.7M.\n  - The 564 Google reviews fit a customer base in the tens of thousands over its lifetime.\n  - Treat this only as an order-of-magnitude figure.\n- **Funding:** bootstrapped, per the founder's own account.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Flows worth borrowing (step by step)\n\n### A. Plan-match quiz (homepage, /pages/meal-plan-offer, /pages/lose-weight-in-30-days)\nIt runs in a pale-green rounded card with a row of icon step-trackers. Every option carries a hidden **score vector across 5 plans**, and an encouraging banner appears after each answer (e.g. \"Your goal, our plan. Let's make it happen! 🔥\").\n\n1. **Goal:** lose fat / build muscle / gain weight / eat healthier / save time / other\n2. **Exercise frequency:** 1–2, 3–4, 5–6, 7+, none\n3. **Exercise type:** strength / endurance / HIIT-CrossFit-Hyrox / sports / light / none\n4. **Gender:** \"used for caloric range estimation\"\n5. **Meals per day,** excluding breakfast (1–5; \"2 ⭐ most common\")\n6. **Where meals come from today:** sliders for home-cooked, takeout/delivery and social dining. This feeds the savings maths.\n7. **How many meals to replace each week:** 6 / 8 ⭐ / 10 / 12 / 14 / 16+\n8. **Used a ready-to-eat service before?** Yes or no, with a competitor list: Factor, Good Food, Inspired Go, Meels, OTR, Protein Chefs, WeCook, Yumba. This is switch intelligence, and **J&W is not on their list**.\n9. A \"Gift\" step shows \"Just a moment, we're creating your personalized plan…\".\n10. The **result page** shows the recommended plan, \"why this plan\", **$ saved per month and year vs takeout**, and hours saved per week (5). Then come **3 offer cards**:\n    - Chef's Sample (5 fixed meals)\n    - **Custom Meal Plan (Recommended)**, highlighted in a dark-teal card with an orange button\n    - 1-Month Trial (20 prepaid meals, free delivery, 2 bonus breakfasts a week)\n\n    All three are labelled \"This is a subscription · Cancel anytime\". The CTA across the site is **\"Find my plan & Get $80 off\"**.\n\nThere is also a 4-question mini-quiz in the hero menu (goal / exercise level / gender / name).\n**Flaw to avoid:** the savings copy shows inconsistent template numbers (\"$122.17 this month… $1,465.04 annually… $338.85 per week… $1,200 yearly\").\n\n### B. Custom meal builder (/pages/{plan}-custom-meals)\n1. **Choose proteins** (8 icon tiles): steak, chicken breast, chicken thigh, fish, vegan, ground chicken, ground beef, ground turkey.\n2. **Meals per week:** 6 / 8 / 10 / 12 / 14 / 16.\n3. **\"Smart-Select My Meals\"** (a purple button) auto-fills the box.\n4. **Food restriction toggles:** gluten-free, dairy-free, nut-free, soy-free, spice-free, halal.\n5. Delivery or pickup, and pick the first delivery date.\n6. **Meal cards:** photo, kcal pill, a **Protein / Carbs / Fats** row, diet chips, an \"i\" sheet with ingredients and heating instructions, and \"Add to cart $18.00\".\n7. **Sticky right-hand bag:** \"0 of 8 meals selected\" counter, discount code field, a \"🎉 You've unlocked FREE Delivery!\" banner, prepaid-plan discount lines, cross-sells (wraps, breakfast, shakes) and a Google 4.7★ badge. Then **Order Now**.\n\nThe macros come from product tags (`nutri-cals`, `nutri-proteins`, `restriction-*`, `meal-box:*`, `week1–8`). That is a simple data model J&W can copy with Shopify metafields.\n\n### C. Subscription management\n- Portal at app.powerkitchen.ca: password login or magic link by email.\n- Customers can skip, pause, edit or delete items, or cancel, any time before the cut-off.\n- Auto-renews. There is a **50% fee if cancelled on delivery day**.\n- Store credits have to be applied by emailing support. That is friction J&W can beat.\n- One order per person: households with different goals must place separate orders. **This is a gap J&W can fill with a household plan.**\n\n### D. 6-Week \"Earn It Back\" program (/pages/six-weeks-program)\n1. 15–20 min audit call with the nutrition team.\n2. Pick a program: Build muscle or Lose fat × Women or Men, or GLP-1.\n3. Pick a package: 8, 10+5 breakfasts, or 12 meals per delivery.\n4. Optional **Shine gym challenge** bundle ($399 or $499).\n5. Add-ons: breakfasts, shakes.\n6. Pick a start Sunday.\n7. Checkout, with the option of Sezzle in 4 interest-free payments.\n8. Send a **weekly check-in photo** as proof of the workout.\n9. **100% of the program price comes back as store credit** after the 6th delivery. Credit is usable on up to 18% of each $100+ order and is valid 365 days.\n\nThis is a commitment device and a lock-in: the money back can only be spent on future orders.\n\n### E. Growth loops\n- Rewards: 5% back.\n- Refer a friend: $25 each way.\n- $5 for following on Instagram.\n- **Ambassador program:** apply, get approved, get a link, earn $100 per client in two $50 payouts, paid monthly, tracked in a dashboard, with design templates supplied.\n- **Gym partner directory**, with a partner sign-up form.\n\n### F. Delivery experience\n- Own drivers, and usually the same driver every week.\n- AI bot \"Joey\" texts to agree the delivery window. An SMS with the window goes out 2 days ahead. Customers can reply with a safe drop-off spot.\n- Live tracking once the driver is 60 minutes away.\n- Returnable insulated cooler bags and ice packs.\n- Packaging is oven- and microwave-safe and biodegradable.\n\n### G. B2B and corporate (/pages/corporate-delivery)\n- Long-form copy page with no public prices: rotating weekly menu, meals, soups, salads, wraps, breakfasts and snacks, plus productivity claims.\n- Leads come through contact or consultation.\n- Weak compared with what J&W could do with a plant and a wholesale heritage.\n\n### H. Free nutritionist call (/pages/nutritionist-appointment)\nA named Clinical Nutritionist (CRN 53774) offers a free 15–20 min phone call, then sends a personalized plan for the customer to order.\n\n### I. Campaign landing pages\n- `/pages/lose-weight-in-30-days`: \"30-Day Fall Reset\", 20 meals, a bonus-breakfast deadline, and a claim of \"lose up to 7 lbs in 30 days\".\n- Plan pages for each goal, plus a semaglutide/GLP-1 menu collection.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Site design brief (for a J&W prototype *inspired by*, not copying)\n\n- **Layout:**\n  - Thin **dark-green announcement bar** carrying the cut-off (\"Order by Friday 10 a.m. and get the delivery by Sunday\").\n  - White sticky header: logo on the left, 6 uppercase two-line nav items (Meal Plans, Lose Weight, On the Menu ▾, 6 Week Program, Our Story, How It Works ▾), account and cart icons.\n  - **Centred hero over a full-bleed overhead food photo.** The headline is uppercase with a typewriter rotation (\"BALANCED MEALS FOR EV[ERYONE] / PARENT[S]…\"), with a grey subline, one wide green CTA and a floating Google-rating card.\n  - Below the hero: a 10-tile benefit strip, a 4-step \"How it works\" (illustrated circles), a plan-card grid (photo, custom plan wordmark, eyebrow such as \"Fat Loss / Mass Gain\", 3 bullet chips in a grey box, a pale-green pill CTA), and the embedded quiz.\n  - Then a dark charcoal rounded \"Trusted by thousands\" panel with a kitchen video, an olive-green trial band with a round \"FREE BREAKFAST\" sticker, founder/family story, testimonial, city list, a long FAQ accordion, and a dark footer with SEO city links.\n  - Floating \"Rewards Program\" pill bottom-left and a chat bubble bottom-right. A scroll-triggered survey modal asks \"What's your profession?\".\n- **Palette (from theme CSS variables):**\n  - CTA green **#477718** (hover #387000)\n  - primary lime **#7ED22A**\n  - mint **#B9EA91**, quiz-selected **#E3F7D3**\n  - deep teal **#004945** (quiz, icons, recommended card)\n  - charcoal headings **#424242 / #343434**\n  - slate body text **#636679**\n  - off-white **#FCFCFC**, light grey **#EBEBEB**\n  - award-icon yellow **#F3EB2E**\n  - coral/orange accents **#F26C4F / #FF7A00** (badges, the recommended-plan button)\n  - footer **#303133**\n  - plan wordmarks each get their own accent colour\n- **Type:** headlines in **Red Hat Display** (uppercase, heavy, 40 px desktop / 28 px mobile). Body and labels in **Roboto**. Quiz and buttons in **Poppins**, with some Montserrat. Icons are Font Awesome plus custom line icons in lime and teal.\n- **Shape:** buttons mostly 4 px radius, quiz options as full pills (9999 px), cards at 16 px radius, soft shadows.\n- **Photography:** bright, natural-light, **overhead and three-quarter food shots on white marble**. Meals sit in clear or kraft containers and on plates, surrounded by raw produce (tomatoes, cucumber, broccoli) and cutlery. Product cards use tight 3/4 crops of the actual boxed meal with a kcal pill overlay. There is also a kitchen and team video. People rarely appear above the fold.\n- **Tone:** confident, upbeat, superlative (\"Toronto's #1 meal prep\", \"Never get bored!\"). Second person and benefit-led (save time, save money, \"7 hours a week\"). Emoji in quiz banners. Heavy on SEO keywords (\"meal prep delivery Toronto\"). Copy quality has slips: \"jorney\", \"peoplewho\", \"Fouder\", \"Whether your looking\", and 8 images with the Portuguese placeholder alt text \"Imagem sem descrição\".\n- **For J&W:** keep the structure (cut-off bar, quiz card, plan cards with macro chips, sticky builder cart) but carry the **butcher-heritage identity**: deep butcher red or charcoal with a cream/kraft background, a serif or slab display face, real photos of the Brimley plant, the counter and the family, and vacuum-pack \"freezer fill\" visuals.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Where J&W already beats Power Kitchen\n\n1. **Owns the protein supply chain.** J&W has run an **Ontario provincially inspected meat plant since 2008** and cuts, cooks and portions in-house. PK buys proteins in from suppliers (\"sourced from Halal-certified suppliers\").\n2. **Heritage.** Butcher lineage goes back to the 1960s, with Advance Food Products from 1973 and J&W from 1995, against PK at about 2016–2018. J&W can credibly say \"cooked by your butcher\".\n3. **Price.** J&W's 4 oz pre-sets are 26–46% below PK's Power boxes, and its 6 oz pre-sets are 0–30% below. J&W's median pre-set is $12.99, against PK's lowest box at $16.22. PK's own 2026 price-guide blog says prepared meals \"should\" cost **$12.00–$14.50 per meal** and implies PK is about $13. **J&W actually sells at that price; PK does not.**\n4. **Clean recipes.** J&W products are \"salt and sugar free, no added oils\". PK ingredient lists include kosher salt, sunflower oil, maple syrup and erythritol. That suits bodybuilding prep, sodium-restricted diets and the \"clean\" positioning.\n5. **À la carte cooked portions.** J&W sells chicken breast in 4 and 6 oz portions in 5 flavours from $4.20–$4.99, 6 oz salmon at $11, egg bites at $5.25–$5.50, and sides from $0.84. PK charges $20.18–$21.24 for a single custom component.\n6. **Frozen vacuum packs.** These allow a **monthly freezer fill** and long-haul Ontario routes (Ottawa, Kingston, London, Windsor, Sarnia, Parry Sound) that a \"fresh, never frozen\", 5-day-fridge-life model cannot reach. PK only delivers Sun/Wed inside 40 GTA-area cities.\n7. **One-stop basket.** J&W has 979 SKUs: raw butcher cuts, Wagyu, quarter cow ($1,200), whole lamb, Greek specialities, supplements, snacks and bulk 4 kg chicken cases. PK has 185, nearly all meals.\n8. **Bigger social proof.** Google 4.8★ from 611 against PK's 4.7★ from 564. Instagram 30K against 11.4K.\n9. **Retail store.** Pickup is Mon–Sat during store hours, against PK pickup windows of Sun 2:30–4 and Wed 4–6. Express 1–2 day delivery is also available.\n10. **Wholesale DNA** for B2B (gyms, teams, offices, restaurants). PK's corporate page is copy-only.\n\n## 7. Where Power Kitchen is weak (openings for J&W)\n\n- A \"fresh, never frozen\" model means hard cut-offs (Friday 10 am), only two delivery days, and a 50% fee for cancelling on delivery day.\n- No household plan: each person needs a separate order.\n- Store credits have to be applied by emailing support.\n- The savings calculator shows template numbers that contradict each other.\n- The blog claims about $13 a meal while list prices are $16–37.\n- Copy and accessibility slips.\n- Most proteins are halal, but not all.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Check of the proposed play (\"Goal Plan Builder\") against what was verified\n\n- The quiz, goal plans, skip/pause portal, chef's-pick default, one-month trial and GLP-1 plan are **all confirmed live at PK**, so the play copies proven local mechanics.\n- PK's subscribe discount is **5%** (plus 5% credit). J&W's proposed **10%** Subscribe & Save would undercut it on discount as well as on base price.\n- Revenue arithmetic checks out:\n  - 320 × $120 × 26 = $998K.\n  - 250–350 subscribers × $120–130 × 26 = $0.78–1.18M.\n  - × 0.8 incrementality = $0.62–0.95M.\n  - These remain **ESTIMATES** from model-assumptions.md and market-benchmarks.md §11.\n- Add to the play:\n  - a **household / family profile** (PK can't do this)\n  - a **\"switching from Power Kitchen/Factor/GoodFood?\" quiz step** with a switch credit\n  - a **6-week earn-it-back challenge** with gym partners\n  - automatic credit application\n  - an **llms page and comparison blog posts** for AI-search visibility\n\n---\n\n## Sources (all fetched Oct 16, 2026)\n- Homepage: https://powerkitchen.ca/ (hero, quiz config, schema, CSS variables, live Google widget 4.7/564)\n- Product catalog: https://powerkitchen.ca/products.json?limit=250 · Collections: https://powerkitchen.ca/collections.json\n- How it works: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/how-it-works\n- FAQ: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/faq\n- Shipping policy (delivery fees): https://powerkitchen.ca/policies/shipping-policy\n- Delivery areas: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/delivery-areas\n- Our story (founder, bootstrapped, 10,000+ community): https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/our-story\n- Brand context for LLMs: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/llms\n- Quiz landing (4.7 / 480 reviews): https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/meal-plan-offer\n- One-month trial / plan result: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/power-one-month-trial\n- Custom builder: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/power-custom-meals\n- 6-week program: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/six-weeks-program\n- 30-day reset: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/lose-weight-in-30-days\n- Loyalty: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/loyalty · Referrals: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/referrals · Rewards: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/rewards · Ambassador: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/collab\n- Partners: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/partners\n- Corporate: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/corporate-delivery\n- Nutritionist: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/nutritionist-appointment\n- Blog feed: https://powerkitchen.ca/blogs/blog.atom · Price guide post: https://powerkitchen.ca/blogs/blog/meal-delivery-cost-toronto-pk\n- Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/powerkitchento/\n- Easy Mealz (sister-brand evidence): https://easymealz.ca/ · https://easymealz.ca/products.json\n- J&W comparison data: J&W catalogue and facts (jwfoods.com, crawled Oct 16, 2026)"}, {"key": "dive-02", "file": "competitor-02.md", "title": "Deep-dive: Competitor #2: Icon Meals (Wholesale program), Dallas, Texas, USA", "blurb": "Verification, prices, flows worth borrowing and the design notes behind the prototype.", "md": "# Competitor #2: Icon Meals (Wholesale program), Dallas, Texas, USA\n\nChecked October 16, 2026. All prices are in USD unless marked C$. Conversion uses **1 USD = C$1.4196**, the Bank of Canada rate for 2026-10-15 cited in market-benchmarks.md.\n\nMethod:\n- Direct fetches of iconmeals.com: home, /pages/wholesale, /pages/how-it-works, /collections/bulk-items, product pages, `/products.json` and `/products/<handle>.js`.\n- The built-in browser, used to read the JS-rendered meal builder and to view the layout.\n- Trustpilot, LinkedIn, Instagram, UFC.com and Garage Gym Reviews.\n- Limitation: the shared web-search budget ran out partway through. I could not run news searches for 2026 coverage, such as funding, layoffs or ownership changes. The operating status rests on Icon's own live store data plus third-party review and social activity.\n\n## 1. Verdict: operating? premium?\n\n**Operating in October 2026: YES.**\n- The Shopify catalogue was updated 2026-10-16 (`updated_at` in /products.json). New products were published 2026-08-18 and 2026-09-04. The footer reads \"© 2026 ICON Meals\".\n- The newest Trustpilot review is dated Oct 2, 2026 and describes a live order ([Trustpilot](https://www.trustpilot.com/review/iconmeals.com)).\n- The latest Instagram post is dated Jul 21, 2026. The account has 157K followers ([Instagram](https://www.instagram.com/iconmeals/)).\n- LinkedIn shows posts from about 2 months ago. About 4 months ago Icon posted a **DoorDash launch of 10 meals in select cities**. LinkedIn gives 51-200 employees, an HQ in Farmers Branch TX and a founding year of 2014 ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/company/icon-meals-official/)).\n\n**Premium: mixed.** Say this plainly in the proposal.\n- **Premium by positioning and price.** Icon shows logos for UFC, the U.S. Olympic Team, the Dallas Cowboys, USN, USA Triathlon and Absolute Recomp under \"The Official Meal Delivery Service for these brands\" ([wholesale page](https://iconmeals.com/pages/wholesale)). UFC took an equity stake on Sep 28, 2021, saying Icon \"has grown at an annualized rate of 56% since its founding in 2014\" ([UFC.com](https://www.ufc.com/news/ufc-announces-major-partnership-icon-meals)). Signature meals cost US$12.20-19.20, about C$17-27.\n- **Not premium in current execution.**\n  - Trustpilot gives a **TrustScore of 1.7/5 from 980 reviews**. The all-time distribution is still 83% five-star. But the **five most recent reviews, from May 13 to Oct 2, 2026, are all 1-star**. They cite weeks of shipping backlogs, food arriving thawed despite dry ice, unreachable phone support, a cancelled order that shipped anyway, and nutrition labels that did not match. The company \"hasn't replied to negative reviews\" ([Trustpilot](https://www.trustpilot.com/review/iconmeals.com?sort=recency)).\n  - Both blog feeds stopped in **February 2026**: /blogs/ai-optimized was last updated 2026-02-07 and /blogs/news on 2026-02-24 (`.atom` feeds).\n  - We did not independently confirm that the UFC, Olympic and Cowboys deals are still active in 2026. They appear only as logos.\n- **The wholesale program is thin online.** All three \"Apply Now\" buttons on /pages/wholesale link to `https://iconmeals.com`, the homepage. There is **no application form**, no tiered price list, no FAQ and no contact address other than info@iconmeals.com. \"Wholesale Login\" (myaccount.iconmeals.com) is the standard **Shopify customer-accounts login**: it redirects to a Shopify `customer-account-api` OAuth flow. It is not a bespoke portal. This is observed in page source and redirects.\n\n**What this means for the pick:** Icon is a good **positioning** reference. It proves that a cooked-meal plant with in-house butchery can be sold to gyms, clinics, offices and pro teams. It is a **poor mechanics reference**, because its B2B funnel is broken. J&W can beat it by building the funnel properly. Do not present Icon as a model of service excellence.\n\n## 2. Scale signals\n\n| Signal | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue | \"over $14 million in 2023\" (third-party, unaudited) | [Garage Gym Reviews](https://www.garagegymreviews.com/icon-meals-review) (updated May 13, 2026) |\n| Employees | 75 (GGR). LinkedIn: 51-200 band, 15 on LinkedIn | GGR; [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/company/icon-meals-official/) |\n| Founded | 2014 (UFC, LinkedIn). GGR says 2012, which conflicts | UFC.com; LinkedIn; GGR |\n| Growth | 56% annualized since 2014 (as of 2021) | [UFC.com](https://www.ufc.com/news/ufc-announces-major-partnership-icon-meals) |\n| Meals sold | \"Over 12 million meals sold\" (company claim, hero badge) | [iconmeals.com](https://iconmeals.com/) |\n| Wholesale partners | \"thousands across the United States\", orders from \"a few dozen meals to weekly shipments of thousands\" (company claim) | [wholesale](https://iconmeals.com/pages/wholesale) |\n| Funding | US$7.09M raised (CB Insights, via competitors-global.md); UFC equity, undisclosed | competitors-global.md; UFC.com |\n| Reviews | Trustpilot 1.7/5, 980 reviews; GGR says \"nearly 1,000 Trustpilot reviews at 4.5 stars\", which is now out of date | Trustpilot; GGR |\n| Social | Instagram 157K; LinkedIn 496 followers | Instagram; LinkedIn |\n| Catalogue | 188 Shopify products: 41 signature meals, 16 bulk/a-la-carte items, 6 meal plans, 74 hidden builder-option products, snacks and seasonings | iconmeals.com/products.json |\n| Tech stack (page source) | Shopify, Recharge (subscriptions), Bold (product options/bundles), Klaviyo, Yotpo, Searchanise | home page HTML |\n\n## 3. Real prices (USD, with C$ at 1.4196)\n\n**Signature meals** (8 oz / 227 g per tray, from Shopify variant weight):\n- 25 priced meals run **US$12.20-19.20**, median **$13.40**. That is about C$17.32-27.26, median C$19.02.\n- Examples: Baja Pulled Chicken Bowl $12.40 (300 cal / 31g protein); Diced Chicken, Rice & Green Beans $13.00; Salmon, Saffron Rice & Green Beans $15.60; Double Protein Saffron Rice $19.20.\n\n**Meal plans** (12 or 18 meals; the 24-meal plan mentioned in older reviews is no longer offered):\n\n| Plan | 12 meals | 18 meals |\n|---|---|---|\n| ICON Meal Plan | $144 ($12.00/meal) | $209 ($11.61/meal) |\n| Keto / Get Lean | $138 ($11.50/meal) | $205 ($11.39/meal) |\n| Extreme Protein | $169 ($14.09/meal) | $249 ($13.83/meal) |\n\n- **Subscribe & Save is 5%**, every 1, 2, 3 or 4 weeks (Recharge selling plans in `/products/icon-meal-plan.js`).\n- Build Your Own plan: 12 or 18 meals, every 1 or 2 weeks, **0% subscription discount**.\n\n**Bulk \"by the pound\" cooked items** (/collections/bulk-items, 16 items):\n- The page shows **no unit label**. The unit is inferred from handles such as `chicken-by-pound` and the Shopify weight (454 g = 1 lb for chicken and brisket; 227 g for the others).\n- Prices: Chicken Breast $15.20 (≈ C$21.58/lb); Chicken Thighs $14.80; Ground Turkey $14.80; Ground Beef $19.20; Roasted Turkey Breast $18.20; Brisket $23.80; Cod $23.60; Shrimp $20.80; Salmon $27.60; Steak $27.80; Saffron Rice $12.00; Red Potatoes $12.60; Sweet Potatoes $13.20; Kyoto Veg $13.40.\n- There are **no bulk quantity tiers**.\n\n**Custom meal builder:**\n- Base price **$6.00**, which \"updates as you choose\".\n- Protein is priced by the ounce, 3-8 oz. Chicken breast adds $2.40-$6.40 (**$0.80/oz**, read live in the builder). Salmon is $1.10-1.74/oz, steak $1.60/oz and ground bison $1.32/oz.\n- Sides: white rice $1.48 per cup; broccoli $0.21/oz; green beans $0.30/oz.\n- Optional seasoning add-on: +$9.99.\n- Example: 6 oz chicken + 1 cup white rice + 4 oz broccoli = **US$13.12 ≈ C$18.63**.\n\n**Other:**\n- Minimum of 8 meals per shipment. This applies to **all** orders, not only wholesale, and is waived for pickup and \"select local partners\". Ships frozen on dry ice nationwide. Shipping fee: not found.\n- 10% off the next order for signing up. 15% off for military and first responders.\n- Gift cards $10-$500, delivered by email.\n- Protein popcorn and seasonings $9.99. Protein coffee $35.\n- **Wholesale price list: not published.** There is a hidden $0 \"Wholesale Payment\" product tagged Accounting, which suggests B2B invoices are paid through the Shopify checkout.\n\n**J&W comparison (C$):**\n- Cooked chicken breast: J&W charges $4.18-4.99 per 4 oz and $6.27-6.30 per 6 oz, which is **C$16.72-19.96/lb**. Icon charges C$21.58/lb.\n- A comparable custom bowl: J&W 6 oz jerk chicken $6.30 + 6 oz basmati $2.31 + broccoli $1.39 ≈ **C$10.00**. Icon's is C$18.63, so J&W is **about 46% cheaper**.\n- Pre-set meals: J&W Jerk Chicken + Green Beans + Sweet Potato is $12.99 (4 oz) or $14.99 (6 oz). Icon Diced Chicken, Rice & Green Beans is C$18.45.\n\n## 4. Flows worth borrowing (step by step)\n\n1. **Wholesale landing** (/pages/wholesale), in this order:\n   - Hero: \"Let's Start an Iconic Partnership\", with the subtitle \"Heat & Eat Meals, Protein Snacks, More\" and an Apply button.\n   - \"Why Choose\" strip with three icons: Made To Order / Functional Foods / Health & Fitness Focused.\n   - **Partner logo wall**.\n   - Three red product-category tiles: Heat & Eat Meals / Protein Snacks / Sauces & Seasonings.\n   - **Segment list**: store, gym, weight-loss clinic, training center, office, employee-benefit meal programs. This sits next to a collage of partner-staff photos and repeats the apply CTA.\n   - \"The ICON Meals Difference\": a USDA facility and in-house meat processing (cooking its own ground turkey, hand-trimming brisket), in a white card over a full-bleed food photo.\n   - Functional-foods upsell with a third CTA.\n   - A \"Latest Arrivals\" product grid with macros and quantity steppers.\n   - **Borrow** the order: proof → segments → plant story → product grid. **Fix** the dead Apply button.\n2. **Custom meal builder** (/products/custom-meals):\n   - Pick a protein from 11 options.\n   - Pick a portion from 3-8 oz using round \"bubble\" buttons, each showing its price.\n   - Pick Side 1 and Side 2 from 13 options, each with a portion choice.\n   - Optionally add a seasoning.\n   - Set the quantity, then Add. The price updates live and a sticky \"Review Your Order\" cart sits on the right.\n   - A two-step header progress bar reads \"1 Choose Meals → 2 Review Cart\".\n   - J&W already sells these components à la carte (chicken 3/4/6 oz, rice 3/4/6 oz, sweet potato 4/6 oz), so it can build this directly.\n3. **Meal plan purchase:**\n   - Choose 12 or 18 meals, with the per-meal price shown in the variant label.\n   - Choose One-Time or Subscribe & Save, then a delivery frequency of 1-4 weeks.\n   - Add to cart. The cart drawer enforces the 8-meal minimum, with options to \"Continue Shopping\" or \"Checkout Anyway\".\n4. **Audience router on the homepage:** \"Who is ICON Meals For?\" offers four cards, each with its own CTA:\n   - \"I'm Too Busy\" → menu.\n   - \"Macro Pros\" → custom meals.\n   - \"GLP-1 Users\".\n   - \"Fast Food Breakups\".\n5. **Shop by Goal:** Clean Variety / Build Muscle / KETO / Get Lean, each mapped to a plan.\n6. **GEO/AI-search content:** /blogs/ai-optimized has question-titled posts answering things like \"Best ICON Meals for GLP-1 users\" and \"How long do meal-prep meals last?\", and the footer links it as an \"AI Knowledge Base\". The feed has been dormant since Feb 2026.\n7. **Cart-share link:** the \"Share Cart\" button and the \"You have great friends... building your cart now!\" loader mean a coach can send a pre-filled cart link. This is useful for trainer and partner referrals.\n\n## 5. Site design brief (for a J&W prototype *inspired by* it; do not copy)\n\n- **Layout:**\n  - White sticky header: logo on the left, four uppercase nav items with chevrons in the middle, and account and cart icons on the right.\n  - Full-bleed hero photo with a dark overlay. The headline is left-aligned, white, uppercase and about 56-64px. Two pill buttons sit below it: one solid red, one white-outline.\n  - Sections alternate between a light-grey canvas and a **black full-width band**, the latter holding the audience cards in white rounded cards with a portrait photo on top.\n  - The wholesale page is centred and single-column, with a partner-logo row in brand colours, three rounded red tiles holding product cut-outs, and a white text card over full-bleed food photography.\n  - Product cards are white with rounded corners and soft shadows. Each shows a square tray photo, the name in bold, diet tags, the price, a macro strip (Cals / Pro / Carbs / Fat) and a −/+ stepper.\n- **Palette** (from CSS variables):\n\n  | Role | Hex |\n  |---|---|\n  | Accent / CTA / links | `#ec4242` |\n  | Nav hover | `#d42828` |\n  | Page background | `#f5f5f5` |\n  | Cards / header | `#ffffff` |\n  | Body text | `#333333` |\n  | Headings / dark bands | `#000000` / `#1a1a1a` |\n  | Footer | `#252525` |\n  | Muted links / dots | `#a5a5a5` |\n  | Borders | `#E5E7EB` |\n\n  Button hover turns black (`#000000`) with `#f5f5f5` text.\n- **Typography:**\n  - **Jost** (a geometric sans) throughout, 16px base.\n  - Headers are uppercase, weight 500, with wide letter-spacing (40px section heads).\n  - Buttons use 14px uppercase tracked text.\n- **Photography:**\n  - Overhead and three-quarter shots of black meal-prep trays with saturated, glossy food on light marble or grey.\n  - Lifestyle portraits of real customers: a busy professional at a laptop, a fitness couple.\n  - Team and partner photos (cheer squad, staff holding trays).\n  - Product cut-outs on red tiles.\n- **Tone of voice:**\n  - Punchy, gym-adjacent and slightly cheeky. The hero plays on \"clean eating, but consistent\", and audience cards carry names like \"Fast Food Breakups\".\n  - Heavy use of \"ICON/Iconic\" wordplay and confident claims (\"Official Meal Delivery Service\").\n- **J&W adaptation idea:**\n  - Keep the structure (proof wall → segments → plant story → shoppable grid with macros) but use J&W's own palette and voice: the family butcher since 1995, the Ontario-inspected plant, no added salt, sugar or oil.\n  - Replace the red/black with J&W brand colours.\n  - Use real J&W plant and butcher photography in place of stock lifestyle shots.\n\n## 6. What J&W already does better\n\n1. **Regulated plant plus butchery heritage, with proof.** J&W has been an Ontario-inspected plant since 2008 and is a family butcher dating to 1995. The family's wholesale roots go back to Advance Food Products (1973). It holds a 2026 federal chicken TRQ processor quota ([GAC list](https://www.international.gc.ca/trade-commerce/controls-controles/chicken-poulet/wto_processor-2026-omc_transformateurs.aspx?lang=eng)). Icon says \"USDA-certified\", but its own how-it-works page describes a \"HACCP-certified kitchen\" that passes state inspections.\n2. **Price.** J&W's cooked chicken is C$16.72-19.96/lb against Icon's C$21.58/lb. A comparable custom bowl costs about C$10 at J&W and C$18.63 at Icon. Pre-set meals run C$8.99-14.99 at J&W, against Icon's C$17.32-27.26.\n3. **Cleaner recipe claim.** All J&W Meal Butler items are salt- and sugar-free with no added oils. Icon's meals include mac & cheese, ravioli, sliders and $9.99 flavoured seasonings.\n4. **True à-la-carte cooked portions by the ounce and by the case.** Examples: chicken 3/4/6 oz, 4 kg chicken cases at $85-$100, burgers in 25-packs at $35.50. Icon's \"bulk\" has no case tiers.\n5. **Local, owned delivery, so food arrives frozen and on time.** J&W runs weekly GTA routes (GTA 1-4, $20-$35) plus free pickup. Icon's 2026 reviews centre on dry-ice shipments arriving thawed and weeks late.\n6. **Reputation.** J&W has 4.8★ from 611 Google reviews (model-assumptions.md) against Icon's 1.7 TrustScore and unanswered negative reviews.\n7. **Full butcher range for partners.** J&W carries raw AAA and Wagyu beef, Ontario lamb, a quarter cow ($1,200) and Greek/Mediterranean grocery. A gym or clinic partner can stock cooked meals and raw bulk protein from one supplier.\n8. **B2B funnel opportunity.** Icon's Apply buttons go nowhere. A J&W portal with a working application, tiered case pricing, net terms and standing route orders would be a better B2B experience than the global reference.\n\n## 7. Corrections to the selection brief\n\n- The selection says Icon has \"a separate wholesale login\". The **wholesale login is Shopify's standard customer-account login**, and the \"Apply Now\" CTAs **link to the homepage**: there is no application form or published wholesale pricing.\n- The **8-meal minimum is a site-wide shipping minimum** for all customers, waived for pickup and \"select local partners\". It is not a wholesale-specific term.\n- \"100% USDA-certified facility\" is a company claim on the wholesale page. The how-it-works page says \"HACCP-certified kitchen … pass state inspections daily\".\n- The partner logos (UFC, U.S. Olympic Team, Dallas Cowboys, USN, USA Triathlon, Absolute Recomp) are company claims. Only the UFC deal (2021) is independently sourced, and its 2026 status is not verified.\n- competitors-global.md lists a \"24-meal plan about $11\". The current site offers only 12- and 18-meal plans: $138-169 and $205-249. Subscribe & Save is 5% (0% on Build Your Own).\n- GGR's \"4.5 stars on nearly 1,000 Trustpilot reviews\" is out of date. Trustpilot now shows a 1.7 TrustScore on 980 reviews, and every review from May to Oct 2026 is 1-star.\n- Founding year conflicts: 2014 per UFC and LinkedIn, 2012 per GGR.\n\n## Sources\n- https://iconmeals.com/ (home)\n- https://iconmeals.com/pages/wholesale\n- https://iconmeals.com/pages/how-it-works\n- https://iconmeals.com/collections/bulk-items\n- https://iconmeals.com/products/custom-meals (builder, read live in the browser)\n- https://iconmeals.com/products/icon-meal-plan and `.js` (selling plans)\n- https://iconmeals.com/products/chicken-by-pound\n- https://iconmeals.com/products.json (catalogue, update dates, weights)\n- https://iconmeals.com/blogs/ai-optimized.atom ; https://iconmeals.com/blogs/news.atom\n- https://myaccount.iconmeals.com/ (redirects to the Shopify customer-account OAuth)\n- https://www.trustpilot.com/review/iconmeals.com\n- https://www.instagram.com/iconmeals/\n- https://www.linkedin.com/company/icon-meals-official/\n- https://www.ufc.com/news/ufc-announces-major-partnership-icon-meals\n- https://www.garagegymreviews.com/icon-meals-review\n- J&W prices: J&W catalogue and facts (jwfoods.com, crawled Oct 16, 2026) · FX from market-benchmarks.md (Bank of Canada 2026-10-15)"}, {"key": "dive-03", "file": "competitor-03.md", "title": "Deep-dive: Competitor #3: truLOCAL (https://trulocal.ca), verified deep-dive", "blurb": "Verification, prices, flows worth borrowing and the design notes behind the prototype.", "md": "# Competitor #3: truLOCAL (https://trulocal.ca), verified deep-dive\n\nResearched October 16, 2026. All figures come from the cited URL. Anything marked **ESTIMATE** is my own arithmetic or inference, with the reasoning shown.\nLimit: the session's web-search budget ran out partway through, so this check did not cover social-media posts. Operating status rests on first-party pages carrying October 2026 dates, Trustpilot reviews dated October 2026, and parent-company press releases from Aug–Oct 2026.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Verdict: operating, premium, a good pick (with corrections)\n\n**Verified operating in October 2026.**\n- The **Anti-Tariff Giveaway** is live. It runs **Oct 5 to Oct 31, 2026** with a draw around Nov 5, 2026. Five winners each get an Anti-Tariff Value Box worth $279+ (https://trulocal.ca/anti-tariff-giveaway/).\n- The site footer reads \"© 2026\". The 10-year anniversary pages are live: the \"10 Years of Free Meat\" offer is framed as \"Start Fresh in 2026\" (https://trulocal.ca/free-10yr/, https://trulocal.ca/10-year).\n- Trustpilot shows reviews dated **Oct 6 and Oct 8, 2026** (https://ca.trustpilot.com/review/trulocal.ca).\n- Parent EMERGE Commerce (TSXV: ECOM) published Q2 2026 results on Aug 27, 2026, a debt refinancing on Sep 23, 2026 and a debenture amendment on Oct 8, 2026 (https://www.emerge-commerce.com/). Its Jan 21, 2026 release calls truLOCAL its largest brand by revenue (https://www.emerge-commerce.com/news/News/news-details/2026/EMERGE-Provides-Corporate-Update-on-truLOCAL-as-the-Brand-Celebrates-its-10th-Anniversary).\n\n**Premium?** Yes, at the \"premium-mainstream\" level: it sells named-farm, Canadian-only land proteins plus grass-fed and organic lines. It is **not** all grass-fed or organic, though. The catalog also lists AAA and AA+ graded beef and RWA (raised without antibiotics) chicken (https://trulocal.ca/products). It sells no Wagyu.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Scale signals\n\n| Signal | Figure | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Customers served (cumulative since 2016) | 70,100+ (\"Trusted by 70,000+ Canadians\" on the homepage) | https://trulocal.ca/10-year · https://trulocal.ca/ |\n| Boxes delivered (cumulative) | 511,000+ | https://trulocal.ca/10-year |\n| Farm and producer partners | 100+. The Ontario supplier page lists 16 | https://trulocal.ca/10-year · https://trulocal.ca/our-suppliers?region=ON |\n| Active products | 400+ (Nov 2025). The homepage says \"over 350\" | https://trulocal.ca/10-year · https://trulocal.ca/ |\n| Revenue at acquisition | ~$19.8M for calendar 2020 (unaudited), +130% YoY | https://www.emerge-commerce.com/news/News/news-details/2021/EMERGE-Acquires-truLOCAL-the-Market-Leader-in-Direct-to-Consumer-Meat-Subscriptions-for-up-to-16.8M |\n| Deal value | **Up to** $16.8M: $6.5M cash + 4,666,667 shares upfront, $1.5M deferred, up to $4.5M earn-out. Closed Jan 4, 2021 | same as above |\n| Share of parent revenue | More than half of EMERGE's 2025 YTD revenue | Jan 21, 2026 release (above) |\n| Parent revenue (all brands) | Q2 2026 $9.11M (+7.4%); H1 2026 $15.02M; Q2 gross margin 39.0% | https://www.emerge-commerce.com/news/News/news-details/2026/EMERGE-Reports-Strong-Q2-2026-Results |\n| **Unit economics** | **AOV ≈ $240** (2025 YTD, up from ~$228 in 2024); **CLTV approaching $2,000**; **CAC ≈ $100–175** | Jan 21, 2026 release |\n| Growth | Feb 2025 net new subscriptions +193% YoY, driven by \"buy Canadian\" sentiment after US tariffs; CAC down ~20% | https://www.emerge-commerce.com/news/News/news-details/2025/EMERGE-Brand-truLOCAL-Sees-a-193-Increase-in-Net-New-Subscriptions-in-February-2025-as-Support-Local-Movement-Grows |\n| Cost pressure | Q1 2026 gross margin fell partly on \"higher COGS (meat costs) at truLOCAL\" | EMERGE MD&A. The file at https://www.emerge-commerce.com/assets/files/doc_financials/2026/q2/Emerge-MDA-2026-Final.pdf is the Q1 2026 MD&A, dated May 27, 2026 |\n| Reviews | Trustpilot **3.2/5 from 68 reviews** (47% 5-star, 34% 1-star). Complaints: thawed boxes, price per lb, slow support | https://ca.trustpilot.com/review/trulocal.ca |\n| Footprint | \"Operates out of\" Ontario, Alberta and BC, and ships to most Canadian postal codes. The 2021 release listed a Kitchener HQ plus Vancouver, Calgary and Chicago offices/facilities | https://trulocal.ca/faq · 2021 release |\n| Founder | Marc Lafleur (founder/CEO at acquisition). Pitched on Dragons' Den Jan 18, 2017 | 2021 release · https://trulocal.ca/10-year |\n\n**ESTIMATE, truLOCAL revenue today:** truLOCAL is \">50% of EMERGE 2025 YTD revenue\" and EMERGE's H1 2025 revenue was $13.5M. That puts truLOCAL at **roughly $13M+/yr**, below its $19.8M pandemic peak in 2020. Treat this as low confidence, because EMERGE does not disclose segment revenue.\n\n**ESTIMATE, lifetime orders:** a CLTV of ~$2,000 divided by an AOV of ~$240 gives **≈8 orders per customer lifetime**, if CLTV is revenue-based. That matters for J&W's model. A subscriber does **not** reliably buy 11–12 boxes a year, so churn has to be modelled.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Pricing (CAD, captured Oct 16, 2026)\n\n**Subscription tiers** (https://trulocal.ca/pricing/, https://trulocal.ca/faq):\n\n| Tier | Price | Servings | Points to build with | $/serving (my maths) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Starter | $155 | up to 36 | 9 | $4.31 |\n| Family (\"Most Popular\") | $279 | up to 80 | 20 | $3.49 |\n| Mega (\"Best Value\") | $349 | up to 104 | 26 | $3.36 |\n\n- Free shipping on every box with no minimum. Ships in 1–3 days and arrives in 2–3 days. Packaging is curb-side recyclable and stays frozen 24–36 h in transit (https://trulocal.ca/pricing/, https://trulocal.ca/faq).\n- **Extra Points** let a member go over the points limit. The price per Extra Point depends on box size and is not published (https://trulocal.ca/faq).\n- A **renewal reminder email goes out 48 h** before billing. Members can skip, pause indefinitely or cancel online, with no contract (https://trulocal.ca/faq).\n- **No à-la-carte items.** One-off purchases are pre-set boxes only (https://trulocal.ca/faq).\n- **Referral offer:** the new customer gets 15% off the first box (base price only), and the referrer gets 500 Rewards Points (https://trulocal.ca/faq).\n- Item-level prices are hidden. Product pages show no price, weight or point value, only an \"Order Now\" link to sign-up (https://trulocal.ca/products/01KKVH6TDAEEDHJBQECTC1TFNQ/new-tru-local-grass-fed-ribeye-steak).\n\n**One-time \"Curated Boxes\"** (no subscription, free delivery, and a complimentary \"$25 Promo Card\" badge): Backyard BBQ $170 · Classic Clean & Lean $185 · Chicken Lovers $185 · Grass-Fed Steak Lovers $200 · Essentials $210 · Ultra Clean & Lean $250 · Meat Me in the Grass $250 (https://trulocal.ca/curated-boxes/).\n\n**One-time \"Value Boxes\"** (sale price / struck-through price): Everyday Essentials $200/$251 · Chicken Breast & Ground Beef $200/$252 · Student $235/$279 · AAA Striploin $235/$279 · Chicken Breast $249/$279 · Lean Ground Beef $249/$349 · Classic Beef $270/$325 · AAA Top Sirloin $279/$349 · **Anti-Tariff $279/$349** · Grass-Fed Ribeye $299/$349 · Grass-Fed Steak $305/$375 (https://trulocal.ca/value-boxes/).\n\n- The Anti-Tariff box holds **14 boneless skinless chicken breasts + 7 lb lean ground beef + 6 lb lean ground chicken + 6 top sirloin steaks** (https://trulocal.ca/anti-tariff-giveaway/).\n- **ESTIMATE:** with breasts and steaks at 6–8 oz each, the box holds about 20.5–23 lb, which works out to **≈$12–14/lb at $279** and ≈$15–17/lb at $349.\n\n**Free Meat for Life** (https://trulocal.ca/free-life/, https://trulocal.ca/free-10yr/):\n- The new customer picks **one** free item for every box while the subscription stays active: 2 top sirloin steaks, 4 chicken breasts, 2 salmon fillets, or ground beef (2 packs on /free-life/, 1 pack on /free-10yr/).\n- Each choice has its own promo-coded entry link: LIFECHK, LIFEBEEF, LIFESTK, LIFESLM.\n- It is for new customers only and cannot be combined with other offers. The 10-year version is **capped at 120 boxes**.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Flows worth borrowing (step by step)\n\n**A. Subscription sign-up, 4 steps** (https://trulocal.ca/get-started/)\n1. **Email + postal code** (\"Find out if we serve your area\"). The email is captured before the customer sees a price, which builds the abandoned-cart list. A consent line says offers will follow.\n2. **Pick a box size:** Starter, Family or Mega, sized by servings and points.\n3. **\"Pick your Proteins\":** a points-budget builder. Each product costs points, and Extra Points cover going over budget. Items can roll over to the next order.\n4. **Checkout.** The free-for-life item is pre-applied when the customer arrives through a promo-coded link.\n\n**B. Subscription self-management** (https://trulocal.ca/faq)\n- My Account → Manage Subscription → skip / pause (any length) / change frequency / cancel.\n- A reminder email goes out 48 h before renewal. The box locks at billing.\n\n**C. Corporate gifting, 4 steps** (https://trulocal.ca/gifts/)\n1. **Choose the boxes:** curated, value, build-your-own, or gift cards of any amount.\n2. **Add a branded message.**\n3. **Upload the recipient list.**\n4. **truLOCAL packs and ships to each door,** from the next business day or on a scheduled date.\n\nSupporting details:\n- The quote form asks for quantity in bands: <10, 11–25, 26–50, 51–100, 100+.\n- There is no minimum. \"Tiered volume discounts\" are mentioned, but no percentages are published.\n- Contact is sales@ or a 1-800 number, with a 24-hour response promise.\n- Dietary and vegetarian swaps are available on request.\n\n**D. Converting one-time buyers.** Curated and value boxes sell with no subscription. Curated boxes carry a complimentary **$25 promo card**, which presumably works as a next-order or subscription hook (https://trulocal.ca/curated-boxes/). Value boxes show a struck-through price as an anchor.\n\n**E. Viral contest for lead generation** (https://trulocal.ca/anti-tariff-giveaway/)\n- Entry is a form plus a skill-testing question.\n- Bonus entries:\n  - +1 for sharing a referral link.\n  - **+5 for each friend** who enters through that link.\n  - +20 for a purchase.\n  - +20 for a 100-word essay on local meat, which also produces user-generated content.\n- The parent company owns the referral platform Viral Loops (https://www.emerge-commerce.com/).\n\n**F. Comparison table.** Three columns: truLOCAL vs Major Grocery Stores vs Other Meat Boxes. Six rows: Canadian-sourced, fast fulfilment, free shipping, customisable, easy pause/skip/cancel, flash-frozen and vacuum-sealed. Tapping a column opens an explanation sheet (https://trulocal.ca/).\n\n**G. Storytelling pages.** These include a 10-year timeline (first Instagram post → first box → Dragons' Den → 50K boxes → acquisition → 500K boxes) and \"Meet our LOCAL partners\" farm cards (https://trulocal.ca/10-year, https://trulocal.ca/).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Site design (enough for a designer to brief from; ideas only, do not copy)\n\n- **Stack and feel.** A custom (non-Shopify) web app with images served through imgix. The page is light and white, with generous whitespace and section bands alternating between white and a very pale green tint.\n- **Palette** (read from https://trulocal.ca/static/css/style.beebcab62128.css; frequency rank in brackets):\n\n| Role | Hex |\n|---|---|\n| Primary action green | `#44A847` (#1, CTA buttons) |\n| Hover/darker green | `#3D9740` |\n| Body text charcoal | `#353535` |\n| Near-black | `#1E1E1E` |\n| Pale tint section background | `#F6F9F6` |\n| Hairline border | `#DFE5DF` |\n| Sage accents | `#97CA98`, `#8C988C`, `#AFBCAF` |\n| Error/alert red | `#DD5151` |\n| Orange | `#EC9744` |\n| Info blue | `#56AEE5` |\n\n  The **brand box** itself is maple-leaf red on a very dark green/black panel, with a yellow sticky-note \"Proudly Canadian\" label (hero photo).\n- **Typography.** **Montserrat** (300–800) everywhere, with tight letter-spacing (-0.02em). Headings are heavy (700–800). Nunito 700 and Lora are loaded as secondary fonts, and D-DIN Condensed is used for small uppercase badges.\n- **Buttons.** Pill-shaped (radius 3rem), weight 800, about 56 px tall, solid green with white text. Cards use a radius of about 0.6rem.\n- **Layout (homepage):**\n  1. Region selector and nav: Get Started · Pricing · Our Products · Curated Boxes · Giveaway · Corporate Gifts.\n  2. Hero: the offer headline plus \"Claim Offer\" and a free-shipping note.\n  3. Four value pillars: Quality / Convenience / Affordability / Community.\n  4. Three-step \"How it works\".\n  5. Menu carousel of product cards. The cards show name and portion only, no price.\n  6. Comparison table.\n  7. Three customer-photo testimonials.\n  8. Satisfaction guarantee.\n  9. Farm-partner cards.\n  10. Closing CTA.\n  11. Newsletter.\n  12. Press logos (Forbes, Cision, Yahoo).\n  13. A floating \"Order Now / Free Shipping\" button.\n- **Photography.**\n  - Hero: an overhead flat-lay on dark rustic wood. Eight white plates of grilled proteins (chicken, short ribs, sausages, bacon, steaks) surround the branded box.\n  - How-it-works: lifestyle shots, such as a hand holding a phone showing the site.\n  - Testimonials: real customer unboxing photos of vacuum-sealed packs on kitchen counters, which read as authentic user-generated content.\n- **Tone.** Warm, patriotic and community-led (\"Buy Canadian\", \"I love local\", anti-tariff). The copy is plain and benefit-first, with heavy offer framing (free meat for life, free shipping, giveaways) and reassurance (pause anytime, no contracts, satisfaction guaranteed).\n\n**For a J&W prototype \"inspired by\":**\n- Keep the structure: offer hero → pillars → 3 steps → menu → comparison → reviews → partners/heritage → CTA.\n- Give J&W its **own** identity, for example deep butcher red/oxblood with cream and charcoal, plus a serif/condensed pairing that signals a \"since 1973 Toronto butcher\".\n- Swap farm cards for **\"Inside our Ontario-inspected plant\"** and **family-heritage** cards.\n- Use a flat-lay of J&W's **cooked Meal Butler portions next to raw steaks**.\n\n---\n\n## 6. What J&W already does better\n\n1. **It owns an Ontario-inspected plant (since 2008)** and cuts, portions, cooks and vacuum-seals in-house (jw-facts.md). truLOCAL aggregates 100+ suppliers, and its About page names no inspection body or own plant (https://trulocal.ca/about-us/).\n2. **Heritage.** The family business dates to 1973 (Advance Food Products) and J&W to 1995, versus truLOCAL's 2016 start. That supports a richer \"three generations of Toronto butchers\" story than a 10-year timeline.\n3. **Cooked, portioned, à-la-carte Meal Butler line,** with no added salt, sugar or oil, e.g. a 4 oz cooked chicken breast for $4.20–$4.99. truLOCAL sells **no à-la-carte items** (https://trulocal.ca/faq), and its prepared range is a handful of pizzas, pierogi, pot pies, BBQ chicken and cooked chicken bites (https://trulocal.ca/products).\n4. **Price per lb** (J&W catalog, jw-catalog.json):\n\n| Item | J&W price | $/lb |\n|---|---|---|\n| AAA ribeye, 10 × 11–12 oz | $175 | **$23.33–25.45/lb** |\n| AAA striploin case, 8 × 8–9 oz | $110 | $24.44–27.50/lb |\n| Portioned chicken breast case, ~4 kg | $85 | **≈$9.64/lb** |\n| Ground chicken breast | $9.99/lb | $9.99/lb |\n| Wagyu/Kobe AAA ground | $14.99/lb | $14.99/lb |\n\n   **ESTIMATE:** a J&W build of truLOCAL's Anti-Tariff box costs about **$225** at current retail. The truLOCAL box is $279 on sale and $349 regular. The J&W build:\n   - 14 × 6 oz breast from the case: ≈$51\n   - 7 lb ground beef at the 5 lb/$40 bulk rate: ≈$56\n   - 6 lb ground chicken: ≈$60\n   - 6 AAA top sirloin steaks, 7–8 oz at $10: $60\n\n   This is **not like-for-like**: truLOCAL leans grass-fed and organic, and J&W's comparison uses AAA cuts.\n5. **Premium range truLOCAL lacks:**\n   - Wagyu striploin, 8–9 oz, $35\n   - Canadian Prime ribeye\n   - Bison and venison\n   - Whole Ontario lamb ($400) and a quarter cow ($1,200)\n   - Whole striploin or ribeye **cut to the customer's chosen thickness**\n\n   truLOCAL sells fixed portions only.\n6. **Shorter, controlled GTA cold chain.** Scheduled routes run from Scarborough, with free store pickup and 1–2 day express delivery in core GTA. truLOCAL relies on couriers with a 24–36 h frozen window, and thawed-box complaints recur in its Trustpilot reviews (https://ca.trustpilot.com/review/trulocal.ca).\n7. **Social proof.** J&W has Google 4.8★ from 611 reviews (model-assumptions.md), versus truLOCAL's Trustpilot 3.2/5 from 68 reviews. These are different platforms, so treat the comparison as directional.\n8. **Bulk and case buying** for athletes, families and food-service (55 lb chicken, 4 kg cases), plus a physical butcher counter.\n\n**Where truLOCAL is stronger (be honest in the proposal):**\n- Grass-fed and organic named-farm positioning.\n- National shipping. J&W's provincially inspected meat **can only be sold within Ontario** (model-assumptions.md).\n- Free shipping with no minimum. J&W has a $100 minimum plus a $20–35 route fee.\n- Mature subscription and points tooling.\n- A parent company with referral software (Viral Loops) and M&A appetite. EMERGE says it is hunting tuck-in competitors and corporate-gifting businesses (Jan 21, 2026 release).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Checks on the \"J&W Butcher Club\" play\n\n- **Per-serving prices barely undercut truLOCAL (ESTIMATE).** The proposed Starter tier is $129 for ~30 servings = **$4.30/serving**, against truLOCAL's $4.31. Family is $229/70 = $3.27 (6% below truLOCAL's $3.49), and Freezer Fill is $299/95 = $3.15 (6% below $3.36). Add J&W's $20 route fee and J&W is at or above truLOCAL per serving at every tier: $4.97 / $3.56 / $3.36 against $4.31 / $3.49 / $3.36. Two options:\n  - **Option 1:** include free route delivery for members, and size tiers at truLOCAL's serving counts (36/80/104), which puts J&W at ~$3.58/$2.86/$2.88 per serving.\n  - **Option 2:** lead with **$/lb** and the AAA/Wagyu/custom-cut story instead of per-serving prices.\n\n  Check either option against the 30% raw-box margin before publishing.\n- **The \"0.6% of truLOCAL's base\" framing needs a caveat.** 70,100 is *cumulative customers since 2016*, not active subscribers, and the active count is not disclosed.\n- **Churn.** truLOCAL's own unit economics imply ≈8 lifetime orders per customer (CLTV ~$2,000 / AOV ~$240). Model 250–400 *active* members as a steady state that needs continuous acquisition. **ESTIMATE:** at ~8 monthly orders per lifetime, about 1.5× the base must be replaced each year (12 ÷ 8), which means 375–600 new members a year to hold 250–400 active. That costs ≈$38–105K/yr at truLOCAL's CAC of $100–175, or ≈$23–72K at the $60–120 GTA CAC in model-assumptions.md.\n- **The AOV benchmark supports the model.** truLOCAL's ~$240 AOV sits just above J&W's $159–229 average-box assumption, so that assumption is conservative.\n- **Wild Fork timing is confirmed and stronger than stated.** **Four** Ontario stores close on or before Dec 14, 2026: Whitby, Oakville, **Ancaster** and Newmarket. Online service ran until Oct 13, 2026 (https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2026/10/wild-fork-exits-canada-abandoning-ambitious-ontario-expansion-plans/). Ancaster is in Hamilton, on J&W's Thursday GTA 3 route.\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- https://trulocal.ca/ (homepage)\n- https://trulocal.ca/pricing/\n- https://trulocal.ca/faq\n- https://trulocal.ca/get-started/\n- https://trulocal.ca/how-it-works/\n- https://trulocal.ca/free-life/\n- https://trulocal.ca/free-10yr/\n- https://trulocal.ca/gifts/\n- https://trulocal.ca/10-year\n- https://trulocal.ca/curated-boxes/\n- https://trulocal.ca/value-boxes/\n- https://trulocal.ca/anti-tariff-giveaway/\n- https://trulocal.ca/products\n- https://trulocal.ca/products/01KKVH6TDAEEDHJBQECTC1TFNQ/new-tru-local-grass-fed-ribeye-steak\n- https://trulocal.ca/about-us/\n- https://trulocal.ca/our-suppliers?region=ON\n- https://trulocal.ca/blog/\n- https://trulocal.ca/static/css/style.beebcab62128.css (palette and type)\n- https://ca.trustpilot.com/review/trulocal.ca\n- https://www.emerge-commerce.com/ and https://www.emerge-commerce.com/news/News\n- https://www.emerge-commerce.com/news/News/news-details/2026/EMERGE-Provides-Corporate-Update-on-truLOCAL-as-the-Brand-Celebrates-its-10th-Anniversary\n- https://www.emerge-commerce.com/news/News/news-details/2026/EMERGE-Reports-Strong-Q2-2026-Results\n- https://www.emerge-commerce.com/news/News/news-details/2025/EMERGE-Brand-truLOCAL-Sees-a-193-Increase-in-Net-New-Subscriptions-in-February-2025-as-Support-Local-Movement-Grows\n- https://www.emerge-commerce.com/news/News/news-details/2021/EMERGE-Acquires-truLOCAL-the-Market-Leader-in-Direct-to-Consumer-Meat-Subscriptions-for-up-to-16.8M\n- https://www.emerge-commerce.com/assets/files/doc_financials/2026/q2/Emerge-MDA-2026-Final.pdf (contains the Q1 2026 MD&A)\n- https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2026/10/wild-fork-exits-canada-abandoning-ambitious-ontario-expansion-plans/\n- https://www.cbc.ca/1.5861576 (returned HTTP 403 and was not re-read; the deal value is confirmed from EMERGE's own release instead)\n- J&W: J&W catalogue and facts (jwfoods.com, crawled Oct 16, 2026) · https://www.jwfoods.com"}, {"key": "dive-04", "file": "competitor-04.md", "title": "Deep-dive: Competitor #4: WeCook (WeCook for Business), Montreal, QC", "blurb": "Verification, prices, flows worth borrowing and the design notes behind the prototype.", "md": "# Competitor #4: WeCook (WeCook for Business), Montreal, QC\n\nChecked October 16, 2026. All prices are in CAD. WeCook says its meals are not taxable (FAQ).\n\nMethod:\n- Direct fetches of wecookmeals.ca. The site is a Laravel + Vue single-page app, so most text loads by script. I read:\n  - the public JSON endpoints the site itself calls: `/api/plans`, `/api/menus`, `/api/menus/422/meals`, `/api/menus/422/meals/<slug>/details`, `/api/products`, `/api/snacks`, `/api/faqs/faq/search`, `/api/config/*`;\n  - the site's English string file `/js/i18n.js`, which holds all UI copy, including the logged-in B2B dashboard;\n  - `/css/app.css`, for colours and fonts.\n- The built-in browser, used to render /en, /en/business, /en/week-menu, /en/gift-cards, /en/about-us and /en/retail-products, take screenshots and read computed styles. I declined non-essential cookies.\n- Third-party pages: Trustpilot, Retail Insider (two articles) and Strategy.\n- Limitation: the shared web-search budget was used up before this pass started. I made no news searches for 2026 coverage, such as funding, ownership changes or layoffs. The operating status rests on WeCook's own live data, Trustpilot reviews dated October 2026 and 2025 trade press.\n\n## 1. Verdict: operating? premium?\n\n**Operating in October 2026: YES, and actively.**\n- The `/api/menus` endpoint lists weekly menus published through **Nov 8-14, 2026**. Menu #422, for the **week of Oct 18, 2026**, has 15 meals. The browser shows \"Week of October 18 2026\" on /en/week-menu.\n- The footer reads \"© 2026 WeCook. All rights reserved.\" The server's Date header was Fri, 16 Oct 2026.\n- Trustpilot lists **4.8, \"Excellent\", from 6,584 reviews, 672 of them in the last 12 months**. The five newest reviews run from Oct 6 to Oct 8, 2026, and all are 5-star. WeCook replies to 80% of negative reviews ([Trustpilot](https://www.trustpilot.com/review/wecookmeals.ca)).\n- WeCook is expanding. The About page and FAQ now say it delivers in \"Quebec, Ontario, the Maritimes, and **now Western Canada**\". The site's tax config includes BC, AB, SK and MB. A retail line (\"Find us in the fresh aisle\") is live, with one SKU enabled: the Grilled Chicken Burrito Bowl.\n\n**Premium: YES. This is premium mass-market, not luxury.**\n- The executive chef is Gabriel Drapeau (named on WeCook's retail page and in the PK Subban copy). Retail Insider says the menu is backed by \"an executive chef from Montreal's celebrated restaurant Joe Beef\" ([Retail Insider, Aug 26 2025](https://retail-insider.com/?p=177907)).\n- Meals are fresh and never frozen, with no preservatives (site copy). The menu has 15 new meals a week from a database of 500+ recipes (FAQ).\n- Brand partnerships:\n  - P.K. Subban, two collaborations ([Strategy, Aug 26 2025](https://strategyonline.ca/2025/08/26/wecook-pk-subban/)).\n  - A Schwartz's Deli pop-up in Toronto on Jul 30, 2025 ([Retail Insider, Jul 28 2025](https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2025/07/wecook-partners-with-schwartz-for-one-day-immersive-pop-up-in-toronto/)).\n  - A collaboration with Maripier Morin was announced next (Strategy).\n- Prices are mid-market: **$11.99-15.49 a meal** on consumer plans and about **$11.75** on the B2B platform. The positioning is \"premium and accessible\", in the CEO's words to Strategy.\n\n**The pick holds.** WeCook for Business is the clearest live Canadian example of an employer-subsidy meal platform. Section 7 lists the corrections to the selection notes.\n\n## 2. Scale signals\n\n| Signal | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Meals | 90,000 a week | [Retail Insider, Aug 2025](https://retail-insider.com/?p=177907) |\n| Meals (company claim) | \"+4 million meals delivered per year\" | [About us](https://www.wecookmeals.ca/en/about-us) |\n| Customers | \"+150,000 unique customers\" | About us |\n| Growth | \"demand has increased by 1000% since 2020\", \"600 new jobs\", \"two production facilities\" | About us |\n| Employees | \"nearly 400\" | Retail Insider, Aug 2025 |\n| Founded | 2012, as Nutrition Fit Plus | Retail Insider; site brand-change modal |\n| Ownership | Desjardins Capital, Investissement Québec, and Claridge Food Group as majority holder | Retail Insider, Aug 2025 |\n| CEO | Michel Gagné, President & CEO | Retail Insider; Strategy |\n| Revenue | Not found. ESTIMATE: 90,000 meals/wk × ~$13 × 52 ≈ **$60M/yr gross**, from the plan prices below | Derived |\n| Reviews | Trustpilot 4.8 from 6,584; 81% 5-star, 15% 4-star, 1% 1-star | [Trustpilot](https://www.trustpilot.com/review/wecookmeals.ca) |\n| Markets | QC, ON, Maritimes, Western Canada | FAQ \"Where do you deliver?\" |\n| Social proof on the B2B page | Logo wall: Fédération de natation du Québec, PlanetHoster, MEGA Pompage de béton, Advanced Racing and a sports-club crest. These are mostly small Quebec firms | /en/business (screenshot) |\n| Tech stack (page source) | Laravel + Vue SPA; Klaviyo; VWO A/B testing; Zendesk chat; Trustpilot widget; Nuvei payments with email OTP at checkout; CloudSponge contact import for referrals; Cloudflare Turnstile; GTM; CookieYes; iOS and Android apps | home HTML |\n\n## 3. Real prices (live from `/api/plans` and the menu/product APIs, Oct 16 2026)\n\n**Consumer subscription plans.** The price is per meal, and it falls as the plan grows:\n\n| Plan (meals/week) | Small (300-400 g) | Regular (440-550 g) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 6 meals | $14.49 | $15.49 |\n| 9 meals | $13.49 | $14.49 |\n| 14 meals | $12.99 | $13.99 |\n| 22-30 meals | $11.99 | $12.99 |\n\n- **Family size** (serves 4-5): $29.95. It counts toward the plan's meal total (FAQ).\n- **Premium meals** carry a surcharge. 4 of the 15 meals each week are premium. For the week of Oct 18: Smoked Pulled Pork **+$1.95**; Shrimp Tuscan Pasta, Maple-Soy Salmon Poke and Grilled Beef Steak Marsala **+$4.25** each.\n- **Minimum order:** 6 meals. Orders can be planned **up to 5 weeks ahead**, and weeks can be skipped.\n- **Delivery:** the FAQ contradicts itself. One entry says \"The delivery charge is $7.99\", with the first delivery free when a promo code is used. Two other entries answer \"Is delivery free?\" with \"Yes\". Home delivery runs 7am-9pm and office delivery 7am-5pm, in an insulated box with ice packs.\n- **Acquisition offer** on the homepage hero: \"**$100 total discount** on your first 4 orders\" (code PROMO100 in the page config). Other copy on the page offers \"$20 off your first order\".\n\n**Add-ons** (`/api/products`, `/api/snacks`):\n- 4 egg bites: $9.99, down from $11.99 (≈ $2.50 each).\n- 6 protein bites: $13.99.\n- 4 protein brownies or cookies: $14.95.\n- 4 × 300 mL juices or smoothies: $20.95.\n- Mox shake: $13.99.\n- Shaker: $9.99.\n\n**WeCook for Business:**\n- \"$11.75-ish when you order through our platform\". The CEO puts traditional catering at about $20-25 a meal.\n- The employer \"can decide to contribute $1 per meal, or 100%\".\n- The program fits companies of \"20 employees or 500 employees\". Larger firms get dedicated on-site fridges ([Retail Insider, Aug 2025](https://retail-insider.com/?p=177907)).\n- The dashboard says: \"*Meal prices are adjusted according to the total volume ordered by all users.\" So there is a company-wide volume tier (i18n `meal-b2b-section.price-per-meal-note`).\n\n**Gift cards:**\n- Amounts: $25, $50, $100, $200, or a custom amount (minimum $10).\n- **A 10% bonus is live today**: \"You have an extra of $5.00!\" on $50.\n- Gift cards can be paid with WeCoins.\n\n**Loyalty and referral:**\n- WeCoins levels: Bronze 0-29,999, Silver 30,000-59,999, Gold 60,000+.\n- A $20 credit costs 50,000 WeCoins.\n- Referral reward: \"Refer 6 friends = 1 year of FREE snacks.\"\n\n**Nutrition sample.** Steak-Spiced Turkey Protein Power Bowl (`/details` API):\n\n| Size | Calories | Protein | Sodium |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Small | 420 | 33 g | **795 mg** |\n| Regular | 570 | 47 g | **1,015 mg** |\n\nThe ingredients include canola oil and salt.\n\n## 4. Flows worth borrowing (step by step)\n\n### A. B2B signup and employer onboarding (the core of \"J&W at Work\")\n1. Landing page /en/business. The hero shows the Trustpilot strip, then the H1 \"The ready-to-eat meal perk your team will actually use\", then a lead form placed **above the fold**.\n2. Form fields:\n   - First name, Last name, Email, Company name, Phone.\n   - **\"For who\"** dropdown: Business / Gym / Sports team / Retail / Other / For me. Choosing \"For me\" redirects the visitor to consumer plans.\n   - Number of employees.\n   - Notes (200 characters maximum).\n   - A checkbox: \"I am already a WeCook client\".\n3. The CTA reads \"Talk to our team\". On success the page says: \"Your business account request has been delivered… we will communicate with you over the next business days\". This is sales-assisted, not self-serve.\n4. Sales approves the account. The admin then gets an email: \"Here is your temporary password to login to your WeCook business portal.\"\n5. On first login a **guided tour** runs: \"Here is your Business Program\", then \"Here you can invite employees\", then \"Manage invitations\".\n6. **The allowance engine.** It sets a default allowance for all users, with per-employee overrides. The types are:\n   - $X per employee, on all items;\n   - $X per employee, on meals only;\n   - N meals per employee;\n   - $X per meal, up to a cap.\n\n   The caps are max meals per employee, max meals paid by the company, and max per order.\n7. **Invite employees** by email. The employee's address must match the company's email domain (\"The email address entered does not belong to the company's domain\"). The admin sees Pending and Accepted lists, can resend an invite after 24h, and can delete a user, which removes that user's future selections.\n8. Each employee picks meals weekly on the platform. Personal and corporate accounts are kept separate, and an account-migration flow handles existing consumers.\n9. **The joint weekly order.**\n   - The admin sees the upcoming delivery dates, a meal overview of everything the team picked, and a \"**Refresh order**\" button for live changes.\n   - If nobody orders that week, the delivery \"will automatically be cancelled — simple, no admin burden.\"\n10. The **receipt and invoice** split the order into \"Total covered by the company\" and \"Total paid by the employee(s)\". Delivery goes to the office, 7am-5pm.\n11. Limitation: business accounts **cannot use the mobile app** yet (FAQ).\n\n### B. Consumer subscription (from the FAQ \"How does it work\" plus the live UI)\n1. Email capture in the hero, tied to the $100-off offer.\n2. Choose a delivery date and location: postal code, home or office.\n3. Choose a portion size (Small or Regular), then a plan (6, 9, 14 or 22-30 meals).\n4. Split the plan's count between individual and family-size meals.\n5. Pick from 15 meals and **customize the side**: rice & veg, potatoes & veg, quinoa & veg, or veg only.\n6. Set **preferences**: ingredients to exclude, and a preferred side style. WeCook uses these to auto-select meals.\n7. Enter delivery details, then pay by Visa, MC, Amex, Apple Pay or Google Pay, with an email OTP.\n8. Manage the account under My Deliveries:\n   - Plan 5 weeks ahead and skip weeks.\n   - Turn on \"**Premium Feature**\", which lets auto-selection include surcharge meals and earns WeCoins.\n   - Add snacks or oats on a recurring frequency, such as weekly or biweekly.\n   - Cancelling triggers a retention flow (`/api/unsubscribe/retention`).\n\n### C. Gift card\n1. Choose an amount (presets or a custom amount of $10+). A 10% bonus is shown.\n2. Personalize: recipient name and email, sender, and a message.\n3. **Choose the date the email is sent**, or choose \"Print yourself\".\n4. Optionally \"Send the bonus gift card to a different person\".\n5. Pay by credit card **or with WeCoins**. The recipient redeems the card at /activate-gift-cards.\n\n### D. Gamified loyalty\n- Members earn WeCoins on orders, on badges (first breakfast order, first sous-vide order and others) and on social challenges (FB, IG and TikTok endpoints).\n- Levels give perks.\n- The WeCoins Market redeems coins for credit, freebies (egg bites, shaker) or gift cards.\n- Coins are lost on unsubscribe, which acts as a churn brake.\n\n## 5. Site design (for a designer's brief; borrow ideas only, never copy content)\n\n**Layout**\n- **Header:** sticky and white, at about 56 px. On the left, a lowercase green wordmark; the B2B pages add a small \"for business\" sub-label. On the right, an FR/EN toggle, a solid green CTA (\"Try WeCook\" or \"Log In\") and a hamburger menu.\n- **Consumer hero:** split. A white rounded card on the left holds:\n  - the H1;\n  - the subhead;\n  - a **pale-yellow promo pill**;\n  - the email field and CTA;\n  - a \"Proudly Canadian\" maple-leaf stamp in the corner.\n\n  On the right is a full-bleed photo of green branded boxes and clear meal trays on marble.\n- **B2B hero:** 50/50. On the left, a lifestyle photo of office workers eating from trays at a table. On the right, a Trustpilot strip, a large green H1 and the lead form, which works as the hero itself.\n- **Section order (B2B):**\n  1. An auto-scrolling **benefit marquee**: \"More affordable than takeout\", \"Fridge stocked, zero hassle\"…\n  2. \"Set it up once\" as **3 numbered step cards**, each with a product/UI photo.\n  3. \"Perks that finally make sense\", an asymmetric photo pair with copy.\n  4. \"**Myth-Busters**\", a 2×2 photo grid with green objection-handling headlines.\n  5. A logo band on cream.\n  6. A closing form block, \"Culture is built in moments, not meetings\", with a Trustpilot badge and a photo.\n  7. A 5-column footer.\n- **Menu page:** category tabs with round icons on pale green (Ready-to-eat, Family, Snacks, Beverages, Breakfasts), a week picker, and a 4-column card grid. Each card has:\n  - a square photo;\n  - a **green circular protein sticker** (\"52 g protein\");\n  - a \"Family size available\" outline pill;\n  - a dark-green \"Premium meals\" band on surcharge dishes.\n\n**Palette** (from app.css frequency counts and computed styles)\n\n| Role | Hex |\n|---|---|\n| Primary green | **#03894E / #00894E** |\n| Darker greens (hover, bands) | #026B3D, #027040, #025732 |\n| Text charcoal | **#232522**; headings in grey #393C3F |\n| Mid grey | #979797 |\n| Cream bands | **#FBF8EA**, #F9F4EF, #FBFAF6 |\n| Promo yellow | #FDCF32 / #FAB418 |\n| Family-size orange | #FF9D1E / #F37601 |\n| Error red | #F44336 |\n| Base | white |\n\n**Typography**\n- Headings: **Rubik 700**. H1 48/57.6 px, H2 32 px, H3 24 px, set in green.\n- Body: **museo-sans** (Adobe Fonts) at 16/24.\n- Accents: **Fierce Vector**, a heavy condensed display face, is used on the retail page (\"Ready to eat. Reinvented.\"). **Permanent Marker** is used as a handwritten accent.\n- Buttons: 8 px radius, white on green, 14-16 px bold.\n- Inputs: 44 px tall, 8 px radius.\n\n**Photography**\n- Bright, natural-light food shot top-down on linen, stone or marble, in clear plastic trays or ceramic bowls, with chunky visible ingredients.\n- Lifestyle images show diverse people eating at work or at a family table.\n- The retail line uses colour-blocked studio backgrounds (pink, mustard) with close-ups of a person eating.\n\n**Tone**\n- Friendly, punchy and benefit-first, with light humour: \"Heat, eat, repeat\", \"WeCook, so you don't have to!\", \"Your office fridge says more about your company than your About Us page\".\n- Bilingual FR/EN.\n- B2B copy speaks to HR and culture outcomes (productivity ROI, retention, \"cheaper than churn\") rather than to food.\n\n## 6. What J&W already does better\n\n- **A real butcher with an Ontario-inspected plant.** J&W has been provincially inspected since 2008, with family butcher roots going back to George Trougakos in the 1960s, Advance Food Products in 1973 and J&W in 1995. WeCook is a ready-meal kitchen. Its raw-meat landing page (\"Our meats from Quebec\") and its vacuum-sealed DTC line appear wound down: the site carries the string \"We no longer offer vacuum-sealed foods\", and the sealed-menu API returns placeholder $1.00 prices. Its retail page now pushes sous-vide meals instead. J&W can sell an office the meal **and** the steaks for the team BBQ or holiday boxes.\n- **À la carte, macro-exact portions.** J&W's cooked chicken breast is 4 oz for $4.20-4.99, salmon 6 oz for $11.00, egg bites $4.25-5.50 per 2-pack, and pre-set meals $8.99-14.99 (4 oz or 6 oz protein). WeCook sells fixed dishes in two sizes with a 6-meal minimum. A trainer or athlete can build exact macros only at J&W.\n- **Salt-free, sugar-free, no added oil.** J&W states \"All products are salt and sugar free, with no added oils\". WeCook's protein bowl carries **795-1,015 mg sodium** and contains canola oil. That gives J&W a clean pitch to hospitals, clinics and wellness-driven HR teams.\n- **A frozen, vacuum-sealed format.** It suits an unattended office freezer with no weekly expiry pressure and less waste. WeCook's fresh trays must be eaten by the use-by date, and WeCook has to supply fridges.\n- **Price parity or better on comparable items.** J&W pre-set meals at $9.99-12.99 sit at or below WeCook's $11.99-15.49 plan prices, with no plan minimum. Egg bites are about equal per piece: J&W about $2.13-2.75, WeCook about $2.50.\n- **GTA home turf.** J&W has its Scarborough plant and storefront (free pickup) and its own weekly GTA routes: GTA 2 on Wednesday covers Toronto, Scarborough and North York; GTA 4 on Friday covers Markham, Vaughan and Richmond Hill. WeCook ships from Quebec through third-party couriers. It is not a local brand, and its B2B logos are Quebec firms.\n- **Breadth.** J&W also carries raw cuts, Wagyu, whole and half animals, Greek specialties, supplements and snacks, which make it a one-stop shop for gym-goers and families.\n\n**Where WeCook is clearly ahead** (gaps J&W must close):\n- a working employer dashboard and allowance engine;\n- tiered plans and subscriptions;\n- 6.5K reviews of social proof;\n- an app;\n- gamified loyalty;\n- the polish of a chef-and-celebrity brand.\n\n## 7. Checks and corrections on the selection notes\n\n- **\"Verified Oct 16 2026\" (Retail Insider):** the page was re-fetched today, but the **article is dated Aug 26, 2025**. The figures of 90K meals a week, ~400 staff, $11.75 a meal, $1-100% subsidy and 20-500 employees are therefore **14 months old**. The B2B page and dashboard copy are still live, which supports that the program is current.\n- **Scope:** \"Ontario incl. GTA, Quebec, New Brunswick, Halifax\" is out of date. WeCook now says \"Quebec, Ontario, the Maritimes, and Western Canada\".\n- **Joe Beef:** this rests on Retail Insider's wording. WeCook's own site names the executive chef (Gabriel Drapeau) but does not mention Joe Beef.\n- **Schwartz's Toronto pop-up:** confirmed. It ran Jul 30, 2025, 4-8pm, at Avenue Diner, 222 Davenport Rd, with $5 smoked-meat dishes.\n- **PK Subban:** confirmed. The Strategy article covers the **second** collaboration, P.K.'s Spaghetti and Meatballs, on the menu from Sep 7 to Oct 4, 2025.\n- **Consumer price:** competitors-gta-mealprep.md said \"official price list not found, $10-13 anecdotal\". The live API now gives **$11.99-15.49 a meal** (table above) and **$29.95 for family size**.\n- **\"Fresh, never frozen\":** true at delivery. The FAQ does let customers freeze meals.\n- **The J&W play's staff price of ~$11-13 is consistent** with WeCook's ~$11.75 B2B rate and $11.99-12.99 high-volume consumer tiers. J&W should not price above about $13 for a pre-set meal at work.\n\n## 8. Implications for \"J&W at Work\"\n\n- Copy the mechanics, not the look:\n  - the allowance types and caps;\n  - domain-locked invites;\n  - the weekly joint order that auto-cancels;\n  - the live order roll-up;\n  - the company/employee split invoice;\n  - the volume-tiered price.\n\n  The MVP can run on Shopify B2B (company accounts, catalogs and payment terms) plus a discount-code or wallet layer for the subsidy. Native B2B needs Shopify Plus; check J&W's plan before committing.\n- Lead with what WeCook cannot claim:\n  - \"Ontario-inspected butcher, 30 years in Scarborough\";\n  - \"salt-free, sugar-free, no added oil\";\n  - \"freezer-stocked, no weekly waste\";\n  - \"macro-exact portions\".\n- Sell first to the healthcare, logistics and manufacturing sites around Brimley Rd. WeCook's B2B social proof there is thin.\n- Add the B2B lead form's \"For who\" segmentation (Business / Gym / Team / Retail). It routes leads to plays #2 and #4 from one page.\n\n## Sources\n\n- WeCook home (EN): https://www.wecookmeals.ca/en\n- WeCook for Business: https://www.wecookmeals.ca/en/business\n- Weekly menu (week of Oct 18 2026): https://www.wecookmeals.ca/en/week-menu\n- Plans API: https://www.wecookmeals.ca/api/plans\n- Menus API: https://www.wecookmeals.ca/api/menus and https://www.wecookmeals.ca/api/menus/422/meals\n- Meal detail API (nutrition): WeCook meal detail API (page no longer available)\n- Products and snacks APIs: https://www.wecookmeals.ca/api/products · https://www.wecookmeals.ca/api/snacks\n- FAQ API: https://www.wecookmeals.ca/api/faqs/faq/search\n- UI string file (B2B dashboard, gift card, loyalty copy): https://www.wecookmeals.ca/js/i18n.js\n- Stylesheet (palette, fonts): https://www.wecookmeals.ca/css/app.css\n- About us: https://www.wecookmeals.ca/en/about-us\n- Gift cards: https://www.wecookmeals.ca/en/gift-cards\n- Retail products: https://www.wecookmeals.ca/en/retail-products\n- Trustpilot: https://www.trustpilot.com/review/wecookmeals.ca\n- Retail Insider, \"WeCook launches 'WeCook for Business'…\" (Aug 26 2025): https://retail-insider.com/?p=177907\n- Retail Insider, Schwartz's Toronto pop-up (Jul 28 2025): https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2025/07/wecook-partners-with-schwartz-for-one-day-immersive-pop-up-in-toronto/\n- Strategy, \"WeCook hopes pop-ups and partnerships score with customers\" (Aug 26 2025): https://strategyonline.ca/2025/08/26/wecook-pk-subban/\n- J&W facts: J&W catalogue and facts (jwfoods.com, crawled Oct 16, 2026)"}, {"key": "dive-05", "file": "competitor-05.md", "title": "Deep-dive: Competitor #5: Omaha Steaks (Business Gifts), the global B2B gifting benchmark", "blurb": "Verification, prices, flows worth borrowing and the design notes behind the prototype.", "md": "# Competitor #5: Omaha Steaks (Business Gifts), the global B2B gifting benchmark\n\nPrepared for Minastech (Erwin Garcia) for the J&W Foods / J&W Meal Butler proposal. Research date: **October 16, 2026.**\nMethod: each page was opened in a real browser, because omahasteaks.com returns **HTTP 403 (Akamai bot block) to WebFetch and curl**. The pages themselves are live. business.omahasteaks.com pages were read with both WebFetch and the browser. The web-search budget for this run was used up, so no third-party news or revenue sources were added. Every figure below comes from Omaha Steaks' own pages, J&W's catalog, or the Canadian government.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Verdict: operating and premium (verifiedOperating = true)\n\n- **Live in October 2026.** The footer reads \"© 2026 OmahaSteaks.com LLC … v26.10.7\", a build stamped October 2026. The nav shows \"Christmas Gifts\" and a \"Semi-Annual Sale\" running promo code SEMI25 ($25 off $149+) ([business gifts](https://www.omahasteaks.com/business-gifts), [home](https://www.omahasteaks.com/)).\n- **Recent news:**\n  - **Sept 9–10, 2026:** a \"No Bull\" brand platform and business transformation. Stores now double as local fulfilment hubs. Average delivery time fell from \"more than six days in early 2025 to two days today\" ([press release](https://www.omahasteaks.com/press/press-releases/omaha-steaks-unveils-business-transformation-and-new-no-bull-brand-platform-doubling-down-on-value-and-transparency)).\n  - **Sept 22, 2026:** a new Grapevine, TX store, with free same-day or next-day delivery within 75 miles ([press release](https://www.omahasteaks.com/press/press-releases/omaha-steaks-to-celebrate-grand-opening-of-new-grapevine-retail-store-with-community-open-house-event)).\n  - Earlier store openings in Chandler and Ballwin, and a Nebraska Athletics partnership on its X feed ([Media Room](https://www.omahasteaks.com/press/)).\n- **Premium credentials:** founded 1917 and run today by the fifth-generation Simon family. Todd Simon is Chairman and Nate Rempe is President & CEO. It calls itself \"America's Original Butcher\" and \"the nation's largest direct response marketer of premium beef and gourmet foods\". It runs two processing plants and two distribution centres in Omaha, NE ([Century of Steak](https://www.omahasteaks.com/info/Century-of-Steak); [press release](https://www.omahasteaks.com/press/press-releases/omaha-steaks-unveils-business-transformation-and-new-no-bull-brand-platform-doubling-down-on-value-and-transparency)). It claims beef aged \"35+ days\" and \"900+ years combined\" master-butcher experience ([large business gifts](https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/large-business-gifts)).\n- **Critical caveat for J&W:** \"**Omaha Steaks does not ship to Canada.**\" It ships only to the contiguous US, Puerto Rico and the US Virgin Islands ([Shipping](https://www.omahasteaks.com/info/Shipping-Information)). Omaha is therefore **a model to learn from, not a rival in the GTA.** GTA companies that want a premium \"Choice\" meat gift with local delivery have no Omaha-scale option today, and J&W can take that space.\n\n## 2. Scale signals\n\n| Signal | Figure | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Business clients since 2020 | **5,000+** (company claim, \"Omaha Steaks internal data in 2025\") | [business-gifts](https://www.omahasteaks.com/business-gifts) (verified live in browser, Oct 16, 2026) |\n| Custom gift certificates issued for corporate clients since 2020 | **350k+** | same |\n| Coolers delivered to business gift recipients since 2020 | **700k+** | same |\n| Gift survey (Harris Poll for Omaha, Apr 2023, n=2,004) | 89% say a gift increases happiness at work; 68% prefer quality food over merch/branded items | same |\n| Retail stores | \"Nearly 50 store locations nationwide\". The store finder listed 44 | [Century of Steak](https://www.omahasteaks.com/info/Century-of-Steak); [Stores](https://www.omahasteaks.com/info/Stores) |\n| Reviews | Steakhouse Grill Gift 4.5★ (686 reviews); Filet Mignon Burgers 4.8★ (229) | [product page](https://www.omahasteaks.com/product/Steakhouse-Grill-Gift-81977) |\n| Delivery speed | Average fell from 6+ days (early 2025) to 2 days (Sept 2026); same-day ship if ordered by 6pm ET | [press](https://www.omahasteaks.com/press/press-releases/omaha-steaks-unveils-business-transformation-and-new-no-bull-brand-platform-doubling-down-on-value-and-transparency); [Shipping](https://www.omahasteaks.com/info/Shipping-Information) |\n| Revenue / funding | **Not found.** Search budget was used up, and the site publishes none. It is private and family-owned | n/a |\n\nESTIMATE (labelled): Omaha's own figures imply about **1,000+ business clients a year** (5,000 over about 5 years). They also imply about **2 coolers or certificates per client per year at roughly 100+ gifts each**, so each client buys around 200 gifts. The arithmetic: (350k + 700k) / 5,000 clients ≈ 210 gifts per client over the period. This is a rough split. Omaha does not publish client counts per year.\n\n## 3. Pricing (USD, captured Oct 16, 2026)\n\n**Featured business gift boxes** on [business-gifts](https://www.omahasteaks.com/business-gifts):\n- Celebration Grill Gift: **$81**, 14 items (2 × 10 oz bone-in NY strips, 2 × 6 oz pork chops, 2 × 5 oz chicken breasts, 4 × 4 oz burgers, 4 franks).\n- Steakhouse Grill Gift: **$148**, 16 items (2 × 5 oz filets, 2 × 5 oz top sirloin filets, 2 × 1 lb sirloin tips, 4 chicken breasts, 4 × 6 oz filet-mignon burgers, 1 lb bacon, seasoning).\n- Tasteful Business Gift: **$211**, 22 items. Ribeye Reserve Collection: **$212**, 22 items (4 × 12 oz ribeyes plus more). Both ship free.\n\n**2026 Holiday Gift Guide** (\"Popular Business Food Gifts From $100–$200\", all \"Ships FREE!\") on [how-to-order](https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/how-to-order-corporate-gifts):\n- Holiday Steak Dinner Gift **$100**: 4 marinated top sirloins, 4 scalloped potatoes, 4 tartlets, seasoning.\n- Holiday Entertainer Collection **$150**.\n- Surf & Turf Celebration Gift **$175**: 4 filets plus 4 lobster-tail skewers.\n- Steak Lovers Holiday Collection **$180**.\n\n**Choice Gift Certificate levels:** prices are **not published**. Buyers are told to \"Ask About Price Levels\" through a quote form ([certificates](https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/corporate-gift-certificates)). The rewards page gives two indirect clues: a \"Platinum Certificate\" worth **$200** and \"2 Diamond Certificates\" worth **$500**, so about $250 each ([Business Rewards](https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/business-rewards)).\n\n**Volume discounts:** no percentages or price bands are published. The site says only \"Volume discounts may be available for qualifying orders\" ([large gifts](https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/large-business-gifts)). The lead form's quantity buttons (<10, 10–50, 50–100, 100–200, 200–500, 500+) are **lead-qualification buckets, not price tiers.**\n\n**Spend-per-gift buckets on the lead form:** Under $100 / $100–$150 / $150–$250 / Over $250. These show where the core of the market sits, roughly $100–$250 per recipient.\n\n**Business Rewards (annual spend tiers)** on [business-rewards](https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/business-rewards):\n- Qualified Business Member: free; business-only offers, volume discounts, Choice certificates, free gift messaging.\n- **Premier, $5,000+/yr:** *free logo placement on qualifying shipments*, plus a Platinum Certificate ($200 value).\n- **President's Club, $20,000+/yr:** 2 Platinum certificates ($400), a gift box ($100), and 10% off the buyer's personal shopping.\n- **President's Club Elite, $50,000+/yr:** 2 Diamond certificates ($500) and ten $100 gift cards ($1,000), which can be passed on to employees or clients.\n- The page itself calls these \"current placeholder values\".\n\n**Shipping (consumer):** $24.99 under $149 and FREE at $149+. Alaska/Hawaii add $109. One charge per delivery address ([Shipping](https://www.omahasteaks.com/info/Shipping-Information)). **Subscriptions:** \"Subscribe Today Save 10%\" on items such as burgers ([product page](https://www.omahasteaks.com/product/Steakhouse-Grill-Gift-81977)). **Stores:** $10 off $50+ in store ([Stores](https://www.omahasteaks.com/info/Stores)).\n\n## 4. Flows worth borrowing, step by step\n\n**A. Landing-page lead capture (pops up on arrival)** ([business-gifts](https://www.omahasteaks.com/business-gifts))\n1. A \"Business Gift Offers\" modal opens, promising \"exclusive volume discounts, branded gift selections and easy ordering from our Business Gift Experts\".\n2. Fields: First, Last, Business Name, Work Email, Business Phone, Comments.\n3. Two pill-button questions qualify the lead in one tap: gift count (<10 … 500+) and spend per gift (<$100 … >$250).\n4. A Business Gift Expert follows up. Sales gets a pre-sorted lead without the buyer typing a budget.\n\n**B. Business-gifts landing structure** (a useful wireframe)\n1. Hero: \"Corporate Gifts Made Easy\" with a single \"Get Volume Pricing\" CTA.\n2. Proof band of four big numbers (100+ yrs, 5,000+, 350k+, 700k+).\n3. Three program cards: Employee Gifts, Client Gifts, Marketing Promotions.\n4. \"Built around your program\": branding, curation, choice, large programs.\n5. Survey stats: 89% and 68%.\n6. Business Rewards teaser, then a free Gift Guide download.\n7. Case study in Challenge, Solution, Result format with a quote.\n8. Insights articles, then \"Get Expert Help\" (volume, branding, flexible payment, support).\n9. Four shoppable boxes, then resource cards (Large, How to Order, Certificates, Holiday).\n10. Sticky action row: Call, Order Form, Quote, Gift Guide.\n\n**C. Choice Gift Certificate, six steps** ([certificates](https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/corporate-gift-certificates))\n1. **Choose:** the certificate level, quantity, timing and payment structure.\n2. **Customize:** logo, gift message, recipient name, campaign title or occasion wording. Logos are available only \"for qualifying custom programs\".\n3. **Distribute:** mail to each recipient, ship in bulk to the office, or hand out at events. The quote form offers Printed (Bulk), Printed (Mailed), E-Certificate or Not Sure.\n4. **Redeem:** online, by phone or by mail using the unique code.\n5. **Choose:** the recipient picks from the curated collections at that level.\n6. **Enjoy:** the gift is delivered, with standard shipping included.\n- **Pay-on-redemption:** qualifying large programs pay only for certificates actually used.\n- Use-case tiles: employee recognition, client appreciation, sales incentives/SPIFs, **safety awards**, loyalty and promotions (casino and dealer programs), events and golf outings.\n\n**D. Multi-recipient ordering, four steps** ([how-to-order](https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/how-to-order-corporate-gifts); [Large Orders](https://www.omahasteaks.com/info/Large-Gift-Orders))\n1. Describe the program: audience, count, budget, occasion and timing.\n2. Choose gifts, or have an expert curate a custom box.\n3. Fill in **one Business Gift Order Form**, an **Excel .xlsx** file hosted on Pardot. It holds names, addresses, gift choice and a message for each recipient. Email it to largeorders@omahasteaks.com, fax it, or call 1-800-858-5335 (Mon–Fri 7am–10pm, Sat 8–7, Sun 9–5). Card details are taken by phone, never by email.\n4. A dedicated expert coordinates shipments. The buyer pays with **a single invoice or one card charge**.\n- With no recipient addresses, the buyer switches to Choice certificates and recipients enter their own address when they redeem.\n- Repeat-program case study: the client \"sent its updated list to its Account Manager\" the following year. Year-over-year retention is the business model.\n\n**E. Holiday planning calendar** ([holiday business gifts](https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/holiday-business-gifts))\n- July: set goals, audience and budget. August: review options. September: pick gifts and gather recipients. October: finalize branding and quantities. November: schedule early deliveries. December: use certificates for late needs.\n- J&W should be at the October step right now.\n\n**F. \"Customize Your Box\" points builder** ([builder](https://www.omahasteaks.com/servlet/OnlineShopping?Dsp=8500&sku=81977))\n1. Start from a preset gift such as the Steakhouse Grill Gift at $148.\n2. Each item carries a point value: chicken breasts 1 pt, NY strips 2–6 pts, Private Reserve filets 12 pts. The box needs **at least 5 points with no maximum**.\n3. Category tabs: Steaks / Burgers & Franks / Chicken & Pork / Seafood / Meals, Sides & Sweets.\n4. A sticky footer shows \"Reset Box · 5 Points Used · $148 · Continue →\", under a two-step progress bar (Home, then Customize).\n- This maps directly onto a J&W Meal Butler week or a J&W Choice certificate, where points equal the certificate value.\n\n**G. AI and service layer**\n- A right-edge \"Chat\" tab runs a **Simple AI (usesimple.ai) assistant with voice capability**, welcoming visitors with \"How can we help you today?\". The iframe was observed on the page.\n- A live phone line runs 24/7 (1-800-960-8400). The business desk is 1-800-228-2480.\n\n## 5. Site design brief (for a J&W prototype inspired by it; ideas only, no content copied)\n\n- **Layout:**\n  - Full-bleed dark photo hero, with a left-aligned headline and a single white pill CTA.\n  - A thin burgundy promo strip sits under a white header, which has a centred crest logo, \"Shop All\" and a horizontally scrolling category nav.\n  - Sections are centred h2s between hairline rules. The stat band uses four columns of big numerals.\n  - Program cards are three white tiles with line icons. Feature rows pair an image on the left with a four-item stacked list on the right.\n  - Business pages put a \"… at a Glance\" summary card at top right of the hero, a check-mark benefits row, numbered step cards, tier cards (\"START HERE / PREMIER / ENHANCED / HIGHEST\"), an FAQ accordion and an inline lead form.\n- **Palette (read from computed CSS):**\n  - Text near-black **#231F20** (#1F1F1F on the business subdomain), on white **#FFFFFF**.\n  - Deep burgundy **#890419** for the promo strip and primary pill buttons. Logo red **#CC2127**. Link burgundy **#862732**.\n  - Warm greys **#CDC7C2** and **#F5F3F1** (warm off-white panels). Cool greys **#D1D3D4**, **#E9E9E9** and **#F6F6F6**. Mid grey **#5D5D5D**. Cream **#FAF7F2**.\n  - Accents used sparingly: teal #00797F, chat blue #165077.\n- **Typography:**\n  - Main site: H1 in **neue-aachen-pro**, a bold condensed slab-serif display (52px, white on photo). H2/H3 and body in **neue-haas-grotesk-display**, a neutral grotesk (32 / 24 / 16 px, weights 600 / 450).\n  - Business subdomain: plain **Arial Bold** (H1 51px, -1.28px tracking) with fully rounded pill buttons (radius 999px). The two sites look inconsistent.\n- **Photography:**\n  - Moody, low-key, close-up photos of hands holding the branded red box, and seared steaks in cast iron with steam and herbs. Warm tungsten tones and a shallow depth of field.\n  - A doorstep shot of the box on brick steps sells \"it arrives at the door\".\n- **Tone of voice:** confident and service-led (\"Corporate Gifts Made Easy\", \"We help handle the rest\"). It quantifies everything, builds trust through heritage, and since Sept 2026 adds the self-aware \"No Bull\" line.\n- **For J&W:**\n  - Keep the dark hero, the big-number proof band, the \"at a Glance\" card and the tier cards.\n  - Swap the burgundy for J&W's own brand colour, and use Mediterranean warmth (olive and terracotta) as accents.\n  - Swap the American heritage story for the J&W family story: George Trougakos's downtown butcher shop, Advance Food Products from 1973, J&W from 1995, an Ontario-inspected plant since 2008.\n\n## 6. Features to borrow (ranked for J&W's Q4 2026)\n\n1. **J&W Choice e-certificate:** one code per recipient, redeemable for a box, a Meal Butler week or a Butcher Club month, with delivery booked on J&W's own route day. This solves the perishable-delivery and unknown-address problems. Under CRA rules it stays \"non-cash\" only if it can be used at a single retailer and cannot be turned into cash. The [CRA page](https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/businesses/topics/payroll/benefits-allowances/gifts-awards-social-events/gifts-awards-long-service-awards.html), modified 2026-07-27, confirms the $500/yr non-cash limit including taxes and these card conditions.\n2. **Pay-on-redemption** for larger programs: a strong close, and the cost to J&W is low because unredeemed codes are never produced.\n3. **Two-tap lead qualifier** (gift count, then spend per gift) on a business landing page, with an AI or concierge follow-up.\n4. **Points-based \"Customize Your Box\"** builder with a sticky footer showing points, price and Continue.\n5. **Annual-spend rewards tiers.** Free logo placement at C$5K+, a personal-use certificate at C$5K+, and more at C$20K+, with Canadian thresholds to be set by J&W. This locks in repeat programs.\n6. **Recipient-list ordering:** one spreadsheet, one invoice. J&W can go a step further with an in-browser CSV upload and a live preview, where Omaha still emails an Excel file.\n7. **Holiday planning calendar plus \"Plan your holiday program\" CTA**, and year-round occasions (safety milestones, realtor closings, client thank-yous, Greek Easter).\n8. **Proof band and case study** (Challenge, Solution, Result) once J&W has its first 3–5 corporate clients.\n9. **Stores as fulfilment hubs.** Omaha cut delivery from 6+ days to 2 by shipping from stores. J&W's Brimley Rd plant already sits inside its delivery radius, so the claim is \"GTA delivery on your route day, or pickup today\".\n\n## 7. What J&W already does better\n\n- **It serves the GTA at all.** Omaha does not ship to Canada ([Shipping](https://www.omahasteaks.com/info/Shipping-Information)). J&W already runs weekly refrigerated routes across the GTA, Durham, Hamilton/Halton and York, plus long-haul to Ottawa, London, Windsor and others, and free pickup in Scarborough (jw-facts.md).\n- **Local, inspected and traceable.** J&W has run an Ontario-inspected meat plant since 2008 and can cut to order in Scarborough. Its chicken is air-chilled, grain-fed and Ontario-raised with no hormones or antibiotics (jw-facts.md). Omaha's local story is about Nebraska.\n- **Butcher heritage of a comparable kind:** a family butcher-grocer in downtown Toronto from the early 60s, Advance Food Products from 1973, and J&W from 1995. The scale differs, but it is the same \"family butcher\" story, and in Toronto it is local.\n- **Cooked, portioned, healthy meals à la carte:** about 292 Meal Butler items, all \"salt and sugar free, with no added oils\", in 4–8 oz portions (jw-facts.md). Omaha's prepared foods are indulgent, such as scalloped potatoes, tartlets, Chicken Kiev and nuggets. A J&W corporate gift can be a **wellness gift**, such as \"a week of clean meals for your team\". Omaha cannot offer that.\n- **Greek and Mediterranean identity:** A Taste of Greece Box at $130 and about 154 Mediterranean SKUs (jw-catalog.json). This gives GTA's Greek-Canadian businesses and Greek Easter gifting a reason to choose J&W.\n- **Price per gift in CAD:** Gentleman's Box $129.99, Taste of Greece $130, Long Weekend $199, Girls Night $89.99, BBQ Dad Pack $84.99 (jw-catalog.json). These sit in the same $85–$200 range as Omaha's USD $81–$212 boxes. **But J&W delivers on its routes for a flat $20–$35 with a $100 minimum, and pickup is free** (jw-facts.md), while Omaha charges US$24.99 per address under $149.\n- **Wholesale and whole-animal depth:** AAA/Prime/Wagyu, cases, quarter cow $1,200 and whole lamb. These support a premium \"J&W Reserve\" tier and office-catering add-ons.\n\n**Gaps J&W must close before launching** (from the jw-catalog.json audit):\n- No corporate or B2B page.\n- Gift cards are e-cards only, at $10/$25/$50/$100 CAD, with no branding, bulk purchase or certificate option.\n- The **\"A Taste of Greece Box Special\" description contains pasted voice-dictation text** (\"Do you know what I know who it is so yeah don't worry…\"). It must be fixed before the box is sold to companies.\n- No reviews or ratings, while Omaha shows 686 reviews on a single gift.\n\n## 8. Corrections to the pick's write-up\n\n- The [business-gifts page](https://www.omahasteaks.com/business-gifts) **is live.** The 403 comes from Akamai bot protection blocking WebFetch and curl. The page loaded normally in a browser on Oct 16, 2026, and the 5,000+, 350k+ and 700k+ figures appear on it, labelled \"Omaha Steaks internal data in 2025\".\n- \"Volume pricing at bands from under 10 to 500+ recipients\" overstates the page. Those bands are **quantity buckets on the lead form**. No volume-discount tiers or percentages are published; the wording is only \"may be available for qualifying orders\".\n- \"Certificates carry a company logo\": logos are offered only \"for qualifying custom programs\". *Free* logo placement is a Business Rewards **Premier** benefit, which requires US$5,000+ in annual spend.\n- Omaha's B2B flow is **not self-serve.** It runs on a lead form, an expert or account manager, and an emailed Excel order form. A self-serve CSV-upload studio would put J&W *ahead* of Omaha on ease of use.\n- Omaha **does not ship to Canada.** It is a benchmark for how to run a business-gifting program, not a GTA competitor for the same buyers.\n- Choice certificate price levels are not published. The rewards page implies a Platinum certificate at about $200 and a Diamond certificate at about $250.\n\n## 9. Revenue note for the \"Corporate Gift Studio\" play\n\n- These are the orchestrator's estimates, and they stay labelled as ESTIMATES.\n  - Conservative case: 60 companies × 25 gifts × $130 ≈ $195K.\n  - Upside case: about 115 firms × 60 boxes × $120 ≈ $830K (market-benchmarks.md §11; competitors-gta-meat-grocery.md).\n- Omaha's own buckets support pricing the J&W tiers at about **C$85 / C$130 / C$200 / C$350.** Its lead form centres on $100–$250 per gift, and its 2026 holiday guide runs $100–$180.\n- **Pay-on-redemption and rewards tiers** are the two Omaha tools most likely to turn first-year buyers into repeat annual programs. Its case study describes a repeat program where the client simply sent an updated list.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.omahasteaks.com/business-gifts (browser, Oct 16, 2026)\n- https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/corporate-gift-certificates\n- https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/large-business-gifts\n- https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/how-to-order-corporate-gifts\n- https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/holiday-business-gifts\n- https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/business-rewards\n- https://www.omahasteaks.com/info/Large-Gift-Orders\n- https://www.omahasteaks.com/info/Shipping-Information\n- https://www.omahasteaks.com/info/Stores\n- https://www.omahasteaks.com/info/Century-of-Steak\n- https://www.omahasteaks.com/press/ and the Sept 9/10 and Sept 22, 2026 press releases (linked above)\n- https://www.omahasteaks.com/product/Steakhouse-Grill-Gift-81977 and https://www.omahasteaks.com/servlet/OnlineShopping?Dsp=8500&sku=81977\n- https://www.omahasteaks.com/buy/Gifts/Gift-Cards\n- https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/businesses/topics/payroll/benefits-allowances/gifts-awards-social-events/gifts-awards-long-service-awards.html\n- Local: J&W catalogue and facts (jwfoods.com, crawled Oct 16, 2026)"}, {"key": "dive-06", "file": "competitor-06.md", "title": "Deep-dive: Competitor #6: Calo (Riyadh, GCC and UK), the AI-personalised meal-plan benchmark", "blurb": "Verification, prices, flows worth borrowing and the design notes behind the prototype.", "md": "# Competitor #6: Calo (Riyadh, GCC and UK), the AI-personalized meal-plan benchmark\n\nPrepared for Minastech (Erwin Garcia) for the J&W Foods / J&W Meal Butler proposal. Research date: **October 16, 2026.**\n\n**Method.** I read calo.app/en-sa and its sub-pages with WebFetch and curl, and opened them in a real browser for layout and type. Colours and fonts come from Calo's own CSS files. Prices come from the plan configuration embedded in the plans page's `__NEXT_DATA__`; those values exclude VAT, and the page gives `vatPercentage: 15`. The wizard steps come from the plans-page JavaScript, which names each step.\n\n**Limits.** The web-search budget for this run was used up, so I added no third-party news beyond Wamda and CB Insights. From Canada, calo.app/en-gb redirects (HTTP 308) to the global /en page, so I could not open the UK storefront directly. UK facts below come from Calo's own blog and from detoxkitchen.co.uk.\n\n**Currency.** SAR is pegged at 3.75 per USD. I converted at **1 SAR ≈ C$0.379**, using the BoC rate of 1.4196 CAD per USD for 2026-10-15 (see market-benchmarks.md). This is an approximation.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Verdict: operating and premium (verifiedOperating = true)\n\n- **Live in October 2026:**\n  - The menu page shows this week's menu, dated **\"11 Oct – 17 Oct\"** ([menu](https://calo.app/en-sa/menu)).\n  - The newest blog post is dated **October 7, 2026** ([blog](https://calo.app/en-sa/blog), [post](https://calo.app/en-sa/blog/food-allergy-meal-subscription)).\n  - The footer reads \"© 2026 Calo Inc.\" ([home](https://calo.app/en-sa)).\n  - The plans-page config holds a pricing experiment, `personalizedTier: \"pricingKSAMay2026Control\"`, and a Ramadan 2026 feature flag dated 2026-02-03 to 2026-03-19 ([plans](https://calo.app/en-sa/plans-and-packages), embedded config).\n- **UK is live under the Calo brand:**\n  - Detox Kitchen's site now says it \"is now Calo\". Calo launched as a standalone UK brand on 1 March (the page gives no year), and DK meals became Calo-branded from 28 April. The DK site links to calo.app/en-gb ([detoxkitchen.co.uk](https://detoxkitchen.co.uk/)).\n  - Calo's April 2026 UK posts include \"Calo vs COOK\", \"Calo vs Simmer Eats\", \"Calo vs Prep Kitchen\" and three London Marathon posts ([blog](https://calo.app/en-sa/blog)).\n  - CB Insights lists Calo acquiring **Fresh Fitness Food on April 24, 2025** ([CB Insights](https://www.cbinsights.com/company/fresh-fitness-food/financials)).\n  - **freshfitnessfood.com no longer resolves** (DNS lookup failed on Oct 16, 2026), so FFF appears to have been folded into Calo UK.\n- **Not in Canada.** The country selector lists World, Saudi Arabia, UAE, Kuwait, Qatar, Bahrain and Oman, and the UK is reached via /en-gb. **There is no Canadian or North American presence**, so the model is open in the GTA.\n\n## 2. Scale signals\n\n| Signal | Figure | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Customers / meals (SA page) | **280K** happy customers in GCC · **18M** meals delivered (company claim) | [calo.app/en-sa](https://calo.app/en-sa) |\n| Customers / meals (global page, newer copy) | **290K** customers · **19M** meals (company claim) | [calo.app/en](https://calo.app/en) (en-gb redirect) |\n| Funding | **US$64M Series B**: US$25M (Dec 2024, Nuwa Capital lead) plus US$39M extension (July 2025, AlJazira Capital lead). Oversubscribed beyond a US$50M target. Other investors: STV, Khwarizmi, Al Faisaliah, Oraseya | [Wamda, 29 Jul 2025](https://www.wamda.com/2025/07/calo-raises-39-milliion-series-b-extension-boost-global-growth) |\n| Revenue | \"High-growth nine-figure annualized revenue\" (US$). **The press-release text contains an unfilled \"($ million)\" placeholder, so no number was ever published.** 50%+ YoY growth in H1 2025, led by KSA, UAE and Kuwait | same |\n| Meals per year | 10M+ meals in 2024 | same |\n| Physical sites | Wamda (Jul 2025): 10+ sites incl. hospital outlets. **Café page (Oct 2026): 12 locations.** Six are shown in Riyadh: Box@ Mall (24h, Car Hop), Kingdom Center, **Kingdom Hospital**, and employee-only cafés at SNB, Zakat Authority and Roshn Sedra | [Wamda](https://www.wamda.com/2025/07/calo-raises-39-milliion-series-b-extension-boost-global-growth); [The Cafe](https://calo.app/en-sa/calo-cafe) |\n| Menu breadth | 95+ meals a week, 25+ new each month, 30+ snacks and desserts. UK: 100+ new meals a week, 20+ daily options | [home](https://calo.app/en-sa); [Calo vs COOK, 20 Apr 2026](https://calo.app/en-sa/blog/calo-vs-cook) |\n| Corporate | \"100+\" businesses use Calo for corporate events. Logos: Surface, Alinma Bank, STC, NBB | [corporate-and-business](https://calo.app/en-sa/corporate-and-business) |\n| KSA coverage | 25+ cities from Riyadh and Jeddah kitchens | [home](https://calo.app/en-sa) |\n| Support | 24/7, \"average 90-second response time\" (DK rebrand FAQ) | [detoxkitchen.co.uk](https://detoxkitchen.co.uk/) |\n| Food safety | HACCP + ISO 22000:2018 kitchens, monthly audits | [allergy post, 7 Oct 2026](https://calo.app/en-sa/blog/food-allergy-meal-subscription) |\n| Gym partner | Armah Sports (premium gyms): co-located retail and distribution. Armah's founder sits on Calo's board | [Wamda](https://www.wamda.com/2025/07/calo-raises-39-milliion-series-b-extension-boost-global-growth) |\n| App ratings | **Not found.** The App Store URL I tried returned 404, and the search budget was used up. The plans header has a Trustpilot rating slot (component found in the JS), but no value was visible on the SA page | — |\n\n## 3. Pricing (verified Oct 16, 2026)\n\n**KSA, from the plans-page config.** Prices below include 15% VAT, computed from the ex-VAT config values. CAD figures are approximate at C$0.379/SAR.\n\n**Per meal, Balanced / Vegetarian:**\n- Lunch or dinner: **SAR 36** (≈ C$13.65)\n- Breakfast: **SAR 26** (≈ C$9.85)\n- Snack: **SAR 12**\n\n**Per meal, High Protein / Low Carb:**\n- Lunch or dinner: SAR 36\n- Breakfast: SAR 30\n- Snack: SAR 18\n\n**Per meal, Custom Macros:**\n- Lunch or dinner: **SAR 47.50** (≈ C$18.00), **+32% vs Balanced**\n- Breakfast: SAR 37.50\n- Snack: SAR 18\n\nThe home page advertises \"Starting at SAR 31/meal and SAR 23/breakfast\". Those are the **ex-VAT** figures.\n\n**Per day, monthly or quarterly cycle (weekly cycle adds SAR 12/day):**\n\n| Package (config name) | Contents | Balanced | High Protein | Custom Macros |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `businessLunch` (the minimum) | lunch + snack | **SAR 50** (≈ C$19) | SAR 56 | SAR 67.50 |\n| `skipDinner` | B + L + snack | SAR 74 | SAR 84 | SAR 103 |\n| `skipBreakfast` | L + D + snack | SAR 84 | SAR 90 | SAR 113 |\n| `full` | B + L + D + snack | **SAR 110** (≈ C$41.70) | SAR 120 | **SAR 150.50** (≈ C$57) |\n| `ramadan` / `futoor` | seasonal | SAR 108 / 72 | 126 / 90 | 149 / 101.50 |\n\n- Weekly cycle: SAR 62 to 162.50 a day, and the config carries a SAR 10 weekly delivery charge (unit not stated). Monthly and quarterly delivery is free.\n- **Minimums:** 2 meals a day, including lunch or dinner, and 5 days a week.\n- **Payment:** card, Apple Pay, mada, and the Tabby and Tamara BNPL services. Tamara has a SAR 390 minimum. Pay in 4 is offered on monthly and 3-month plans.\n- **Renewal:** auto-renew is on by default for monthly and weekly plans and off for quarterly.\n- **Promo:** code TRY15 for 15% off ([allergy post](https://calo.app/en-sa/blog/food-allergy-meal-subscription)).\n\n**Bundles:**\n- **1x1:** a 1-month plan plus a 1-month gym membership.\n- **3x3:** a quarterly plan plus a 3-month Fitness Time membership.\n- Monthly gym partners: OnePass, BodyMasters, BodyMotions, NineRound ([plans](https://calo.app/en-sa/plans-and-packages)).\n- **Calo Pro** (annual only), \"worth 8,000\" (currency not stated):\n  - a 12-month gym membership\n  - a WHOOP tracker\n  - an \"Essentials Kit\" with a smart scale and bottle\n  - up to 12 instalments; pause up to 60 days; refund only within 2 hours ([Calo Pro](https://calo.app/en-sa/calo-pro-product-article)).\n  - The price placeholder did not render.\n\n**UK:**\n- **£9–£10 per meal**, free daily delivery nationwide (excluding Northern Ireland and the Highlands) ([Calo vs COOK, Apr 20 2026](https://calo.app/en-sa/blog/calo-vs-cook)).\n- The DK rebrand cut Detox Kitchen meal prices by about 30% ([detoxkitchen.co.uk](https://detoxkitchen.co.uk/)).\n\n**Café:** prices not shown. First app order 30% off ([The Cafe](https://calo.app/en-sa/calo-cafe)).\n\n**Sanity check for J&W's \"Personal Macro Plan\" at about C$180/week** (ESTIMATE, my arithmetic on the prices above):\n- Calo Custom Macros, lunch + snack × 5 days = SAR 337.50 ≈ **C$128/week**.\n- Calo Custom Macros, full day × 5 days = SAR 752.50 ≈ **C$285/week**.\n- C$180/week sits inside Calo's own custom-macro range, so it is defensible.\n\n## 4. Flows worth borrowing (step by step)\n\n**A. Plan configurator** ([plans-and-packages](https://calo.app/en-sa/plans-and-packages)). The page has two columns, with a sticky summary on the right.\n1. Pick a diet type from five cards with emoji illustrations: Balanced, Custom Macros, Low-Carb, High Protein, Vegetarian. Each card has \"View menu →\" and \"Select Plan\". Custom Macros uses \"Build my plan\" instead.\n2. Choose meals per day from chips (Breakfast, Lunch, Dinner, Snack). The minimum is 2, including lunch or dinner.\n3. Pick days from an S–S row of circle toggles. The minimum is 5. The layout also has a \"batched deliveries\" section.\n4. Choose a payment cycle (Weekly, Monthly, or 3-months), with optional gym-membership logos.\n5. The summary card, \"Your package awaits — Starting from 🔥 50/Day\", updates live. Then press Continue.\n6. Create an account by phone number (+966) with reCAPTCHA.\n7. Pay at checkout.\n\n**B. Custom Macros calculator.** This is a modal stepper; the step names come from the plans-page JS. I opened the first step only and submitted nothing.\n1. Birthday (DD/MM/YYYY)\n2. Gender (two emoji cards)\n3. Height in cm (50–300)\n4. Weight\n5. Activity: four emoji cards, Sedentary / Lightly active / Very active / Highly active\n6. Goal: Lose weight / Gain weight / Build muscle / Maintain\n7. Target\n8. Calories: a searchable dropdown in 5-kcal steps, pre-calculated\n9. Protein\n10. Carbs\n11. Result: a stacked macro bar with an \"Edit macros\" link, then \"plan selected\"\n\nAnalytics events fire at each stage (`custom_macros_start / calculated / edited / plan_selected`), so Calo measures funnel drop-off.\n\n**C. Self-serve subscription management, in the app:**\n- **What customers can change:** edit menu, swap any item, skip, pause, change address or delivery time.\n- **Cutoffs:** GCC up to **48h** before delivery ([FAQ](https://calo.app/en-sa/high-protein-meal-plan)); UK **1 business day** ([DK FAQ](https://detoxkitchen.co.uk/)).\n- **Pausing:** Account Settings → Pause Subscription → choose a duration.\n- **Ingredient exclusions:** unlimited, per meal (UK).\n- **Build your own meal:** offered \"if nothing on the daily menu hits the spot\".\n- **App:** stores favourites, dislikes and allergies, and offers AI macro/calorie and progress tracking (UK, [Calo vs COOK](https://calo.app/en-sa/blog/calo-vs-cook)).\n\n**D. Corporate catering checkout** ([corporate-and-business](https://calo.app/en-sa/corporate-and-business)). The pitch is no calls, quotes or PDFs.\n1. Pick items, serving sizes and quantities from a 100+ dish menu with calories shown. Formats: bowls, sandwiches, ready-to-eat and ready-to-heat. Filters: nut-, gluten- and dairy-free.\n2. Pay by card or BNPL.\n3. Food is delivered within 48h.\n4. Serve.\n\nThere is no form and every CTA reads \"Place Your Order\". Use cases: board meetings, networking events, team lunches and birthdays.\n\n**E. Affiliate (coaches and creators)** ([calo-affiliate](https://calo.app/en-sa/calo-affiliate)):\n1. Fill in a 30-second form.\n2. Review takes 15–30 days, and approved partners get an account manager.\n3. The partner gets a personal discount code; their client gets a first-order discount.\n4. The partner earns \"tokens\" each time the code is used.\n\nThe site also has `/referral/[id]`, wallet and cashback routes (seen in the build manifest).\n\n**F. Consultations** ([consultations](https://calo.app/en-sa/consultations)): a 15–20-minute nutritionist call to calculate calories and pick a plan, and a 10-minute general call about pricing and delivery. Users click \"Book a call\" and get details on confirmation. The price is not stated.\n\n**G. Market add-ons** ([market.calo.app](https://market.calo.app/en-sa)): snacks, frozen items, supplements, coffee and merch. Subscribers can add Market items to their meal delivery for free.\n\n**H. Café** ([The Cafe](https://calo.app/en-sa/calo-cafe)): the customer orders in the app (Cafe → menu → order) and collects at the counter or by Car Hop. Corporate cafés are open to employees only, and one café is inside a hospital.\n\n**I. AI:** \"**Calo Black**\", an LLM private chef that builds daily menus from conversation, was **announced as a pilot in July 2025** ([Wamda](https://www.wamda.com/2025/07/calo-raises-39-milliion-series-b-extension-boost-global-growth)). I **found no public Calo Black page or mention** on calo.app in October 2026. The Calo Pro page, the blog and the plans page do not mention it. Treat it as an unconfirmed pilot, not a shipped product.\n\n## 5. Site design (for an inspired J&W prototype; ideas only, not copy)\n\n- **Stack:** Next.js with self-hosted fonts and full EN/AR (RTL) support.\n- **Palette** (read from CSS and computed styles):\n  - **Primary green `#24A170`**: CTA pill buttons and stepper icons.\n  - Hover and dark greens `#28864C`, `#1C7E57`, `#166144`; deepest `#114B34`.\n  - Brighter green `#3CAD68` / `#28B17B`.\n  - Mint tints `#EAF7F2`, `#EDF5F0`, `#BCE7D6`, `#98D9B1`, `#8ED2B7`, used for testimonial and section backgrounds.\n  - **Text charcoal `#343B42`**; secondary text `#697886`.\n  - Borders `#EBEDEF`, `#DDE0E4`; disabled `#BDC4CB`; light panels `#F9FAFB` / `#F5F6F7`.\n  - Macro accents: purple `#987DDE`, amber `#F4B248`, blue `#64ACEC` (coloured dots for protein, carbs and fat). Error red `#E35044`.\n- **Typography:**\n  - A humanist sans, served as an obfuscated next/font file. It renders like **Lato**.\n  - H1 is **64px / 72px at weight 900**, in charcoal.\n  - Body 16px. Buttons 14px bold in **999px pill** shapes.\n  - Accent display face: **Bebas Neue**, condensed all-caps, used in a kinetic green statement band.\n  - The logo is a spaced, all-caps green wordmark.\n- **Shapes:** cards at 12–20px radius (large panels 32px), soft and borderless, on light grey or mint.\n- **Layout, top to bottom:**\n  1. **Split hero.** The left half is white. Its H1 cycles through audience endings (busy people, lose weight, gain muscle, high performers), with one pill CTA and a small green stats line at the bottom. The right half is a full-bleed lifestyle photo.\n  2. Centred \"daily-changing menu\" section: three trust icons, then a horizontal carousel of dish cards. Each card has a kcal chip, the dish name and coloured macro dots, with diet filter pills underneath.\n  3. A three-feature control and flexibility section. Accordion tabs sit on the left and an autoplay product video on the right.\n  4. A three-step \"How it works\" with dark-green number badges.\n  5. A Bebas Neue kinetic text band on brand green.\n  6. Mint-background testimonial cards with names.\n  7. A plan carousel. Each card has an overhead plated-food photo and a chip with an icon and the macro split.\n  8. An FAQ accordion in grey rows with + icons, next to a mint \"Got more questions?\" card that shows support-agent avatars and a \"Chat with us\" pill.\n  9. A creator/UGC carousel.\n  10. A footer with an app QR code and a country/language switcher.\n- **Photography:**\n  - (a) Overhead plated meals on white plates against light stone or grey, in soft natural light. Colourful proteins and vegetables, with the macro chip overlaid.\n  - (b) Lifestyle shots of diverse real-looking people eating from branded bowls in warm interiors (deep green walls, wood benches). Branded tote and cup visible.\n- **Tone:** short, punchy and second person, with a light wit. It leans on two-beat headline pairs (\"Total control. Full flexibility.\") and question-then-answer bands. It promises outcomes (macros hit, no prep, no contracts) and sells flexibility and no lock-in.\n\n## 6. What J&W already does better\n\n1. **Butcher heritage and its own inspected plant.** J&W has been an Ontario-inspected meat plant since 2008. The family has been in Toronto butchering and wholesale since the early '60s and 1973 (Advance Food Products), and J&W was founded in 1995. Calo is a 2019 tech-led meal-plan start-up with no butchery (jw-facts.md; [Wamda](https://www.wamda.com/2025/07/calo-raises-39-milliion-series-b-extension-boost-global-growth)).\n2. **Raw and cooked in one cart.** J&W sells 979 SKUs, including AAA, Canadian Prime and Wagyu beef, quarter cows, whole lambs and gift boxes. Calo sells only prepared meals plus a snack market.\n3. **À la carte by the ounce with no plan minimum.** J&W sells 4, 5, 6 and 8oz cooked portions, egg bites, oats and sides individually. Calo requires at least 2 meals a day for 5 days a week, and its smallest package is SAR 50 a day.\n4. **Salt- and sugar-free with no added oils.** Calo cooks mainly with extra-virgin olive oil and some desserts use butter or coconut oil ([FAQ](https://calo.app/en-sa/high-protein-meal-plan)). J&W suits sodium-restricted and strict-macro customers better.\n5. **Price** (ESTIMATE, my arithmetic):\n   - **J&W built day ≈ C$28.55–32.55:**\n     - egg bites $5.50 + steel-cut oats $4.40\n     - Greek Lemon chicken 4oz $4.99 + cilantro-lime basmati $2.35 + broccoli/cauliflower $1.32\n     - a pre-set dinner at $9.99–13.99 (jw-catalog.json)\n   - **Calo full day ≈ C$41.70 (Balanced) and ≈ C$57 (Custom Macros).** J&W is about 25–45% cheaper than Calo Balanced and about 45–50% cheaper than Calo Custom Macros.\n6. **Shelf life and logistics.** J&W's flash-frozen, vacuum-sealed BPA-free bags travel on one weekly route across a large part of Ontario. Calo's model depends on costly daily fresh delivery.\n7. **Local provenance.** J&W's chicken is air-chilled, grain-fed and Ontario-raised, with no hormones or antibiotics.\n8. **A local, human brand.** J&W has a store at Brimley Rd with free pickup, and customers know Bill and his team by name. It holds a Google rating of 4.8★ from 611 reviews (model-assumptions.md).\n9. **No local Calo.** Calo has no presence in Canada, so J&W can be \"the Calo of the GTA\" in this premium personalized tier.\n\n## 7. Corrections and caveats on the selection\n\n- **Revenue.** \"Nine-figure annualized revenue\" is a company claim **with no number**. The Wamda press text contains an unfilled \"($ million)\" placeholder.\n- **Customer and meal counts.** 280K / 18M is the KSA-page figure. The global page now says **290K / 19M**.\n- **48h edit window.** This is the GCC rule. In the UK it is **24h / 1 business day**.\n- **Calo Black.** It was a **pilot announced in July 2025**. I found no public product in October 2026.\n- **\"Name on each label (the Fresh Fitness Food model)\".** **Not verifiable now.** freshfitnessfood.com no longer resolves, and Calo UK's pages do not mention personalized name labels. Present it as J&W's own idea, not a proven Calo feature.\n- **Physical sites.** There are now **12 café locations**, versus the \"10+\" in Wamda.\n- **UK launch.** The UK runs under the Calo brand: Calo launched as a standalone UK brand on 1 March (the page gives no year), and Detox Kitchen meals moved to the Calo brand from 28 April. The FFF acquisition date is April 24, 2025 (CB Insights).\n\n## Sources\n\n- https://calo.app/en-sa: home (stats, FAQ, plans teaser, community)\n- https://calo.app/en: global page (290K / 19M; en-gb redirects here from Canada)\n- https://calo.app/en-sa/plans-and-packages: configurator, embedded pricing config, wizard JS\n- https://calo.app/en-sa/menu: weekly menu, 11–17 Oct\n- https://calo.app/en-sa/high-protein-meal-plan: FAQ, build-your-own\n- https://calo.app/en-sa/calo-cafe\n- https://calo.app/en-sa/corporate-and-business\n- https://calo.app/en-sa/consultations\n- https://calo.app/en-sa/calo-affiliate\n- https://calo.app/en-sa/calo-pro-product-article\n- https://calo.app/en-sa/blog\n- https://calo.app/en-sa/blog/calo-vs-cook: UK pricing and features, Apr 20 2026\n- https://calo.app/en-sa/blog/food-allergy-meal-subscription: Oct 7 2026\n- https://market.calo.app/en-sa\n- https://detoxkitchen.co.uk/: DK → Calo rebrand\n- https://www.wamda.com/2025/07/calo-raises-39-milliion-series-b-extension-boost-global-growth\n- https://www.cbinsights.com/company/fresh-fitness-food/financials\n- https://www.bankofcanada.ca/rates/exchange/daily-exchange-rates/ (via market-benchmarks.md)"}, {"key": "dive-07", "file": "competitor-07.md", "title": "Deep-dive: Competitor #7: Halenda's Meats and Halenda's Fundraising (Oshawa, Durham Region), the fundraising-channel benchmark", "blurb": "Verification, prices, flows worth borrowing and the design notes behind the prototype.", "md": "# Competitor #7: Halenda's Meats and Halenda's Fundraising (Oshawa, Durham Region), the fundraising-channel benchmark\n\nPrepared for Minastech (Erwin Garcia) for the J&W Foods / J&W Meal Butler proposal. Research date: **October 16, 2026.**\n\n**Method.** I downloaded halendasfundraising.com, halendas.com and the online store (store.themeatdepot.ca) with curl and read the text. I also opened the pages in a real browser to check layout. Post dates come from each site's WordPress REST API and its `article:modified_time` meta tags. Store addresses come from the halendas.com store-locator endpoint. Online-store prices come from the WooCommerce Store API (75 products). Colours and fonts come from each site's inline CSS and theme CSS.\n\n**Limits.**\n- The web-search budget for this run was used up before I started, so I found **no third-party news, review counts or revenue data**.\n- I did not check Instagram or Facebook, which require a login.\n- Everything below comes from Halenda's own sites, plus J&W's catalog for the price comparisons.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Verdict: operating (verifiedOperating = true), premium only in part\n\n**It is operating in October 2026.**\n- The weekly sale page reads \"Sale runs from Thursday, October 15th – Wednesday, October 21st, 2026\" ([on-sale](https://www.halendas.com/on-sale-durham/)).\n- The newest main-site post, published **Oct 6, 2026**, announces the 2026 Ontario's Finest Meat Diamond Award ([post](https://www.halendas.com/halendas-wins-2026-ontarios-finest-meat-diamond-award/)).\n- On the fundraising site, the Purchase, Product and About pages were all updated **Sept 23, 2026**. New success-story posts went up **Sept 2, 2026** and **Aug 24, 2026**. The footer reads \"©2026 Halenda's Meats\" ([purchase](https://halendasfundraising.com/purchase/), [blog](https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/)).\n\n**It is premium only in part. It is a craft-quality, value-priced butcher, not a luxury one.**\n- Quality signals are real:\n  - It has won **three Diamond Awards**: 2017 Double Smoked Bacon, 2024 Ruby Red Bacon and 2026 Stuffed Iberico Back Bacon.\n  - It has won the **Award of Excellence five times**: 2011, 2013, 2015, 2019 and 2024.\n  - It runs an Iberico-style line.\n  - Its charcuterie is sold in supermarkets.\n- Its own positioning is price-led: \"We believe in Quality, Service and Low Prices\". The weekly specials include pork tenderloin at $4.99/lb and pork schnitzel at $3.99/lb.\n- The fundraising arm sells one mass-market product, a pork pepperoni stick.\n- So Halenda's is the right benchmark for the **channel** (fundraising and freezer packs), not for premium positioning.\n\n## 2. Scale signals\n\n| Signal | Figure | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stores | **5 stores, all in Durham**, plus St Jacobs Market. Durham stores: Whitby (1916 Dundas St E), Oshawa Nelson HQ (915 Nelson St), Oshawa King (1300 King St E), Oshawa Taunton (250 Taunton Rd E) and Bowmanville (94 Waverley Rd). St Jacobs Market is at 878 Weber St N, Woolwich. | halendas.com store-locator data ([locations](https://www.halendas.com/halendas-locations/)) |\n| Plants | Homepage says \"6 locations and 2 processing plants\". Fundraising pick-up runs from 915 Nelson St, Oshawa and **15 Brydon Dr, Etobicoke**, a new plant opened Aug 2020 that added \"200%\" capacity. | [home](https://www.halendas.com/), [purchase](https://halendasfundraising.com/purchase/), [new-plant post](https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/the-advantage-of-our-new-plant-15-brydon-dr-etobicoke/) |\n| Heritage | \"Family-owned since 1979\". Founder Michael Halenda. Claims a family tradition of 65+ years. | [about program](https://halendasfundraising.com/about-halendas-fundraising/), [fund home](https://halendasfundraising.com/) |\n| Fundraising volume | First case shipped **June 27, 2013**. **6 million+ meat sticks sold** and \"more than six million dollars\" raised (post of Nov 6, 2025). | [6M post](https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/celebrating-six-million-meat-sticks-sold/) |\n| Implied run-rate (ESTIMATE) | 6M sticks over ~13.3 years ≈ 450K sticks a year. That is ≈ **45K bags or ≈ 1,880 cases a year**, and ≈ **$450K a year raised for groups**. At $261–$300 per case that is ≈ **$0.5M a year of wholesale revenue for Halenda's**. | My arithmetic on the figures above |\n| Retail distribution | Charcuterie in \"select Metro, Sobeys, Foodland, Food Basics and Fortinos\". The charcuterie page itself omits Food Basics. | [locations](https://www.halendas.com/halendas-locations/), [charcuterie](https://www.halendas.com/halendas-charcuterie/) |\n| Industry standing | David Hobson of Halenda's was appointed to the **2026 Meat & Poultry Ontario board** (June 2026). | [post](https://www.halendas.com/david-hobson-joins-mpo-board-of-directors/) |\n| Online store | 75 SKUs on WooCommerce at store.themeatdepot.ca, \"Halenda's Online Store\". Curbside pickup, \"aims to have your order completed in 24-48 hours\". No home delivery found. | [store](https://store.themeatdepot.ca/), WooCommerce Store API |\n| Group count, revenue, reviews | **Not found.** I found no group count, company revenue, Google rating or follower counts (search budget exhausted). | — |\n\n**2026 awards.** The Diamond went to Stuffed Iberico Back Bacon.\n- Platinum: Stuffed Iberico Back Bacon; Iberico Premium Koby Dog.\n- Gold: Iberico BBQ Sausage; Dill Pickle Pepperoni Stick.\n- Silver: Iberico Sweet Heat Dinner Sausage; Hot Honey.\n\nThat makes **six awarded products, one of which also took the Diamond**. It is not a Diamond plus six other awards ([post](https://www.halendas.com/halendas-wins-2026-ontarios-finest-meat-diamond-award/)).\n\n## 3. Pricing (verified Oct 16, 2026)\n\n### 3.1 Fundraising: Pepperoni Stix ([purchase](https://halendasfundraising.com/purchase/), [product](https://halendasfundraising.com/product/halendas-pepperoni-stix/), [FAQ](https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-faqs/))\n- **One case** holds 240 sticks: 24 resealable bags of 10 sticks each (6 mild, 4 hot). The 50 g sticks have 13 g protein each and a 6-month shelf life, and need no refrigeration.\n- Groups sell at **$25 a bag**, so a case brings in $600 at retail.\n\n| Cases | Case cost | Group profit / case | % of sales kept by group |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1–7 | $300.00 | $300 | **50%** |\n| 8–14 | $288.00 | $312 | 52% |\n| 15–24 | $273.00 | $327 | 54% |\n| 25+ | $261.00 | $339 | **56%** |\n\n**Terms and logistics:**\n- Shipping is **$18 per case** anywhere in Ontario, with no minimum. Pick-up is free at Oshawa or Etobicoke.\n- No tax is charged (their claim).\n- Lead time is \"about 2 weeks\", \"up to 4 weeks\" in the busy season.\n- Payment is by card, cheque or e-transfer.\n\n**Stale data on the same page** (a sign of weak data hygiene):\n- A legacy PayPal block still shows **$240 a case** for 1–7 cases, $229.20 for 8–14 and $209.01 for 35–50, with \"$12 per case\" shipping.\n- The example \"15 people… can raise over $3,900\" predates the current prices. On today's 15–24 tier it works out to $4,905.\n- The About page still says \"over $2,000,000 raised\", while the Purchase page says $6,000,000.\n\n**Real campaign results:**\n- A 12-year-old BJJ athlete sold **8 cases in about a month and raised $1,536**, which is $192 a case. That points to older $20-a-bag pricing ([post, Aug 2026](https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/youth-sports-fundraising-success-story-paige/)).\n- A Newcastle dance studio raised **$10,300** ([post, Sept 2026](https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/dance-fundraising-success-story/)).\n\n### 3.2 \"Hibernator\" freezer packs ([page](https://www.halendas.com/products/meat-freezer-packs/); identical on the online store)\n- **#1, $199:**\n  - 4 × 8 oz butterfly pork chops\n  - 2 × 16 oz sirloin\n  - 4 × 8 oz striploin\n  - 3 lb boneless skinless chicken breast\n  - 2 lb breakfast bacon\n  - 6 lb lean ground beef\n  - 8 fresh sausages\n  - ESTIMATE: about 19 lb in total, or about $10.50/lb.\n- **#2, $299:**\n  - 6 × 8 oz pork chops\n  - 4 × 10 oz striploin\n  - 2 × 16 oz sirloin\n  - 6 pork souvlaki\n  - 3 lb chicken breast\n  - 2 lb bacon\n  - 1 back rib\n  - 4 schnitzels\n  - 1 roasting chicken\n  - 8 lb ground beef\n  - 8 sausages\n- **#3, $349:**\n  - 4 × 8 oz bone-in pork chops\n  - 4 × 10 oz striploin\n  - 2 × 16 oz sirloin\n  - 4 lb hip roast\n  - 5 lb chicken legs\n  - 1 jumbo roasting chicken\n  - 2 lb peameal bacon\n  - 6 souvlaki\n  - 6 schnitzels\n  - 5 lb jumbo wings\n  - 8 lb ground beef\n  - 8 sausages\n- All packs are \"custom cut, freezer wrapped and guaranteed tender\". Customers order in store or online.\n\n### 3.3 Everyday retail (store.themeatdepot.ca, Store API, Oct 16, 2026)\n- **Chicken:**\n  - Boneless chicken breast $7.99/lb.\n  - 11 lb bag $82.39 (≈ $7.49/lb).\n  - Bone-in breast $6.69/lb.\n- **Beef:**\n  - Lean ground beef $7.99/lb.\n  - Striploin AA+ $12.49 for a 10 oz steak (reg. $16.24).\n  - Sirloin $35.98 for a 32 oz steak.\n  - \"PRIME\" T-bone $32.48.\n- **Pork and smoked meats:**\n  - Award-winning Kobassa $9.29/lb.\n  - Double Smoked Bacon $10.99.\n- **Prepared foods:**\n  - Perogies $4.99.\n  - Cabbage rolls $2.99.\n  - Buffalo wings $11.99.\n- **This week's specials (Oct 15–21, 2026):**\n  - Pork souvlaki $4.99/lb (save $3).\n  - Ruby Red bacon $7.49/lb.\n  - *New* Iberico Stuffed Pork Loin $7.99/lb.\n  - Porketta $1.99/100 g.\n  - Chuck short ribs AA $14.99/lb.\n\n**Compared with J&W** (jw-catalog.json):\n- Boneless chicken breast bulk 55 lb is $350 (≈ $6.36/lb). That is **≈ 15–20% below Halenda's** $7.49–$7.99/lb.\n- Ground beef bulk 5 lb is $40 ($8.00/lb), **about the same** as Halenda's $7.99/lb.\n- A portioned, vacuum-packed chicken breast case is $85–$92.\n- J&W sells **no meat stick of its own**. It only resells Jack Link's and Noah Martin's Costco items, and those are currently unavailable.\n\n## 4. Flows worth borrowing (step by step)\n\n**A. Two-way profit calculator** ([purchase](https://halendasfundraising.com/purchase/))\n1. The organizer types a **number of cases** and sees the profit.\n2. Or they type a **fundraising goal** in dollars and see the cases needed (\"Get Estimate\").\n3. A tier table sits underneath (1–7, 8–14, 15–24, 25+ cases), with profit % rising from 50% to 56%.\n\nFor J&W, rebuild it as a goal → orders calculator. For example: \"$2,000 goal ≈ 100 Team Packs at $20 donation each\".\n\n**B. Order and fulfil flow**\n1. On \"Order Now\", the organizer picks a number of cases.\n2. They choose delivery ($18/case) or pick-up (Oshawa or Etobicoke).\n3. They pay by card, cheque or e-transfer.\n4. Halenda's \"contacts you just before delivery\".\n\nThe legacy form also asks for **Group Name, Contact, Address, Coupon Code and a required \"Sales Representative\" dropdown**. That shows a rep-driven B2B sales motion with rep attribution. J&W should copy the rep and coach attribution field.\n\n**C. Two campaign modes** ([about](https://halendasfundraising.com/about-halendas-fundraising/))\n1. **\"Order the Stix, then sell\"** (about 95% of groups). The product is in hand, and Halenda's says that sells more.\n2. **\"Collect orders, then buy\"** (about 5%). Halenda's emails a **personalized flyer and order form**, digital or printed, and the group orders once the money is in.\n\nHalenda's also supplies a **custom insert flyer** to personalize the bags. For J&W, mode 2 becomes the online team page. Mode 1 is the hand-sell protein-stick SKU.\n\n**D. School-compliance and objection handling.**\n- Halenda's states that its sticks meet the Ontario school nutrition guideline range, contain no MSG, trans fats or artificial sweeteners, and use allergen-free ingredients.\n- It answers objections directly: \"No more melted chocolate\"; no refrigeration needed; a 6-month shelf life; a \"No Contact Fundraising Protocol\".\n- J&W needs an equivalent FAQ block before a school will say yes.\n\n**E. Segment success stories as SEO.**\n- New posts go up every few weeks, one per segment: dance, youth BJJ, hockey, sports clubs and cadets. Each gives the dollars raised and a call to action (\"Start Fundraising Today!\").\n- Halenda's also has a **\"Buy Canadian\" post** that names US-owned rivals (World's Finest Chocolate, Krispy Kreme, Jack Link's, Little Caesars) ([post](https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/support-local-fundraising-with-halendas/)).\n- J&W can make a stronger \"Ontario-inspected, Scarborough-made\" version.\n\n**F. Named freezer bundles plus a dated weekly sale page.**\n- There are three named tiers with full item lists, orderable in store or online.\n- The weekly sale page shows the sale window, \"Only $X/lb – Save $Y/lb\" and award badges on items.\n- It ends with a \"REVIEW US\" button linking to Google.\n- An e-flyer signup asks for postal code and \"location most frequented\", which segments the list by store.\n\n## 5. Site design (for an inspired J&W prototype; ideas only, not copy)\n\n- **Platform:** WordPress on the \"Fusion\" theme.\n  - Fundraising site by Lure Marketing.\n  - Main site by Beam Local.\n  - Online store by Eyebeam Design (WooCommerce, with an Authorize.net gateway on the fundraising checkout).\n  - Three separate domains with one shared look.\n- **Palette (from CSS):**\n  - Brand orange **#F58025**, used on primary buttons, the scroll-to-top disc and the outlined header CTA. Hover **#E2721B**.\n  - Black text **#000000**.\n  - Dark secondary button **#3F3F3F**.\n  - Footer / dark UI **#1D1D1D** and **#32373C**.\n  - The calculator panel uses a soft sky blue **#56A0D3**.\n  - The rest is white.\n  - The look is warm orange-on-white with charcoal: friendly and practical rather than luxe.\n- **Typography:**\n  - Headings H1, H2, H4 and H5 in **Raleway** (600–700), often uppercase with letterspacing in nav and section titles. Hero headlines are about 68px, centred and white.\n  - H3 and H6, plus hero sub-lines, in **Playfair Display 700**, a serif accent.\n  - Body in **Open Sans** 16px.\n  - Small all-caps Raleway nav.\n- **Layout:**\n  - White utility bar with social icons on the left and an outlined orange \"PURCHASE\" / \"What's on sale this week?\" button on the right.\n  - Horizontal uppercase nav with dropdowns underneath.\n  - Full-bleed photo hero slider with a dark overlay and a centred white headline.\n  - Centred single-column content sections, with a short orange rule as the divider and orange pill buttons.\n  - The Purchase page opens on a full-width macro photo of a pile of pepperoni sticks. Next come the light-blue calculator card, a plain pricing table and a step-by-step order block.\n  - On mobile, the header collapses to a centred logo and hamburger. A floating orange round scroll-to-top button sits bottom right.\n- **Photography:**\n  - Real product macros: sticks, cured meats on wooden boards in natural light.\n  - Moody, warm-toned food shots for the main-site hero.\n  - School-supply and desk imagery on the fundraising hero.\n  - No lifestyle or athlete photography. There is a gap here: no kids, teams or arenas.\n- **Tone of voice:**\n  - Folksy, enthusiastic and exclamation-heavy. Examples: \"Warning Halenda's is habit forming!\", \"Halenda's on the label means quality on the table\", \"No more melted chocolate\".\n  - Volunteer-practical: \"less selling and less administration\", \"finish fundraising faster\".\n  - Patriotic: \"Support Local, Support Canada!\".\n- **What J&W should do differently:**\n  - Keep the clear calculator and tier table.\n  - Add athlete and team photography, a live progress bar, and per-team pages.\n  - Use one domain and current, consistent prices.\n\n## 6. What J&W already does better\n\n1. **It can capture buyer data. Halenda's cannot.** Halenda's sells cases to the *group*, and the group resells bags for cash, so supporters never become Halenda's customers. A J&W Shopify team storefront makes every supporter a J&W account with consent and Smile.io points. This is the whole reason to run fundraising as an acquisition channel.\n2. **A bigger, more useful basket.**\n   - Halenda's fundraises with one SKU, a $25 bag of pork sticks.\n   - J&W can offer freezer packs, chicken-breast cases, chicken burgers and cooked Meal Butler week-packs. These are things families already buy weekly, so the average order is larger.\n   - Example (ESTIMATE): a $120 order with a 15% donation gives $18 per order. A Halenda's bag gives $12.50.\n3. **Healthier, cleaner story for sports parents.**\n   - Meal Butler is \"salt and sugar free, with no added oils\"; chicken is air-chilled, grain-fed, local, and raised without hormones or antibiotics.\n   - Halenda's sticks contain salt, sugar, dextrose and sodium nitrate.\n   - Caveat: a shelf-stable J&W jerky or stick would also need salt and cure. Market it as a high-protein, lower-sodium, clean-label snack, **not** as salt-free.\n4. **Pork-free options.** The Halenda's fundraising stick is pork. A J&W chicken or beef stick, plus chicken packs, works for no-pork households, which are a large share of J&W's Scarborough and Durham catchment. Only 5 J&W SKUs mention halal, so do not claim halal broadly.\n5. **Home delivery across Ontario already exists.** J&W runs weekly routes, including Tuesday Durham (Pickering to Port Perry, covering Oshawa, Whitby and Bowmanville, where every Halenda's store sits), Ontario long-haul, and express in Toronto.\n   - Halenda's online store is curbside-only.\n   - Its fundraising orders ship at $18 a case.\n6. **Cooked, portioned, à-la-carte meal prep.** Halenda's prepared foods are deli sides and party trays (perogies, cabbage rolls, coleslaw, wings). It has no macro-portioned meals at all, while J&W has about 292 Meal Butler and cooked SKUs.\n7. **Price on chicken.** J&W's 55 lb boneless breast is ≈ $6.36/lb against Halenda's $7.49–$7.99/lb. Ground beef is at parity.\n8. **One modern storefront.** J&W runs Shopify with accounts, loyalty and gift cards, against Halenda's three WordPress domains with conflicting prices. J&W's own content gaps still need fixing first (see jw-audit.md).\n\n**Where Halenda's is ahead (be honest in the pitch):**\n- Award record: 3 Diamond Awards.\n- Supermarket listings.\n- Two plants, five stores, and a 13-year fundraising track record with field sales reps.\n- A school-nutrition-compliant, shelf-stable hand-sell product.\n- J&W's plant (provincially inspected since 2008) only matches it.\n- Heritage is roughly a tie: Halenda's 1979 against J&W's 1973/1995 family lineage.\n\n## 7. Corrections and caveats on the selection\n\n1. **The program timeframe is now known.** It started **June 27, 2013**, so the $6M is cumulative over about 13 years, roughly $450K a year to groups and about $0.5M a year of wholesale revenue for Halenda's (ESTIMATE). The selection said the timeframe was not found. **Group count is still not found.**\n2. **Groups keep 50–56%, not 15–20%.** Halenda's groups buy at $261–$300 a case and sell at $600. A J&W storefront that gives 15–20% will be compared with that.\n   - The pitch must lean on: no upfront cash, no product handling, a bigger basket (so more dollars per order), and products families already buy.\n   - Alternatively, add a hand-sell J&W protein stick at 40–50% to the group. That needs product development and a shelf-stable process approved for J&W's provincially licensed plant. Sale would be limited to Ontario, which is also Halenda's footprint.\n3. **Year-2 sizing is a stretch.** The selection's year 2 (100 groups × $4,000 ≈ $400K) is close to Halenda's whole fundraising run-rate, which took 13 years and field reps to build.\n   - Year 1 at about $120K is plausible.\n   - A safer plan is **≈ $0.25–0.45M a year by year 2 including DTC follow-on** (ESTIMATE). This still uses the selection's 10% repeat × $130 × 4 a year logic on 3,000–6,000 buyer households.\n4. **All five stores are in Durham**: Oshawa ×3, Whitby and Bowmanville. The \"GTA\" sites are the Etobicoke plant and pick-up point, not shops, even though the logo names Mississauga and Etobicoke.\n5. **The award wording.** The Diamond winner is one of the six awarded products, so it is not \"Diamond plus six more\".\n6. **Charcuterie retailers.** The charcuterie page lists Metro, Sobeys, Foodland and Fortinos. Food Basics appears only on the locations page.\n7. **Self-claims.** \"Ontario's Fastest Growing Fundraising Program\" is Halenda's own claim and is unverified.\n8. **\"Premium\".** Halenda's is award-winning but value-priced. Use it as the channel benchmark only.\n\n## Sources\n- https://halendasfundraising.com/ (home; ©2026; headline \"Celebrating Six Million Meat Sticks Sold\")\n- https://halendasfundraising.com/about-halendas-fundraising/ (program, two modes, 50–56%, ingredients, school compliance; modified 2026-09-23)\n- https://halendasfundraising.com/purchase/ (calculator, tier table, $25/bag, $18/case shipping, legacy PayPal block; modified 2026-09-23)\n- https://halendasfundraising.com/product/halendas-pepperoni-stix/ ($300/$288/$273/$261 per case)\n- https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-faqs/ (case contents, pick-up Oshawa/Etobicoke, shelf life, phone)\n- https://halendasfundraising.com/contact-us/\n- https://halendasfundraising.com/reviews-testimonials/ (testimonials dated 2020)\n- https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/ (blog index)\n- https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/celebrating-six-million-meat-sticks-sold/ (June 27, 2013 start; Nov 6, 2025)\n- https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/dance-fundraising-success-story/ ($10,300; Sept 2, 2026)\n- https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/youth-sports-fundraising-success-story-paige/ (8 cases, $1,536; Aug 24, 2026)\n- https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/support-local-fundraising-with-halendas/ (Buy Canadian post)\n- https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/the-advantage-of-our-new-plant-15-brydon-dr-etobicoke/\n- https://www.halendas.com/ (home; 6 locations and 2 plants; ©2026)\n- https://www.halendas.com/halendas-locations/ (store locator; retail partners)\n- https://www.halendas.com/products/meat-freezer-packs/ (Hibernator $199/$299/$349)\n- https://www.halendas.com/halendas-charcuterie/\n- https://www.halendas.com/on-sale-durham/ (sale Oct 15–21, 2026)\n- https://www.halendas.com/halendas-wins-2026-ontarios-finest-meat-diamond-award/ (Oct 6, 2026)\n- https://www.halendas.com/david-hobson-joins-mpo-board-of-directors/ (June 2026)\n- https://store.themeatdepot.ca/ (online store; WooCommerce Store API, 75 products)\n- J&W comparisons: J&W catalogue and facts (jwfoods.com, crawled Oct 16, 2026)"}, {"key": "play-01", "file": "play-01.md", "title": "Play 1: Goal Plan Builder", "blurb": "The full play: mechanism, math, KPIs, agents, assumptions and risks.", "md": "# Play 1: Goal Plan Builder\n\nModelled on **Power Kitchen** (powerkitchen.ca, East York, Toronto). Prepared by Minastech for J&W Foods / J&W Meal Butler, October 16, 2026.\nAll money is CAD. Every figure is an **estimate** unless it is J&W's own published price (jwfoods.com, Oct 16 2026) or a cited source. Macros are **estimates** (USDA reference values in `site/assets/catalog.js`); J&W publishes no nutrition panels today.\n\n---\n\n## At a glance\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **One-liner** | A 60-second quiz matches GTA shoppers to one of six goal plans built from real Meal Butler meals, sold as a skip-anytime subscription (weekly, every 2 weeks or a monthly Freezer Fill) with 10% off. |\n| **Why it makes money** | Standing orders turn one-off Meal Butler carts into predictable, recurring revenue at about 40% gross margin, using meals J&W already cooks. |\n| **Focus** | Quiz-led goal plans on a skip-anytime subscription |\n| **Start** | Month 2 (December 2026). November is the build month. |\n| **Year 1 (Nov 2026 to Oct 2027), added revenue** | Low **$175K** · Mid **$350K** · High **$580K** |\n| **Run-rate (year 2), added revenue** | **~$757K** (low ~$380K, high ~$1.2M) |\n| **Gross margin** | **40%** (subscription meal prep after the 10% discount, model-assumptions.md) |\n| **JW's own costs** | ~$7K setup + ~$7,450 a month in year 1 (staff, ads, offers, software, discount leakage) |\n\n---\n\n## 1. What Power Kitchen proves\n\n**Who they are.** A family-owned, bootstrapped GTA meal-prep company (District 28 Company Limited) with its kitchen at 46 Hollinger Rd, about 10 km from Brimley Rd. It sells eight goal plans (Power, Pro Athlete, GLP-1 Support, Low Carb, Clean Bulking, Keto, Lean Muscle, Vegan) through a plan-matching quiz and an auto-renewing subscription that customers can skip, pause or edit before a cut-off.\n\n**Proof, verified Oct 16 2026 (competitor-01.md):**\n- Goal boxes list at **$16.22 to $36.95 a meal**. Power and Lean Muscle are $17.85 to $23.95; Pro Athlete $21.77 to $26.97; GLP-1 Support $21.76 to $27.96 (products.json).\n- Subscribe discount is **5%**, plus 5% back in store credit. One-Month Trial is 20 prepaid meals for $365 to $535. Delivery is $9.99 to $14.99, free over $180.\n- **4.7 stars from 564 Google reviews** (live widget). 185 SKUs, all updated Oct 16 2026. Own driver fleet to about 40 cities on Sunday and Wednesday. Weekly blog posts, Instagram 11.4K.\n- Martech that signals real paid spend: Klaviyo, Triple Whale, Zoho PageSense, Sezzle, a loyalty app, an SMS bot for delivery windows.\n- Revenue is not published. Estimate, low confidence: about **$3 to 7M a year** (400 to 900 orders a week at $150 to $170).\n\n**Ideas we borrow (ideas only, never their copy, photos or logos):**\n1. A short quiz that routes to a named goal plan and shows a price per meal.\n2. Goal plans with macros printed on every meal card.\n3. A sticky builder bag with a \"meals chosen\" counter and a smart auto-fill.\n4. A skip, swap, pause portal with a hard weekly cut-off.\n5. A chef's default box when the customer does nothing (also a Protein Chefs idea).\n6. A \"have you used another service?\" quiz step, used as switch intelligence.\n7. A prepaid multi-week commitment program (their 6-Week \"Earn It Back\").\n\n**Where J&W already beats them:**\n1. **Owns the protein.** An Ontario-inspected meat plant since 2008; J&W cuts, cooks and portions in-house. Power Kitchen buys proteins in.\n2. **Price.** J&W plan meals land at **$7.30 to $13.02 with Subscribe & Save** (below), against $16 to $27 for Power Kitchen's comparable plans. On like-for-like 4 to 6oz meals that is roughly **25 to 45% less per meal** (estimate; portion bases differ and neither side states raw or cooked weight).\n3. **Frozen vacuum packs.** Fresh rivals last 3 to 10 days, which forces a weekly cycle. J&W can sell a **monthly Freezer Fill** and reach long-haul Ontario routes (Ottawa, Kingston, London, Windsor, Barrie).\n4. **Clean recipes.** \"Salt and sugar free, with no added oils\" (J&W's own claim). Power Kitchen ingredient lists include salt, oils and sweeteners.\n5. **Households.** Power Kitchen needs one order per person. J&W can put several people's plans in one box with one delivery fee.\n6. **Bigger social proof.** Google 4.8 from 611 reviews and 30K Instagram followers, against 4.7/564 and 11.4K.\n7. **Store and pickup.** Free pickup at Brimley Rd Mon to Fri 9 to 6 and Sat 10 to 4, against Power Kitchen's two short pickup windows.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The mechanism: what J&W changes\n\nJ&W adds a quiz, six goal plans and a subscription to jwfoods.com. **Every plan is built only from Meal Butler items J&W sells today**, priced as the sum of the real item prices, then 10% off when the customer subscribes. Nothing new has to be cooked.\n\n### 2.1 The six plans (prices from jwfoods.com, Oct 16 2026; macros are estimates)\n\n| Plan | For | Meals in the rotation (real SKUs and prices) | Est. per meal (kcal / P / C / F) | Default box | One-off | Subscribe & Save (10%) | Per meal (S&S) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Lean** | Fat loss | Turkey, Spinach & Rice 4oz $12.99 · Tilapia, Asparagus & Brown Rice 4oz $11.99 · Jerk Chicken, Green Beans & Sweet Potato 4oz $12.99 · Tenders, Broccoli & Sweet Potato 4oz $9.99 · Teriyaki Chicken, Green Beans & Rice 4oz $9.99 · Arabic 7 Spice plate (chicken 4oz $4.50 + brown basmati 3oz $1.16 + broccoli 4oz $1.39 = $7.05) | 337 / 37g / 33g / 4g | 10 meals | $112.96 | **$101.66** | **$10.17** |\n| **Lean Bulk** | Muscle gain | Souvlaki, Broccoli & Greek Potatoes 6oz $15.99 · Flank Steak, Green Beans & Potato 6oz $17.49 · Salmon, Brussels & Quinoa 6oz $16.79 · Jerk power plate (Jerk Chicken 6oz $6.30 + white basmati 6oz $2.31 + broccoli $1.39 = $10.00) · Sirloin plate (Ground Top Sirloin 6oz $10.99 + sweet potato 6oz $2.34 + green beans $0.84 = $14.17) · Souvlaki cilantro-lime plate (Souvlaki 6oz $6.93 + Cilantro Lime Rice $2.35 + Mixed Veggies $0.66 = $9.94) | 523 / 54g / 40g / 13g | 10 meals | $144.65 | **$130.18** | **$13.02** |\n| **Athlete** | Trains 5+ days | Beef Flank Fajita Wrap $9.99 · Chicken Fajita Wrap $8.99 · Salmon, Brussels & Quinoa 6oz $16.79 · Souvlaki rice plate (Souvlaki 6oz $6.93 + white basmati 6oz $2.31 + broccoli $1.39 = $10.63) · Flank Steak, Green Beans & Potato 6oz $17.49 · Teriyaki Chicken, Green Beans & Rice 4oz $9.99 | 476 / 44g / 40g / 12g | 10 meals + Breakfast 5-pack | $120.28 | **$108.25** | **$10.83** |\n| **Protein & Greens** (working name for \"low carb\") | Fewer starches | Souvlaki 6oz $6.93 + Cauliflower Rice $0.96 + Broccoli $1.39 = $9.28 · Salmon 6oz $10.85 + Asparagus $1.89 + Cauliflower Rice $0.96 = $13.70 · Korean BBQ Flank 4oz $8.99 + Cauliflower Rice + Green Beans = $10.79 · Roasted Turkey 6oz $8.40 + Spinach $1.10 + Broccoli & Cauliflower $1.32 = $10.82 · Haddock 6oz $8.25 + Asparagus + Cauliflower Rice = $11.10 · Korean BBQ Chicken 4oz $4.99 + Broccoli & Cauliflower $1.32 + Brussels $0.84 = $7.15 | 301 / 46g / 12g / 8g | 14 meals | $148.66 | **$133.79** | **$9.56** |\n| **GLP-1 Companion** | Smaller appetites, incl. people on GLP-1 medicines | Tenders 3oz $3.14 + Quinoa 3oz $1.95 + Brussels $0.84 = $5.93 · Lemon Chicken Rice Soup 10oz $8.99 · Salmon 4oz $7.99 + Broccoli $1.39 + Quinoa 3oz $1.95 = $11.33 · Roasted Turkey 4oz $5.60 + Spinach $1.10 + Brown Basmati 3oz $1.16 = $7.86 · Chicken Chili 10oz $9.99 · Egg White Bites, Spinach & Feta $5.25 | 272 / 27g / 23g / 7g | 14 small meals | $113.62 | **$102.26** | **$7.30** |\n| **Busy Family** | Households with kids | Souvlaki, Broccoli & Greek Potatoes 4oz $13.99 · Teriyaki Chicken, Green Beans & Rice 4oz $9.99 · Kids meatball plate (Beef Meatballs 4oz $2.19 + white basmati 4oz $1.54 + Mixed Veggies $0.66 = $4.39) · Kids tenders plate (Tenders 3oz $3.14 + sweet potato 4oz $1.56 + broccoli $1.39 = $6.09) · Chicken Chili 10oz $9.99 · Tenders, Broccoli & Sweet Potato 4oz $9.99 | 367 / 35g / 36g / 8g (avg of adult and kid plates) | 20 plates | $187.30 | **$168.57** | **$8.43** |\n\nBox prices cycle through the six meals in order (10 meals = each meal once, plus the first four again). Sizes are 10, 14, 20, 28 or 40 meals.\n\n**Notes on the plans**\n- \"Protein & Greens\" is used instead of \"Low Carb\" or \"Keto\". J&W cooks without added oil, so the plan is not keto, and \"low carb\" style wording is a regulated nutrient content claim in Canada. A labelling review must clear every plan name and macro statement before launch.\n- GLP-1 Companion is food, not medical advice. Every mention carries \"Follow your prescriber's advice on protein and calories.\"\n- The **route minimum is $100 after the discount**. Lean 10, Lean Bulk 10 and Athlete 10 clear it. Protein & Greens and GLP-1 Companion default to 14 meals so they clear it. Smaller boxes are pickup-only, or the builder nudges a Breakfast 5-pack.\n\n### 2.2 How the subscription works\n- **Cadence:** weekly, every 2 weeks (default), or a **monthly Freezer Fill**. The Freezer Fill is the J&W-only option, made possible by frozen vacuum packs. One delivery a month means one route fee a month (e.g. $20 on the Wednesday route instead of $80 for four weekly boxes). Orders over $200 may also qualify for J&W's existing free-delivery-from-$200 offer (jwfoods.com/pages/free-delivery; confirm the rule with J&W).\n- **Cut-off:** J&W's current rule, \"order by Sunday for next week's delivery.\" Customers can skip, swap or pause (1 to 8 weeks) until Sunday night. Skipped weeks are never charged.\n- **Delivery:** the existing Tue to Fri GTA routes ($20 to $27) and long-haul routes ($35), $100 minimum, or free pickup at 2201 Brimley Rd. No new delivery days and no new vans.\n- **Butcher's Pick:** if the customer changes nothing by Sunday night, the kitchen's chosen box for their plan ships. It rotates flavours (Greek Lemon, Korean BBQ, Arabic 7 Spice, Teriyaki, BBQ, Jerk) within the plan's macros.\n- **Household box:** each person in the home gets a profile and a plan. All of it ships in one box with name tags on the packing list: one delivery, one fee, one Sunday reminder. Example: Alex on Lean Bulk (10 meals, $144.65) + Sam on GLP-1 Companion (10 small meals, $83.46) + kids (3 meatball plates + 3 tenders plates, $31.44) = $259.55, or **$233.60 every 2 weeks** with Subscribe & Save.\n- **Credits apply on their own.** Switch credits, referral credits and Smile.io points apply at checkout with no email to support. Power Kitchen makes customers email support to use store credit.\n- **Concept offers:** a **$25 switch credit** on the first subscribed box for anyone coming from another meal service (the quiz asks; Goodfood is distressed and LiveFit has closed). A **6-Week Reset**: six weekly boxes prepaid at 12% off, launched for January. For example Lean Bulk 10 meals × 6 = $867.90, so **$763.75 prepaid**. The route fee still applies to each delivery.\n- **Breakfast 5-pack (Concept):** 3 Egg White Bites, Spinach & Feta ($5.25) + 2 Steel Cut Oats ($4.40) = $24.55, or $22.10 with Subscribe & Save.\n\n### 2.3 What the customer sees (mirrors the prototype)\nQuiz (about 60 seconds) → result card with the plan, a short reason, est. macros, price per meal and three offers (Try one box · Subscribe & Save, recommended · 6-Week Reset) → builder pre-filled with the Butcher's Pick → checkout on J&W's real routes → My Plan portal to skip, swap, pause, change cadence or cancel (with a save offer).\n\n### 2.4 What this play is not (kept distinct from plays 2 to 7)\n- Not the **AI Coach** (Play 6). The quiz here is a short, rules-based router to six fixed plans. Chat-based personal macro plans, grams on labels and in-store body scans belong to Play 6. Subscribers who come in through the coach chat are counted in Play 6 only.\n- Not the **Butcher Club** (Play 3). Cooked meals only. Raw-meat boxes are Play 3; that play reuses this play's subscription app.\n- Not the **Pro Partner Portal** (Play 2) or **Team Fuel** (Play 7). No gym, team or school channel here. The 6-Week Reset is sold direct, not through gym partners.\n- Not **J&W at Work** (Play 4) or the **Gift Studio** (Play 5). Consumers only.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The money\n\n### 3.1 Year 1 (Nov 2026 to Oct 2027), mid case\n\nInputs (model-assumptions.md and market-benchmarks.md):\n- **Average box** $125 after 10% off ($139 before). 26 scheduled deliveries a year (an every-2-weeks equivalent across the weekly, every-2-weeks and monthly mix), 10% skipped. So **$243.75 a month** per active subscriber ($125 × 26 × 0.9 ÷ 12).\n- **New subscribers** (people who were not already weekly Meal Butler buyers): Dec 25 · Jan 60 (New Year push, 6-Week Reset) · Feb 45 · Mar 35 · Apr 35 · May 30 · Jun 25 · Jul 25 · Aug 30 · Sep 40 · Oct 40 = **390**.\n- **Monthly churn 9%.** That sits between standing-order replenishment (5 to 8%) and meal kits (8 to 15%) (Eightx).\n- **One-off plan boxes** from quiz takers who don't subscribe: 220 in year 1 at $135.\n- **Incrementality 0.8.** Some of these customers would have ordered anyway (model-assumptions.md: 0.75 to 0.85).\n\n| Month | New subs | Active at month end | Subscription revenue | One-off boxes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Nov 2026 | 0 (build) | 0 | $0 | $0 |\n| Dec | 25 | 25 | $3,047 | $1,350 |\n| Jan 2027 | 60 | 83 | $13,132 | $4,725 |\n| Feb | 45 | 120 | $24,747 | $3,375 |\n| Mar | 35 | 145 | $32,270 | $2,700 |\n| Apr | 35 | 167 | $37,897 | $2,700 |\n| May | 30 | 182 | $42,408 | $2,025 |\n| Jun | 25 | 190 | $45,294 | $2,025 |\n| Jul | 25 | 198 | $47,312 | $2,025 |\n| Aug | 30 | 210 | $49,757 | $2,025 |\n| Sep | 40 | 231 | $53,810 | $3,375 |\n| Oct | 40 | 251 | $58,717 | $3,375 |\n| **Total** | **390** | **avg 140** | **$408,390** | **$29,700** |\n\n**Formula (mathYear1):** subscribers ramp from 0 (November build month) to 251 by Oct 2027 on 390 new subscribers and 9% monthly churn. That gives 1,675 subscriber-months (average 140 active) × $243.75 = **$408K**, plus 220 one-off plan boxes × $135 = **$30K**. Gross $438K × 0.8 incrementality = **$350K added revenue** (mid).\n\n- **Low:** 245 new subscribers, 12% churn, $120 box with 12% skipped, 113 one-off boxes at $125, incrementality 0.75. Gross $237K, so **~$175K**.\n- **High:** 555 new subscribers, 7% churn, $130 box, 325 one-off boxes at $140, incrementality 0.85. Gross $682K, so **~$580K**.\n\n### 3.2 Run-rate (year 2, Nov 2027 to Oct 2028)\n\n**Formula (mathRunRate):** start with 251 active, add 35 a month, churn 8.5% (better skip, pause and save flows). Average 312 active × $2,925 a year ($125 × 26 × 0.9) = **$914K**, plus 240 one-off boxes × $135 = **$32K**. Gross $946K × 0.8 = **~$757K a year** of added revenue.\n- Low: 25 a month at 11% churn → ~$380K. High: 45 a month at 7.5% churn → ~$1.2M. The high case would need a second packing shift.\n- Cross-check: market-benchmarks.md §11 says ~320 active every-2-weeks subscribers at $120 is about $1M gross. This play's year-2 average of 312 at a $125 box with skips gives $914K gross, which is consistent.\n\n### 3.3 Margin, costs and contribution\n\n**Gross margin 40%** on subscription boxes after the 10% discount; 45% on one-off boxes. The blend is treated as 40% to stay conservative.\n\n**J&W's own costs** (estimates, excluding Minastech's fee):\n\n| Item | Setup | Year 1 (Dec to Oct) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Nutritionist review of plan rules and ~31 plan SKUs' macros | $2,500 | |\n| Lab nutrition analysis, top 8 plan SKUs (~$200 each, not verified) | $1,600 | |\n| Plan photos and short videos (in-house plus a freelancer) | $1,200 | |\n| Plan labels: design and first print run | $800 | |\n| Staff training on the portal and packing by plan (36 h × $25) | $900 | |\n| Software: subscription app ~$200/mo, Klaviyo email and SMS share ~$250/mo, AI agent tokens ~$100/mo, reviews app $15/mo | | $6,215 |\n| Paid ads: ~175 paid new subscribers × ~$85 CAC (within the $60 to $120 range) | | $15,000 |\n| Offers: $25 switch credit × ~150 + $20/$20 referrals × ~60 | | $6,150 |\n| Meal Butler lead, plan coordination: 12 h/week × 46 weeks × $25 loaded | | $13,800 |\n| Part-time cook/packer from February: avg 27 h/week × 38 weeks × $25 | | $25,650 |\n| Plan label on each box: $0.60 × ~3,500 boxes | | $2,100 |\n| Discount leakage: ~60 existing weekly buyers who switch get 10% off revenue J&W already had | | $13,000 |\n| **Total** | **$7,000** | **$81,915 (~$7,450 a month)** |\n\n- Year 1 mid: $350K × 40% = **$140K gross profit**, minus ~$89K all-in costs = **~$51K contribution** before Minastech's fee.\n- Year 2: $757K × 40% = **$303K gross profit**, minus ~$120K running costs (staff ~$69K at volume, ads ~$16K, discount leakage ~$18K, offers, labels, software) = **~$183K contribution**.\n- Delivery: subscribers pay the existing route fee ($20 to $35), which covers an owned-route stop at $8 to $14. Freezer Fill boxes over $200 that ride free cost J&W about $8 to $14 a stop. That cost is small and already in the margin range.\n- The 40% margin is treated as covering food, packaging, card fees and today's kitchen labour per meal, in line with the Goodfood benchmark (41.7%). The part-time cook/packer line is added on top to be safe.\n\n### 3.4 Sanity check\n- J&W baseline is about $2.5M (range $1.2 to 4.0M) with 15 to 25 staff. Year 1 mid of $350K is +14% on the mid baseline. The year-2 run-rate of $757K is +30%.\n- Volume: about **68 extra boxes a week** on average in year 1 (~850 meals) and **~146 a week** in year 2 (~1,800 meals). The audit estimates J&W runs 60 to 160 delivery orders a week today, so this roughly doubles route orders by year 2. Production headroom at the plant **must be confirmed** before the January push.\n- This is within the typical range for one play ($60K to $450K year 1; run-rate under $900K).\n\n---\n\n## 4. KPIs\n\n| KPI | Target |\n|---|---|\n| Quiz completions | 800 a month on average; 1,200 in January 2027 |\n| Quiz → first plan order | 7% |\n| Plan buyers who choose Subscribe & Save | 60% or more |\n| Active subscribers | 140 average in year 1; 250 by Oct 2027; 310 average in year 2 |\n| Monthly churn | 9% or lower in year 1; 8.5% in year 2 |\n| Skipped deliveries | 12% or fewer of scheduled boxes |\n| Average box after discount | $125 or more |\n| Paid CAC per new subscriber | $90 or less |\n| Freezer Fill share of subscribers | 20% |\n| M3 / M12 subscriber retention | 70% / 45% |\n\n---\n\n## 5. AI agents this play needs (merge into the shared roster)\n\n| Agent | Job in this play | What the prototype shows |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Macro Butler (plan matcher)** | Scores quiz answers into one of six plans, sets box size and cadence, splits a household into profiles, and writes a two-line \"why this plan\" note from owner-approved templates. Medical, allergy or eating-disorder signals go to a person. Chat-based personal plans hand off to Play 6. | The result card (\"Why we picked Lean Bulk for you\") and the household split. |\n| **Nutrition Data Agent** | Computes est. macros for the ~31 plan SKUs from recipes, cooked yields and Health Canada CNF values. Writes them to Shopify metafields and queues each panel for a nutritionist's sign-off. Flags claim wording (\"high protein\", \"low carb\", GLP-1). | \"est.\" macro chips on every card; team tile \"Macro sign-off: 9 of 31 verified\". |\n| **Butcher's Pick Menu Agent** | Drafts next week's default box for each plan within stock, par levels and margin, and rotates flavours so subscribers don't get bored. The owner approves. | Team approval card: \"Butcher's Pick, week of Mar 29: Lean swaps Tilapia pre-set for Haddock plate (tilapia below par)\". |\n| **Subscription & Reorder Agent** | Sends Saturday SMS and email reminders before the Sunday cut-off. Handles OK, SKIP, SWAP and PAUSE replies in plain language. Proposes cancel-save offers within owner-set limits and follows up failed payments. CASL consent is checked. | SMS preview on My Plan; the cancel-save modal; team save-offer approvals. |\n| **Production Forecast Agent** | After the Sunday cut-off, turns subscription lines into Monday's cook, portion and pack sheet, box counts by route day, and the raw purchase list. | Team prep sheet table and route-impact panel. |\n| **Lifecycle Marketing Agent** | Quiz-abandon emails, a welcome series for each plan, the January Reset campaign and the switch campaign. The owner approves every send. | Team \"campaign drafts awaiting approval\". |\n| **Review & Reputation Agent** | Asks subscribers for a review after their second box and drafts replies. Low-star replies need a person's approval. No incentives without disclosure. | Team review-reply queue. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Who at J&W does the work\n- **Meal Butler lead** (named at kickoff): 8 to 12 h/week on plan menus, AI-draft approvals and subscriber messages.\n- **Owner** (Bill): about 1 h/week reviewing the Monday digest and approving offers.\n- **Kitchen:** one part-time cook/packer from February, 20 h/week rising to 35 h/week by October.\n- **Store counter:** about 2 h/week handing out pickup plan boxes.\n- **Minastech** builds the quiz, plans, subscription setup and agents (fee is separate and not counted here).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Assumptions\n1. Average subscription box $125 after 10% off ($139 before). 26 scheduled deliveries a year, 10% skipped, so $2,925 a year per active subscriber.\n2. 390 new subscribers in year 1 (December to October). None in November, the build month. Existing weekly Meal Butler buyers who switch are **not** counted as revenue; only their discount cost is counted.\n3. Monthly churn 9% in year 1 and 8.5% in year 2 (Eightx: replenishment 5 to 8%, meal kits 8 to 15%).\n4. 220 one-off plan boxes in year 1 and 240 in year 2, at $135.\n5. Incrementality 0.8 (model-assumptions.md: 0.75 to 0.85).\n6. Gross margin 40% after the 10% discount (model-assumptions.md).\n7. About 45% of new subscribers come from paid ads at about $85 CAC. The rest come from Instagram (30K), email, the store counter and referrals.\n8. Delivery runs on the existing routes. The customer pays the current $20 to $35 fee or picks up free.\n9. The plant can absorb about 850 extra meals a week in year 1 and about 1,800 in year 2 (to confirm with J&W).\n10. Prices are J&W's online prices on Oct 16 2026. Macros are estimates until verified.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Risks and how to handle them\n1. **Claims and macros.** J&W shows no nutrition panels, and \"high protein\", \"low carb\" and GLP-1 wording are regulated or sensitive under Canada's Food and Drug Regulations. *Handle it:* label macros \"est.\" until a nutritionist signs off, use \"Protein & Greens\" instead of \"Low Carb\", and get a labelling review before launch.\n2. **Discount leakage.** Existing weekly buyers may move to 10% off (~$13K in year 1). *Handle it:* Subscribe & Save applies to plan boxes only, not to à la carte carts.\n3. **Kitchen capacity and stockouts** on plan SKUs (e.g. tilapia or salmon). *Handle it:* the Production Forecast Agent sets par levels, and Butcher's Pick swaps within the plan.\n4. **Churn above the model.** Meal kits churn 8 to 15% a month, and 44% of cancellations happen in the first 90 days. *Handle it:* skip and pause, monthly Freezer Fill, save offers and failed-payment follow-up. The low case uses 12% churn.\n5. **One route day per zone.** Fresh rivals deliver Sunday and Wednesday. *Handle it:* the frozen format makes once-a-week (or once-a-month) enough. Express ($30) stays for urgent orders.\n6. **Overlap with Play 6 (AI Coach).** Both draw on the same subscribers. *Handle it:* count quiz-led subscribers here and chat-led personal plans in Play 6 only.\n7. **Key-person risk.** The brand leans on the owners. *Handle it:* front the plans with the Meal Butler team and the product.\n8. **Comparative pricing.** *Handle it:* customer-facing copy quotes a public price range (\"about $16 to $27 a meal, Oct 2026\"), not a named rival, and the figures are rechecked each quarter.\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n- Power Kitchen homepage, quiz and live Google widget (4.7, 564): https://powerkitchen.ca/\n- Power Kitchen product catalogue and prices: https://powerkitchen.ca/products.json?limit=250\n- Power Kitchen collections (5% subscription discount): https://powerkitchen.ca/collections.json\n- Power Kitchen how it works and FAQ (skip, pause, cut-offs): https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/how-it-works · https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/faq\n- Power Kitchen shipping policy (fees, free over $180): https://powerkitchen.ca/policies/shipping-policy\n- Power Kitchen quiz landing page: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/meal-plan-offer\n- Power Kitchen custom builder: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/power-custom-meals\n- Power Kitchen 6-week program: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/six-weeks-program\n- Power Kitchen one-month trial: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/power-one-month-trial\n- Power Kitchen loyalty and referrals: https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/loyalty · https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/referrals\n- Power Kitchen story (family-owned, bootstrapped): https://powerkitchen.ca/pages/our-story\n- Power Kitchen 2026 price guide post: https://powerkitchen.ca/blogs/blog/meal-delivery-cost-toronto-pk\n- J&W Foods store and prices: https://www.jwfoods.com/ · https://www.jwfoods.com/products.json\n- J&W delivery routes and cut-off: https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/home\n- J&W free delivery from $200: https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/free-delivery\n- J&W about us: https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/about-us\n- J&W Google 4.8 from 611 reviews (aggregator): https://yably.ca/reviews/toronto/j-w-foods-2201-brimley-road\n- J&W Instagram (30K): https://www.instagram.com/jwfoods/\n- Subscription churn and cohort benchmarks: https://www.eightx.co/blog/average-subscription-churn-rate-by-category\n- Subscription vs one-time AOV, subscribe-and-save norms: https://eightx.co/blog/average-ecommerce-aov-subscription-vs-onetime-2026\n- Recharge subscriber trends (F&B subscriber worth 3+ non-subscribers): https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/recharge-subscriber-trends-insights-report-highlights-the-growing-value-of-subscribers-to-ecommerce-brands-302250623.html\n- Meta ads CPA benchmarks: https://www.triplewhale.com/blog/facebook-ads-benchmarks\n- Klaviyo email benchmarks: https://www.klaviyo.com/products/email-marketing/benchmarks\n- Delivery driver wages, Toronto: https://www.jobbank.gc.ca/marketreport/wages-occupation/21667/ON\n- Goodfood Q2 FY2026 loss (displaced customers): https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2026/04/goodfood-market-reports-7-million-net-loss-in-q2/\n- Goodfood gross margin benchmark (41.7%): https://cdn.makegoodfood.ca/images/investors/docs/2025/q4/Goodfood_Q4_FY25_Earnings_Press_Release_en.pdf\n- Internal: research/competitor-01.md, selection.json, jw-facts.md, jw-audit.md, model-assumptions.md, market-benchmarks.md, competitors-gta-mealprep.md, ai-agents-research.md, site/assets/catalog.js"}, {"key": "play-02", "file": "play-02.md", "title": "Play 2: J&W Pro Partner Portal", "blurb": "The full play: mechanism, math, KPIs, agents, assumptions and risks.", "md": "# Play 2: J&W Pro Partner Portal\n\nPrepared for Minastech (Erwin Garcia) for J&W Foods / J&W Meal Butler · October 16, 2026\nAll money in CAD. Every figure is an ESTIMATE unless it is a J&W published price (jwfoods.com, Oct 16 2026) or carries a source. Shared figures come from `model-assumptions.md`.\n\n**One-liner (hub card):** A wholesale portal where GTA gyms, trainers, clinics, supplement shops and teams apply in two minutes, get tiered case pricing on Meal Butler meals and butcher cases, and receive standing weekly drops on J&W's own routes.\n\n**Why it makes money:** Gyms, trainers and clinics already send people to J&W by word of mouth. A real partner program turns that into standing weekly case orders that fill the plant and make the routes denser, with almost all of the volume new to J&W.\n\n**Focus:** B2B wholesale portal for gyms, clinics, trainers and shops.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Modelled on: Icon Meals (Wholesale program), Dallas, Texas\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| URL | https://iconmeals.com/pages/wholesale |\n| Scope | Global (Dallas, Texas, USA) |\n| What they do | A cooked meal-prep plant with in-house meat processing that sells \"heat and eat\" meals to stores, gyms, weight-loss clinics, training centres and offices, from \"a few dozen meals\" to \"weekly shipments of thousands\" (company claim). It has a separate wholesale login and a partner-logo wall (UFC, U.S. Olympic Team, Dallas Cowboys). |\n| Proof | Over US$14M revenue in 2023 with 75 staff ([Garage Gym Reviews](https://www.garagegymreviews.com/icon-meals-review)). UFC took an equity stake in 2021, citing 56% annualized growth since 2014 ([UFC.com](https://www.ufc.com/news/ufc-announces-major-partnership-icon-meals)). Claims 12M+ meals sold and \"thousands\" of wholesale partners. Shopify store updated Oct 16 2026 (competitor-02.md). |\n| Honest caveats | The wholesale funnel is thin. All three \"Apply Now\" buttons link to the homepage, there is no application form, no price list, no FAQ, and the \"Wholesale Login\" is the standard Shopify customer login. Trustpilot shows 1.7/5 from 980 reviews, and every review from May to Oct 2026 is 1-star (shipping delays, thawed food). Icon is a **positioning** reference, not a service model. |\n| What we borrow (ideas only) | The page order (proof, segments, plant story, shoppable grid with macros and steppers). The audience router (\"who is this for?\"). A low per-order minimum (Icon: 8 meals). The custom-portion logic. The shared-cart idea for coaches. |\n| J&W's edge | An Ontario-inspected plant with in-house butchery since 2008, family wholesale roots back to 1973, and a 2026 federal chicken TRQ processor quota. Prices are lower: cooked chicken C$16.72-19.96/lb vs Icon's C$21.58/lb, and a comparable bowl costs about C$10 vs C$18.63 (competitor-02.md §3). Food arrives frozen on J&W's own vans, not on dry ice. J&W has 4.8★ from 611 Google reviews. And J&W can ship the thing Icon never built: a working application, a tiered price list and net terms. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. The mechanism: what J&W changes\n\nJ&W already sells \"semi-wholesale\" online: 4 kg chicken cases ($92-$100), a 10 lb salmon case ($175), a 25-pack of chicken burgers ($35.50) and a \"BUY IN BULK & SAVE\" banner. Trainers recommend J&W to clients (\"I recommend all of my clients\", Google review via Chamber of Commerce). What's missing is a front door for businesses. This play adds five things:\n\n1. **A partner page and a 2-minute application** for six groups: gyms and CrossFit boxes, personal trainers and coaches, physio/dietitian/weight-loss clinics, supplement and health shops, boxing/MMA/fight gyms, and teams and academies (team nutrition for travel days and camps). An AI pre-check maps the address to a route day, suggests a tier and a starter order, and a person (the account lead) approves within one business day.\n2. **Tiered partner pricing (Concept)**, set by the average weekly order over four weeks, as a discount off J&W's own web price:\n\n   | Tier | Average weekly order | Meal Butler cooked food | Butcher cases | Extras |\n   |---|---|---|---|---|\n   | Partner | $150+ | 15% off | 5% off | Free drop on route day, member code, net-15 after 4 paid orders |\n   | Pro | $300+ | 20% off | 8% off | + loaned reach-in freezer, co-branded labels |\n   | Elite | $600+ | 25% off | 10% off | + first pick of production slots, quarterly menu tasting |\n\n   Founding offer: the first 10 accounts get Pro pricing for 12 months. Partners agree to sell at J&W's web price or higher, never lower, so jwfoods.com is never undercut.\n3. **Concept case packs built from real portion prices** (see §3). Example: Jerk Chicken, Green Beans & Sweet Potato (6 oz) is $14.99 on jwfoods.com. A 12-pack is $179.88 at web price and $143.90 at Pro. The gym sells each meal at $14.99 and keeps $3.00.\n4. **Standing weekly orders on the existing routes.** Minimum 12 cooked meals or one butcher case per order. Order by Sunday, delivered on the partner's route day (GTA 1 Tue, GTA 2 Wed, GTA 3 Thu, GTA 4 Fri) or picked up free at Brimley Rd. Standing orders of $150+ drop free (J&W credits the $20-$27 route fee). Card for the first four orders, then net-15 by invoice.\n5. **Partner tools:** a free tasting tray of 24 portions on the first route day; a loaned, branded reach-in freezer with a temperature sensor once an account averages $300+ a week for eight weeks; \"Fuelled by J&W at [Partner]\" co-branded labels that keep J&W's plant details, ingredients and allergens; and a member code (10% off a member's first jwfoods.com order, 5% of members' online spend back to the partner as J&W credit for 12 months). Member-code DTC sales are tracked but **not counted** in this play's revenue.\n\n**Build:** on J&W's existing Shopify store. Either a wholesale-pricing app on the current plan (ESTIMATE ~$100/month) or Shopify's native B2B features (company accounts, catalogues, payment terms), which have been tied to Shopify Plus; confirm current plan rules. Recommendation: start with the app and move to Plus only if partner sales pass ~$40K a month.\n\n**Timeline:**\n- **Nov 2026:** price list, partner agreement, application form, wholesale app, label templates and nutrition analysis started. Recruit 5 founding partners from existing trainer and gym customers and Instagram.\n- **Dec 2026:** first drops (startMonth 2). Tasting trays go out before the January gym rush.\n- **Jan to Apr 2027:** \"New year, stocked freezer\" outreach along the GTA 1-4 corridors. First loaned freezers. Net-15 opens for accounts with 4 paid orders.\n- **Aug 2027:** CanFitPro Toronto booth (J&W exhibited in 2014; confirm 2027 dates).\n- **Sep to Oct 2027:** varsity and academy season starts. Target 34 active accounts.\n\n**Kept distinct from the other six plays:** offices and employer-funded meals are Play 4 (J&W at Work). Team and school fundraisers are Play 7 (Team Fuel Fundraiser); here a team buys food for its own athletes. Consumer subscriptions are Play 1 and the AI coach is Play 6. Restaurants and J&W's existing informal trade accounts are not counted.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Partner price list (real J&W products, Concept discounts)\n\nCooked food at Partner 15% / Pro 20% / Elite 25%. Butcher cases at 5% / 8% / 10%. Web prices from jwfoods.com on Oct 16 2026.\n\n| Product (catalogue id) | Web price | Partner | Pro | Elite |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Jerk Chicken, Green Beans & Sweet Potato, 6 oz (`jerk-chicken-broccoli-sweet-potato-chunks`) | $14.99 | $12.74 | $11.99 | $11.24 |\n| Souvlaki, Broccoli & Greek Potatoes, 6 oz (`chicken-souvlaki-broccoli-sweet-potato-chunks`) | $15.99 | $13.59 | $12.79 | $11.99 |\n| Tenders, Broccoli & Sweet Potato, 4 oz (`chicken-broccoli-sweet-potato-fries`) | $9.99 | $8.49 | $7.99 | $7.49 |\n| Egg White Bites, Spinach & Feta, 2-pack (`copy-of-feta-omelette-cups-cooked-portioned-2-pkg`) | $5.25 | $4.46 | $4.20 | $3.94 |\n| Case of Portioned Chicken Breast, 4 kg (`chicken-breast-portioned-4-5-6-8-oz-box-broken-down-and-pkg-in-vacuum-bags`) | $92.00 | $87.40 | $84.64 | $82.80 |\n| Marinated Chicken Breast Case, 4 kg (`chicken-breast-marinated-korean-4kg`) | $100.00 | $95.00 | $92.00 | $90.00 |\n| Ontario Ground Turkey Breast, 10 x 1 lb (`ontario-ground-turkey-10x1lb-case`) | $129.00 | $122.55 | $118.68 | $116.10 |\n| Premium Chicken Burgers, 25-pack, 6 oz (`chicken-burgers-1`, full catalogue) | $35.50 | $33.73 | $32.66 | $31.95 |\n| Canadian Atlantic Salmon, 10 lb case (`canadian-atlantic-salmon`, full catalogue) | $175.00 | $166.25 | $161.00 | $157.50 |\n\n**Concept packs** (all labelled Concept, priced from real web prices):\n\n| Concept pack | Contents | Web value | Partner | Pro | Elite |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 48-Meal Freezer Restock | 6 each of 8 pre-set meals (Souvlaki 6 oz, Jerk 6 oz, Turkey 6 oz, Salmon 6 oz, Tenders 6 oz, Teriyaki 4 oz, Flank 6 oz, Tilapia 6 oz) | $721.32 | $613.12 | $577.06 ($12.02/meal) | $540.99 |\n| Mixed 24-Meal Case | 6 each: Souvlaki 6 oz, Jerk 6 oz, Turkey 6 oz, Tenders 6 oz | $371.76 | $316.00 | $297.41 ($12.39/meal) | $278.82 |\n| Pro 12-Pack (single recipe) | 12 x Jerk Chicken, Green Beans & Sweet Potato 6 oz | $179.88 | $152.90 | $143.90 | $134.91 |\n| Clinic Small-Portion 12-Pack | 4 each: Turkey, Spinach & Rice 4 oz; Tilapia, Asparagus & Brown Rice 4 oz; Salmon, Brussels & Quinoa 4 oz | $151.88 | $129.10 | $121.50 | $113.91 |\n| Fight Camp 12-Pack | 12 x Tenders, Broccoli & Sweet Potato 4 oz | $119.88 | $101.90 | $95.90 | $89.91 |\n| Breakfast Box of 24 | 12 Egg White Bites (2-pack) + 12 Steel Cut Oats 6 oz | $115.80 | $98.43 | $92.64 | $86.85 |\n| Trainer Client Week (per client) | 4 Souvlaki 6 oz + 3 Tenders 6 oz + 3 Turkey 6 oz + 5 Egg White Bites 2-packs | $183.15 | $155.68 | $146.52 | $137.36 |\n\n**Why a partner says yes:** a gym selling 48 meals a week from a loaned freezer at Pro pays $577.06 and, selling at web price, takes in $721.32, so it keeps about $144 a week (about $7,200 a year) for zero equipment cost. Members avoid the $100 route minimum and pick up after class.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The money\n\n### Year 1 (Nov 2026 to Oct 2027), with ramp from startMonth 2 (Dec 2026)\n\nActive accounts (net of ~2.5%/month churn), mid case:\n\n| Dec | Jan | Feb | Mar | Apr | May | Jun | Jul | Aug | Sep | Oct |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 4 | 8 | 12 | 16 | 19 | 22 | 24 | 26 | 28 | 31 | 34 |\n\n**Mid:** 224 account-months x 4.33 weeks = 970 account-weeks x $260 average weekly order (new accounts start near $180; freezer accounts reach $400+) = $252,200 gross x 0.85 incremental = **$214,400 net new revenue**. Gross profit at 22% = **$47,200**.\n\n**Low:** ramp to 20 accounts by Oct (124 account-months) x 4.33 = 537 account-weeks x $220 = $118,140 x 0.80 = **$94,500**.\n\n**High:** ramp to 50 accounts by Oct (330 account-months) x 4.33 = 1,429 account-weeks x $300 = $428,700 x 0.85 = **$364,400**.\n\n### Run-rate (year 2, Nov 2027 to Oct 2028)\n\nAccounts grow from 34 to about 52: **44 average active x $330/week x 50 weeks = $726,000 gross x 0.85 = $617,100 net new revenue** (gross profit at 22% = $135,800). Range: low 30 x $280 x 50 x 0.80 = $336,000; high 55 x $380 x 50 x 0.85 = $888,250.\n\n### Notes on the math\n\n- **Margin, 22% blended (model-assumptions.md).** At Pro, cooked food keeps about 31% margin after the 20% discount (from 45%), and cases about 21% after 8% off (from ~27%). A 65/35 cooked/case mix gives about 27-28% before Elite discounts, credits, shrink and mix risk. We plan on the model's 22%. Freezers, van time and staff are counted separately in costs, not inside the margin.\n- **Incrementality, 0.85.** Most volume is new (a gym freezer sells to people who would not have placed a $100 route order). Some trainers and gym owners already buy cases at retail and will move to partner pricing. The Application Checker flags existing customers so the overlap stays visible.\n- **Funnel to 40 gross wins in year 1:** ~2,000 businesses contacted along the route corridors x ~4% reply (Apollo SMB test: 4.2%) = ~80, plus ~60 inbound from Instagram (30K), the store and trainer referrals = ~140 conversations, ~80 tasting trays, ~50% tasting-to-account = ~40 wins, less ~6 churned = 34 active.\n- **Pool:** ~2,000 GTA gyms and clubs (ESTIMATE from IBISWorld's 4,195 Ontario establishments), 59 Ontario CrossFit affiliates, ~31,460 fitness instructors and trainers in the Toronto region (Job Bank), ~8-12K independent trainers (ESTIMATE). 52 accounts is a small slice of this.\n- **Sanity check vs J&W:** year-2 run-rate is ~25% of the $2.5M mid-case revenue. It needs roughly 940 extra cooked meals and ~50 butcher cases a week. Plant capacity is the first thing to confirm with J&W (market-benchmarks.md §12).\n- **JW's own costs and contribution.** Year 1: ~$26,000 setup + ~$3,400/month Dec-Oct + ~$1,600 in Nov = ~$65,000, against $47,200 gross profit, so about **-$18,000 in year 1** (mostly freezers). Year 2: ~$5,300/month (~$64,000) + 8 more freezers ($14,400) = ~$78,000, against $135,800 gross profit, so about **+$58,000**. That is before the plant's fixed-cost absorption and before member-code DTC sales. This is a volume and utilization play, not a high-margin one.\n- **Upside not counted:** member-code DTC sales. Example: 44 partners x 3 member orders a month x $130 x 12 = ~$206,000 a year at 45% margin. Excluded here to avoid double counting with Plays 1 and 6.\n- **Levers to improve contribution:** push the cooked share of the mix to 75% (+2 points of margin), buy refurbished freezers, and keep Elite (25% off) for accounts over $600 a week.\n\n---\n\n## 5. KPIs\n\n| KPI | Target |\n|---|---|\n| Active partner accounts | 34 by Oct 2027; ~52 by Oct 2028 |\n| Average weekly order per account | $260 in year 1; $330 in year 2 |\n| Reply to applications | Within 1 business day, every time |\n| Tasting tray to account | 50% or better |\n| Account churn | 2.5% a month or lower |\n| Partner revenue on standing orders | 70% or more |\n| Freezer sell-through | 75%+ of par each week; shrink and credits under 3% |\n| On-time partner drops | 98%+ |\n| Days sales outstanding (net-15) | 20 days or fewer |\n| Member-code online orders | 3 per partner a month (tracked, not counted) |\n\n---\n\n## 6. AI agents this play needs\n\n| Agent | Job | On screen in the prototype |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Partner Scout** (B2B prospecting) | Builds target lists of gyms, CrossFit boxes, clinics, supplement shops, fight gyms and teams along the GTA 1-4 route corridors (Google Maps, websites, Clay enrichment). Scores fit, drafts personal outreach with a tasting-tray offer, books tastings. Runs a CASL check on every message. The account lead approves each sequence. | Team view, \"AI drafts\": outreach email to Birchmount Strength Co. with a CASL check and Approve / Edit / Skip. |\n| **Application Checker** (onboarding and credit) | Reads each application. Checks the business is real (website, Google listing, address), maps it to a route day, suggests a tier and starter order, flags existing retail customers, drafts the welcome email and price list. The owner approves tier and terms. Net-15 only after 4 paid orders. | Customer view: the instant pre-check card after \"Send application\". Team view: Applications queue with AI notes and Approve as Pro / Partner / Decline. |\n| **Par Level Planner** (partner reorders) | Each Friday, uses the partner's freezer count (typed or photo), four weeks of sell-through and the season (January rush, summer dip, team season) to draft next week's standing order and suggest par changes. The partner approves by Sunday. Nothing ships without approval. | Partner portal: \"Suggested order for Wednesday\" with on-hand, sold and suggested quantities. Team view: par-change draft for Brimley Barbell Club. |\n| **Production & Route Planner** (shared with the production forecast and route agents) | Rolls approved partner orders into Monday's cook, portion and vacuum-pack sheet and the raw-material order. Checks plant load against capacity. Slots partner drops into GTA 1-4 with the dispatcher. | Team view, \"Production and route impact\": +180 pre-set meals, 8 chicken cases, stops by route day, capacity flag at 90%. |\n| **Label & Claims Checker** | Builds \"Fuelled by J&W at [partner]\" labels from J&W's master recipe file (ingredients, allergens, net weight, best-before, plant details). Blocks health or disease claims (for example GLP-1 or diabetes) in partner copy. A person signs off every new label. | Customer view: live label preview. Team view: a draft with \"great for GLP-1 patients\" removed. |\n| **Partner Receivables agent** | Issues net-15 invoices from approved orders, reconciles payments, sends polite reminders at 3 and 7 days overdue, and recommends pausing a standing order at 30 days, which the owner approves. | Team view: Receivables aging table and a reminder draft to Harbour & Hill Nutrition. |\n| **Partner desk chat** (the shared Butler Concierge in partner mode) | Answers partner questions on minimums, tiers, freezer loans, cut-offs and labels from the approved price list and agreement. Hands any pricing exception to the account lead. | Chat \"Ask the Butler · Partner desk\". |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Who does the work at J&W\n\n- **Account lead (an existing team member or a new B2B hire):** 8-10 h/week in year 1 (applications, tastings, account calls, approving agent drafts), rising to ~15 h/week in year 2.\n- **Production lead:** +3 h/week folding partner orders into the Monday cook sheet. Extra cooking and packing labour sits inside the 22% margin; expect about one more cook/packer FTE by mid year 2.\n- **Drivers:** +3 h/week by spring 2027, +6 h/week by Oct 2027 (about 12 minutes a stop on existing GTA 1-4 routes).\n- **Bill or Stavros:** about 1 h/week approving tiers, terms, exceptions and new labels.\n\n## 8. JW's costs to run it (excluding Minastech's fee)\n\n**Setup (one-time, year 1): ~$26,000**\n- 10 loaned glass-door reach-in freezers with J&W wrap at ~$1,800 each (ESTIMATE; get three local quotes, refurbished units could save a third): $18,000\n- Nutrition-facts analysis and resale label templates for ~20 partner SKUs (ESTIMATE): $2,500\n- Thermal label printer and co-brand label stock: $700\n- 10 Wi-Fi freezer temperature sensors (~$50 each, ESTIMATE): $500\n- Counter cards, QR stands, sell sheets, member flyers: $1,300\n- CanFitPro Toronto 2027 booth and samples (ESTIMATE): $3,000\n\n**Monthly (year-1 average, Dec to Oct): ~$3,400**\n- Account lead 10 h/week x $30 loaded: $1,300\n- Added route stops ~90/month x ~$10 (model: $8-14 per owned-route stop): $900\n- Tasting trays, 4 new accounts/month x ~$90: $360\n- Software: Clay Launch $185 + wholesale app ~$100 + Claude API ~$25: $310\n- Labels and flyers: $150\n- Ads to gym owners and trainers (Meta, LinkedIn): $250\n- Bad-debt reserve (~0.5% of revenue): $100\n\nYear 2 runs at about $5,300/month plus ~8 more freezers.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Assumptions\n\n- Startup in Dec 2026 (startMonth 2), after a November build.\n- Mid ramp to 34 active accounts by Oct 2027, ~44 on average in year 2, with ~2.5% monthly account churn.\n- Average weekly order $260 (year 1) and $330 (year 2). Year 2 has 50 ordering weeks.\n- Account mix by Oct 2027 (ESTIMATE): 12 gyms and fight gyms, 9 trainers and coaching studios, 5 clinics, 5 supplement and health shops, 3 teams and academies.\n- Concept discounts: cooked 15/20/25%, cases 5/8/10%. Founding partners get Pro pricing for 12 months.\n- Blended gross margin 22% (model-assumptions.md). Incremental factor 0.85 (model range 0.75-0.85).\n- Owned-route cost ~$10 per added stop. Freezer ~$1,800 each including wrap.\n- Partners resell at J&W's web price or higher, in person only.\n- All partners are in Ontario and on existing GTA 1-4 routes or pickup.\n- Member-code DTC sales, offices (Play 4) and fundraisers (Play 7) are excluded.\n\n## 10. Risks and how to handle them\n\n1. **Thin margin.** At 22%, a few off-list deals wipe out the profit. Only tier prices in the portal; any exception needs the owner's approval.\n2. **Cannibalization.** Existing case-buying trainers may switch to partner pricing. The Partner tier needs a business and $150+ a week. The Application Checker flags existing customers.\n3. **Resale labelling.** Food resold by a third party needs full prepackaged labels (ingredients, allergens, net weight, best-before, dealer name and address, and a Nutrition Facts table unless an exemption applies; bilingual rules apply). J&W publishes no nutrition panels today. Budget the analysis and confirm the rules with a labelling consultant before the first freezer goes out.\n4. **Cold chain in someone else's freezer.** Require −18°C or colder, fit a Wi-Fi sensor, pull stock after any temperature excursion, and put responsibility in the partner agreement.\n5. **Credit risk on net-15.** Card only for the first four orders. The Receivables agent chases from day 3, and the owner pauses accounts at 30 days.\n6. **Plant capacity and staff.** Year 2 needs ~940 extra meals a week. The Production planner flags load at 90%. Confirm headroom before signing Elite accounts.\n7. **Health claims at clinics.** No medical or GLP-1 claims on labels or in copy. The clinic's dietitian chooses the menu. J&W shares ingredient lists.\n8. **Provincial licence.** The plant is Ontario-inspected, so partners must be in Ontario. Current routes already are.\n9. **Growing faster than service.** Icon's 1.7 Trustpilot score shows what happens when a brand scales past its delivery capacity. Pace new accounts to route and plant capacity, and protect J&W's 4.8★.\n10. **One contact.** Give every partner a named account lead.\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- Icon Meals wholesale: https://iconmeals.com/pages/wholesale\n- Icon Meals home: https://iconmeals.com/\n- Icon Meals bulk items: https://iconmeals.com/collections/bulk-items\n- Icon Meals custom meal builder: https://iconmeals.com/products/custom-meals\n- Icon Meals on Trustpilot: https://www.trustpilot.com/review/iconmeals.com\n- UFC partnership with Icon Meals (2021): https://www.ufc.com/news/ufc-announces-major-partnership-icon-meals\n- Garage Gym Reviews, Icon Meals review: https://www.garagegymreviews.com/icon-meals-review\n- J&W delivery routes and fees: https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/home\n- J&W about us: https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/about-us\n- J&W products used (examples): https://www.jwfoods.com/products/chicken-burgers-1 · https://www.jwfoods.com/products/chicken-breast-marinated-korean-4kg · https://www.jwfoods.com/products/jerk-chicken-broccoli-sweet-potato-chunks\n- J&W Google rating (via Yably): https://yably.ca/reviews/toronto/j-w-foods-2201-brimley-road\n- Trainer review quote (via Chamber of Commerce): https://canada.chamberofcommerce.com/business-directory/ontario/scarborough/food-products-supplier/534332-j-w-foods\n- J&W restaurant supply (2018): https://www.alignable.com/toronto-on/jw-foods-inc/jwfoods-com-jul-2018\n- 2026 WTO chicken TRQ processor list: https://www.international.gc.ca/trade-commerce/controls-controles/chicken-poulet/wto_processor-2026-omc_transformateurs.aspx?lang=eng\n- Ontario gyms (IBISWorld): https://www.ibisworld.com/canada/industry/ontario/gym-health-fitness-club-operation/2146/\n- CrossFit affiliates in Canada: https://www.crossfit.com/gyms/canada\n- Fitness instructors and trainers, Toronto region (Job Bank): https://www.jobbank.gc.ca/marketreport/outlook-occupation/21170/geo9219\n- GTHL 2025-26 team entries: https://gthlcanada.com/article/gthl-announces-team-entries-for-2025-2026-season\n- Clay pricing 2026: https://www.joinvalley.co/blog/clay-pricing-and-credits-explained-2026\n- Apollo SMB test (4.2% reply rate): AI Agent Rank (page no longer available)\n- CFIA food business licensing (interprovincial trade): https://inspection.canada.ca/en/food-licences/food-business-activities\n- Internal: research/competitor-02.md, research/selection.json (pick #2), research/jw-facts.md, research/jw-audit.md §5 and §10, research/model-assumptions.md, research/market-benchmarks.md §4, §8, §10, §11, research/ai-agents-research.md (Agents 6, 12, 13), site/assets/catalog.js, research/jw-catalog.json"}, {"key": "play-03", "file": "play-03.md", "title": "Play 3: J&W Butcher Club", "blurb": "The full play: mechanism, math, KPIs, agents, assumptions and risks.", "md": "# Play 3: J&W Butcher Club\n\nModelled on **truLOCAL** (https://trulocal.ca, Canada, founded Kitchener ON 2016). Ideas only, never their copy.\nPrepared for Minastech (Erwin Garcia) · October 16, 2026 · All CAD. Every figure is an ESTIMATE unless it is a J&W published price or a cited source.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The play in one paragraph\n\nA raw-meat membership for GTA households. Members pick a box size (Starter, Family or Freezer Fill), choose how often (every 2, 4, 6 or 8 weeks) and spend a points budget on real J&W cuts. The box is cut, vacuum-sealed and frozen at 2201 Brimley Rd and rides **free** on the member's normal J&W route day. Every box includes one **free-for-life item**, by default a Meal Butler cooked chicken 4 x 4 oz pack, which puts J&W's higher-margin cooked line in front of raw-meat buyers. The top of the range is **beef and lamb shares** (1/16, 1/8, 1/4 beef, whole and half Ontario lamb) with a cut sheet, planning weight and deposit. Launch is timed for Wild Fork's Ontario exit: its Whitby, Oakville, Ancaster and Newmarket stores close by Dec 14, 2026, and all four towns are on J&W routes.\n\n**Why it makes money:** it turns occasional freezer stock-ups into a recurring box on routes J&W already drives, at butcher-counter prices well below Toronto's premium shops, and it uses a free cooked pack to move raw buyers into Meal Butler.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What truLOCAL proves (verified in competitor-03.md)\n\n| Idea we borrow | truLOCAL evidence |\n|---|---|\n| Serving-sized tiers | Starter $155 (up to 36 servings), Family $279 (up to 80), Mega $349 (up to 104) (https://trulocal.ca/pricing/) |\n| Points-budget box builder, extra points, rollover | 9 / 20 / 26 points; \"Extra Points\" to go over (https://trulocal.ca/faq, /get-started/) |\n| Free item in every box, for life | Pick 1 of steak, chicken, salmon or ground beef; new customers only (https://trulocal.ca/free-life/) |\n| Self-serve skip, pause, cancel; reminder before billing | 48 h reminder, no contract (https://trulocal.ca/faq) |\n| Comparison table vs grocery and other boxes, tap to explain | Homepage (https://trulocal.ca/) |\n| One-time boxes beside the subscription | Curated $170–250, Value $200–305 (https://trulocal.ca/curated-boxes/, /value-boxes/) |\n\nScale: 70,100+ customers and 511,000+ boxes since 2016 (company claim, https://trulocal.ca/10-year). About $19.8M revenue at acquisition (2020) and bought by EMERGE Commerce for up to $16.8M. 2025 AOV about $240, CLTV approaching $2,000, CAC $100–175 (EMERGE, Jan 21, 2026). Net new subscriptions +193% YoY in Feb 2025 on \"buy Canadian\". Weak spot: Trustpilot 3.2/5 (68 reviews), with thawed boxes and price per lb as common complaints.\n\n**J&W's edge:** its own Ontario-inspected plant (since 2008); AAA ribeye at about $23–25/lb in the 10-pack vs $39–60/lb at Toronto premium butchers; Wagyu, bison, venison, Ontario lamb and cut-to-thickness steaks that truLOCAL does not offer; weekly GTA routes instead of courier; a cooked Meal Butler line to use as the free item; Google 4.8★ from 611 reviews.\n\n**What we deliberately do not copy:** corporate gifting (that is Play 5, Corporate Gift Studio) and cooked meal-plan subscriptions (Play 1, Goal Plan Builder). The Club is raw meat plus shares. Gift memberships, if offered, are sold and counted in Play 5.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Mechanism (concrete, with real J&W products and prices)\n\n### 3.1 Box sizes (Concept)\n\nA **box point = about $5 of J&W's own shelf price**, rounded to the nearest point.\n\n| Box (Concept) | Price per box | Points | Shelf value | Est. weight, mixed box | Servings (est., 5 oz raw) | $/point |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Starter | **$139** | 28 | ~$140 | about 10–14 lb | up to 45 | $4.96 |\n| Family | **$239** | 48 | ~$240 | about 17–25 lb | up to 80 | $4.98 |\n| Freezer Fill | **$319** | 66 (64 + 2 bonus) | ~$330 | about 25–34 lb | up to 110 | $4.83 |\n\n- Steak-heavy boxes weigh less (a 66-point \"Steak night\" box is about 13 lb). Chicken-heavy boxes reach the \"up to\" servings.\n- At the \"up to\" level J&W is $2.90–3.09 per serving vs truLOCAL's $3.36–4.31. On a mixed box it is about the same per serving, so the page leads with **$/lb, cut quality and the free item**, not per-serving claims.\n- Every box: free delivery on the member's route day (non-members pay $20–35 with a $100 minimum), one free-for-life item, box locks Sunday 11:59 pm, skip, swap, pause or cancel online.\n- Extra points: $5 each. Unused points roll to the next box, up to 5.\n- Frequency: every 2, 4, 6 or 8 weeks (ButcherBox and truLOCAL pattern). Default every 4 weeks.\n\n### 3.2 Free-for-life item (pick one, comes in every box while a member)\n\n| Option | Real J&W basis | Value |\n|---|---|---|\n| Meal Butler cooked chicken breast, 4 x 4 oz, member's flavour (default) | Teriyaki $4.60, Souvlaki $4.62, BBQ $4.20, Jerk $4.20, Greek Lemon $4.99, Korean BBQ $4.99 per 4 oz | $16.80–19.96 |\n| Wagyu/Kobe AAA ground, 1 lb | GROUND - BEEF WAGYU KOBE GROUND AAA - 1 LB BAG $14.99 | $14.99 |\n| Wild sockeye fillet, 5–7 oz | SALMON SOCKEYE - SKIN-ON, Canadian WILD $10.50 | $10.50 |\n\nCost to J&W about $7–11 per box (ESTIMATE). The default cooked pack is the cross-sell: it gets raw buyers eating Meal Butler every month.\n\n### 3.3 The menu members spend points on (real J&W items)\n\n| Item (J&W listing) | Pack | Price | Points | $/lb |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CASE of PORTIONED CHICKEN BREASTS (4 kg; 4/5/6/8 oz) | 4 kg | $85.00 | 17 | $9.64 |\n| Same, HALF CASE (2KG BAG - Random Portions) | 2 kg | $48.00 | 10 | $10.89 |\n| GROUND - CHICKEN BREAST - 1LB BAG | 1 lb | $9.99 | 2 | $9.99 |\n| CHICKEN THIGHS - BONELESS SKINLESS (regular) | 2.5–3 lb | $18.99 | 4 | $6.33–7.60 |\n| CHICKEN DRUMSTICKS | 2–3 lb | $9.99 | 2 | $3.33–5.00 |\n| CHICKEN WINGS (drums & flats) | 2 lb | $14.99 | 3 | $7.50 |\n| Premium Chicken Burgers, 25 x 6 oz | 9.4 lb | $35.50 | 7 | $3.79 |\n| Marinated Chicken Breast Case 4 kg (Korean BBQ / Teriyaki) | 4 kg | $100.00 | 20 | $11.34 |\n| ONTARIO GROUND TURKEY BREAST - 1LB BAG | 1 lb | $15.99 | 3 | $15.99 |\n| AAA BEEF TOP SIRLOIN STEAKS 7–8 oz | 1 steak | $10.00 | 2 | $20.00–22.86 |\n| BEEF - NEW YORK STRIPLOIN AAA 8–9 oz | 1 steak | $15.00 | 3 | $26.67–30.00 |\n| Same, case 8 x 8–9 oz | 8 steaks | $110.00 | 22 | $24.44–27.50 |\n| BEEF RIBEYE BONELESS AAA 11–12 oz | 1 steak | $24.50 | 5 | $32.67–35.64 |\n| AAA BEEF RIBEYE STEAK SPECIAL 10x11-12oz | 10 steaks | $175.00 | 35 | $23.33–25.45 |\n| BEEF TENDERLOIN AAA/CAB 6–8 oz | 1 steak | $19.00 | 4 | $38.00–50.67 |\n| PICANHA (TOP SIRLOIN CAP) 9–10 oz | 1 steak | $14.50 | 3 | $23.20–25.78 |\n| WAGYU STRIPLOIN STEAK 8–9 oz | 1 steak | $35.00 | 7 | $62.22–70.00 |\n| GROUND - BEEF WAGYU KOBE GROUND AAA - 1 LB BAG | 1 lb | $14.99 | 3 | $14.99 |\n| GROUND - BEEF BRISKET - 1LB BAG | 1 lb | $10.99 | 2 | $10.99 |\n| GROUND BEEF - BULK MIX SPECIAL - 5lb | 5 lb | $40.00 | 8 | $8.00 |\n| BEEF SHORT RIBS - KOREAN STYLE CUT - PLAIN | 2 lb | $35.00 | 7 | $17.50 |\n| PORK TENDERLOIN - 2 LOINS/PKG | ~2.5 lb | $15.00 | 3 | ~$6.00 |\n| BONELESS PORKCHOP CASE SPECIAL 10x6oz | 3.75 lb | $36.00 | 7 | $9.60 |\n| SAUSAGES - PORK - SPARTAN \"LOUKANIKO\" - 4/PK | 4 | $11.50 | 2 | n/a |\n| PORK BACON - SLICED/CENTRE CUT | 1 lb | $9.00 | 2 | $9.00 |\n| LAMB CHOPS - RAISED IN SOUTHERN ONTARIO - 8/PKG | 8 chops | $52.00 | 10 | n/a |\n| GROUND - LAMB - 1LB BAG | 1 lb | $14.99 | 3 | $14.99 |\n| BISON RIBEYE STEAKS 6 oz | 1 steak | $18.50 | 4 | $49.33 |\n| GROUND - BISON - 1LB BG | 1 lb | $16.00 | 3 | $16.00 |\n| VENISON STRIPLOIN STEAKS 6 oz | 1 steak | $19.99 | 4 | $53.31 |\n| SALMON SOCKEYE - SKIN-ON, Canadian WILD 5–7 oz | 1 fillet | $10.50 | 2 | $24.00–33.60 |\n\nSteaks carry a thickness choice (1\", 1¼\", 1½\"). J&W already cuts whole striploins and ribeyes \"to your liking\" (AAA WHOLE STRIPLOIN $299, AAA WHOLE RIBEYE $375).\n\nReady boxes (presets), checked against the points table:\n- **Lean & simple** (Starter, 27 pts + 1 rolls over): half case chicken, ground chicken x3, Ontario ground turkey, top sirloin x2, wild sockeye x2. Shelf $134.96, ~10.1 lb, ~32 servings.\n- **Wild Fork switcher** (Family, 48 pts): 4 kg chicken case (6 oz), Wagyu/Kobe ground x2, ground beef 5 lb, top sirloin x4, wild sockeye x3, pork tenderloin. Shelf $241.48, ~21.3 lb, ~68 servings, ~$11.22/lb at $239.\n- **Greek Sunday** (Family, 47 pts + 1 rolls over): Ontario lamb chops, Spartan Loukaniko x2, ground lamb, pork tenderloin, half case chicken, top sirloin x4, ground chicken x2, bacon, Wagyu/Kobe ground. Shelf $236.96, ~17.3 lb.\n- **Steak night** (Freezer Fill, 66 pts): AAA ribeye 10-pack, NY striploin x4, Wagyu striploin, picanha x2, Wagyu/Kobe ground x2. Shelf $328.98, ~13 lb.\n\nMeal Butler extras can be added outside points at regular prices (for example Souvlaki chicken 4 oz $4.62, beef meatballs 4 oz $2.19, egg white bites $5.25, lemon chicken rice soup $8.99, Souvlaki, Broccoli & Greek Potatoes $13.99).\n\n### 3.4 Beef and lamb shares\n\nToday J&W lists a QUARTER COW PURCHASE at $1,200 with a cut list but no weight, ONTARIO RAISED WHOLE LAMB ($400 deposit + $75 at pickup) and HALF LAMB ONTARIO $250. J&W already takes deposits (RIBEYE CANADIAN PRIME DEPOSIT $100).\n\n| Share | Price | Planning weight (est., to confirm) | Est. $/lb | Deposit |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1/16 beef (Concept) | $325 = $1,200 ÷ 4 + $25 split-and-wrap | about 25–30 lb | $11–13 | $100 |\n| 1/8 beef (Concept) | $625 = $1,200 ÷ 2 + $25 | about 50–60 lb | $10–13 | $150 |\n| 1/4 beef (real listing) | $1,200 | about 100–120 lb | $10–12 | $300 |\n| Ontario whole lamb (real) | $475 ($400 + $75) | J&W to state | n/a | $400 |\n| Ontario half lamb (real) | $250 | J&W to state | n/a | $250 |\n\nRowe Farms and VG Meats sell 1/16 to 1/2 beef at $13.83–17.11/lb with stated weights (competitors-gta-meat-grocery.md §2). J&W pools 1/16 and 1/8 orders into whole quarters and cuts only when a quarter is fully reserved, so there are no orphan cuts. Cut sheet choices follow J&W's own quarter-cow list: striploin, sirloin, short loin, ribeye (boneless or bone-in), roasts, ground, brisket, shank, flank, skirt, bones.\n\n### 3.5 Launch: founding members and Wild Fork towns\n\n- Nov 2026: build, waitlist page, Instagram (30K followers) and email to existing customers.\n- First boxes ship the week of **Tue Dec 1, 2026** (cut-off Sun Nov 29).\n- Founding member credit: $30 off the first box for anyone joining by Sun Jan 31, 2027.\n- Wild Fork towns: Whitby (Tuesday GTA 1, $20 fee today), Oakville and Ancaster/Hamilton (Thursday GTA 3, $27), Newmarket (Friday GTA 4, $20). Geo-ads and a \"your route day\" landing card. Factual use of the Wild Fork name only, with a \"not connected to Wild Fork\" line, after a legal check.\n- Referral: the friend gets $25 off the first box, the member gets 4 bonus points on the next box.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The math (mid case unless stated)\n\n### 4.1 Year 1 (Nov 2026 to Oct 2027), startMonth = 2 (Dec 2026)\n\nAssumptions: 415 joins over 11 months; 11% monthly churn; 0.95 boxes per active member per month; $235 average order ($220 box + $15 add-ons); 0.75 incrementality.\n\n| Month | Joins | Active (end) | Boxes | Box revenue |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Nov 2026 | 0 (waitlist) | 0 | 0 | $0 |\n| Dec | 55 | 55 | 52 | $12.3K |\n| Jan 2027 | 35 | 84 | 80 | $18.7K |\n| Feb | 30 | 105 | 99 | $23.4K |\n| Mar | 30 | 123 | 117 | $27.5K |\n| Apr | 35 | 145 | 137 | $32.3K |\n| May | 40 | 169 | 160 | $37.7K |\n| Jun | 40 | 190 | 181 | $42.5K |\n| Jul | 35 | 204 | 194 | $45.6K |\n| Aug | 35 | 217 | 206 | $48.4K |\n| Sep | 40 | 233 | 221 | $52.0K |\n| Oct | 40 | 247 | 235 | $55.2K |\n| **Total** | **415** | | **1,683** | **$395.5K** |\n\nShares year 1: 30 x 1/16 ($9,750) + 15 x 1/8 ($9,375) + 8 x 1/4 ($9,600) + 8 whole lamb x $475 ($3,800) + 6 half lamb x $250 ($1,500) = **$34.0K**.\n\n**Year 1 = ($395.5K + $34.0K) x 0.75 = $322K new revenue** (gross Club revenue $429.6K).\n\n| Scenario | Joins | Churn | AOV | Incrementality | Year 1 new revenue | Year 2 run-rate |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Low | 249 | 13% | $215 | 0.75 | **$169K** | $305K |\n| Mid | 415 | 11% | $235 | 0.75 | **$322K** | **$612K** |\n| High | 540 | 9.5% | $245 | 0.80 | **$484K** | $957K |\n\n### 4.2 Run-rate (year 2, Nov 2027 to Oct 2028)\n\n36 joins a month, 11% churn: active members average 287 (steady state about 327). 287 x 0.95 x 12 = 3,268 boxes x $235 = $768.0K, plus shares $47.6K (1.4 x year 1) = $815.6K gross x 0.75 = **$612K a year**.\n\n### 4.3 Math notes\n\n- **Churn.** truLOCAL's CLTV (~$2,000) ÷ AOV (~$240) implies about 8 lifetime orders. Our 0.95 boxes a month ÷ 11% churn = 8.6 lifetime boxes. That matches, and it sits between replenishment churn (5–8%) and meal-kit churn (8–15%) in market-benchmarks.md.\n- **Boxes per month.** Cadence mix 15% every 2 weeks, 60% every 4, 15% every 6, 10% every 8 gives 1.14 scheduled boxes a month; about 12–15% skips brings it to 0.95.\n- **AOV.** Tier mix 35% Starter / 45% Family / 20% Freezer Fill = $220, plus $15 of extra points and Meal Butler add-ons = $235. truLOCAL's ~$240 AOV supports it.\n- **Margin.** 30% blended, per model-assumptions.md (raw boxes 25–35%). Build-up: if J&W's portioned raw shelf price carries about 35%, the free item (~4.5 pts), tote and liner (~1.4 pts) and Freezer Fill bonus points (~0.6 pts) bring the box to about 28.5%; $15 of add-ons at 45% lifts the blend to about 30%. If J&W's real raw margin is lower, cut the bonus points and the sirloin-level free options first.\n- **Incrementality 0.75** (low end of 0.75–0.85) because this play converts some of J&W's existing raw buyers.\n- **Cannibalization cost** not covered by the factor: members who would have ordered anyway stop paying the route fee and now get a free item. About 25% of boxes x ($20 fee + ~$10 item) = **~$12.6K in year 1, ~$24.5K in year 2**. Counted in contribution below.\n- **No separate Wild Fork line.** Early screening added \"150 households x $150 x 10 months = $225K\" for Wild Fork switchers. We do not add it; those households are inside the 415 joins, so there is no double count.\n- **Sanity check.** Year 1 gross Club revenue is ~17% of J&W's ~$2.5M mid-case revenue; new revenue is +13% in year 1 and +24% at run-rate. Year 2 is about 63 boxes a week and about 1,250 lb a week of extra frozen raw volume.\n- **Contribution (ESTIMATE).** Year 1: gross profit $322K x 30% = $96.6K, less operating costs ~$60.4K, setup $11K and cannibalization ~$12.6K = **about +$12.6K**. Year 2: $612K x 30% = $183.6K, less ~$76.8K costs and ~$24.5K cannibalization = **about +$82K a year**. This is a low-margin, steady play. Its value is recurring volume and the cooked-line cross-sell, not big profit per box.\n\n### 4.4 J&W's own costs (excluding the Minastech fee)\n\n**Setup ≈ $11,000:** 500 reusable insulated Club totes at ~$10 ($5,000); product, cut-sheet and box photography ($2,500); printed cut sheets, inserts, labels ($1,000); subscription and bundle app setup and testing, ~40 staff hours ($1,000); test-quarter weigh and yield test ($500); launch landing page and ad creative by staff ($1,000).\n\n**Monthly at run-rate ≈ $6,400** (year 1 average ≈ $5,000):\n\n| Line | Run-rate / month | Basis |\n|---|---|---|\n| Paid ads (Meta, Google geo) | $1,400 | $1,800/mo Nov–Jan launch, then $1,400; paid CAC ≈ $82 |\n| First-box and referral credits | $460 | $25 friend credit + 4 bonus points to the referrer |\n| Route stops | $1,900 | 272 boxes x 70% new stops x $10/stop |\n| Club lead staff time | $1,690 | ~15 h/week x $26 loaded |\n| Head butcher time | $390 | ~3 h/week x $30 |\n| Apps, SMS, Klaviyo share, AI tokens | $450 | subscription + bundle app ~$150–300, SMS ~$0.15/msg, tokens < $50 |\n| Tote replacement | $150 | ~10% loss a month |\n\nYear 1 acquisition all-in ≈ $25.3K (ads $18K + founding credits $2.7K + referrals $4.6K) = **~$61 blended per member**, ~$82 per paid member (model range $60–120). Packaging liners and the free item sit inside the 30% gross margin, not here.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Who at J&W does the work\n\n- **Club lead** (an existing online or counter staffer): ~8 h/week at launch, ~15 h/week by year 2. Packing (~10 min a box), member messages the agents can't close, approving agent drafts.\n- **Head butcher:** ~3 h/week. Cut-to-thickness steaks, share cut sheets, pooled-quarter cutting, weekly yield log.\n- **Owner:** ~1 h/week approving offers, ad budgets and save-flow rules.\n- **Drivers:** ~22 extra stops a week by April 2027 and ~44 by year 2, on existing route days.\n\n---\n\n## 6. AI agents this play needs (to merge into the shared roster)\n\n| Agent | Job in this play | Where it shows in the prototype |\n|---|---|---|\n| Box Reminder & Save agent (shared roster: Auto-reorder and subscription agent) | Texts each member 3 days before the Sunday cut-off with their picks (reply YES, SKIP or SWAP), handles swaps in plain language within the points budget, runs the cancel-save flow (skip, slower cadence, smaller box, pause) inside owner rules, chases failed payments | Team: \"Thursday reminder\" approval and Retention queue. Customer: My Club skip, pause and cancel |\n| Butler Concierge | Answers members on chat, SMS and Instagram (points, delivery day, shares, thawed packs). Sends refunds, allergy questions and out-of-Ontario requests to staff | Ask the Butler chat; Concierge log with a refund awaiting approval |\n| Production forecast & pick-list agent | After the cut-off, totals every box and free item into Monday's pick list: cases to pull, steaks to cut by thickness, cooked packs for the kitchen | Team: Monday pick list with Approve |\n| Carcass & share-pooling agent (shared roster: Carcass and cut-yield optimizer) | Pools 1/16 and 1/8 orders into full quarters, suggests \"Cut of the month\" point deals for overstocked cuts, logs yield so planning weights become real weights | Team: Beef share board; short-rib offer in Approvals |\n| Route & delivery agent | Slots Club boxes into existing route days, shows new stops and added minutes, sends delivery-window texts | Team: Route impact table |\n| Lifecycle marketing agent | Drafts waitlist, welcome, Wild Fork-town, share-season (BBQ, Greek Easter May 2, 2027, fall freezer fill) and win-back emails in J&W's voice | Team: spring email draft in Approvals |\n| Paid ads optimizer | Runs geo ads in Club towns (Whitby, Oakville, Ancaster, Newmarket first), reads CAC by town weekly, proposes budget moves under a monthly cap | Team: \"Move $10/day to Newmarket\" proposal |\n\nEvery agent proposes and a person approves. No silent charges: members confirm or the box ships as already set, and every change is shown before the cut-off.\n\n---\n\n## 7. KPIs\n\n| KPI | Target |\n|---|---|\n| Active members | 120 by Mar 31, 2027 · 245 by Oct 31, 2027 · ~300 by Oct 2028 |\n| Joins per month | 30–40 (55 in launch month) |\n| Monthly churn | ≤ 11% (stretch 8%) |\n| Boxes per active member per month | ≥ 0.95 |\n| Average order incl. add-ons | ≥ $235 |\n| Paid CAC / blended CAC | ≤ $90 / ≤ $65 |\n| Members buying Meal Butler cooked items within 60 days of their first free pack | ≥ 15% |\n| Shares reserved, year 1 | 67 (53 beef, 14 lamb) |\n| Members in Wild Fork towns by Mar 31, 2027 | 40+ |\n| Thawed or quality complaints | < 1% of boxes |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Assumptions\n\n1. Soft launch Tue Dec 1, 2026; November is build and waitlist (no revenue).\n2. 415 joins in year 1 (55 in December, 30–40 a month after) and 36 a month in year 2.\n3. 11% monthly churn; 0.95 boxes per active member per month.\n4. $235 average order: $220 box (35/45/20 tier mix) + $15 add-ons.\n5. 30% blended gross margin; 0.75 incrementality.\n6. A point = about $5 of J&W shelf price; J&W's raw shelf prices hold through year 1.\n7. Route stop cost $10 (model range $8–14); 70% of Club boxes are new stops.\n8. Share planning weights (quarter ~100–120 lb take-home) are estimates until J&W weighs a test quarter.\n9. Shares year 1: 30 x 1/16, 15 x 1/8, 8 x 1/4, 8 whole and 6 half lamb; year 2 is 1.4x.\n10. Wild Fork switchers sit inside the join numbers, not in a separate line.\n11. J&W's vans and route times keep boxes frozen; long-haul boxes get an insulated tote with gel or dry ice.\n12. Shopify Subscriptions (or Recharge/Loop) plus a bundle app can run a points builder. A custom builder is a fallback.\n\n## 9. Risks\n\n1. **Thin margin.** Raw is about 30%. The free item and free delivery can push a Starter box under 25% if J&W's real shelf margin is below 35%. Confirm SKU costs before launch.\n2. **Cannibalization.** Existing raw buyers join and stop paying route fees. Track each member's spend before joining; the $139 minimum box protects order size.\n3. **Churn above 11%.** Meal-kit churn runs 8–15%. Mitigate with skip and pause, slower cadences, the Thursday reminder and payment retries (failed payments cause about half of subscription churn).\n4. **Cold chain and capacity.** Year 2 adds ~1,250 lb a week of frozen volume. Check van freezer space, Brimley freezer space and route times, especially Friday long-haul.\n5. **Share weights.** Unstated weights invite disputes. Weigh a test quarter and sell with a guaranteed minimum take-home weight.\n6. **Wild Fork name.** Trademark and ad-platform rules limit use in ad text. Use factual town and date messaging; legal review first.\n7. **Ontario-only.** J&W is provincially inspected, so Club boxes cannot leave Ontario. The Friday long-haul route covers Ottawa, Kingston, London and Windsor.\n8. **Meat cost inflation.** truLOCAL's parent cited higher meat COGS in Q1 2026. Review point values quarterly.\n9. **App fit.** Points-budget bundles may need a paid bundle app or custom code on Shopify.\n10. **Overlap.** Keep the Club raw-only so it does not cannibalize Play 1 (cooked plans) or Play 5 (gifting).\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- truLOCAL: https://trulocal.ca/ · https://trulocal.ca/pricing/ · https://trulocal.ca/faq · https://trulocal.ca/get-started/ · https://trulocal.ca/free-life/ · https://trulocal.ca/free-10yr/ · https://trulocal.ca/curated-boxes/ · https://trulocal.ca/value-boxes/ · https://trulocal.ca/10-year · https://trulocal.ca/gifts/\n- truLOCAL reviews: https://ca.trustpilot.com/review/trulocal.ca\n- EMERGE Commerce, truLOCAL 10th-anniversary update (AOV, CLTV, CAC), Jan 21, 2026: https://www.emerge-commerce.com/news/News/news-details/2026/EMERGE-Provides-Corporate-Update-on-truLOCAL-as-the-Brand-Celebrates-its-10th-Anniversary\n- EMERGE acquisition of truLOCAL (2021): https://www.emerge-commerce.com/news/News/news-details/2021/EMERGE-Acquires-truLOCAL-the-Market-Leader-in-Direct-to-Consumer-Meat-Subscriptions-for-up-to-16.8M\n- EMERGE, +193% net new subscriptions Feb 2025: https://www.emerge-commerce.com/news/News/news-details/2025/EMERGE-Brand-truLOCAL-Sees-a-193-Increase-in-Net-New-Subscriptions-in-February-2025-as-Support-Local-Movement-Grows\n- Wild Fork exits Canada (Retail Insider, Oct 2026): https://retail-insider.com/retail-insider/2026/10/wild-fork-exits-canada-abandoning-ambitious-ontario-expansion-plans/\n- J&W: https://www.jwfoods.com · https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/home (routes and fees) · https://www.jwfoods.com/products/quarter-cow-purchase · https://www.jwfoods.com/products/aaa-beef-ribeye-steak-special-10x10oz · https://www.jwfoods.com/products/portioned-4kg-cases-of-frozen-chicken-breasts · https://www.jwfoods.com/products/untitled-mar22_05-40 · https://www.jwfoods.com/products/canadian-prime-rib-deposit\n- J&W reviews (Google 4.8★ / 611 via aggregator): https://yably.ca/reviews/toronto/j-w-foods-2201-brimley-road\n- Price benchmarks: https://sanagansmeatlocker.com/products.json · https://rowefarmsonline.ca/products.json · https://vgfarmtocity.ca/products.json · https://olliffe.ca/products.json · https://butcherbox.ca/\n- ButcherBox (free-for-life hook, 2/4/6/8-week cadence): https://www.butcherbox.com/ · https://hip2keto.com/butcher-box-price/\n- Churn benchmarks: https://www.eightx.co/blog/average-subscription-churn-rate-by-category\n- Internal: research/competitor-03.md, selection.json (pick 3), model-assumptions.md, jw-facts.md, jw-audit.md, market-benchmarks.md, competitors-gta-meat-grocery.md, competitors-global.md, ai-agents-research.md, jw-catalog.json, site/assets/catalog.js"}, {"key": "play-04", "file": "play-04.md", "title": "Play 4: J&W at Work", "blurb": "The full play: mechanism, math, KPIs, agents, assumptions and risks.", "md": "# Play 4: J&W at Work\n\n**One line:** A workplace meal perk for GTA employers. The company covers part or all of each Meal Butler meal, staff pre-order by the Sunday cut-off, and J&W drops one order of named bags at the site on a route it already drives. Sites with 75+ staff on site also get a loaned freezer, restocked weekly.\n\n**Modelled on:** WeCook for Business (Montreal, sells in Ontario). We borrow ideas only, never its copy, logos or photos.\n\n**Why it makes money:** One dock drop of 50+ prepaid meals, on a route the J&W van already drives, is the cheapest and highest-margin order J&W can take. The employer subsidy keeps staff ordering every week.\n\n**Headline numbers (estimates):**\n\n| | Low | Mid | High |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Year 1 added revenue (Nov 2026 to Oct 2027, live from Jan 2027) | $68K | **$162K** | $323K |\n| Run-rate, year 2 | | **$622K** | |\n| Gross margin | | **40%** | |\n| JW's running cost, year 1 | | ~$54.5K (incl. $10.8K freezers) | |\n\nStart month: **3 (January 2027)**. November and December go to the build, labels and three friendly pilots. HR wellness budgets reset in January.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What WeCook for Business proves\n\nVerified in `competitor-04.md` (checked Oct 16 2026):\n\n- WeCook delivers about **90,000 meals a week** with **nearly 400 staff** ([Retail Insider, Aug 26 2025](https://retail-insider.com/?p=177907)). Trustpilot shows **4.8 from 6,584 reviews** ([Trustpilot](https://www.trustpilot.com/review/wecookmeals.ca)).\n- On the B2B platform the CEO puts meals at **\"$11.75-ish\"**, against **$20-25** for traditional catering. Employers can **\"contribute $1 per meal, or 100%\"**. The program fits **20 to 500 employees**, and larger firms get on-site fridges (Retail Insider). The article is 14 months old, but the B2B page and dashboard copy were live on Oct 16 2026.\n- Consumer plans cost **$11.99-15.49 a meal**, with a 6-meal minimum ([WeCook plans API](https://www.wecookmeals.ca/api/plans)).\n- Mechanics behind the dashboard (from WeCook's own UI strings):\n  - an allowance engine with four types ($ per employee, $ on meals only, N meals per employee, $ per meal up to a cap) and caps;\n  - invites locked to the company email domain;\n  - a weekly joint order that cancels itself when nobody orders;\n  - a live order roll-up;\n  - a receipt split into \"covered by the company\" and \"paid by the employee(s)\";\n  - a company-wide volume price tier.\n- Its B2B social proof is a set of small Quebec firms. It ships from Quebec through couriers and has no GTA plant.\n\n## 2. What J&W changes (the mechanism)\n\n1. **The employer picks a perk.** The options are dollars off each meal ($2 up to the full price), lunches covered each week (1 to 5 per person), or everything covered up to a weekly cap. HR can add an optional monthly budget cap. The company's share is drawn from a **prepaid company wallet** that is topped up monthly by card or EFT, so J&W carries no receivables.\n2. **Staff join with their work email** and order from a 20-item **At Work menu** of real Meal Butler items by J&W's existing Sunday cut-off. Examples:\n   - Teriyaki Chicken, Green Beans & Rice, $9.99\n   - Tenders, Broccoli & Sweet Potato, 4 oz, $9.99\n   - Tilapia, Asparagus & Brown Rice, 4 oz, $11.99\n   - Jerk Chicken, Green Beans & Sweet Potato, 4 oz, $12.99\n   - Salmon, Brussels & Quinoa, 4 oz, $12.99\n   - Souvlaki, Broccoli & Greek Potatoes, 4 oz, $13.99\n   - Chicken Souvlaki Wrap, $5.99\n   - Chicken Chili, $9.99\n   - Egg White Bites, Spinach & Feta, $5.25\n   - Steel Cut Oats, $4.40\n3. **Work pricing (Concept).** This is a company-wide volume tier, borrowed as an idea from WeCook:\n   - under 40 items a week: published prices;\n   - **40-99 items: 5% off every item**;\n   - **100+ items: 10% off**. A $12.99 pre-set meal then costs $11.69 before the allowance.\n4. **One drop on an existing route.** Each week's orders go out as one stop of sealed, **named bags** on the route day already serving that city:\n   - Tuesday: Durham\n   - Wednesday: Toronto, Scarborough, North York, Etobicoke, Mississauga\n   - Thursday: Halton and Hamilton\n   - Friday: York and Peel\n\n   Delivery is **free when the week's team order is $250+**. Below that, the usual $20 route fee applies. If nobody orders in a week, nothing ships and nothing is billed.\n5. **The At Work Freezer (Concept).** Sites with 75+ staff on site and $250+ a week in combined orders get a **loaned glass-door freezer**:\n   - J&W restocks it on the route day, and the production agent sets par levels per site.\n   - Staff scan a QR code and pay with their allowance or a card.\n   - J&W counts stock at each visit. If unpaid items pass **3%** of what was stocked, the company covers the difference at work prices.\n\n   This suits hospitals, care homes, warehouses and plants, where night shifts and closed cafeterias are the norm.\n6. **Team Table packs (Concept).** These are priced at the sum of J&W's published prices and ride the same drop:\n   - **Lunch & Learn Pack for 12, $147.88**: 4 Souvlaki, Broccoli & Greek Potatoes, 4 Teriyaki Chicken, Green Beans & Rice, and 4 Salmon, Brussels & Quinoa.\n   - **Breakfast Meeting Pack for 12, $115.80**: 12 Egg White Bites, Spinach & Feta and 12 Steel Cut Oats.\n   - **Team BBQ Pack for about 25, $134.95**: a No-Salt Chicken Souvlaki Case (5 kg, about 50 raw skewers) at $95, Tzatziki 1.5 L at $15, and 5 packs of Whole Grain Greek Pita at $4.99 each. This is the butcher advantage WeCook cannot match.\n7. **HR gets:**\n   - one monthly split invoice;\n   - a monthly report covering participation, meals, company and staff spend, estimated protein per lunch, and the per-person company-paid total for payroll;\n   - a free quarterly 30-minute lunch-and-learn for accounts ordering 40+ items a week;\n   - a step-challenge prize: a $60 Meal Butler credit each quarter (Concept).\n8. **Sales are assisted, not self-serve.** A lead form with a \"Who are the meals for?\" question routes each lead:\n   - gyms and clinics that resell go to Play 2;\n   - gifts go to Play 5;\n   - teams and schools go to Play 7;\n   - \"just me\" goes to Play 1.\n\n   The B2B prospecting agent drafts outreach to employers along the route corridors. The account lead or the owner runs free tastings (8 pre-set meals, $99.92 at published prices, about $55 food cost).\n\n### Weekly cycle\n\n| Day | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| Monday | Next week's menu opens. The previous week's company share is posted to the wallet. |\n| Thursday | The order desk agent drafts reminders for staff who opted in and have not ordered. Staff approve the batch. |\n| Sunday 11:59 pm | Cut-off (J&W's existing rule). Weeks with no orders cancel automatically. |\n| Monday-Tuesday | Cook, portion and pack named bags (J&W's existing 2-3 business-day fulfilment). Freezer pars are set. |\n| Tue / Wed / Thu / Fri | Dock drop and freezer restock on the city's route day. The site contact gets an ETA window. |\n\n### Why J&W wins against WeCook here\n\n- **Frozen, vacuum-sealed meals** suit an unattended office freezer. There is no use-by pressure and no Friday waste. WeCook's fresh trays need fridges and quick turnover.\n- **The Scarborough plant and its own routes.** The Scarborough, Markham, North York, Vaughan and Mississauga business parks sit on the Wednesday and Friday runs. WeCook ships from Quebec by courier.\n- **Prices at or below WeCook's.** J&W's pre-set meals cost $9.99-13.99 for 4 oz, against WeCook's $11.99-15.49, with no plan minimum per employee.\n- **Macro-exact à la carte portions**, plus a promise of no added salt, sugar or oil. WeCook's sample bowl carries 795-1,015 mg sodium and canola oil (`competitor-04.md` section 6). This goes in the business case only. Customer copy never names a competitor.\n- **A butcher behind the meals.** The same account can buy a BBQ pack, or holiday boxes through Play 5 (counted there, not here).\n\n## 3. The math\n\nAll figures in CAD and all are ESTIMATES. Shared assumptions come from `model-assumptions.md`: cooked-meal margin 45% less the tier discount, incrementality 0.85 (the top of 0.75-0.85, because this is a new buyer), route cost $8-14 a stop, and 48 order weeks a year.\n\n**Unit economics of one account at steady state.** A typical site has 100-140 staff on site, three on-site days a week and a $3-4 allowance.\n- 25-30% of on-site staff order each week, at 2.2 items each, which gives **about 55 items a week**.\n- **Average net item price: about $10.00.**\n  - Lunches are 75% of items, at an average published 4 oz price of $12.32 across the 9 menu meals.\n  - Breakfast items are 25%, at about $5.\n  - That gives a $10.49 list mix, less about 5% for the volume tier.\n- One account is worth about $550 a week, or about $26.4K a year.\n- At 40% margin, the gross profit is about $10.6K a year. The added route cost is about $530 a year (48 stops × $11).\n\n**Year 1 (Nov 2026 to Oct 2027, live from Jan 2027), mid case:**\n- Team orders:\n  - Live accounts ramp from 3 in Jan to 16 in Oct (3, 4, 6, 8, 10, 11, 12, 12, 14, 16), which is **96 account-months**.\n  - 96 account-months × 4 order weeks × 40 items a week (new accounts start at about half volume) × $10.00 = **$153,600**.\n- Freezers: installed from Mar, ramping from 1 to 6 (1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 5, 5, 6), which is **30 freezer-months**. 30 × 4.2 weeks × $250 a week = **$31,500**.\n- Team Table packs: 96 account-months × 0.4 packs × $150 = **$5,760**.\n- Gross: $190,860 × 0.85 incrementality = **$162,231, about $162K**.\n\n**Low ($68K):**\n- 9 accounts by Oct, which is 57 account-months, at 30 items a week: 57 × 4 × 30 × $10 = $68,400.\n- 3 freezers, which is 12 freezer-months: 12 × 4.2 × $200 = $10,080.\n- Packs: 57 × 0.2 × $150 = $1,710.\n- Total: $80,190 × 0.85 = $68,162.\n\n**High ($323K):**\n- 24 accounts by Oct, which is 145 account-months, at 50 items a week and $10.50: 145 × 4 × 50 × $10.50 = $304,500.\n- 10 freezers, which is 50 freezer-months: 50 × 4.2 × $300 = $63,000.\n- Packs: 145 × 0.55 × $150 = $11,963.\n- Total: $379,463 × 0.85 = $322,543.\n\n**Run-rate, year 2 (Nov 2027 to Oct 2028):**\n- 22 average live accounts (16 rising to about 28) × 55 items × $10.00 × 48 weeks = $580,800.\n- 9 freezers × $300 × 50 weeks = $135,000.\n- 22 accounts × 5 packs × $150 = $16,500.\n- Gross: $732,300 × 0.85 = **$622,455, about $622K**.\n\n**Gross profit and JW's own costs:**\n- Year 1:\n  - Gross profit is about $65K (40% of $162K).\n  - Costs are about $54.5K: $13.8K setup plus 11 × $3.7K a month.\n  - That is **roughly break-even**, and the freezers are reusable capex.\n- Year 2:\n  - Gross profit is about $249K.\n  - Running costs are about $80K: a coordinator at 20 h a week, more packing hours, about 22 extra stops a week and 3 more freezers.\n  - That leaves **about $170K contribution** before Minastech's fee.\n\n**Sanity checks:**\n- Year 1 mid is about 6.5% of J&W's ~$2.5M mid-case revenue. The run-rate is about 25%. Both sit within the $60-450K year-1 and below-$900K run-rate guardrails.\n- 28 live accounts by Oct 2028 is about **0.2%** of the ~11-14K GTA sites with 50+ staff ([York Region factsheet](https://yorklink.ca/2025-york-region-business-counts-factsheet/), `market-benchmarks.md` section 6a). Toronto alone has ~810K office jobs ([Toronto Employment Survey 2025](https://www.toronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/97af-CityPlanning-Toronto-Employment-Survey-Two-Pager-Summary-2025.pdf)).\n- The benchmark in `market-benchmarks.md` section 11 (40 offices × 25 staff × 2 meals × $13 × 48 ≈ $1.25M) is about twice this plan. The plan assumes lower prices and fewer accounts.\n- Year-2 volume is about **1,470 extra items a week** (about 1,210 pre-ordered plus about 260 from freezers). That is likely +1 cook/packer FTE. Plant headroom must be confirmed with J&W (`market-benchmarks.md` section 12).\n- Selection planning midpoint: about $0.6M run-rate. The selection note gave $0.2-0.3M for year 1. This plan is more conservative because of the January start.\n\n**Not counted here, to avoid double counting:** gifts bought by the same employers (Play 5), resale by gyms and clinics (Play 2), team and school fundraisers (Play 7), and staff who later subscribe at home (Plays 1 and 6).\n\n## 4. Costs to J&W (excluding Minastech's fee)\n\n**Setup: about $13,800.**\n- 6 glass-door merchandiser freezers at about $1,800 each: $10,800. This is an estimate; plan on $1,500-2,500 each.\n- QR signage, freezer wraps and printed menus: $1,500.\n- Pilot agreement template and an insurance check for loaned equipment: $1,500.\n\n**Monthly: about $3,700 (year-1 average).**\n- Part-time At Work coordinator, 12 h a week × about $28/h loaded: $1,450.\n- Named-bag pick, pack and label labour, about 0.75 h per account-week × $24/h: $745.\n- Extra stops on existing routes, about 10 a week × $11, plus freezer restocks: $475.\n- Software and AI tokens (prospecting tool share, B2B or wallet app, Claude tokens): $300.\n- Paid search and LinkedIn for \"office lunch program\" terms in the 416/905: $400.\n- Tasting boxes (4 a month × about $55 food cost) and step-challenge prizes: $330.\n\n**Year 2:** about $80K a year, as volume grows (see section 3).\n\n## 5. Who does the work\n\n- **Account lead or the owner:** 6 h a week on tastings, HR calls and pilot check-ins.\n- **At Work coordinator (part-time):** 12 h a week in year 1, rising to 20 h in year 2. Covers onboarding, wallet top-ups, freezer counts, invoices and approving agent drafts.\n- **Kitchen:** 4-8 extra packing hours a week for named bags, part of a second cook/pack shift by year 2.\n- **Drivers:** 10-15 minutes per extra dock drop on existing routes, plus a 20-minute freezer restock.\n- **Minastech:** builds the portal, sets up the agents and trains the team (fee separate).\n\n## 6. KPIs\n\n| KPI | Target |\n|---|---|\n| Employer accounts live | 16 by Oct 2027; 28 by Oct 2028 |\n| Weekly participation (on-site staff who order) | 25-30% |\n| Items per account per week | 40 in year 1 (ramp), 55 at steady state |\n| Average net item price | about $10.00 |\n| Tasting to pilot / pilot to paid | 40% / 70% |\n| Account retention at 6 months | 85%+ |\n| Freezer sales per freezer | $250 a week in year 1, $300 in year 2; shrink under 3% |\n| Gross margin | 40%+ |\n| Added time per dock drop | under 15 minutes (under $14 a stop) |\n\n## 7. AI agents this play needs\n\n| Agent | Job here | On screen in the prototype |\n|---|---|---|\n| **B2B prospecting and account agent** | Builds lists of GTA employers with 50+ staff along J&W's route corridors (Scarborough, Markham, North York, Vaughan, Mississauga). Drafts CASL-compliant intro emails to HR, books tastings, and watches live accounts for falling participation, with save-offer suggestions. | Team view: the drafted intro email to Maple Ridge Packaging, the Don Mills Design Co. save-offer and the pipeline board, all waiting for approval |\n| **At Work order desk agent** (new) | Runs each employer's weekly order. It applies allowances and caps, drafts Thursday reminders for opted-in staff, closes at the Sunday cut-off, cancels empty weeks, rolls picks into one named-bag pick list per site, and drafts the split company/staff invoice. | Staff app allowance meter and confirmation; team view reminder-SMS approval and the Northline split invoice |\n| **Production forecast and prep-sheet agent** | Adds At Work volume to Monday's cook, portion and pack plan, and sets freezer par levels per site from sell-through | Team view cook-plan change (648 items) and the Milliken freezer par change |\n| **Route and delivery experience agent** | Slots dock drops and freezer restocks into the existing Tue-Fri routes and texts each site contact an ETA window | Team view route impact (Wednesday: 7 drops, about +52 min) |\n| **Nutrition data and label agent** | Drafts the full prepackaged labels needed for meals sold from a workplace freezer: ingredients, allergens, Nutrition Facts, the front-of-pack symbol and from-frozen heating times. Also supplies the protein figures in the HR report. A labelling consultant approves. | Protein badges (est.) on menu cards; average protein in the HR report |\n| **Butler Concierge 24/7 support and sales chat** | Answers HR and staff questions on pricing, route day, allergens, the freezer and the taxable-benefit caveat. Hands allergies and complaints to staff. | The \"At Work Desk\" chat on this page |\n\nHuman checkpoints:\n- Every outbound email or SMS, every price or allowance change, and every freezer par change is approved by J&W staff.\n- The owner approves any pricing outside the published tiers.\n\n## 8. Assumptions\n\n- Launch in January 2027 with 3 friendly pilots. November and December go to the build, labels and recruiting.\n- About 5 tastings a month. 40% become pilots and 70% of pilots convert, so about 1.4 net new accounts a month after a little churn.\n- An average account has 100-140 staff on site, 25-30% weekly participation and 2.2 items per person on 3 on-site days, which gives 55 items a week at steady state. New accounts start at about half that.\n- The average item is about $10.00 net: 75% lunches (avg $12.32 published) and 25% breakfast (about $5), less about 5% for the volume tier.\n- 48 order weeks a year (holidays and summer skips).\n- About 40% of accounts qualify for a freezer, at about $1,800 each. Sales are $250 a week in year 1 and $300 in year 2. The low end of the $300-900 smart-fridge benchmark is used because the menu is frozen.\n- Gross margin is 40%: the cooked 45%, less the tier discount, shrink and named-bag packaging.\n- Incrementality is 0.85. Some staff already buy J&W at home.\n- Drops ride existing routes at about $8-14 a stop. Delivery is free on $250+ weeks.\n- The company share is prepaid through a wallet, so there are no receivables.\n- J&W confirms microwave times from frozen for each At Work item before launch. J&W supplies heating steps for each item before launch; none are published yet.\n\n## 9. Risks\n\n- **A slow B2B sales cycle.** HR budgets are set in Q4 and Q1. The mitigations are free-delivery pilots, a January launch and a route-day pitch (\"our truck is already in Markham on Fridays\").\n- **Labels gate the freezer.** Meals sold away from J&W's store need full prepackaged labels: ingredients, allergens, Nutrition Facts, and the front-of-pack symbol, which has been mandatory since Jan 2026. Budget a labelling consultant, with the label agent drafting.\n- **Claims must match the labels.** Some jwfoods.com ingredient lists conflict with the site-wide promise of no salt, sugar or added oil. Chicken Breast Souvlaki, for example, lists salt and oil. Reconcile these before pitching clinics and hospitals.\n- **Participation fades after the first month.** The mitigations are menu rotation, Thursday reminders, allowance nudges from the account agent and the quarterly lunch-and-learn.\n- **Hybrid work cuts on-site days.** The frozen format and take-home help, but volume may sit at the low end.\n- **Taxable benefit.** Subsidized meals can be a taxable benefit for staff. J&W must not give tax advice. The portal reports each person's company-paid amount for payroll.\n- **Plant capacity.** About 1,470 extra items a week by year 2 may need a second cook/pack shift.\n- **Freezer shrink or damage** under honour-system QR pay. The mitigations are the 3% clause, weekly counts and a lock option for public-facing sites.\n- **Competitive response.** WeCook already sells B2B in Ontario. J&W's local routes, the freezer format and the butcher range are the moat.\n- **Founder bandwidth.** Tastings depend on Bill or the account lead. Protect 6 h a week.\n\n## 10. How this stays distinct from the other six plays\n\n| Play | Buyer | Difference |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2. Pro Partner Portal | Gyms and clinics that **resell** to members | Here the **employer** funds meals for its own staff. Nothing is resold. |\n| 5. Corporate Gift Studio | Employers buying **gifts** | A gift order from an At Work account is counted in Play 5 only |\n| 1 and 6. Goal Plan Builder, AI Coach | Individuals at home | Staff who later subscribe at home count there |\n| 7. Team Fuel | Teams and schools raising money | The lead form routes them to Play 7 |\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- WeCook for Business: https://www.wecookmeals.ca/en/business\n- WeCook plans API (consumer prices): https://www.wecookmeals.ca/api/plans\n- WeCook UI string file (B2B dashboard mechanics): https://www.wecookmeals.ca/js/i18n.js\n- Retail Insider, \"WeCook launches 'WeCook for Business'\" (Aug 26 2025): https://retail-insider.com/?p=177907\n- Trustpilot, WeCook: https://www.trustpilot.com/review/wecookmeals.ca\n- Strategy, WeCook and P.K. Subban (Aug 26 2025): https://strategyonline.ca/2025/08/26/wecook-pk-subban/\n- City of Toronto Employment Survey 2025: https://www.toronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/97af-CityPlanning-Toronto-Employment-Survey-Two-Pager-Summary-2025.pdf\n- York Region business counts (StatCan CBC, Jun 2025): https://yorklink.ca/2025-york-region-business-counts-factsheet/\n- Byte smart-fridge pricing (phase-2 comparison): https://www.bytetechnology.co/pricing\n- Job Bank, delivery driver wages, Ontario: https://www.jobbank.gc.ca/marketreport/wages-occupation/21667/ON\n- J&W delivery routes and cut-off: https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/home\n- J&W products and prices: https://www.jwfoods.com/products.json\n- J&W Google rating (4.8, 611 reviews) via Yably: https://yably.ca/reviews/toronto/j-w-foods-2201-brimley-road\n- Internal: `research/competitor-04.md`, `research/selection.json` (pick 4), `research/model-assumptions.md`, `research/market-benchmarks.md` (sections 6a, 8, 9, 10, 11), `research/jw-facts.md`, `research/jw-audit.md`, `research/ai-agents-research.md` (agents 2, 3, 6, 12, 13), `site/assets/catalog.js`"}, {"key": "play-05", "file": "play-05.md", "title": "Play 5: Corporate Gift Studio", "blurb": "The full play: mechanism, math, KPIs, agents, assumptions and risks.", "md": "# Play 5: Corporate Gift Studio\n\nPrepared by Minastech for J&W Foods / J&W Meal Butler. Date: October 16, 2026. All money in CAD. Every figure is an estimate unless it is J&W's own published price or a cited source.\n\n**One line:** a self-serve gift studio where GTA employers, realtors, brokers and clinics send J&W boxes with their logo to a whole list, or send a Pick-a-Box certificate that each person redeems on J&W's own delivery routes.\n\n**Why it makes money:** corporate gifting sells J&W's existing $85 to $200 boxes 20 at a time at about 40% gross margin. It brings in holiday cash and puts J&W in front of hundreds of new households each season.\n\n**Modelled on:** [Omaha Steaks Business Gifts](https://www.omahasteaks.com/business-gifts) (global, Omaha, Nebraska). We borrow ideas only, never their copy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What we borrow from Omaha Steaks, and what J&W does better\n\nOmaha Steaks is a family butcher (founded 1917) with a dedicated business-gifting arm. It claims 5,000+ business clients, 350,000+ custom gift certificates and 700,000+ coolers delivered to business recipients since 2020. These are company claims from \"Omaha Steaks internal data in 2025\", verified on the live page on Oct 16, 2026 (competitor-05.md §2).\n\n| Omaha idea (verified) | J&W version |\n|---|---|\n| \"Choice\" certificates: each recipient picks from a curated level and redeems online, by phone or mail | **J&W Pick-a-Box certificate** (our own name). Four levels. The recipient picks a box and books their own route day, or picks up free at Brimley Rd |\n| Lead form with two one-tap qualifiers (gift count, spend per gift) | **\"Your price in two taps\"** right in the hero. It shows a real price at once, not a sales call |\n| Multi-recipient ordering on an emailed Excel form, one invoice | **In-browser list upload.** It sorts every address onto J&W's real route days live, flags anyone outside Ontario, and turns missing addresses into certificates |\n| Pay-on-redemption for large programs | Same, for 100+ certificate programs on approved accounts |\n| \"Customize Your Box\" points builder with a sticky footer | A sticky studio bar (gifts · box · total · Continue) |\n| Business Rewards by annual spend (US$5K / $20K / $50K) | **Gift Desk Rewards** at $2,500 / $7,500 / $15,000 a year, sized for GTA small and mid-size firms |\n| Holiday planning calendar | **\"Plan the year once\"**: a year-round occasion calendar with reminders |\n| Dark photo hero, \"at a glance\" card, big-number proof band, tier cards, FAQ, sticky action row | Same structure, in J&W navy with Mediterranean olive and terracotta |\n\n**Where J&W wins:**\n- **Omaha does not ship to Canada** ([Shipping](https://www.omahasteaks.com/info/Shipping-Information)). No Omaha-scale \"let them choose\" meat gift with local delivery exists in the GTA.\n- J&W already runs its own weekly refrigerated routes. They go to Durham on Tuesday, Toronto and Mississauga on Wednesday, Halton and Hamilton on Thursday, and York and Peel on Friday, plus long routes to Ottawa, London and Windsor. Fees are $20 to $35, and pickup is free (jw-facts.md).\n- It has its own Ontario-inspected plant (since 2008), and a family butcher story in Toronto that goes back to the early 1960s.\n- **Clean Meal Butler meals as a gift.** The meals are salt and sugar free, with no added oils, which makes a wellness gift Omaha cannot offer.\n- A Greek-Canadian identity (A Taste of Greece Box, 154 Mediterranean SKUs) suits Greek Easter gifting.\n- Omaha's B2B flow is not self-serve. J&W's studio would be ahead of it on ease of use.\n\n## 2. The mechanism (what J&W changes)\n\n1. **Four gift levels built on boxes J&W already sells.** Every level stays under the CRA's $500 yearly non-cash gift limit ([CRA](https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/businesses/topics/payroll/benefits-allowances/gifts-awards-social-events/gifts-awards-long-service-awards.html)).\n\n   | Level | Real J&W boxes (price) | Concept additions |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | **Thank You · $90** | Ultimate BBQ Dad Pack $84.99 (AAA T-bone, AAA tenderloin, teriyaki salmon, 2 kg Korean BBQ chicken tenders). Girls Night Box $89.99 (chicken, pork and shrimp souvlaki, 4 top sirloin steaks, spanakopita, party bites) | Meal Butler Taster $87.93 |\n   | **Well Done · $130** | Gentleman's Box $129.99 (2 AAA ribeyes 9-10 oz, 2 brisket bacon, hot Italian sausage, lean ground beef, 6 lamb chops). A Taste of Greece Box $130 (gyro, four souvlaki, Greek and Macedonian sausage, pita, tzatziki, 32 mini spanakopita). Steak Mania Combo $120 (8 steaks) | Meal Butler Clean Week $130 |\n   | **Big Thanks · $200** | Long Weekend Box $199 (8 × 8 oz AAA striploins, back ribs, sausages, burgers, sliders, buns, fries) | Greek Easter Table $199 (Mar 1 to May 1, 2027) |\n   | **Reserve · $350** | none | J&W Reserve Box $349 |\n\n2. **Three ways to deliver:**\n   - **One office drop** on the office's route day, for one route fee.\n   - **Home delivery to each person** on their own route day, at J&W's usual fee per address ($20 GTA, $27 Halton/Hamilton, $35 long routes).\n   - **A Pick-a-Box certificate** by email or printed card. Delivery is included for $20 a certificate, or the recipient picks up free at Brimley Rd. J&W waives the $100 route minimum for gift deliveries and redemptions.\n3. **Branding:** the buyer's logo and message go on a printed card in every box, or on the certificate. This is free on every order, and the buyer gets a proof before printing.\n4. **Volume pricing (Concept):** 10 to 49 gifts save 5%, 50 to 99 save 8%, 100 to 249 save 10%, and 250+ is quoted. The discount applies to the gifts only, never to delivery.\n5. **Payment:** card at checkout, or invoice Net 30 for approved businesses (first order: 50% deposit). Large certificate programs (100+) can pay only for certificates that get redeemed.\n6. **Year-round:**\n   - An occasion calendar covers Lunar New Year (Feb 6, 2027), Employee Appreciation Day (Mar 5), Easter (Mar 28), Administrative Professionals Day (Apr 21), Greek Easter (May 2), Victoria Day (May 24), Father's Day (Jun 20), Canada Day, Labour Day and Thanksgiving (Oct 11, 2027).\n   - Always-on gifts: realtor closings, work anniversaries, safety milestones and welcome-aboard boxes.\n   - Reminders go out three weeks ahead with last year's list ready.\n7. **Gift Desk Rewards (Concept):**\n   - **Partner, $2,500+ a year:** priority December route slots, and the list is saved for next year.\n   - **Preferred, $7,500+:** Partner perks, plus a J&W Reserve Box for the buyer ($349 value) and one named contact.\n   - **Patron, $15,000+:** Preferred perks, plus pay-on-redemption certificates and a team tasting night at Brimley Rd.\n8. **New customers:** each Pick-a-Box redemption asks for an email, with a CASL consent box left unchecked. Each box carries a \"first order\" card for the recipient.\n\n### Launch plan\n- **Holiday 2026 lean launch, live Mon Nov 9, 2026:**\n  - A landing page with the two-tap price, the four levels and logo cards.\n  - Office drops and home deliveries entered as Shopify draft orders.\n  - Pick-a-Box certificates issued as bulk Shopify gift-card codes with a branded email.\n  - A quote form, plus outreach to existing customers who run businesses and to businesses along the routes.\n- **Holiday 2026 deadlines:** order by Sunday, Dec 6 for logo cards and route delivery the week of Dec 14–18. Without logo cards, order by Sunday, Dec 13. Certificates go out by email up to Dec 24 and can be redeemed in January.\n- **Full studio, live Feb 1, 2027:** self-serve list upload with route sorting, a redemption page that only shows each level's menu, the occasion calendar, rewards and the Greek Easter Table.\n- **Holiday 2027:** planning emails go out Sep 14, 2027 and pre-booking opens in October 2027. That revenue lands in year 2.\n\n### Fixes J&W must make before selling to businesses\n- The A Taste of Greece Box description starts with pasted voice-dictation text (\"Do you know what I know who it is…\"). It also has an outdated \"only until Thursday\" line.\n- Gendered box names (Gentleman's, Girls Night, Dad Pack) suit consumer gifting. The studio shows them under neutral level names, with the real product name underneath.\n- Gift cards today are e-cards at $10 / $25 / $50 / $100 only, with no branding or bulk issue.\n\n## 3. Money\n\n### Year 1 (Nov 2026 – Oct 2027), mid case, with ramp from Nov 2026\n\n| Line | Formula | Gross |\n|---|---|---|\n| Holiday 2026 sprint (partial season, live Nov 9) | 25 accounts × 20 gifts × $120 | $60,000 |\n| Realtor and broker closing gifts (from Jan, ramp 0 → 35 agents) | avg 15 active × 10 months × 1.2 gifts × $95 | $17,100 |\n| Occasion campaigns, Lunar New Year to Thanksgiving | 25 accounts × 15 gifts × $110 | $41,250 |\n| Always-on milestone certificates (from Mar) | 8 accounts × 3 gifts/month × 8 months × $100 | $19,200 |\n| Follow-on orders from new-to-J&W recipients | 1,247 gifts × 60% new × 8% order once × $130 | $7,781 |\n| **Gross** | | **$145,331** |\n| **× 0.85 incrementality** (some existing customers already buy boxes for clients) | | **$123,532 ≈ $124K** |\n\nYear-1 range:\n- **Low $56K:** 15 × 18 × $115 holiday, 10 agents, 15 occasion accounts, 5 milestone accounts, 5% follow-on, × 0.75.\n- **High $269K:** 45 × 25 × $125 holiday, 25 agents at 1.4 gifts × $100, 40 occasion accounts × 18 × $115, 12 milestone accounts × 4 gifts, 10% follow-on, × 0.85.\n\nYear-1 monthly shape (gross, booked): Nov $21K · Dec $39K · Jan $3K · Feb $6K · Mar $10K · Apr $12K · May $14K · Jun $9K · Jul $6K · Aug $4K · Sep $7K · Oct $14K = $145K. Nov and Dec are 41% of year 1, because the first holiday season is only partial.\n\n### Run-rate (year 2, a full year)\n\n| Line | Formula | Gross |\n|---|---|---|\n| Holiday 2027 full season | 70 accounts (≈17 retained at 70% + 53 new) × 24 gifts × $122 | $204,960 |\n| Closings | 40 agents × 12 months × 1.2 × $95 | $54,720 |\n| Occasions | 40 accounts × 15 × $110 | $66,000 |\n| Milestones | 15 accounts × 3 × 12 × $100 | $54,000 |\n| Follow-on | 3,396 gifts × 60% × 8% × $130 | $21,191 |\n| **Gross** | | **$400,871** |\n| **× 0.85** | | **$340,740 ≈ $340K** |\n\nGross profit at 40%: year 1 ≈ $49K, run-rate ≈ $136K.\n\n### Sanity checks\n- Year 1 is about 5% of J&W's ~$2.5M mid-case revenue. The run-rate is about 14%.\n- The upside case in market-benchmarks.md is 115 firms × 60 boxes × $120 ≈ $830K, which is 1% of the ~11–14K GTA locations with 50+ staff. Our year 2 uses about 70 accounts at 24 gifts, well inside that.\n- The $120 average gift sits inside the $50–150 per-box estimate (market-benchmarks.md §6a) and Omaha's $100–$250 lead-form buckets.\n- **Capacity:**\n  - Holiday 2026 is about 500 gifts over 5 weeks, roughly 100 a week, with a peak near 280 in the week of Dec 14.\n  - Holiday 2027 is about 1,700 gifts over 7 weeks, around 250 a week. It needs one seasonal packer and an extra Wednesday van run in December.\n  - Certificates move part of the production into January.\n\n### Notes on the math\n- The 40% margin is the model's assumption for premium gift boxes. The $120 average already nets out volume pricing. On a box that earns 40% at list, an 8% discount brings margin down to about 35%, so big orders earn less per box.\n- No certificate breakage is counted as revenue. Prepaid certificates carry no expiry date. Pay-on-redemption programs bill only redeemed codes.\n- Revenue from other plays is not counted here:\n  - No office meal programs (Play 4) and no wholesale (Play 2).\n  - No Butcher Club subscriptions (Play 3) or fundraisers (Play 7).\n  - If a later version lets certificates redeem for a Butcher Club month, the certificate value counts here once, and any later months count in Play 3.\n- Follow-on orders are one-time orders only, so they are not double counted with subscriptions.\n\n### Costs to J&W (excluding Minastech's fee)\n- **Setup about $5,500:**\n  - Branded sleeves, insulated liners and card stock for the first 1,000 gifts: $2,400\n  - Photos of the levels and the certificate design: $700\n  - 20 tasting boxes for prospects, at cost: $1,200\n  - Card and label printer: $400\n  - Setup time, 20 h: $800\n- **Monthly average about $2,400 (year 1 ≈ $29K):**\n  - Ads (LinkedIn, Meta, Google): $730. Peaks at $1,500 in Nov–Dec and Sep–Oct, $350 otherwise.\n  - Gift Desk hours: $900. That is 20 h/week in Nov–Dec and 6 h/week otherwise, at $25/h loaded.\n  - Logo cards and sleeves, about $3 a gift: $310\n  - Extra December van runs: $200\n  - Seasonal packer: $150\n  - Software and AI: $125 (bulk certificate app, invoicing, shared prospecting data, Claude API)\n- **Year 1 contribution:** about $49K gross profit, less $29K running costs and $5.5K setup, leaves about $15K before Minastech's fee. Year 2 is about $136K, less about $45K, leaving about $90K.\n\n### KPIs\n| KPI | Target |\n|---|---|\n| Corporate accounts, Holiday 2026 | 25 by Dec 18, 2026 |\n| Gifts delivered, Holiday 2026 | 500 |\n| Average gift after volume pricing | $115–$125 |\n| Quote-to-order rate | 40%+ |\n| Pick-a-Box certificates redeemed within 90 days | 80%+ |\n| Holiday 2026 accounts back for Holiday 2027 | 70%+ |\n| Active realtor and broker closing accounts | 35 by Oct 2027 |\n| Redeemers who opt in to J&W email | 30%+ |\n| December on-time route delivery | 98%+ |\n\n## 4. Who does the work at J&W\n- **Gift Desk lead** (a named person, chosen at kickoff): 20 h/week in Nov–Dec and 6 h/week otherwise. Handles quotes, proofs, buyer calls and approving AI drafts.\n- **Butchery and packing:** +25–40 h/week in December, including one seasonal packer, and +2–3 h/week otherwise.\n- **Drivers:** office drops and home deliveries ride the existing route days, plus one extra Wednesday run a week in December.\n- **Owner (Bill or Stavros):** about 1 h/week. Approves quotes over $5,000, invoice terms and the Reserve Box spec.\n- **Minastech** builds the studio and the agents.\n\n## 5. AI agents this play needs\n| Agent | Job | Where it shows on screen |\n|---|---|---|\n| Gift Concierge (quotes and orders) | Answers buyers in chat and email. Turns two-tap answers into a quote with a level mix, volume pricing, delivery and terms. Drafts the Shopify draft order or invoice | Gift Desk chat; the \"Price in two taps\" result; team view: quote drafts to approve |\n| Recipient List Checker | Cleans an uploaded list (duplicates, postal codes). Maps each address to a J&W route day and fee. Flags out-of-Ontario addresses, PO boxes and missing addresses, and suggests certificates | Step 3 route-sorted table; team view: \"List check\" drafts |\n| Card and Message Writer | Drafts gift messages by occasion in the buyer's voice, checks length, and prepares the card proof | \"Write it for me\" in Step 2; team view: card proofs to approve |\n| Occasion and Renewal agent | Builds each account's gifting calendar. Sends reminders three weeks before each occasion with last year's list pre-filled. Runs the Holiday 2027 renewal | \"Plan the year once\"; team view: scheduled reminders and last-call emails |\n| B2B Prospecting agent (shared) | Builds lists of GTA employers, realtors, brokers and clinics along the route corridors. Drafts CASL-checked outreach and books tastings | Team view: new leads and sequence drafts to approve |\n| Production and Route Planner (shared) | Turns booked gifts into a weekly cut list, packing hours and added stops per route day. Flags van capacity | Team view: \"Production and route impact\" |\n| Certificate and Redemption agent | Issues codes and answers redemption questions. Sends day-14 and day-45 nudges. Reconciles pay-on-redemption invoices | Recipient redemption screen; team view: certificate ledger |\n\n## 6. Assumptions\n- The lean Holiday version is live by Mon Nov 9, 2026, and the full self-serve studio by Feb 1, 2027.\n- Holiday 2026 is a partial season (25 accounts × 20 gifts). The buyers are mostly existing J&W customers who run businesses, Instagram followers (30K), and businesses reached by outreach along the routes.\n- The average gift is $120 (mix of $85 to $350 after volume pricing).\n- Gross margin is 40% (model assumption for gift boxes). The premium extras are listed under costs.\n- Incrementality is 0.85.\n- 60% of recipients are new to J&W, and 8% of those order once ($130) within the year.\n- No breakage revenue is counted.\n- 70% of Holiday 2026 accounts come back in year 2.\n- J&W's real route fees apply, and the $100 route minimum is waived for gift deliveries and redemptions.\n- Recipients must be in Ontario, because the plant is provincially inspected.\n\n## 7. Risks\n- **A late start for Holiday 2026.** Many firms choose gifts in October. Season 1 will be small. Certificates (no lead time) and existing customers are the fastest wins.\n- **December capacity.** Gift volume collides with J&W's own holiday retail. Cap gifts per week (about 150 in Dec 2026) and use certificates to move redemptions into January. Watch the Meal Butler January peak.\n- **Ontario-only sales.** National employers can't include staff outside Ontario. The studio must screen addresses before payment.\n- **Cold chain at office drops.** Require a named receiver, a delivery window and a freezer or cooler nearby. Use insulated liners.\n- **Credit risk on invoices.** Net 30 only for approved accounts, with a 50% deposit on first orders.\n- **Gift card and tax rules.** J&W confirms certificate terms (no expiry on prepaid codes) with its accountant or lawyer. Buyers own their CRA treatment. J&W gives no tax advice, only a gift record.\n- **Product copy.** The Taste of Greece description bug and the gendered names must be fixed first.\n- **Seasonality.** About half of the run-rate lands in Nov–Dec.\n- **Reputation.** Corporate buyers read reviews. Put the 4.8★ Google rating (611 reviews) on the page, and keep a professional Gift Desk face.\n\n## Sources\n- Omaha Steaks Business Gifts: https://www.omahasteaks.com/business-gifts (live in browser, Oct 16, 2026)\n- Omaha corporate gift certificates: https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/corporate-gift-certificates\n- Omaha large business gifts: https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/large-business-gifts\n- Omaha how to order: https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/how-to-order-corporate-gifts\n- Omaha holiday planning: https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/holiday-business-gifts\n- Omaha Business Rewards: https://business.omahasteaks.com/resources/business-rewards\n- Omaha shipping (no Canada): https://www.omahasteaks.com/info/Shipping-Information\n- Omaha box builder: https://www.omahasteaks.com/servlet/OnlineShopping?Dsp=8500&sku=81977\n- CRA gifts and awards: https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/businesses/topics/payroll/benefits-allowances/gifts-awards-social-events/gifts-awards-long-service-awards.html\n- J&W boxes (contents read Oct 16, 2026):\n  - https://www.jwfoods.com/products/gentlemans-box-special-1\n  - https://www.jwfoods.com/products/a-taste-of-greece-box-special\n  - https://www.jwfoods.com/products/summer-bbq-box-special\n  - https://www.jwfoods.com/products/girls-night-box-special\n  - https://www.jwfoods.com/products/ultimate-bbq-dad-pack\n- J&W delivery routes: https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/home · J&W story: https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/about-us\n- J&W Google rating 4.8★ / 611: https://yably.ca/reviews/toronto/j-w-foods-2201-brimley-road\n- GTA business counts: https://yorklink.ca/2025-york-region-business-counts-factsheet/\n- truLOCAL corporate gifting (tiered discounts from 10 gifts): https://trulocal.ca/gifts/\n- Local research files: competitor-05.md, selection.json (pick 5), jw-facts.md, jw-audit.md, model-assumptions.md, market-benchmarks.md §6a and §11, competitors-gta-meat-grocery.md, competitors-global.md, ai-agents-research.md (Agents 3, 5, 6, 12, 13), site/assets/catalog.js, jw-catalog.json"}, {"key": "play-06", "file": "play-06.md", "title": "Play 6: Meal Butler AI Coach", "blurb": "The full play: mechanism, math, KPIs, agents, assumptions and risks.", "md": "# Play 6: Meal Butler AI Coach\n\nPrepared by Minastech (Erwin Garcia) for J&W Foods / J&W Meal Butler · October 16, 2026\nModelled on **Calo** (Riyadh, GCC and UK). Ideas only: no Calo copy, logos or photos.\nAll money in CAD. Every figure is an estimate unless it is a J&W published price or a cited source.\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **One-liner** | A free AI coach on jwfoods.com and in Instagram DMs turns your goal into a week of Meal Butler portions, and a Personal Macro Plan weighs every portion to your grams and puts your name on the bag. |\n| **Why it makes money** | It sells a premium, personal tier ($119–$219 a week) built from portions J&W already cooks and weighs, and the free coach turns Instagram and website questions into complete carts. |\n| **Year 1 (Nov 2026–Oct 2027)** | low **$113K** · mid **$191K** · high **$276K** added revenue (estimate) |\n| **Run-rate (year 2)** | **≈ $417K** a year (range $299K–$535K) |\n| **Gross margin** | **45%** (cooked and portioned, model-assumptions.md) |\n| **Starts** | Month 3: public launch Mon Jan 4, 2027. Nov–Dec 2026 is build and a 10-client pilot |\n| **JW's own costs** | ≈ $7,000 setup + ≈ $3,200 a month at ~60 clients (excludes Minastech fee) |\n\n---\n\n## 1. What Calo does, and what we borrow\n\nCalo sells subscription meal plans in Saudi Arabia, the Gulf and, since 2025, the UK (it bought Fresh Fitness Food in April 2025 and Detox Kitchen now trades as Calo). Its sharpest idea is **Custom Macros**: a short stepper (birthday, sex, height, weight, activity, goal) calculates calories, protein, carbs and fat, the customer can edit them, and the plan is cooked to those numbers. Custom Macros meals cost about 32% more than its Balanced meals (SAR 47.50 vs SAR 36 for lunch or dinner, incl. VAT, per its plans-page config). Customers can skip, pause, swap or edit meals up to 48 hours ahead in the GCC (one business day in the UK). It offers a 15–20 minute nutritionist call, and in July 2025 it announced a pilot of \"Calo Black\", an LLM private chef that learns preferences through conversation (no public product found in Oct 2026).\n\n**Proof it works (company claims and press):** 280K GCC customers and 18M meals (KSA page; the global page now says 290K and 19M), 10M+ meals in 2024, 50%+ year-on-year growth in H1 2025, a US$64M Series B, and 12 café locations including one inside a hospital ([Wamda](https://www.wamda.com/2025/07/calo-raises-39-milliion-series-b-extension-boost-global-growth); [calo.app](https://calo.app/en-sa); competitor-06.md). Calo has no Canadian presence.\n\n**Ideas J&W borrows (rebuilt in J&W's voice):**\n1. Goal intake that ends in editable daily macros, with the maths shown.\n2. A plan priced above the standard menu because it is cooked to your numbers.\n3. Self-serve skip, pause and swap before a cut-off (J&W's is the Sunday order deadline).\n4. A conversational AI that builds the week, with a free human call behind it.\n5. Macro dots on every dish card and a sticky live summary while you build.\n\n**Ideas that are J&W's own (not verified at Calo):** your name, the day and the macros on every bag (the Fresh Fitness Food label idea could not be verified, since freshfitnessfood.com no longer resolves); an InBody scan at the Brimley Rd store every 4 weeks (the One Life Meals idea, $25 a scan, [onelifemeals.com](https://www.onelifemeals.com/)); Instagram DMs as the front door (30K followers).\n\n**Where J&W wins:** its own Ontario-inspected plant since 2008 and a family butcher line back to the 1960s; portions sold by the ounce with no plan minimum; no added salt, sugar or oil; flash-frozen, so one weekly route covers much of Ontario; a store customers can walk into for a scan; 4.8★ from 611 Google reviews; and a built day at roughly $29–$33 against about $42 (Balanced) to $57 (Custom Macros) at Calo (competitor-06.md §6, estimate).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The mechanism (what J&W changes)\n\n1. **A free coach.** \"Ask the Butler\" on jwfoods.com and in Instagram DMs (DM the word PLAN to @jwfoods) asks seven questions: goal, age/sex/height/weight, training days, which meals, foods you skip, weekly budget and city. It calculates daily targets with dietitian-approved rules and builds Monday to Friday from real Meal Butler SKUs. Example for a 38-year-old woman, 165 cm, 78 kg, training 3 days, losing fat: **1,610 kcal · 140 g protein · 160 g carbs · 45 g fat (est.)**. Monday lunch is Chicken Breast Souvlaki 175 g + White Basmati Rice 200 g + Broccoli 110 g; breakfast is Egg White Bites, Spinach & Feta ($5.25) + Steel Cut Oats ($4.40).\n2. **Buy it two ways.** À la carte in J&W's standard 4oz and 6oz sizes (that whole default week is **$159.12** at today's prices), or as a **Personal Macro Plan** where each portion is weighed to your grams and packed as a meal with a personal label.\n3. **The Personal Macro Plan (Concept).** Lunch & Dinner (10 meals) $119 / $139 / $159 or Full Day (15 meals) $169 / $189 / $219 a week, by size (Regular up to 120 g protein a day, Plus 121–160 g, Performance over 160 g). Included: gram-weighed meals, your name and macros on every bag, a weekly check-in the AI drafts and a person at J&W approves, an InBody scan at 2201 Brimley Rd every 4 weeks, and a free 15-minute call with a partner registered dietitian to start. Skip or pause before the Sunday cut-off; no contract. Delivery is J&W's normal route fee, or free pickup.\n4. **Re-plan loop.** Every 4 weeks the scan result and the client's feedback go to the check-in agent, which drafts new grams and swaps within the dietitian's limits; the coach desk approves before anything changes.\n5. **Safety first.** Any mention of diabetes, kidney disease, pregnancy, allergies, eating-disorder history, medications or an age under 18 stops the automatic plan and routes the person to a human or the partner dietitian.\n6. **Production.** At the Sunday cut-off the production agent turns confirmed plans into a Saturday weigh-and-pack sheet and a label print run. Personal meals ride on the existing route days.\n\n**Standing orders created from the coach (Subscribe & Save) are counted in Play 1 (Goal Plan Builder), not here.** This play counts only the Personal Macro Plan, the extra value of coach-built one-time carts, and walk-in scans.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Concept products and pricing basis\n\n| Concept product | Price | Basis (J&W's real prices) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Personal Macro Plan · Lunch & Dinner (10 meals, Mon–Fri) | Regular $119 · Plus $139 · Performance $159 a week | Same 10 meals at exact grams and J&W per-ounce prices: about $110 (130 g protein/day) to $139 (140 g/day, Full Day split). E.g. Chicken Breast Souvlaki 6oz $6.93 = $1.155/oz; White Basmati 6oz $2.31; Broccoli 4oz $1.39. Standard-size equivalent about $100–$111. |\n| Personal Macro Plan · Full Day (15 meals, Mon–Fri) | Regular $169 · Plus $189 · Performance $219 a week | Adds 5 breakfasts of Egg White Bites ($5.25) + Steel Cut Oats ($4.40) = $48.25. Default week at exact grams $186.95; in standard sizes $159.12. A 185 g-protein week is about $214 at exact grams. |\n| Size rule | Regular ≤120 g protein a day · Plus 121–160 g · Performance >160 g | Protein drives portion cost. Keeps food cost near 55% of the plan price so gross margin stays about 45%. |\n| InBody scan | $25 walk-in · included every 4 weeks for plan members | One Life Meals charges $25 in the GTA (competitors-gta-mealprep.md). |\n| Free 15-minute call | $0 | The coach desk (food, orders, plan) or the partner registered dietitian (health conditions, medications). |\n\nBlended weekly value: 45% Lunch & Dinner (avg $137 across sizes at a 30/50/20 Regular/Plus/Performance mix) and 55% Full Day (avg $189) = **$166**, plus about $4 of add-ons = **$170 a week** used in the model. Per meal that is about $12.60–$13.90, below the $14–$30 GTA premium meal-prep range (model-assumptions.md) and roughly 35% under Calo Custom Macros (≈ C$285 for a 5-day full day).\n\n---\n\n## 4. The math\n\n### Shared assumptions (model-assumptions.md)\n- Gross margin 45% (cooked and portioned). Incrementality 0.75–0.85. CAC $60–120. Churn 6–8% (standing order, best) to 8–15% (meal kit).\n- 4.33 weeks a month. Clients are active 40 of 52 weeks (0.77) because of skips, pauses and holidays.\n- **Net factor 0.70 on Personal Macro Plan revenue:** about 40% of clients will be existing customers who already spend roughly $90 a week (so only ~49% of their plan is new), and about 15% of new clients would otherwise have joined Play 1. 0.6 × 0.85 + 0.4 × 0.49 ≈ 0.70.\n- **$33 incremental per coach-built one-time cart:** 30% of these carts are new customers ($130 AOV × 0.6 incrementality = $23.40) and 70% are existing customers who add about $18 of breakfasts and sides (× 0.8 = $10.08).\n- PMP monthly churn 9% (between standing-order and meal-kit benchmarks).\n\n### Year 1 ramp (Nov 2026 = month 1)\n\n| Month | Nov | Dec | Jan | Feb | Mar | Apr | May | Jun | Jul | Aug | Sep | Oct | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Coach conversations | 0 | 150 | 600 | 700 | 800 | 850 | 900 | 900 | 850 | 900 | 1,000 | 1,100 | 8,750 |\n| PMP clients enrolled (avg) | 0 | pilot | 12 | 20 | 28 | 35 | 40 | 44 | 45 | 47 | 52 | 57 | 380 client-months |\n| Coach-built one-time carts | 0 | 0 | 40 | 60 | 80 | 100 | 110 | 120 | 120 | 125 | 140 | 150 | 1,045 |\n| Walk-in scans ($25) | 0 | 0 | 10 | 15 | 20 | 25 | 25 | 25 | 20 | 25 | 30 | 35 | 230 |\n\nNew PMP clients needed: about 9–10 a month (roughly 1% of coach conversations) to grow to ~60 enrolled while 9% churn a month.\n\n**Year 1 (mid):**\n- Personal Macro Plan: 380 client-months × 4.33 × 0.77 = 1,267 paid weeks × $170 = $215,383 gross × 0.70 = **$150,768**\n- Coach-built carts: 1,045 × $33 = **$34,485**\n- Walk-in scans: 230 × $25 = **$5,750**\n- **Total ≈ $191,000**\n\n**Low:** 60% of the clients at $160 a week, 70% of carts and scans = 760 paid weeks × $160 × 0.70 = $85,140 + $24,140 + $4,025 = **$113,300**.\n**High:** 140% of the clients at $180, 130% of carts and scans = 1,774 × $180 × 0.70 = $223,491 + $44,830 + $7,475 = **$275,800**.\n\n### Run-rate (year 2)\n- PMP: 70 enrolled on average (60 → 80) × 52 × 0.77 × $170 = $476,476 gross × 0.70 = **$333,533**\n- Coach-built carts: 180 a month × 12 × $33 = **$71,280**\n- Walk-in scans: 40 a month × 12 × $25 = **$12,000**\n- **Total ≈ $417,000** (low case at 50 clients ≈ $299K; high case at 90 clients ≈ $535K)\n\n### Profit after JW's own costs\n- Year 1: 45% × $191K = $86K gross profit − $36K play costs (setup + monthly) ≈ **$50K contribution** before Minastech's fee.\n- Year 2: 45% × $417K = $188K − ~$42K ≈ **$146K contribution**.\n- Upside not counted: repeat orders from coach-acquired new customers, store purchases on scan visits, raw-meat add-ons to plan deliveries.\n\n### Sanity check\nJ&W mid-case revenue is ~$2.5M (range $1.2–4.0M). $191K is about 7.6% of that in year 1, inside the usual $60–450K for one play. 60 enrolled clients is 0.2% of the 30K Instagram audience.\n\n---\n\n## 5. KPIs\n\n| KPI | Target |\n|---|---|\n| Coach conversations (site + Instagram) | 1,000+ a month by Oct 2027 |\n| Conversation → cart or plan | 12–15% |\n| Personal Macro Plan clients enrolled | 57–60 by Oct 2027; 80 by Oct 2028 |\n| New plan clients | ~10 a month |\n| Plan monthly churn | ≤ 9% |\n| Active weeks per enrolled client | ≥ 77% (40 of 52) |\n| Average plan value | ≥ $170 a week |\n| Members re-scanning every 4 weeks | ≥ 70% |\n| Medical or allergy mentions reaching a person | 100%, within 1 business day |\n| Personal portions within ±5 g of target | ≥ 95% |\n| Coach SKUs with dietitian-reviewed macros | 100% before launch |\n| Plans and weekly changes approved by a person | 100% |\n\n---\n\n## 6. AI agents this play needs\n\n| Agent | Job | On the prototype screen |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Macro Butler coach** (Claude Sonnet) | Runs the intake conversation on jwfoods.com and in Instagram DMs, calculates targets with dietitian-approved rules, builds Mon–Fri from tagged Meal Butler SKUs within budget and skips, explains swaps with price and macro changes, returns a cart link or a plan draft. Reads only the approved catalogue, prices and routes. | Intake stepper, results card, \"Here's your week\" planner, chat replies |\n| **Nutrition data and label agent** (Claude Opus, batch) | Computes per-SKU macros from J&W recipes, cooked yields and Health Canada's Canadian Nutrient File, stores them in Shopify metafields, flags claims for review, and generates each personal label (name, day, grams, macros) for the print run. A dietitian approves the data. | Macro dots and \"est.\" values on cards; the personal label that prints |\n| **Instagram DM concierge** | Answers DMs to @jwfoods (delivery day, prices, reheating), starts the coach when someone types PLAN, books scans, hands medical, allergy and complaint messages to a person with a summary. | Instagram DM demo; DM drafts in the team queue |\n| **Check-in and re-plan agent** | Weekly reads feedback, skips and (every 4 weeks) InBody results; drafts gram and swap changes within the dietitian's limits. Nothing changes until a person approves. | \"Re-plan after scan · Maria S.\" approval; scan card \"What the coach changed\" |\n| **Safety triage agent** (Claude Haiku) | Screens every intake and message for conditions, medications, pregnancy, allergies, eating-disorder signals or under-18 users; stops the plan, sends a holding reply, routes to the dietitian or staff. | \"Let's get a person on this\" card; escalations in the team view |\n| **Production sheet agent** | At the Sunday cut-off turns confirmed plans into a Saturday weigh-and-pack sheet by component, grams and route day, plus the label print order; feeds the main production forecast. | \"Saturday weigh-and-pack\" table and route split |\n\nRun cost (estimate, ai-agents-research.md §3): coach chat about $0.03–0.05 per conversation on Sonnet, so roughly $50–75 a month at 1,500 conversations; re-plans and labels under $50 a month.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Who at J&W does the work\n\n- **Coach desk (a team member or a hired part-time nutrition coach):** ~4 h a week at launch, ~8 h by Oct 2027, ~10 h in year 2. Approves plans and weekly changes, runs scans, takes the free calls.\n- **Kitchen lead:** ~2–4 h a week on the Saturday weigh-and-pack and label run.\n- **Store lead:** ~1–2 h a week on Instagram escalations and scan bookings at the counter.\n- **Partner registered dietitian (contract):** ~12 h at setup, then ~4 h a month for onboarding calls, escalations and rule reviews.\n- **Owner (Bill or Stavros):** 30 minutes a week on the dashboard.\n\n---\n\n## 8. JW's own costs (excludes Minastech's fee)\n\n**Setup ≈ $7,000 (estimate):** dietitian review of rules, escalation script and macro database 12 h × $150 = $1,800 · lab nutrition tests on the top 10 coach SKUs ≈ $2,000 (shared with Play 1; count once in the plan) · label-printing scale or thermal printer and label design ≈ $1,500 · founding pilot, 10 clients' first week free ≈ $950 food cost · staff training 16 h ≈ $400 · launch reels and props ≈ $300.\n\n**Monthly ≈ $3,200 at ~60 clients (≈ $2,700 average in year 1; ≈ $3,500 at 75 clients):** coach desk ~8 h/wk × $25 ≈ $870 · extra weigh-and-pack time ~3.5 h/wk × $22 ≈ $370 · labels ≈ $150 · partner dietitian ≈ $600 · InBody lease ≈ $300 (estimate, quote needed; buying is roughly $10–15K) · software (Claude API, Instagram messaging tool, client-profile store) ≈ $250 · Meta ads ≈ $700 (heavier Jan–Mar).\n\n**Year 1 all-in ≈ $36,000.** The subscription app is shared with Play 1 and not counted here. Delivery uses existing routes at the normal fee.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Assumptions\n\n1. Launch Jan 4, 2027 (month 3), after macros for the coach's ~60 SKUs are reviewed and a December pilot with 10 regulars.\n2. Average plan value $170 a week (size mix 30/50/20; 45% Lunch & Dinner, 55% Full Day; ~$4 add-ons).\n3. Clients are active 40 of 52 weeks; monthly churn 9%.\n4. Net factor 0.70 on plan revenue (existing-customer spend and Play 1 overlap removed).\n5. $33 incremental per coach-built one-time cart; standing orders from the coach count in Play 1.\n6. Gross margin 45%; plan prices keep food cost near 55% of price; extra labour is counted as a cost, not in margin.\n7. Coach conversations grow to ~1,100 a month by Oct 2027 from site chat and Instagram DMs.\n8. InBody lease about $300 a month and dietitian rate about $150 an hour (estimates; get quotes).\n9. Macros are estimates from recipes, cooked yields and the Canadian Nutrient File until lab tests confirm best-sellers.\n10. Meals are packed in J&W's pre-set meal format, one personal label per meal.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Risks and how we handle them\n\n- **Health advice.** The coach gives general nutrition guidance only. A registered dietitian signs off the rules; conditions, medications, pregnancy, eating-disorder signals and under-18s go to a person. Staff are \"coaches\"; only the partner uses \"registered dietitian\" (a protected title in Ontario).\n- **Macro accuracy and label claims.** J&W publishes no nutrition panels today. Keep \"estimate\" wording, lab-test top sellers, and confirm personal-label content and \"salt and sugar free\" claims with a labelling consultant (CFIA / Health Canada).\n- **Kitchen complexity.** Gram-weighed meals add pack time and room for error. Cap the plan at ~100 clients until the weigh-and-pack workflow is proven; one fixed Saturday run.\n- **Overlap.** Some plan clients are existing buyers or would have joined Play 1. Handled with the 0.70 net factor and by counting coach standing orders in Play 1.\n- **Privacy.** Weight, body composition and GLP-1 use are sensitive personal information under PIPEDA. Get clear consent, keep only what the plan needs, never use health details for ad targeting, and let people delete their data.\n- **AI mistakes.** The coach reads only approved data; prices come from Shopify; every plan and change is approved by a person; transcripts are sampled weekly.\n- **Instagram rules.** Automated DMs must follow Meta's messaging policies (including the 24-hour reply window).\n- **People.** If the coach desk depends on one person it stalls when that person is away. Write a playbook and train a second person.\n- **Price acceptance.** J&W's customers are value-minded. The free coach and à la carte option stay; review plan prices after 3 months.\n\n---\n\n## 11. How it stays distinct from the other six plays\n\n- **Play 1 Goal Plan Builder:** a quiz that routes to six fixed goal plans with Subscribe & Save at 10%. Play 6 is a conversation that builds a one-off week for one person, plus a premium gram-weighed plan with labels, scans and human-approved check-ins. Coach standing orders are Play 1 revenue.\n- **Play 2 Pro Partner Portal:** trainers may send clients to the coach, but wholesale cases stay in Play 2.\n- **Play 3 Butcher Club:** raw-meat boxes; the coach only suggests raw add-ons.\n- **Play 4 J&W at Work:** employer-paid meals; Play 6 sells to individuals.\n- **Plays 5 and 7:** gifting and fundraising; no overlap.\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- Calo: [home (KSA)](https://calo.app/en-sa) · [plans and packages](https://calo.app/en-sa/plans-and-packages) · [high-protein plan FAQ](https://calo.app/en-sa/high-protein-meal-plan) · [consultations](https://calo.app/en-sa/consultations) · [Calo vs COOK (UK)](https://calo.app/en-sa/blog/calo-vs-cook) · [pause help article](https://help.calo.app/en/articles/4704801-how-to-pause-the-subscription-plan) · [global page](https://calo.app/en)\n- [Wamda: Calo raises US$39M Series B extension (Jul 2025)](https://www.wamda.com/2025/07/calo-raises-39-milliion-series-b-extension-boost-global-growth)\n- [CB Insights: Fresh Fitness Food acquired by Calo (Apr 24, 2025)](https://www.cbinsights.com/company/fresh-fitness-food/financials)\n- [CB Insights: Fresh Fitness Food](https://www.cbinsights.com/company/fresh-fitness-food/financials)\n- [One Life Meals: $25 InBody scan](https://www.onelifemeals.com/) · [Power Kitchen](https://powerkitchen.ca/) (Play 1 model, for distinction)\n- J&W: [jwfoods.com](https://www.jwfoods.com/) · [delivery routes](https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/home) · [products.json](https://www.jwfoods.com/products.json) · [Instagram @jwfoods](https://www.instagram.com/jwfoods/) · [Yably: Google 4.8★ / 611](https://yably.ca/reviews/toronto/j-w-foods-2201-brimley-road)\n- [Eightx: subscription churn by category](https://www.eightx.co/blog/average-subscription-churn-rate-by-category)\n- [Claude API pricing](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing)\n- [Health Canada: Canadian Nutrient File](https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/nutrient-data.html)\n- [Noom: AI assistant alongside human coaches](https://www.fintechfutures.com/press-releases/noom-introduces-ai-enabled-products-to-enhance-on-demand-health-care-and-interactive-coaching)\n- [Fortune: Amazon Rufus shoppers 60% more likely to buy](https://www.fortune.com/2025/11/02/amazon-rufus-ai-shopping-assistant-chatbot-10-billion-sales-monetization)\n- Internal: research/competitor-06.md, selection.json (pick 6), jw-facts.md, jw-audit.md, model-assumptions.md, market-benchmarks.md, competitors-gta-mealprep.md, competitors-global.md, ai-agents-research.md, site/assets/catalog.js"}, {"key": "play-07", "file": "play-07.md", "title": "Play 7: Team Fuel Fundraiser", "blurb": "The full play: mechanism, math, KPIs, agents, assumptions and risks.", "md": "# Play 7: Team Fuel Fundraiser\n\nPrepared by Minastech (Erwin Garcia) for J&W Foods / J&W Meal Butler · October 16, 2026\nAll money in CAD. Every figure is an ESTIMATE unless it is a J&W published price (jwfoods.com, Oct 16 2026) or carries a source. Shared figures come from `model-assumptions.md`. Sample groups, players and people in this file are fictional.\n\n**One-liner (hub card):** A fundraising storefront where hockey teams, schools, soccer clubs, cadets and community groups each get their own J&W page, share link and live progress bar, and families buy freezer packs and Meal Butler meals with the group's share shown under every price.\n\n**Why it makes money:** It turns the families around a team or school into J&W customers at about $33 each, paid to the group instead of to Meta. Every order is pre-sold and delivered in one drop on a route J&W already drives.\n\n**Focus:** Community fundraising that brings new households to J&W.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Modelled on: Halenda's Fundraising (Oshawa, Durham Region)\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| URL | https://halendasfundraising.com/ |\n| Scope | GTA (Durham Region). Head office 915 Nelson St, Oshawa. Five stores, all in Durham (Oshawa ×3, Whitby, Bowmanville), plus St Jacobs Market. Fundraising pick-up at Oshawa and 15 Brydon Dr, Etobicoke (competitor-07.md §2). |\n| What they do | A family butcher (since 1979) that runs a school and team fundraising program on one product: Pepperoni Stix. Groups buy a case of 240 sticks (24 bags of 10) for $261–$300 and sell each bag at $25, keeping 50–56%. Shipping is $18 a case anywhere in Ontario, pick-up is free. It offers two modes: \"order the sticks, then sell\" (about 95% of groups) and \"collect orders, then buy\" with a personalized flyer and order form (about 5%) ([purchase](https://halendasfundraising.com/purchase/), [about](https://halendasfundraising.com/about-halendas-fundraising/)). |\n| Proof | First case shipped June 27, 2013. \"Six million meat sticks sold\" and \"more than six million dollars\" raised for groups (post of Nov 6, 2025) ([post](https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/celebrating-six-million-meat-sticks-sold/)). Implied ≈ 450K sticks, ≈ 1,880 cases and ≈ $0.5M of wholesale revenue a year (ESTIMATE, competitor-07.md §2). Recent stories: a Newcastle dance studio raised $10,300 (Sept 2026); a 12-year-old BJJ athlete sold 8 cases in a month and raised $1,536 (Aug 2026). Pages updated Sept 23, 2026. Group count and company revenue were not found. |\n| What we borrow (ideas only) | The two-way profit calculator (goal → orders, orders → profit) on a light-blue card. A plain rate table. A numbered order block. The \"collect orders, then buy\" mode, rebuilt as an online team page. The sales-rep attribution field on the order form, rebuilt as per-player links. An objection-handling FAQ for schools. Segment success stories (dance, hockey, cadets) as a content habit. From **Rowe Farms' meat fundraisers** we borrow one more idea: the group's donation shown under each product price ([Rowe Farms](https://rowefarmsonline.ca/pages/meat-fundraisers), competitors-gta-meat-grocery.md §4.2). |\n| Where Halenda's is ahead (say it plainly) | 13 years of fundraising, field sales reps, two plants, three Diamond Awards, supermarket listings, and a shelf-stable product a child can sell door to door. Groups keep 50–56% of Halenda's sales. J&W's share will be 12–20%, so J&W must win on dollars per order, zero upfront cash and food families already buy. |\n| J&W's edge | **Halenda's never meets the supporter.** It sells cases to the group, and the group sells bags for cash, so buyers never become Halenda's customers. J&W sells to each family directly on a team page, with consent, so every buyer becomes a J&W customer. The basket is bigger: a typical $135 J&W order sends about $21 to the group, against $12.50–$14.13 per $25 bag. J&W has pork-free packs (the Halenda's stick is pork), cooked Meal Butler meals for busy sports weeks, and its own weekly routes, including the Tuesday Durham route that passes every Halenda's store. One Shopify storefront with current prices, against three WordPress domains with conflicting price tables (competitor-07.md §3.1, §6). |\n\n---\n\n## 2. The mechanism: what J&W changes\n\nJ&W already sells the right products: a 25-pack of chicken burgers ($35.50), 4 kg chicken breast cases ($92), a 5 lb ground beef special ($40), family boxes ($84.99–$199) and about 292 Meal Butler cooked items. It has no fundraising offer and almost no non-brand discovery (jw-audit.md §9). This play adds six things.\n\n1. **A team page for every group.** Each school, team, club or community group gets `jwfoods.com/team/<name>` with its name, goal, a live progress bar, days left, the drop time and place, and a \"who are you ordering for?\" picker. Every player or family gets a personal link (`?p=maya14`) so the organizer can see who sold what. Pages are unlisted by default and show first names, last initials and jersey numbers only.\n\n2. **A short Team Fuel menu at regular store prices.** Families pay what they would pay at Brimley Rd. J&W pays the group a share from its own margin, and the exact dollar amount shows under every price.\n\n   | Pack | What's in it (real J&W products and prices) | Price | Group gets |\n   |---|---|---|---|\n   | **Team Freezer Pack** (Concept bundle) | Premium Chicken Burgers, 25 × 6 oz ($35.50) + Case of Portioned Chicken Breast, 4 kg, 6 oz pieces, about 23 ($92) + Ground Beef Bulk Mix, 5 lb ($40). About 23 lb of meat | $167.50 | 12% = **$20.10** |\n   | **Game-Week Meal Pack** (Concept bundle, Meal Butler) | 3 × Teriyaki Chicken, Green Beans & Rice 4 oz ($9.99) + 3 × Tenders, Broccoli & Sweet Potato 4 oz ($9.99) + 2 × Jerk Chicken, Green Beans & Sweet Potato 4 oz ($12.99) + 2 × Turkey, Spinach & Rice 4 oz ($12.99) + 2 × Egg White Bites, Spinach & Feta ($5.25). About 37 g protein per meal (est.) | $122.40 | 20% = **$24.48** |\n   | **Kids Favourite Combo** (J&W box) | 4 chicken breasts 6–8 oz, 2 packs beef hot dogs, 2 × 1 lb ground beef, 1 lb cooked beef meatballs, 1 lb AAA beef stew, 2 kg breaded chicken fingers | $95.00 | 12% = **$11.40** |\n   | **Long Weekend Box** (J&W box) | AAA striploin steaks, ribs, sausages, burgers, sliders, buns and fries | $199.00 | 12% = **$23.88** |\n\n   Add-ons with the share shown: Beef Chili 10 oz $12 ($2.40) · Lemon Chicken Rice Soup $8.99 ($1.80) · Egg White Bites $5.25 ($1.05) · Breakfast Wrap, Egg, Spinach & Feta $5.99 ($1.20) · Chicken Souvlaki Wrap $5.99 ($1.20) · Marinated Chicken Breast Case 4 kg $100 ($12.00) · AAA New York Striploin, case of 8 × 8–9 oz $110 ($13.20) · No-Salt Chicken Souvlaki Case 5 kg $95 ($11.40).\n\n3. **Group share rates (Concept):** 20% on Meal Butler cooked and portioned items, 12% on butcher boxes, freezer packs and cases, and 5% on any order placed through the team link for six months after the drop. Cooked food carries about 45% gross margin and raw about 30% (model-assumptions.md), so J&W keeps about 25% and 18% after the share. The higher cooked rate steers organizers toward J&W's best-margin, most distinctive product.\n\n4. **One drop, one day.** Orders close on a Sunday, which matches J&W's existing \"order by Sunday\" cut-off. The following week, on the route day for that area, a J&W van parks at the arena, field, hall or studio for a 60-minute pickup window. Every order is bagged, labelled with the family's name and kept cold, and a J&W staffer stays with it. Team drops have no fee and no $100 minimum. Families can also pick up free at 2201 Brimley Rd, or choose home delivery on their usual route day at the normal fee and minimum. Drop days: Tuesday (Durham, GTA 1), Wednesday (Toronto, Scarborough, North York, Etobicoke, Mississauga, GTA 2), Thursday (Oakville to Hamilton, GTA 3), Friday (York and Peel, GTA 4). Schools default to Brimley pickup or home delivery until their board approves a school drop.\n\n5. **Perks that cost little and help groups raise more:**\n   - **\"Fuel for Game Day\" talk:** at $1,500 in campaign sales, a J&W butcher brings a free 30-minute talk and a tasting tray to one practice. It covers early-ice breakfasts, tournament coolers and reheating at the hotel. General food tips, not medical advice. Cost about $70.\n   - **Raffle box:** at $3,000 in campaign sales, J&W adds a Long Weekend Box ($199) for the group's raffle or year-end barbecue. Cost about $139 (ESTIMATE, 70% of list).\n   - **Keep fuelling the team:** 5% of orders through the team link for six months after the drop.\n\n6. **Every buyer becomes a J&W customer, with consent.** Checkout asks for express consent to email and SMS (CASL). Those who say yes get a welcome flow: storage and reheating tips, then **$10 off their first regular order of $100 or more within 60 days**, then a reminder that the team still earns 5% through the link. The organizer sees names and order totals for the pickup list, never buyers' contact details.\n\n**No upfront money and no cash handling.** Families pay J&W online. The group gets a statement of every order and its share by e-transfer or cheque within 10 business days of the drop. The payment is a fundraising payment from J&W, not a charitable donation, so buyers do not get tax receipts.\n\n**Later, not counted: Team Fuel Jerky (Concept).** A J&W-made beef or chicken jerky that players could sell by hand would close the gap with Halenda's 50% model. It needs a tested recipe, shelf-life work, labels and approval under J&W's provincial licence, and could only be sold in Ontario. The prototype shows it as \"in development\" with a register-interest form. Its revenue is not in the numbers below.\n\n### Launch plan\n- **Oct 19 – Nov 20, 2026:** build the lean storefront on Shopify (team pages as collections, attribution through an affiliate or referral app, a share ledger), photograph the two Concept packs, write the school pack, fix the product-data issues in §9.\n- **Nov 23 – Dec 6, 2026: pilot, 4 groups.** Recruit from J&W's own customers who coach, teach or sit on a parent council (in-store sign, an Instagram post to 30K followers, an email). Drops the week of Dec 1 and Dec 7: \"Fill the freezer before the holidays.\"\n- **Jan – Feb 2027:** the second half of hockey and ringette, plus school winter campaigns.\n- **Mar – Jun 2027:** spring soccer and baseball, school spring fundraisers, dance and cadets. The Long Weekend Box leads in May.\n- **Jul – Aug 2027:** quiet (camps and tournament teams).\n- **Sep – Oct 2027:** back to school and the new hockey season. Re-book every pilot group.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The money\n\n### Year 1 (Nov 2026 to Oct 2027), mid case, with ramp from startMonth 2 (Dec 2026)\n\nCampaigns by drop month: Nov 0 · Dec 4 · Jan 3 · Feb 4 · Mar 5 · Apr 6 · May 6 · Jun 3 · Jul 1 · Aug 1 · Sep 4 · Oct 5 = **42 campaigns** (about 38 groups).\n\n| Line | Formula | Amount |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fundraiser sales | 42 campaigns × 25 orders × $135 | $141,750 |\n| × 0.80 incremental | About 30% of buyers are existing J&W customers; some of their orders would have happened anyway | **$113,400** |\n| Buyer households | 1,050 orders × 0.9 unique | 945 |\n| New to J&W | 945 × 70% | 662 |\n| Follow-on regular orders | 662 × 12% become repeat = 79 × 4 orders a year × $130 × 5.26 months left on average ÷ 12 | $18,100 |\n| × 0.85 incremental | | **$15,385** |\n| **Year 1 mid** | $113,400 + $15,385 | **$128,785 ≈ $129K** |\n\nYear-1 range:\n- **Low $46K:** 25 campaigns × 18 orders × $125 = $56,250 × 0.75 = $42,188. Follow-on: 284 new households × 8% × 4 × $130 × 5.16/12 = $5,071 × 0.75 = $3,803.\n- **High $271K:** 60 campaigns × 32 orders × $140 = $268,800 × 0.85 = $228,480. Follow-on: 1,210 new households × 18% × 4 × $130 × 5.32/12 = $50,162 × 0.85 = $42,638.\n\nMonthly shape, year 1 (gross booked, fundraiser + follow-on): Nov $0 · Dec $13.5K · Jan $10.5K · Feb $14.1K · Mar $17.8K · Apr $21.6K · May $22.1K · Jun $12.4K · Jul $5.9K · Aug $6.0K · Sep $16.2K · Oct $19.9K = $159.9K gross, or $128.8K after incrementality.\n\n### Run-rate (year 2, Nov 2027 to Oct 2028)\n\n| Line | Formula | Amount |\n|---|---|---|\n| Campaigns | ≈ 27 returning groups (70% of 38) × 1.5 campaigns a year + 45 new groups | 85 |\n| Fundraiser sales | 85 × 27 orders × $138 | $316,710 |\n| × 0.80 | | **$253,368** |\n| Follow-on, year-1 cohort | 79 repeat households × 60% still active × 4 × $130 | $24,767 |\n| Follow-on, year-2 cohort | 85 × 27 × 0.9 × 65% new = 1,343 new households × 12% = 161 × 4 × $130 × 5.67/12 | $39,589 |\n| × 0.85 | ($24,767 + $39,589) × 0.85 | **$54,702** |\n| **Run-rate** | $253,368 + $54,702 | **$308,070 ≈ $308K** |\n\nYear-2 campaign shape: Nov 6 · Dec 8 · Jan 6 · Feb 7 · Mar 9 · Apr 11 · May 11 · Jun 6 · Jul 2 · Aug 2 · Sep 8 · Oct 9.\n\n### Margin and contribution\n- **Product margin:** an assumed mix of 55% raw (30% margin) and 45% Meal Butler (45%) gives 36.75% blended.\n- **Group share:** 55% × 12% + 45% × 20% = **15.6%** of fundraiser sales on average, about $21 on a $135 order.\n- **Margin after the share:** 21.15% on fundraiser sales. Follow-on orders earn 36.75% less the 5% team-link share on about 60% of them = 33.75%.\n- **Displacement cost:** the 20% of fundraiser sales that existing customers would have placed anyway now carries the group share too: year 1 $28,350 × 15.6% = $4,423; year 2 $63,342 × 15.6% = $9,881.\n- **Gross profit after the share and displacement:** year 1 = $113,400 × 21.15% + $15,385 × 33.75% − $4,423 = **$24,754** (19% of counted revenue). Year 2 = $253,368 × 21.15% + $54,702 × 33.75% − $9,881 = **$62,168** (20%). Use **marginPct 20**.\n- **Contribution after J&W's running costs (§6):** year 1 $24,754 − $22,066 − $3,200 setup ≈ **−$0.5K, break-even**. Year 2 $62,168 − $40,000 ≈ **+$22K**.\n- **What J&W really buys:** about 2,000 new households in two years (662 + 1,343). The group share works out to about **$33 per new household** in year 1 ($22,113 ÷ 662) and $37 in year 2, against $60–120 for a new customer through paid ads (model-assumptions.md). Counting J&W's margin on the orders, those households arrive at roughly zero net cost in year 1.\n\n### Sanity checks\n- Year 1 is about 5% of J&W's ~$2.5M mid-case revenue; the run-rate is about 12%. Both sit inside the $60–450K year-1 and sub-$900K run-rate guardrails.\n- **Against Halenda's:** about 1,880 cases a year at an assumed 6 cases per group is roughly 300 group campaigns a year (ESTIMATE; Halenda's group count was not found). J&W's 85 campaigns in year 2 is about 28% of that, spread over a wider route area (Durham, Toronto, York, Peel, Halton).\n- **Market room:** the GTHL alone has 493 teams in 54 clubs and 30,000+ players (market-benchmarks.md §6b). Add soccer, ringette, baseball, schools, cadets, dance and faith groups, and 85 campaigns is a small share.\n- **The selection's year-2 idea** (100 groups × $4,000 ≈ $400K plus a large DTC tail) was trimmed, as competitor-07.md §7 advised. The follow-on tail here (≈ $55K) is smaller than the selection's $0.16–0.31M, because only two cohorts exist by year 2.\n- **Capacity:** year 2 is about 2,300 orders, about 44 a week on average and about 70 a week in April and May. That is 2–3 drops a week at peak, each about 2 hours of van and packer time. All volume is pre-sold before the Monday cook.\n\n### Notes on the math\n- Revenue is counted at full retail price. The group share is treated as a cost inside the margin, not as a discount.\n- The average order ($135) matches J&W's online AOV assumption ($130) plus fundraiser bundling. Pilot sample orders in the prototype average $138–$154.\n- Orders per campaign (25) is an estimate for a mixed set of teams (15–20 families) and schools (more families, lower take-up). It needs the pilot to confirm it.\n- Follow-on orders count one-time regular orders only. If a fundraiser buyer later joins the Butcher Club (Play 3), a Goal Plan subscription (Play 1) or the AI Coach (Play 6), those revenues belong to those plays. The 0.85 factor partly covers that overlap.\n- Nothing here counts corporate gifting (Play 5), office meal programs (Play 4) or partner wholesale (Play 2). A gym or club that becomes a wholesale partner is counted in Play 2 from that point.\n- The jerky line is not counted.\n\n---\n\n## 4. KPIs\n\n| KPI | Target |\n|---|---|\n| Pilot groups live by Dec 6, 2026 | 4 |\n| Campaigns, Nov 2026 – Oct 2027 | 42 (about 38 groups) |\n| Orders per campaign | 25 average |\n| Average order | $135 |\n| Group share per new household | $35 or less |\n| Buyers new to J&W | 70% |\n| Email/SMS opt-in at checkout | 60%+ |\n| New households placing a regular order within 6 months | 12% |\n| Groups that book another campaign within 12 months | 70% |\n| Orders collected in the drop window | 97%+ (the rest picked up at Brimley within 3 days) |\n| Group payouts sent within 10 business days of the drop | 100% |\n| Campaigns reaching 75% of their goal | 60%+ |\n\n---\n\n## 5. AI agents this play needs\n\n| Agent | Job | Where it shows on screen |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Team Fuel Desk** (the shared Butler Concierge in fundraising mode) | Answers organizers and parents on rates, goals, drops, payouts, school rules, allergens (only from approved product data) and the Ontario-only rule. Hands exceptions to the coordinator. | Chat \"Team Fuel Desk\" on the page |\n| **Team Page Builder** (new) | Turns a two-minute form into a draft team page: goal and orders needed, campaign dates, the route-day drop slot, perks, and a share kit (team-app post, text, email, QR flyer). Checks claims: no blanket salt or sugar claims, Ontario-only note, school-board caveat. The coordinator approves before the page goes live. | \"Build my team page\" result in the organizer form; team view draft for Markham Northstar Dance |\n| **Campaign Coach** (new) | Watches each campaign against its goal and pace. Drafts day-3, day-7 and last-48-hours messages for the organizer to send to their own families, plus shout-outs. Forecasts orders at close for production. J&W never messages families who have not opted in. | Progress bar pace chip; team view nudge drafts for the Snowhawks and Cedar Hollow |\n| **Drop and Production Planner** (shared with the production forecast and route agents) | At Sunday close, rolls orders into named bags, a pick list and labels. Adds pack counts to Monday's cook, cut and pack sheet and the raw order. Slots the drop as the last stop on the route day with a 60-minute window, and drafts the transactional text to each family. | Team view \"Production and route impact\" and the Whitby Wolves drop sheet |\n| **Fundraiser Payout agent** (new) | Calculates each group's share by product rate, handles refunds and missed pickups, drafts the statement and the e-transfer or cheque request. The owner approves payouts over $500. | Team view payout ledger and the Whitby Wolves statement |\n| **Lifecycle marketing agent** (shared, Klaviyo) | Runs the welcome flow for opted-in buyers: reheating tips, the $10 first-order offer, the 5% team-link reminder, then the regular J&W flows. Invites organizers to re-book next season. CASL consent and unsubscribe checks on every send. | Team view welcome-flow draft and the new-customer funnel |\n| **Group Prospecting agent** (shared B2B prospecting agent) | Builds seasonal lead lists of minor hockey and ringette teams, soccer clubs, school councils, cadet corps, dance studios and faith groups along the GTA 1–4 corridors. Drafts intro emails with a tasting-tray offer to published volunteer or fundraising contacts. The coordinator approves each sequence. | Team view \"Next season\" pipeline: 32 new leads |\n\n---\n\n## 6. JW's costs to run it (excluding Minastech's fee)\n\n- **Setup about $3,200:**\n  - Photos of the two Concept packs and a team-kit design (flyer, QR poster, bag label): $800\n  - Insulated totes, name labels, \"Team Fuel\" stickers and 4 drop coolers: $900\n  - Pilot tasting trays and the printed school pack: $500\n  - Shopify setup time, 20 h × $25: $500\n  - Label printer: $300\n  - First flyer print run: $200\n- **Year 1 running about $22.1K, or $1,840 a month on average:**\n  - Fundraising coordinator, 5 h/week average × 48 weeks × $25: $6,000\n  - Drop van time, 42 drops × $67 (2 h of driver and packer at about $26 loaded, plus $15 of fuel): $2,814\n  - Named bags and labels, 1,050 orders × $2.33 ($1.50 packaging + 2 minutes of packing): $2,447\n  - \"Fuel for Game Day\" talks, 25 × $70 (1.5 h of staff time + a $25 tasting tray at cost): $1,750\n  - Raffle boxes, 19 × $139 at cost: $2,641\n  - Meta ads to coaches and parent councils in route corridors, $200 × 11 months: $2,200\n  - Flyers and QR posters, 42 × $40: $1,680\n  - Software, $80 × 11 months (attribution app about $50, payout fees about $10, Claude API about $20): $880\n  - Welcome offers, 662 new households × 25% redeem × $10: $1,654\n- **Year 2 running about $40K:** coordinator 8 h/week ($10,000), 85 drops ($5,695), 2,295 orders packed ($5,347), 50 talks ($3,500), 38 raffle boxes ($5,282), ads ($2,400), flyers ($3,400), software ($1,020), welcome offers ($3,356).\n- **Not in this list, because it sits inside the margin:** the group share itself (about $22.1K in year 1, $49.4K in year 2) and the 5% team-link share on follow-on orders (about $0.5K and $1.9K).\n- **Contribution:** year 1 about break-even (−$0.5K); year 2 about +$22K, plus about 2,000 new households.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Who does the work at J&W\n\n- **Fundraising coordinator** (a named team member, as J&W decides): about 3 h/week during the December pilot, 5 h/week on average in year 1, 8 h/week in year 2. Approves team pages, calls organizers, books talks, runs drops and approves agent drafts.\n- **Driver:** about 1.5 h per drop on the existing route day (42 drops in year 1, 85 in year 2).\n- **Packer:** about 2 minutes per order for named bags, plus 30 minutes per drop.\n- **Butcher or the fundraising coordinator:** 1.5 h per \"Fuel for Game Day\" talk, about 2 a month.\n- **Owner (Bill or Stavros):** about 15 minutes a week to approve payouts over $500 and new school partners.\n- **Minastech** builds the storefront and the agents.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Assumptions\n\n- The lean storefront is live by Mon Nov 23, 2026. Four pilot groups come from J&W's own customer base.\n- 42 campaigns in year 1 and 85 in year 2, with 70% of groups returning and running about 1.5 campaigns a year.\n- 25 orders per campaign in year 1 (27 in year 2) at $135 ($138 in year 2).\n- Product mix 55% raw and 45% Meal Butler. Group share averages 15.6%.\n- Margins from model-assumptions.md: cooked 45%, raw 30%.\n- 0.9 unique households per order; 70% new to J&W in year 1 and 65% in year 2.\n- 12% of new households become repeat customers, ordering 4 times a year at $130; 60% of the year-1 repeaters are still active in year 2.\n- Incrementality 0.80 on fundraiser sales (existing customers shifting orders) and 0.85 on follow-on.\n- 60% of follow-on orders use the team link (5% share). 25% of new households redeem the $10 welcome offer.\n- About 60% of campaigns pass $1,500 (talk) and 45% pass $3,000 (raffle box).\n- Team drops run on GTA routes 1–4 only. Long-route areas use home delivery, or a group drop by request for 40+ orders.\n- Ontario-only delivery: J&W's plant is provincially inspected.\n- J&W confirms the price and margin of the 25-pack chicken burgers before launch (see §9).\n\n---\n\n## 9. Risks and how to handle them\n\n- **The share is lower than Halenda's 50–56%.** Lead with dollars per order (about $21 against $12.50–$14.13 per bag), fewer sales per family, no upfront case purchase and food families already buy. If pilot groups still say no, raise the Meal Butler rate to 25% (J&W still keeps about 20%).\n- **Thin margin.** About 21% after the share. Keep the menu short, keep drops on existing route days, and watch contribution per campaign. A campaign below about 12 orders loses money once drop time is counted, so set a 12-order minimum for a team drop (smaller campaigns use Brimley pickup).\n- **Product data must be fixed first.** The 25-pack chicken burgers are listed at $35.50, about $3.79/lb, which may be below the 30% margin model; confirm before launch. The \"Ground Beef Bulk Mix\" title says 5 lb but its description says 10 lb, and its photo file is named \"GROUNDPORK\". J&W's \"salt and sugar free\" line conflicts with some ingredient lists (Beef Chili lists brown sugar and salt). Fundraiser pages make no blanket salt or sugar claims until labels are checked.\n- **Schools.** School boards have fundraising rules, and Ontario's school food and beverage policy (PPM 150) may apply to food sold through a school. Send each principal a one-page school pack. Default schools to Brimley pickup or home delivery until the board approves a school drop.\n- **Cold chain at drops.** Frozen food in coolers, a J&W staffer on site for the whole window, and uncollected orders back to Brimley the same day and held 3 days for free pickup.\n- **Children's names and privacy.** First name, last initial and jersey number only. Families can hide their name. Pages are unlisted and not indexed. Organizers never see buyers' contact details.\n- **CASL.** J&W emails and texts only buyers who gave express consent. Organizers send campaign messages to their own contacts. Drop-day texts are transactional.\n- **Allergens and dietary needs.** Allergen answers come only from approved per-product data or from a person. The Long Weekend Box includes sausages and ribs, so it is flagged for families who avoid pork. J&W does not label packs halal or kosher (only 5 of 979 SKUs mention halal).\n- **Tax and accounting.** The group payment is a marketing cost for J&W, not a charitable gift, and buyers get no tax receipts. J&W's accountant confirms how group payments are recorded, including any HST question.\n- **Peak overlap.** December also carries corporate gifts (Play 5). Cap the pilot at 4 groups and put drops on Tuesday and Wednesday.\n- **Trust.** Schools and faith groups check reviews before they partner. Show the 4.8★ Google rating (611 reviews) and give each group one named coordinator.\n- **Ontario-only sales.** Out-of-province relatives can buy only for pickup or delivery in Ontario; the meat never leaves the province.\n- **Jerky.** Not counted. Development needs approval under the provincial licence and a shelf-life plan. Treat it as a 2027 project, not a launch item.\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n- Halenda's Fundraising home: https://halendasfundraising.com/\n- About the program (two modes, 50–56%, school compliance): https://halendasfundraising.com/about-halendas-fundraising/\n- Purchase page (calculator, tier table, $25 a bag, $18 a case shipping): https://halendasfundraising.com/purchase/\n- Pepperoni Stix case prices ($300 / $288 / $273 / $261): https://halendasfundraising.com/product/halendas-pepperoni-stix/\n- FAQ (case contents, pick-up Oshawa and Etobicoke, shelf life): https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-faqs/\n- Six million meat sticks (start June 27, 2013; post Nov 6, 2025): https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/celebrating-six-million-meat-sticks-sold/\n- Dance studio success story ($10,300): https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/dance-fundraising-success-story/\n- Youth sports success story (8 cases, $1,536): https://halendasfundraising.com/fundraising-resources/youth-sports-fundraising-success-story-paige/\n- Halenda's store locations: https://www.halendas.com/halendas-locations/\n- Halenda's Hibernator freezer packs: https://www.halendas.com/products/meat-freezer-packs/\n- Rowe Farms meat fundraisers (donation shown under each price): https://rowefarmsonline.ca/pages/meat-fundraisers\n- GTHL 2025–26 team entries (493 teams, 54 clubs): https://gthlcanada.com/article/gthl-announces-team-entries-for-2025-2026-season\n- J&W delivery routes and fees: https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/home\n- J&W story: https://www.jwfoods.com/pages/about-us\n- J&W products used: https://www.jwfoods.com/products/chicken-burgers-1 · https://www.jwfoods.com/products/chicken-breast-portioned-4-5-6-8-oz-box-broken-down-and-pkg-in-vacuum-bags · https://www.jwfoods.com/products/ground-beef-10lb-bulk-mix-special · https://www.jwfoods.com/products/kids-favourite-combo-special · https://www.jwfoods.com/products/summer-bbq-box-special · https://www.jwfoods.com/products/beef-chilli\n- J&W Google rating 4.8★ / 611: https://yably.ca/reviews/toronto/j-w-foods-2201-brimley-road\n- CFIA, activities that need a federal licence (interprovincial trade): https://inspection.canada.ca/en/food-licences/food-business-activities\n- Local research files: competitor-07.md, selection.json (pick 7), jw-facts.md, jw-audit.md, model-assumptions.md, market-benchmarks.md (§5, §6b, §8), competitors-gta-meat-grocery.md (§4.2, §4.6, §5), ai-agents-research.md (Agents 3, 5, 6, 12, 13), site/assets/catalog.js, jw-catalog.json"}];
